window.SHOUHAO_CONTENT = {"en":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"zh-CN":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"核心规则","title":"验证码永不外传：最常见、也最致命的盗号入口","excerpt":"六位注册码等于账号钥匙。熟人要码、假客服催码、钓鱼站填码——本质都是同一把刀。本文把话术、原理与拒绝话术一次讲透。","read":"12 分钟","body":"<p>如果你只能记住一条 WhatsApp 防盗规则，请记住这一条：<strong>验证码永远不要告诉任何人，也不要填进任何「看起来很像官方」的网页。</strong>全球多地警方与网络安全机构在通报 WhatsApp 账号接管案件时，几乎都会把「骗取六位验证码」列在最前面。原因很简单——WhatsApp 用手机号作为账号身份，短信或语音里的注册码，就是在新设备上「证明你是机主」的钥匙。</p>\n      <p>很多人误以为盗号一定需要高深黑客技术。现实里，绝大多数成功案例靠的是<strong>社会工程</strong>：骗子利用信任、礼貌、紧迫感和「帮个忙」的心态，让你自己把钥匙递出去。一旦验证码到手，对方就能在自己的手机上完成注册，把你踢下线，再冒充你去骗下一个人。</p>\n\n      <h2>验证码到底是什么</h2>\n      <p>当你或任何人试图用某个手机号登录/注册 WhatsApp 时，系统会向该号码发送一串通常为六位数的注册码（也可能通过语音电话播报）。正确输入这串数字，就等于告诉 WhatsApp：「持有这张 SIM、能收到短信的人，就是账号主人。」</p>\n      <p>因此它和银行卡动态口令、邮箱二次验证码属于同一类敏感信息。区别在于：WhatsApp 的验证码一旦被滥用，损失往往不只是「一个 App」，而是你的整本通讯录、群聊信任关系，以及亲友可能被二次诈骗的金钱风险。</p>\n\n      <h2>骗子最常用的话术长什么样</h2>\n      <p>话术会变，结构很少变。下面这些句子，近年在多个国家的警情通报里反复出现：</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>「验证码发错到你手机了，帮我回一下那六个数字。」——常见于熟人账号已被盗后的连环骗。</li>\n        <li>「我是 WhatsApp / Meta / 客服，你的号异常，把验证码发给我才能解封。」——官方不会这样要码。</li>\n        <li>「银行风控 / 快递丢失 / 税费退款，需要你提供 WhatsApp 验证码配合核验。」——跨渠道冒充。</li>\n        <li>「投票、抽奖、问卷只要 10 秒」——假站先收手机号，再收你刚收到的短信码。</li>\n        <li>「我帮你朋友报名抽奖，他把验证码发你了，你转给我就行。」——把你卷进别人的骗局叙事。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>判断标准只有一个：</strong>只要对方目标是「拿到你手机上刚出现的 WhatsApp 注册码」，无论故事多么感人、多么紧急、对方头像多么眼熟，都按盗号处理。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>为什么「熟人要码」特别危险</h2>\n      <p>骗子很少一上来就用陌生号骗你。更高效的做法是：先拿下 A，再用 A 的聊天窗口找 B。B 看到熟悉的名字和头像，防备会立刻下降。B 交出验证码后，骗子得到 B，再去骗 C——形成<strong>通讯录传染病</strong>。</p>\n      <p>所以当你收到「好友」索要验证码时，正确反应不是「他平时挺好的，应该没事」，而是：<strong>他的号可能已经不在他手里了。</strong>请改用电话打到你通讯录里原来的号码，或当面确认。不要用对方临时发来的「新号码」回拨作为唯一核身手段。</p>\n\n      <h2>正确拒绝话术（可直接复制）</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>「验证码我不会发给任何人，包括亲戚。你如果真是本人，打电话给我。」</li>\n        <li>「WhatsApp 官方不会要验证码。这条我当诈骗处理了。」</li>\n        <li>「我这边不能协助转码。你去重新登录自己的号就行。」</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>说完即可拉黑或举报，不必继续解释。骗子靠的就是把你拖进长对话，让你不好意思拒绝。</p>\n\n      <h2>如果你已经把码发出去了</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>立刻在本机打开 WhatsApp，尝试用自己的号码重新验证登录，把对方踢下线。</li>\n        <li>登录成功后检查「已关联的设备」，登出全部不明会话。</li>\n        <li>重设两步验证 PIN，并确认恢复邮箱仍是你的。</li>\n        <li>马上通知亲友：忽略任何「你」发出的借钱、要码、投票链接。</li>\n        <li>若已发生转账损失，联系银行并考虑报警。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>验证码不是「随便一个数字」，而是账号主权。HoldLine把这条放在第一篇，是因为它挡住的攻击面最大、成本最低、也最容易教给家人。把「谁要码都不给」练成肌肉反应，后面所有进阶设置才会真正发挥作用。</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"防护设置","title":"两步验证完整指南：PIN、恢复邮箱与常见坑","excerpt":"开启两步验证后，光有短信码往往不够。本篇按步骤写清怎么开、PIN 怎么设、邮箱为什么不能省，以及被抢先设 PIN 时怎么办。","read":"14 分钟","body":"<p>两步验证是 WhatsApp 官方反复推荐的基础防护。它的核心思想很朴素：就算骗子暂时拿到了你的短信验证码，在新设备完成注册时，还需要你另外设定的 PIN（或后续升级形态的密码）。少了第二道门，接管成本会显著上升。</p>\n      <p>很多用户「听说要开」却一直拖着，或开了却跳过邮箱。结果真正出事时，不是防不住，就是找回过程被拖成几天。这篇把完整做法与坑点一次写清楚。</p>\n\n      <h2>它能防什么，不能防什么</h2>\n      <p><strong>能显著加强：</strong>对方试图用你的号码在另一台手机上重新注册；你短暂泄露了一次短信码后的补救窗口；降低「只靠骗码」成功率。</p>\n      <p><strong>不能单独解决：</strong>你已经把 PIN 也告诉了对方；手机被木马实时偷看屏幕；你主动扫描了对方的关联二维码（网页端挂马）；你把解锁手机借给别人随意操作。</p>\n      <p>所以两步验证是「必要非充分」条件：必须开，但还要配合验证码纪律与关联设备巡检。</p>\n\n      <h2>开启路径（以常见版本为准）</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>打开 WhatsApp，进入<strong>设置</strong>。</li>\n        <li>点<strong>账户</strong> → <strong>两步验证</strong>。</li>\n        <li>选择开启，按提示创建 6 位 PIN（部分地区/版本可能引导设置更强密码）。</li>\n        <li>再次确认 PIN。</li>\n        <li>添加<strong>你能长期登录的电子邮箱</strong>作为恢复方式，并完成邮箱验证（若有）。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>不同手机品牌的菜单文案可能略有出入（例如从头像进入设置），但「账户 → 两步验证」这一层逻辑通常稳定。若找不到，用应用内搜索「两步」关键词。</p>\n\n      <h2>PIN 应该怎么设</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>不要用生日、纪念日、车牌、手机尾号、身份证片段。</li>\n        <li>不要用 123456、000000、888888、112233 这类模式。</li>\n        <li>不要与微信/支付宝/银行卡/邮箱密码相同或简单变换。</li>\n        <li>建议写进密码管理器，或写在只有你能看到的离线位置；不要发到群里「备份」。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>PIN 的目标不是「好记到不用写」，而是「别人猜不到、也套不走」。记不住可以保管，但不要因此设成弱密码。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>为什么恢复邮箱几乎必填</h2>\n      <p>开启两步验证后，若你忘记 PIN，官方流程往往依赖你登记过的邮箱来协助重置；若没有可用邮箱，你可能需要等待一段时间（公开资料中常见描述为最长约七天）才能在无 PIN 情况下继续。更糟的情况是：盗号者抢先开启两步验证并绑定他们的邮箱，你夺回时会更被动。</p>\n      <p>因此邮箱要满足：</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>是你本人长期使用、能收信的。</li>\n        <li>该邮箱本身也有强密码与二次验证。</li>\n        <li>不要用已经废弃、公司即将离职清理、或与他人共用的邮箱。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>开启之后还要做什么</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>立刻检查「已关联的设备」，清掉不明会话。</li>\n        <li>告诉家人：两步验证的 PIN 同样不能告诉「客服」。</li>\n        <li>每隔几个月或换机后，确认两步验证仍处于开启状态。</li>\n        <li>若曾怀疑泄露，直接关掉再重新开启，换一套新 PIN。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>被要求输入「我没设过的 PIN」</h2>\n      <p>这通常意味着：有人曾控制你的号并启用了两步验证。请先完成短信注册码验证（这一步常能把对方主会话踢下线），再按界面提示处理 PIN。详细应急见本站「陌生 PIN」专题。核心原则是：<strong>不要因为卡在 PIN 就放弃重新验证；也不要把短信码发给自称能帮你「破解 PIN」的人。</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>两步验证花费不到两分钟，却能把「骗到一次短信码就能偷号」变成「还得再过你设的门」。配合恢复邮箱与验证码纪律，它是HoldLine建议的「今天必须完成」事项之一。</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"防护设置","title":"已关联设备完全手册：如何发现静默偷窥","excerpt":"手机还能正常聊天，不代表没有第二双眼睛。网页版/电脑版一旦被挂上，对方可实时同步。教你检查、踢人、以及怎样避免再次中招。","read":"13 分钟","body":"<p>有一种盗号并不戏剧：你没有突然被登出，聊天也还在，直到某天亲友说「你刚才问我要验证码」，或你在设置里看见一个从不认识的浏览器会话。这就是<strong>关联设备静默接入</strong>——攻击者不一定夺走手机主会话，而是让 WhatsApp Web / 桌面端挂在旁边同步消息。</p>\n      <p>对骗子来说，这种方式极其划算：你越晚发现，他们就能读越多群聊、搜集越多借钱话术素材，甚至直接以你的口吻在对话里操作。</p>\n\n      <h2>关联设备机制简述</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp 允许你在手机主客户端之外，把电脑、平板、浏览器等关联为配套设备。正规用法是：在手机上打开「已关联的设备」→「关联新设备」，再扫描官方网页或桌面应用显示的二维码。</p>\n      <p>风险点在于：这个「扫码授权」流程本身是合法功能。骗子要做的，只是把<strong>他们控制的二维码</strong>放到假网站、假投票页、或直接在你解锁的手机上操作完成扫码。</p>\n\n      <h2>如何检查（请现在就做一次）</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>打开 WhatsApp → <strong>设置</strong> → <strong>已关联的设备</strong>（Android 有时在右上角更多菜单）。</li>\n        <li>查看列表中每一个条目：设备类型、大致活跃时间、名称是否眼熟。</li>\n        <li>对任何「我不记得关联过」的项目：点进去 → <strong>退出 / 登出</strong>。</li>\n        <li>若列表很长且你曾公用电脑登录，建议不熟悉的全部退出，需要时再重新官方关联。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>养成习惯：每周固定一天（例如周日晚上）花 30 秒扫一眼列表。借出手机、点击可疑链接、用过酒店电脑之后，立刻加查一次。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>高危场景清单</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>扫描了聊天里发来的「验证 / 投票 / 领奖」二维码。</li>\n        <li>在非官方网站看到二维码，并按指示用 WhatsApp 去扫。</li>\n        <li>把已解锁手机交给别人「帮忙看个东西」「帮我连网页版」。</li>\n        <li>在网吧/展会公用电脑勾选了保持登录，事后忘记登出。</li>\n        <li>电脑中毒后，浏览器里的 WhatsApp Web 会话被共用。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>发现陌生设备后的处理顺序</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>立即登出该设备（以及所有不确定设备）。</li>\n        <li>检查是否仍能控制主手机会话；若曾被挤下线，按夺回流程重新验证号码。</li>\n        <li>重设两步验证。</li>\n        <li>回顾最近点过的链接、扫描过的码，避免重复中招。</li>\n        <li>通知近期聊过敏感话题的联系人提高警惕。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>安全使用电脑版的原则</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>只从官方渠道获取桌面应用，或自己在浏览器输入官方网页地址。</li>\n        <li>关联动作只从手机 App 内发起，不从陌生页面「倒逼」你去扫。</li>\n        <li>公用设备不要保持登录；用完回手机端踢掉。</li>\n        <li>长期不用的电脑会话，宁可多余登出。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>「已关联的设备」是你账号的后门清单。它不是高级功能页，而是安全巡检页。把清查做成习惯，很多「我也不知道怎么被看光」的案例，其实在第一周就能被截住。</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"骗局识破","title":"假投票、假抽奖、假助力：熟人转发也不能信","excerpt":"「帮我女儿投一票」常常来自已被盗的账号。假站要短信码或诱导扫关联码，是近年增长很快的接管手法。","read":"12 分钟","body":"<p>善意是社交软件里最容易被武器化的品质。骗子很清楚：直接跟你说「把验证码给我」会触发警报；但换成「帮小朋友投票，只要十秒」，很多人会点进去。欧洲等地的网络安全机构都曾公开预警：盗号者控制熟人账号后，群发投票/竞赛助力请求，再把流量导向仿冒网站。</p>\n\n      <h2>一场典型攻击如何展开</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>骗子已控制你朋友的 WhatsApp。</li>\n        <li>你收到朋友私聊或群消息：「拜托投一下，今天截止！」并附链接。</li>\n        <li>网页做得像学校、舞蹈比赛、公益投票，要求输入手机号。</li>\n        <li>你的手机收到 WhatsApp/短信验证码——其实是注册码。</li>\n        <li>你把码填进网页，对方立刻在另一台设备完成接管；或</li>\n        <li>网页展示二维码，叫你「用 WhatsApp 扫码完成投票」，实则完成设备关联。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>第一种让你失去主账号；第二种可能让你在「手机仍可用」的情况下被同步偷窥。两者都危险。</p>\n\n      <h2>为什么预览和页面看起来很真</h2>\n      <p>现代钓鱼会准备像样的 logo、倒计时、候选人照片，甚至做多语言。链接预览卡片也能做得像正规站点。你不能靠「好不好看」判断真伪，要靠<strong>它要求你做什么</strong>：</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>要 WhatsApp/短信验证码？高危。</li>\n        <li>要你用「关联设备」扫码？高危。</li>\n        <li>要你下载未知 APK 才能投票？高危。</li>\n        <li>要银行卡拍照、OTP？已经从盗号升级到盗刷。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>收到投票请求时的标准动作</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>先假设对方账号可能已被盗。</li>\n        <li>用电话或当面问：「你是不是在拉票？有没有发链接？」</li>\n        <li>在确认前，不点链接、不填码、不扫码。</li>\n        <li>若对方否认，提醒他检查关联设备并重设两步验证。</li>\n        <li>把原消息举报，避免继续在群里传播。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>真正的公开投票极少需要你提供 WhatsApp 注册码。需要登录的平台，也应走该平台自己的账号体系，而不是「通用手机验证码万能盒」。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>若你已经点过或填过</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>填过短信码：立刻重新验证自己的 WhatsApp 号码夺回，再清设备、开两步验证。</li>\n        <li>扫过二维码：马上到「已关联的设备」踢人。</li>\n        <li>下过安装包：停用未知应用，考虑杀毒/重装评估，并检查短信与辅助功能权限。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>家庭与公司里怎么统一口径</h2>\n      <p>一个人警惕还不够。假投票链接最容易在家族群、同学群、公司群里二次转发，每一次转发都在给骗子「洗白」来源。建议在固定群置顶一句短规则：「任何要 WhatsApp 验证码或扫码投票的链接，先打电话核实；未核实前不要点。」规则越短，越容易被遵守。</p>\n      <p>若你是群管理员，发现此类消息应立刻删除并提醒成员检查关联设备。若发送者是熟人，优先用其他渠道提醒对方「你的号可能已被盗」，而不是只在原聊天里回「这是诈骗」——因为对面可能根本不是本人在看。</p>\n\n      <h2>和「真投票」如何区分</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>正规活动几乎不会要你提供 WhatsApp 注册码。</li>\n        <li>正规活动若需登录，通常走自己的账号体系，而不是通用「短信码万能验证」。</li>\n        <li>正规活动不会诱导你用「已关联的设备」去扫来路不明的二维码。</li>\n        <li>真正紧急的公益/校园投票，也经得起你花一分钟打电话问本人。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>把「区分真假」的负担，从「看页面漂不漂亮」转移到「它有没有越权索取 WhatsApp 钥匙」。这一步想清楚，绝大多数假投票站会原形毕露。</p>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>假投票骗局打的是「熟人 + 善意 + 短时操作」组合拳。把规则改成：<strong>所有要码或扫码的投票，先核实、默认不信</strong>，就能避开一大类当前非常活跃的盗号路径。善意值得保留，但钥匙不能随手交出。</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"骗局识破","title":"熟人连环骗码：通讯录如何变成传播链","excerpt":"一个号失守，往往带着整本通讯录一起失火。拆解连环接管的传播机制，以及如何在第二环就切断。","read":"14 分钟","body":"<p>单独看，「把验证码发给朋友」像一次疏忽；从攻击者视角，这是可复制的增长策略。新加坡、马来西亚等地警方通报过的模式高度相似：骗子先用钓鱼或骗码拿下第一个号 → 事主被挤下线 → 骗子用该身份向整本通讯录索要「误发的验证码」 → 下一名受害人中招 → 链条继续。整个过程往往在两小时内完成十几层扩散，因为每一层发送者都是你「认识并信任」的人。</p>\n      <p>连环骗码也被称为「接管链」或「通讯录传染病」。它不依赖一次群发广告，而是利用人际网络的信任边不断跳跃。对防御方而言，这意味着：<strong>你的安全不只取决于自己守不守纪律，还取决于你通讯录里是否有薄弱环节</strong>——但更重要的是，当你处于「被要码」这一环时，你有机会成为整条链的终点。</p>\n\n      <h2>链条上的每一个角色</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>0 号入口：</strong>最早被骗码、中钓鱼、扫了假投票码，或关联设备被静默挂上的人。他们未必立刻发现被盗。</li>\n        <li><strong>传播节点：</strong>每个新被接管的账号，都自动成为下一波「可信发送者」。头像、昵称、近期聊天语境都是真的——只是操作者换了人。</li>\n        <li><strong>筛选节点：</strong>骗子会优先私聊近期聊过天的联系人、家人、同事，成功率远高于陌生推销。</li>\n        <li><strong>变现节点：</strong>积累够多号后，开始借钱、要银行卡截图、要支付 OTP、拉假投资群，把「信任资产」换成现金。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>对你而言，最关键的觉悟是：当你成为「被要码的那个人」时，你处在切断链条的最佳位置。你一拒绝，这一支就断了；你一妥协，你的通讯录会变成别人的弹药库。</p>\n\n      <h2>典型话术为何总能骗到人</h2>\n      <p>「验证码发错了，帮我念一下」这句话之所以有效，是因为它同时满足了三个心理按钮：<strong>熟人可信</strong>（头像没变）、<strong>互助义务</strong>（帮个小忙）、<strong>操作极简</strong>（复制六个数字）。骗子还会追加「别告诉别人」「我在开会不方便打电话」，阻止你用最有效的核实手段——语音通话。</p>\n      <p>在职场环境里，变体包括「人事系统要验证码」「公司 VPN 登记」；在家庭环境里，变体包括「医院挂号」「学校报名」。故事换皮，核心不变：要你交出手机刚收到的 WhatsApp 注册码。</p>\n\n      <h2>反常信号（即使头像很熟）</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>突然讨论验证码、OTP、银行卡照片、身份证正反面。</li>\n        <li>语气过分急切，阻止你打电话或视频。</li>\n        <li>深夜/工作时间用很不像本人的措辞（过于正式或过于急促）。</li>\n        <li>刚要完码，又开始借钱、拉群、发投资链接。</li>\n        <li>同一时段多个好友同时跟你说「某某在要我的码」。</li>\n        <li>对方称「号换了」却不愿用旧号码接听你的回拨。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>家规级规则：</strong>任何人在 WhatsApp 里要验证码，默认视为「对方号可能被盗」，先电话核实，绝不先给码。这条规则对子女、对父母、对老板都一视同仁。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>切断链条的家庭与公司做法</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>建立共识：验证码请求 = 默认诈骗警报，不是「不好意思拒绝」的社交场合。</li>\n        <li>用短信、邮件或家庭群（非 WhatsApp 亦可）群发一次提醒：「任何人用 WhatsApp 要码都当作号被盗。」</li>\n        <li>公司 IT 或行政准备固定公告模板，发现异常联系人立刻置顶，并指定值班人接听核实电话。</li>\n        <li>中招者夺回后，必须在相关群里用<strong>其他已验证渠道</strong>二次声明「已夺回，此前消息作废」。</li>\n        <li>定期家庭/团队演练：互相扮演骗码者，练习三句拒绝话术。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>如果你是被盗本人：止损时间线</h2>\n      <p>除了按本站「夺回流程」重新验证号码，请把「通知联系人」放在与「清关联设备」同等优先级。建议顺序：</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>夺回主会话并开启或重设两步验证。</li>\n        <li>立即用短信群发核心联系人：「我的 WhatsApp 刚才被盗，凡是要码、借钱、投票链接均非本人。」</li>\n        <li>请一位可信亲友用其他渠道在家族群/同事群代为公告。</li>\n        <li>回顾被盗前 48 小时你发过什么，判断链条可能延伸到哪一层。</li>\n        <li>若已有亲友转账，协助其联系银行并保留证据，必要时通过当地警方渠道报案。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>常见错误是：夺回后以为「结束了」，却没有对外警告，导致骗子在你重新上线前仍用残留信任继续借钱。</p>\n\n      <h2>常见误区</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>误区：「对方是亲戚，给一下没关系。」——亲戚的号同样会被盗，且骗子专挑亲属链。</li>\n        <li>误区：「我只给码不给 PIN 就安全。」——注册码足以完成接管；PIN 是第二道门，不能先送钥匙。</li>\n        <li>误区：「我很快撤消息就行。」——对方自动化脚本可在你反应前完成注册。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>连环骗码不是「偶发人际乌龙」，而是结构化、可规模化的传播攻击。把通讯录里的每一个人都训练成「拒绝点」，比只加固自己的手机更重要——因为攻击总会从最松的那一环进来。记住：你拒绝一次要码，可能同时保护了你背后十几位联系人的号与钱。</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"骗局识破","title":"屏幕共享盗号：视频通话里的隔空取码","excerpt":"假冒官员或银行人员要求共享屏幕，再让验证码出现在你的画面上。你没有「发送」数字，码却已经被看走。","read":"13 分钟","body":"<p>传统骗码需要你把数字打出来发给对方；屏幕共享骗局把这一步省略了。骗子通过 WhatsApp 视频、Zoom、Teams 甚至系统自带的「屏幕共享」功能建立「官方感」，再以账户解冻、税务核查、反洗钱排查、快递理赔为由，要求你立刻共享整个手机屏幕。随后他们指挥你打开短信、打开 WhatsApp 重新登录、或点开银行 App——六位注册码、支付动态口令、甚至两步验证界面都会完整出现在对方画面里。</p>\n      <p>多国警方通报中出现过伪装成执法机关、税务局、央行客服、银行风控的版本。对不熟悉数字业务的用户，这套流程看起来「很专业」：有视频、有工牌截图、有术语、还有倒计时压迫。你可能全程觉得自己「没有发送任何验证码」，但对方已经用眼抄走了数字。</p>\n\n      <h2>为什么共享屏幕格外危险</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>锁屏通知横幅可能在共享中直接显示验证码，无需你点开短信。</li>\n        <li>对方可指导你进入「已关联的设备」扫码、关闭两步验证、修改邮箱。</li>\n        <li>若你同时共享了电脑桌面，浏览器里保存的密码、会话 Cookie 也可能暴露。</li>\n        <li>骗局尾声常要求安装「远程协助」App，实则为长期控制通道。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>视频里常见的压迫话术</h2>\n      <p>「不共享就冻结账户」「不配合就涉嫌违法」「IT 正在远程抢救你的号」——这些都是为了让你在 30 秒内做出决定。正规金融机构与政府部门通常不会在私人 WhatsApp 视频里完成核身；需要核实身份时，应通过你<strong>自行查找</strong>的官方 App、官网或网点渠道进行，具体以官方帮助中心说明与当地正规渠道为准。</p>\n\n      <h2>防御清单（按优先级）</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>任何自称官方、银行、平台客服的视频来电，若要求共享屏幕：默认挂断。</li>\n        <li>挂断后用你自己保存的官方电话或 App 内客服入口回拨，不用聊天里给的号码。</li>\n        <li>若因工作安排必须屏幕共享（如 IT 远程排障），先退出银行与即时通讯 App，关闭锁屏通知预览，屏幕共享时禁止点开任何验证码相关界面。</li>\n        <li>教长辈：微信/FaceTime 里的「共享屏幕」与电脑投屏，对验证码而言与「发码」同等危险。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>家规建议：</strong>把「视频通话 + 共享屏幕 + 要操作手机」写成红色组合。长辈只需记住：见到这个组合就挂断，再让子女陪同事务。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>若已经共享过并可能泄露</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>立即停止使用共享，挂断全部远程会话。</li>\n        <li>在本机重新验证 WhatsApp 号码，清查「已关联的设备」。</li>\n        <li>重设两步验证 PIN 与恢复邮箱；银行与支付账户改密。</li>\n        <li>卸载可疑远程控制类应用，必要时请专业人士做设备体检。</li>\n        <li>通知亲友警惕「你」近期发出的异常消息。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>常见误区</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>「我只共享了一半屏幕就没事」——通知栏验证码仍可见。</li>\n        <li>「对方穿着制服就可信」——视频里的形象均可伪造。</li>\n        <li>「我挂断就安全了」——若码已被抄走，须立刻夺回并重设门禁。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>隔空取码证明：安全不只取决于「你肯不肯把码打出来」，还取决于「谁正在看你的屏幕」。把屏幕共享当成高权限操作，而不是视频聊天的附属功能；对验证码而言，眼睛与手指一样是泄密通道。</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"骗局识破","title":"合并通话听验证码：别把陌生人并进你的电话","excerpt":"骗子诱使你合并三方通话，让线路接听 WhatsApp 语音验证码——你没转发数字，码却被听走。","read":"12 分钟","body":"<p>电话合并（三方通话、会议呼叫）本是日常功能，却被改造成盗号工具。公开报道中的手法大致是：陌生或半陌生来电，自称快递、医保、学校教务处，称需要你和「负责人」三方确认；或假装你的亲友出了急事，请你把电话转接给「律师」「警官」。当你按指示拨打某个号码并点「合并通话」时，线路另一端往往是 WhatsApp 刚发起的<strong>语音验证呼叫</strong>，自动播报六位注册码，骗子全程在线静听。</p>\n      <p>这类骗局在老年用户中尤其常见，因为「帮他接通一下」听起来像普通的电话礼仪，且不涉及在聊天里打字发码，心理防线更低。</p>\n\n      <h2>它和骗短信码有何不同</h2>\n      <p>短信码骗局依赖你「看见并转发」；合并通话骗局依赖你「听见却不知情」。有时骗子还会让你同时收到短信码，再让你在合并线路上「念给客服听」，实质仍是把注册码交给第三方。你可能全程没有在 WhatsApp 里输入任何内容，甚至以为自己只是在做善意转接。</p>\n\n      <h2>诈骗剧本的两段式结构</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>铺垫：</strong>制造紧急情境（涉案、退款、包裹被扣），让你愿意配合操作电话。</li>\n        <li><strong>导火线：</strong>给你一个「官方热线」号码，让你先呼叫再合并；该号码触发 WhatsApp 语音验证或银行 OTP 语音播报。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>警惕任何要求你「先拨某个固定号码再合并」的电话，无论对方自称谁。</p>\n\n      <h2>防护规则</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>不与陌生来电做合并通话；不帮陌生人「转接当局」。</li>\n        <li>亲友急事：挂断可疑线，打通讯录里的老号码核实。</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp 语音验证码与短信码同等机密，不能在任何通话中口播给他人。</li>\n        <li>若对方说「别挂断，合并就行」，几乎都是高危信号。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>运营商客服、银行、WhatsApp 官方均不会通过电话合并让你「收听验证码完成验证」。遇到此类说法，以官方帮助中心说明为准，不要继续通话。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>若你曾经合并过可疑通话</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>立刻查看 WhatsApp 是否仍在线、是否收到「已在其他设备注册」提示。</li>\n        <li>若仍可登录：清关联设备，重设两步验证；若被踢下线：按夺回流程重新验证。</li>\n        <li>向运营商咨询呼叫转移、无条件转移是否被恶意开通。</li>\n        <li>通知近期联系人：若收到「你」异常消息请警惕。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>不要把通话合并当成没有安全含义的操作。对验证码场景而言，耳朵与眼睛一样可以泄密。拒绝陌生合并、拒绝在电话里念码，是成本几乎为零却常被忽视的防线。</p>\n<h2>与运营商呼叫转移的关系</h2>\n      <p>部分诈骗在合并通话之外，还会诱导你在手机上开通「呼叫转移」或「无应答转移」，把将来所有来电转给骗子接听。合并通话偷听的是当下这一通；呼叫转移影响的是未来的每一通。若你曾按陌生指示拨打过特殊代码（例如以 ** 或 ## 开头的功能码），请立刻致电运营商官方客服，查询并关闭全部转移，具体以运营商官方说明为准。</p>\n      <p>日常可养成习惯：每隔几个月向运营商确认业务开通状态；发现无法接到别人电话时，把「是否被转移」与「是否换卡」列入同一优先排查清单。</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"风险加深","title":"SIM 卡劫持与换卡攻击：当短信收件箱不再属于你","excerpt":"号码被转到新卡后，验证码会进攻击者手机。理解运营商侧风险，并学会用两步验证与 SIM PIN 降低伤害。","read":"14 分钟","body":"<p>SIM swapping（换卡/号卡劫持）属于更重的攻击：对方通过伪造身份、社工话术或 利用运营商流程漏洞，让电信公司把你的号码激活到他们控制的 SIM 上。完成后，你的手机可能突然显示「无服务」，而本该发到你这里的短信 OTP，却在骗子手机里响起。WhatsApp 以手机号为主键，因此这是直指账号主权的打击。</p>\n      <p>若未开启两步验证，对方拿到短信码即可重新注册；即便你开了两步验证，你也会立刻陷入「运营商夺回号码」与「WhatsApp 夺回会话」的双线竞速。高调公众人物长期是目标，但普通用户也可能因经营号、投资群、或单纯通讯录质量被选中。</p>\n\n      <h2>可能出现的征兆</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>手机无故长时间无服务，飞行模式开关无效，或提示「未插入 SIM」。</li>\n        <li>突然收不到任何短信，但银行提示异常登录。</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp 提示号码已在其他设备完成注册。</li>\n        <li>亲友收到「你」的借钱消息，而你的手机根本无法打开 WhatsApp。</li>\n        <li>运营商向你发来换卡成功通知，而你并未办理。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>第一时间应急（按顺序）</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>用另一部手机或固话，拨打运营商官方客服（以官方 App、官网或账单上的渠道为准）申报异常换卡，要求冻结过户与补办 SIM。</li>\n        <li>号码恢复后，立刻在本机重新验证 WhatsApp，并清关联设备。</li>\n        <li>通知银行与支付平台关注异常 OTP 与转账；重设关键账户密码。</li>\n        <li>用短信告知亲友：近期任何 WhatsApp 借钱请求勿信。</li>\n        <li>保留运营商工单、短信记录，必要时向当地警方渠道报案。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>日常降风险措施</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>向运营商咨询可否设置换卡/过户的额外口令或到厅办理限制。</li>\n        <li>为 SIM 启用 PIN 码，防止实体卡被他人直接插入使用。</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp 必须开启两步验证，并绑定本人邮箱。</li>\n        <li>重要金融账户优先使用 App 令牌、硬件密钥，而不是单靠短信。</li>\n        <li>社交平台上避免公开完整手机号、身份证信息与住址组合。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>换卡攻击针对的是「谁控制短信收件箱」。运营商侧加锁 + WhatsApp 两步验证 + 失联预案，三件套缺一不可。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>夺回后的复盘</h2>\n      <p>确认骗子是否曾在你的号码上完成 WhatsApp 注册、是否群发过诈骗消息。若有，需用其他渠道澄清，并协助已被骗的亲友止付。考虑是否将号码关联的高价值账户暂时加强监控。</p>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>SIM 劫持把手机号从「联系方式」升级为「安全根」。建立「突然无服务＝最高警报」的条件反射，比事后争夺号码更省时间。</p>\n<h2>与银行、邮箱的联动风险</h2>\n      <p>换卡成功后，攻击者不只盯 WhatsApp，也可能在同一窗口重置银行短信验证、邮箱找回密码。因此在申报运营商的同时，主动致电银行客服说明情况、冻结异常渠道，并检查邮箱异地登录记录。把「手机号失联」视为账户矩阵级事件，而不是单一 App 故障。</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"骗局识破","title":"仿冒官网与钓鱼链接：看起来像，也不要点","excerpt":"从域名、话术压迫感到「要你做什么」，系统教你拆穿假登录页。需要网页版时，只走你自己输入的官方地址。","read":"13 分钟","body":"<p>钓鱼攻击的本质，是伪造你熟悉的信任符号：Meta 绿、锁形图标、客服话术、倒计时、甚至和真站几乎一样的域名拼写。攻击者不需要破解 WhatsApp 的端到端加密，只要诱使你在假「登录柜台上」亲手输入手机号与六位注册码，或下载套壳应用，就能把账号钥匙交出去。</p>\n      <p>近年钓鱼页还演化出「渐进式」流程：先让你填手机号「预约解封」，几秒后真的收到官方短信码——你误以为流程正规，遂在假站填入刚收到的码。攻击成功点，就在于用户把「能收到真短信」误当成「网站是真的」。</p>\n\n      <h2>常见投放渠道</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>WhatsApp 私聊、群发里的短链与「账号将永久封禁」横幅。</li>\n        <li>短信/邮件冒充 WhatsApp Business 审核、退款、高清通话开通。</li>\n        <li>搜索引擎广告位上的「WhatsApp Web 登录」假冒站。</li>\n        <li>已被盗账号转发的「官方公告」与假更新包。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>点开前的四问自检</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>我是否本来就在办这件事？无预兆的「24 小时内不验证就封号」高度可疑。</li>\n        <li>浏览器地址栏是不是我亲手输入的域名？警惕 whatsapp-verify、wa-login、多连字符、奇怪后缀。</li>\n        <li>页面索要的，是不是注册码、银行 OTP、助记词、远程桌面？任意一项都应退出。</li>\n        <li>是否诱导下载 APK、描述文件、或开启无障碍/屏幕录制？常见于安卓侧木马链。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>安全打开网页版/电脑版的唯一姿势</h2>\n      <p>自己打开浏览器，手动输入官方域名或使用官方桌面客户端；在手机 WhatsApp 内发起「关联设备」再扫码。切勿从聊天中的蓝字链跳转登录。扫码页上的二维码只能来自<strong>你刚打开的官方页面</strong>，不能扫聊天里他人发来的图。</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>把「官方入口必须自己建造」写成肌肉记忆：别人递过来的链接，只能当参考，不能当入口。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>点错之后分级处理</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>仅浏览：</strong>关页，不二次点击；警惕后续来电跟进。</li>\n        <li><strong>已填注册码：</strong>立刻本机重验号码，清设备，重设两步验证。</li>\n        <li><strong>已交银行卡/邮箱密码：</strong>对应账户紧急改密，联系银行；邮箱检查异地登录规则。</li>\n        <li><strong>已装未知 APK：</strong>断网卸载，必要时恢复出厂并做好各平台重登加固。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>把钓鱼链接截图举报给 WhatsApp，并在群里简短预警，减少连锁伤亡。</p>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>加密保护传输，不保护你走进假柜台的手。域名自制、行为自检、索码即退——三招能把大多数钓鱼拦在门外。</p>\n<h2>识别「真预览、假网页」组合</h2>\n      <p>聊天里的链接预览可以显示正规域名缩略图，实际跳转却是另一地址。长按链接查看完整 URL，或复制到备忘录检查，再决定是否打开。iOS/Android 上都要警惕「第一次点 A 域名，第二次跟到 B 域名」的重定向链。</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"应急处理","title":"账号被盗夺回全流程：按官方逻辑一步步做","excerpt":"重新用自己的号码完成注册验证，是把他人踢走的关键。本文按顺序写出夺回、清设备、通知亲友与避免二次失守。","read":"16 分钟","body":"<p>发现 WhatsApp 被盗时，人的第一反应常是混乱：想举报、想找人代刷、又被亲友追问「刚才那条借钱是不是你」。请先稳住节奏——根据 WhatsApp 帮助中心的公开说明，当问题是「他人正在使用你的号码」时，<strong>在你自己安全的主手机上，用本号码重新完成注册验证</strong>，是移除非法会话的核心步骤。正确输入你本人收到的短信或语音注册码后，对方在主设备上的使用通常会被挤下线。</p>\n      <p>本流程不保证一分钟搞定：你可能还要处理陌生两步验证 PIN、清理关联设备、对外公告止损。按顺序做，比并行乱试更省时。</p>\n\n      <h2>夺回前 60 秒：四问</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>我还能打开 WhatsApp 吗？能打开就先清「已关联的设备」，别急着和骗子聊天。</li>\n        <li>我是否已被踢回注册页？是则直接进入重验流程。</li>\n        <li>手机是否可能中毒或被远程控制？若是，先断网杀毒，否则夺回也会再失。</li>\n        <li>是否在同时遭遇换卡无信号？先联系运营商，再谈 App 夺回。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>标准夺回步骤</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>在<strong>可信的本机</strong>重装或打开官方 WhatsApp（仅官方商店来源）。</li>\n        <li>输入你的完整国际格式手机号，索取注册码。</li>\n        <li>收码时遮挡屏幕，<strong>绝不</strong>发给任何人；输入本机。</li>\n        <li>若要求两步验证 PIN：输入你记得的；若是陌生 PIN，见本站专题，但仍应完成 SMS 验证以先踢人。</li>\n        <li>登录后 → 设置 → 已关联的设备 → 全部退出不明项。</li>\n        <li>关闭再开启两步验证，换新 PIN，确认恢复邮箱仍属于你。</li>\n        <li>检查隐私与安全相关设置是否被篡改；排除非官方客户端。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>反直觉但重要：仅改两步验证 PIN，不能单独把已登入的骗子请走；重新验证号码才是关键。也不要在夺回前盲发「举报自己账号」类操作，以免干扰恢复，具体以官方帮助中心指引为准。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>夺回中的对外沟通</h2>\n      <p>并行做两件事：技术上踢人，人际上止血。</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>用短信/电话通知家人：过去 24 小时「我」在 WhatsApp 要钱要码均假。</li>\n        <li>请可信同事在非 WhatsApp 渠道代发公司群声明。</li>\n        <li>若已经发生转账，指导受害方立即联系银行止付，并保留聊天证据，视情况通过当地警方渠道报案。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>夺回后 24–72 小时加固</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>再次复查关联设备，开启应用锁。</li>\n        <li>复盘失守点：钓鱼？熟人要码？假投票？共享屏幕？</li>\n        <li>给核心联系人同步你的「核身规则」（例如借钱必打电话）。</li>\n        <li>若经营号被盗，按商务专题发布客户通知。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>常见错误</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>把注册码发给「代找回」陌生人——二次被盗。</li>\n        <li>夺回成功却未公告，亲友继续上当。</li>\n        <li>只改密码不清关联设备，网页端仍被同步。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>夺回拼顺序：先号码主权，再清后门，再人际止损，最后复盘加固。把这篇当应急卡片收藏，比慌忙搜索更可靠。</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"应急处理","title":"遇到陌生两步验证 PIN：等待、踢人与重设策略","excerpt":"盗号者可能抢先设置 PIN。说明短信码验证为何仍关键，不知 PIN 时如何按官方流程处理，以及等待期要做什么。","read":"13 分钟","body":"<p>两步验证本应是盾，却可能被攻击者抢先装在你的账号上，变成挡住你的墙。不少受害人在重新注册时发现：系统要输入两步验证 PIN，而自己从未设置——这说明有人曾控制该号码并启用了两步验证，甚至可能绑定了攻击者的恢复邮箱。</p>\n      <p>此时切忌慌乱寻找「代刷 PIN」服务；那是二次诈骗。公开资料中的官方逻辑是：<strong>短信/电话注册码</strong>证明你持有手机号；<strong>PIN</strong>是额外口令。两套体系要解决不同问题。</p>\n\n      <h2>先分清两种「码」</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>注册码：</strong>发送到手机的六位短信或语音码。输入后，通常会把其他设备上的非法会话挤下线。</li>\n        <li><strong>两步验证 PIN：</strong>你或攻击者早先设定的 6 位口令。若不知道且无法通过邮箱恢复，系统可能要求等待一段时间（常见公开描述为最长约 7 天）后再继续，以具体 App 提示为准。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>关键结论：<strong>不要因为卡在 PIN 就放弃输入注册码</strong>。先完成号码验证，往往已经能解除对方的即时操作权。</p>\n\n      <h2>若记得自己的 PIN 与邮箱</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>正常输入 PIN；或通过邮箱走官方找回流程。</li>\n        <li>登录后立刻换新 PIN、换新恢复邮箱。</li>\n        <li>清关联设备，检查是否有异常群发残留。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>若 PIN 完全陌生</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>仍要完整走完注册码验证，把手机控制权抢回来。</li>\n        <li>按界面提示处理 PIN 等待或邮箱申诉；勿向陌生人透露注册码。</li>\n        <li>等待期间持续用短信/电话警告亲友：任何 WhatsApp 消息均不可信。</li>\n        <li>确认 SIM 未被再次换卡；邮箱主账户加固。</li>\n        <li>等待结束后第一时间登录，设置<strong>仅你知晓</strong>的新 PIN 与邮箱。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>等待七天很难熬，但这是官方风控的一部分。把这段时间用来止损通知与取证，比寻找「秒破 PIN」更靠谱。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>重获控制后的强制动作</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>新 PIN + 可登陆的恢复邮箱；旧 PIN 作废。</li>\n        <li>关联设备清零复查；应用锁开启。</li>\n        <li>复盘最初如何失守，避免第 8 天又被恶意设 PIN。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>陌生 PIN 令人窒息，但路径仍在：注册码踢人、官方规则处理 PIN、等待期对外止血、回来后重建门禁。耐心与顺序，比捷径更安全。</p>\n<h2>如何陪伴等待期的家人</h2>\n      <p>七天等待对长辈尤其煎熬，子女应每日两次简短同步进度，强调「不要找黄牛」。同时帮长辈把重要联系人暂时迁移到电话短信，避免骗子继续冒充。可以在纸上写下官方 App 内的提示文案，防止他们误信聊天里的假客服。</p>\n      <p>等待结束后第一时间完成的不是发状态，而是改 PIN、改邮箱、清设备——顺序不能乱。</p>\n<h2>写给子女的协助要点</h2>\n      <p>若父母卡在陌生 PIN，子女应远程陪跑官方流程，而不是尝试「第三方便捷解锁」。帮助父母用短信联系老同事，说明暂时无法用 WhatsApp，以免诈骗延续。保留运营商、邮箱服务商的工单号，等待期结束后一次性加固，比零散改密更不易遗漏。</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"排查信号","title":"账号异常信号大全：如何尽早发现被盗或被同步","excerpt":"从突然登出、陌生关联设备，到好友反馈你在要码——把预警信号分级，并给出对应动作。","read":"13 分钟","body":"<p>早一小时发现被盗，常常意味着少一轮亲友被骗、少一笔转账。许多案例并非「防不住」，而是信号出现时被视为「小 bug」或「朋友误会」。把下面分级清单当成巡检仪表，出现对应颜色就执行对应动作，而不是等「铁证如山」再补救。</p>\n\n      <h2>红色信号：立刻行动</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>无操作下被迫退出，或提示号码已在其他设备注册。</li>\n        <li>你没点登录，却连续收到多条注册验证码。</li>\n        <li>关联设备列表出现从未见过的浏览器、城市、设备名。</li>\n        <li>多位好友同时反馈：你在索要验证码或催转账。</li>\n        <li>你在线聊天，对方却说「你」在另一头回复不同内容。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>动作：</strong>立即重新验证号码 → 清所有关联 → 重设两步验证 → 短信通知亲友。</p>\n\n      <h2>橙色信号：当天必须处理</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>通知栏闪过不明聊天回复预览，但打开对话你没有发送。</li>\n        <li>群友私信问：刚才发的链接是不是你本人。</li>\n        <li>发现两步验证曾被关闭，或恢复邮箱被改（若可见）。</li>\n        <li>手机借出/点后出现异常耗电、陌生辅助功能开启。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>动作：</strong>关联设备全查；重设两步验证；检查近期点击记录与安装来源；告知密切联系人提高警惕。</p>\n\n      <h2>黄色信号：提高巡检频率</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>频繁收到「官方警告」外链却并非你主动操作。</li>\n        <li>半熟人反复试探能否借钱、能否帮忙扫码。</li>\n        <li>曾在酒店/网吧使用网页版，不确定是否已登出。</li>\n        <li>出国换卡后，短信有延迟，担心 OTP 被别人截获。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>动作：</strong>本周每日看关联设备；拒点陌生链；旅行专题里的回国后复盘。</p>\n\n      <h2>把「好友反馈」当最高优先级</h2>\n      <p>当朋友说「你在要我的码」时，先不要辩解「不可能」，而要先自查设备与会话。误判成本低，漏判成本高。若自查无果，也请朋友在群内简短预警，防止连环。</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>建立「红色电话」：与两位亲友约定，任何账号异常互拨语音确认，不用 WhatsApp 回问。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>记录与取证</h2>\n      <p>出现橙色以上信号，截图关联设备页、验证码短信时间、可疑聊天，便于日后银行或警方渠道举证。不要频繁点击钓鱼链「确认是不是假」。</p>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>信号分级，是为避免麻木与恐慌两个极端。把好友的一句质疑当火警，往往能在变现阶段前截停攻击。</p>\n<h2>建立个人「异常日志」</h2>\n      <p>用手机备忘或纸质本记录每一次异常验证码、陌生设备、好友质疑的日期与截图编号。日后若需要向银行或执法机关说明时间线，有序证据比零碎记忆更有力。日志本身不含敏感码，只记事件类型。</p>\n<h2>误报与漏报的平衡</h2>\n      <p>有时好友忘记自己刚刚发过奇怪链接，会误报你被黑。即便如此，轻量自查（关联设备、最近登录提示）仍是低成本动作。真漏报一次的损失远大于多查几次的三十秒。建议每季度与好友互相「抽检」：问一句「你最近有没有收到可疑消息冒充我？」</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"骗局识破","title":"好友借钱、换号、要保密：完整核实手册","excerpt":"盗号后的变现第一步常常是向通讯录借钱。教你一套不伤感情、又能挡下转账诈骗的核实流程。","read":"13 分钟","body":"<p>盗号后的「冷启动变现」，最常见的就是向通讯录借钱。骗子不是泛泛群发，而是读取历史聊天，模仿你的称呼、口癖、近期话题，写出一封「老同学救急」「工程款差一笔」的私信。你要防的，不是朋友会不会借钱，而是<strong>这条请求是否通过了不可伪造的核身</strong>。</p>\n\n      <h2>高危话术特征</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>强调马上、保密、别让家人知道，阻止第三方介入。</li>\n        <li>称手机坏了、号换了，要求加新号立刻转账。</li>\n        <li>拒绝或拖延视频露脸；语音也可能被 AI 模仿（见本站 AI 专题）。</li>\n        <li>收款方式突然变为礼品卡、USDT、陌生二维码、分多笔小额。</li>\n        <li>先小额「测试到账」，随即追加大额。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>推荐核实流程（固执一点没错）</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>在原聊天里暂停谈钱，说「我打电话确认下」——真朋友能理解。</li>\n        <li>拨打通讯录里<strong>存储已久的旧号码</strong>，不用对方刚发的新号。</li>\n        <li>问两个只有本人知道的非公开细节（上周见面地点、家庭暗号）。</li>\n        <li>仍不确定时，联系其配偶/同事交叉验证。</li>\n        <li>任何催促「来不及挂单/手术费」都不能替代核身；大额可约定延迟到账。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>真正的朋友在被质疑时很少羞辱你的谨慎。逼你「别问那么多、先转」的，更像诈骗脚本在抢窗口。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>已经转出怎么办</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>即刻联系银行/支付方尝试拦截或争议。</li>\n        <li>保存聊天、转账凭证、对方号码与时间线。</li>\n        <li>告知被骗朋友其号可能也被盗，并通过当地警方渠道报案，是否需要由办案机关认定。</li>\n        <li>在共同好友群简短预警，免二次伤害。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>家庭与公司公约模板</h2>\n      <p>「凡通过聊天 App 提出的紧急借款，必须语音或当面确认；无确认不转账。」把这句话写进员工手册与家庭微信群置顶。</p>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>核实流程是友谊的保险，不是不信任。被盗的那位朋友，同样需要你帮他挡住更多受害者。</p>\n<h2>群聊里的「批量借款」</h2>\n      <p>盗号者有时在多个群同时发借款模板，利用有人先转账带动从众心理。看到群里多人已转，并不代表本人已确认——要独立核身。管理员发现异常应暂时全员禁言，并用电话渠道核实。</p>\n      <p>公司财务规定：凡是变更收款账户，必须盖章 PDF 加电话回访，聊天截图不能单独作为依据。</p>\n<h2>小额测试转账的陷阱</h2>\n      <p>骗子先让受害方转几十元「确认友谊」，再追加大额。规则应是：无论金额大小，核身标准一致。不要为了「金额小无所谓」跳过电话确认。部分脚本专做累积小额，避开银行风控阈值。</p>\n<h2>情侣与合伙生意</h2>\n      <p>恋爱关系与合伙人间更容易因「不好意思核实」而转账。约定：涉及合伙款一律走公司账户与书面邮件，不通过个人 WhatsApp 决策。分手或纠纷期间应格外警惕冒充对方借钱。感情越浓，流程越要硬，这才是保护关系。</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"防护设置","title":"隐私设置完整加固：减少被社工利用的个人信息","excerpt":"头像、最后上线、群组邀请、状态可见范围……逐项说明建议值。隐私设置不能代替两步验证，但能减少骗子的素材。","read":"14 分钟","body":"<p>端到端加密守护的是消息在传输中的机密性，并不自动隐藏你的头像、签名、在线时间、群组曝光面。骗子会收集这些碎片，拼出更可信的剧本：知道你刚出国、知道你女儿参赛、知道你常用的称呼。收紧隐私，是缩小社工素材面积的低成本工作。</p>\n\n      <h2>建议逐项调整（设置 → 隐私）</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>头像：</strong>对非联系人收敛，减少陌生人克隆你的形象。</li>\n        <li><strong>最后上线与在线：</strong>按需限制，减轻被盯梢。</li>\n        <li><strong>关于：</strong>不写手机号、住址、排班、小孩学校全名。</li>\n        <li><strong>状态：</strong>控制受众；状态被转发仍会扩散，发前先评估。</li>\n        <li><strong>群组：</strong>「谁可以拉我进群」建议为「我的联系人」，防陌生拉群诈骗。</li>\n        <li><strong>通话：</strong>静音未知来电等选项（视版本），减骚扰。</li>\n        <li><strong>已读回执：</strong>体验功能；安全核心仍在验证码与设备。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>一步步操作习惯</h2>\n      <p>每改一项，用另一部小号或朋友的非联系账号侧测：陌生人能看到什么。每月换机或重大活动（婚礼、旅行）后可再扫一遍，因为 App 更新有时会恢复默认更开放选项。</p>\n\n      <h2>隐私与防盗的边界</h2>\n      <p>隐私收紧救不了「你主动发验证码」，但能让冷启动骗子少一半剧本素材。它与两步验证、关联设备巡检是不同层次，需要叠甲而非替代。</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>给长辈改隐私时，优先做「群邀请限制 + 头像对非联系人隐藏 + 关于栏去敏感信息」三项，性价比高。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>商务号的额外点</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>店铺头像可被仿冒，对外公告要用多渠道一致的品牌收款信息。</li>\n        <li>员工离职后，检查谁还能代表品牌号发消息。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>花十五分钟过一遍隐私页，是低成本的「减少被骗故事版本号」。配合验证码纪律，才能真正让骗子的话术变钝。</p>\n<h2>针对不同人群的推荐档</h2>\n      <p>长辈：群邀请限联系人、头像限联系人、关闭状态对公众展示。青少年：限制在线时间可见、减少陌生人发消息入口。商务号：头像与「关于」用于经营展示，但避免写个人住址；收款信息走固定模板消息而非随意口头更改。</p>\n      <p>每次 App 大版本更新后，隐私默认值可能被改动，换机或更新后应再巡检一轮。</p>\n<h2>截图与外界档案</h2>\n      <p>你的头像和签名可能早已被别人截图扩散。定期检查搜索引擎与人肉站点是否出现你的手机号+头像组合，若发现可向平台申诉删除。隐私设置是减少新增泄露，不是收回已流出素材；但仍值得做。</p>\n<h2>同步通讯录的副作用</h2>\n      <p>允许 WhatsApp 读取通讯录，会把亲友号码上传用于好友发现。若你设备遭窃，这份通讯录也成为攻击列表。可在隐私里关闭联系人上传（以版本选项为准），改用手动添加核心联系人。操作路径变化时查官方帮助中心说明。</p>\n<h2>小结复述</h2><p>隐私是减速带，不是护城河；它与验证码纪律、设备清单一起才构成完整防护。完成设置后记下日期，六个月后复查。</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"防护设置","title":"只用官方 WhatsApp：改装包与非商店安装的风险","excerpt":"所谓防撤回、多开、破解会员，可能是后门。讲清楚为何官方渠道更新如此重要，以及如何自查当前安装来源。","read":"13 分钟","body":"<p>WhatsApp 官方安全说明一再强调：非官方或改装客户端可能导致账号被盗、被封、或持续泄密。所谓「防撤回」「双开」「主题破解」「隐藏在线」的 APK，常在合法功能外壳下，夹带额外权限——读通知、无障碍点击、自动转发验证码——一旦端点沦陷，端到端加密也无法保护你主动显示在屏幕上的注册码。</p>\n\n      <h2>高危来源识别</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>群文件、网盘、论坛短链分发的 APK。</li>\n        <li>名称类似 WhatsApp Plus、GB、Delta 等 fork；图标略有差异。</li>\n        <li>安装向导要求关闭 Play 保护、打开未知来源。</li>\n        <li>要求登录 WhatsApp 同时索取短信全权管理。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>正确安装路径</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS 仅 App Store，开发者应为 WhatsApp LLC。</li>\n        <li>Android 优先 Google Play 或手机厂商官方商店正式版。</li>\n        <li>桌面端从 Meta 官方指引下载，不搜第三方「加速包」。</li>\n        <li>开启系统自动更新，修补已知漏洞。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>自查与迁移</h2>\n      <p>在商店页核对开发者、评价是否异常刷单、权限列表是否过度。若曾长期使用改装版：</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>备份必要聊天；卸载非官方客户端。</li>\n        <li>安装官方版，重新验证号码。</li>\n        <li>清关联设备，重设两步验证，改 PIN。</li>\n        <li>若怀疑木马：在设备安全前提下做杀毒或重置评估。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>「免费高级功能」常是交换条件：你省订阅费，骗子拿整座账号与通讯录。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>组织内推广话术</h2>\n      <p>在公司培训中把「改装 WhatsApp 等于自带后门」写成 IT 红线，与钓鱼识别一起考。</p>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>官方客户端是一切安全假设的地基。地基错了，上面的两步验证只是心理安慰。</p>\n<h2>企业 MDM 与双开器</h2>\n      <p>部分员工使用公司 MDM 或第三方「应用双开」在一份工作手机里登录两个 WhatsApp。若双开器来源不明，同样可能劫持通知栏验证码。企业 IT 应白名单仅允许官方包名，禁止侧载；员工个人机也应避免为图省事装 fork。</p>\n<h2>更新失败时的正确姿势</h2>\n      <p>若官方商店更新反复失败，不要转而下载论坛「完整包」。应清理商店缓存、检查系统时间、联系官方支持渠道。临时使用网页版回复，也比安装陌生 APK 更安全——网页版同样遵守关联设备规则。</p>\n<h2>二手手机预装陷阱</h2>\n      <p>购买二手安卓机时，检查是否预装非官方 WhatsApp 或带有「加速」「清理」类常驻应用。恢复出厂后仅从官方商店安装。若卖家声称「已帮你登好」，坚决重登并改 PIN。</p>\n<h2>小结复述</h2><p>每一次改装尝试都在赌你通讯录的价值高于那几个花哨功能。赌赢的永远是骗子。</p>\n<p>记住：正版免费，改装最贵。</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"日常习惯","title":"手机锁屏与物理安全：防身边人偷关联","excerpt":"很多网页版失守发生在「借看一分钟」之间。强锁屏、通知预览策略与借用后巡检，组成物理层防线。","read":"12 分钟","body":"<p>数字防盗的讨论里，桌边那部已解锁的手机经常被低估。维修店代操作、同事玩笑式拿起、家人「帮我登一下网页版」、酒桌离席去洗手间——攻击者不需要远程技巧，只要在几十秒内完成一次扫码，关联设备会话就能长期同步你的消息。</p>\n\n      <h2>基础配置清单</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>系统锁屏：优先长密码或复杂 PIN，生物识别作便捷层，避免图案好猜。</li>\n        <li>自动锁屏时间设为最短可接受档（如 30 秒–1 分钟）。</li>\n        <li>锁屏通知隐藏验证码与聊天内容预览。</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp 内开启应用锁（指纹/面容），手机解锁后仍要再验一次。</li>\n        <li>敏感群聊可叠加聊天锁定（见相关专题）。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>借用与维修场景</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>不把已解锁手机交给他人独处，哪怕一分钟。</li>\n        <li>必须他人操作时你在场，禁止对方进入「关联设备」或系统 VPN/描述文件页。</li>\n        <li>拿回手机第一件事：打开关联设备列表，踢掉不认识条目。</li>\n        <li>送修前退出 WhatsApp 或确保强锁屏 + 关闭通知；要求店家书面不浏览个人隐私。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>公共环境肩窥</h2>\n      <p>地铁、机场输入注册码时请遮挡；关闭自动朗读通知。攻击者用肉眼肩窥 + 录像同样能拿到 OTP。</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>把手机借出前，可先手动锁屏。礼貌和五分钟解释，胜过事后夺回账号。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>失窃抢手机</h2>\n      <p>设备物理丢失后，除报警与运营商挂失外，尽快用新设备重验 WhatsApp（SIM 仍属你），清关联、改 PIN。若 SIM 也在机上，优先处理换卡。</p>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>物理安全是数字安全的地板。地板没了，站得再高也会摔。</p>\n<h2>儿童与保姆在家场景</h2>\n      <p>孩子会好奇滑动通知，家政人员可能代为接听电话。除应用锁外，可教育家人不要在未成年人面前念出验证码。对保姆借用手机拍照等合理需求，可改用相机模式而非直接给解锁桌面。</p>\n      <p>智能手表与平板若同号登录短信转发，也要纳入物理巡检范围。</p>\n<h2>可穿戴设备风险</h2>\n      <p>智能手表同步显示 WhatsApp 通知时，腕上同样可能暴露验证码。户外跑步、健身房场景应关闭手表上的敏感预览，或暂时静音 OTP 类短信到可穿戴层。借戴他人手表登录同一账号也会留下会话痕迹，应独立评估。</p>\n<h2>摄像头物理遮挡习惯</h2>\n      <p>视频通话诈骗有时引导你切前置摄像头展示环境。养成不用时关闭相机权限或物理滑盖的习惯，减少被诱导展示银行卡、身份证桌面的机会。孩子使用平板视频课时家长宜在场抽查。</p>\n<h2>小结复述</h2><p>把锁屏看成腰带，应用锁看成口袋扣；腰带没系，口袋扣也白搭。两者同时到位。</p>\n<p>睡前充电不把解锁手机放床头够不着的客人边；聚餐手机朝下扣放，减少验证码弹窗曝光。</p>\n<p>锁屏壁纸勿用含家庭住址照片的图像，避免遗失手机时一并泄露。</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"日常习惯","title":"WhatsApp 网页版与电脑版：安全使用长规范","excerpt":"从官方入口、保持登录选项、公用电脑流程到异常会话处理，给经常在电脑上回复消息的人一份完整规范。","read":"14 分钟","body":"<p>电脑端让我们更快回复客户与家人，却也把会话暴露在「更大屏幕、更长登录时间、更多共用环境」里。安全重点不是禁用电脑版，而是坚持：官方入口发起关联、清楚挂着哪些会话、在公共环境强制短生命周期。</p>\n\n      <h2>推荐安全工作流</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>手机打开官方 WhatsApp → 已关联的设备 → 关联新设备。</li>\n        <li>电脑仅打开官方桌面程序，或浏览器地址栏手写官方网址。</li>\n        <li>扫描<strong>电脑屏幕上刚从官方页弹出的码</strong>，不扫聊天图片。</li>\n        <li>私人电脑可勾选保持登录；公用电脑绝不勾选，结束立刻在手机端登出。</li>\n        <li>每周固定检查列表，踢掉不活跃但陌生条目。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>明确禁止</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>扫描聊天里「登录」「投票」「补助」二维码。</li>\n        <li>点击搜索引擎广告里的「WhatsApp Web」。</li>\n        <li>让别人用你的手机代扫「帮我挂一下」。</li>\n        <li>长期把公司前台电脑当服务器 24 小时挂着。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>多设备办公策略</h2>\n      <p>居家与工位各一台电脑时，命名会话有便于识别；离职或设备出售前，必须先在手机端移除关联，再重装系统。</p>\n\n      <h2>异常协同信号</h2>\n      <p>消息显示已读但你没打开；联系人称你发了奇怪附件——立刻查关联列表并重设两步验证。</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>把「周日晚上清网页版」与倒垃圾一样列入家务：30 秒换一周安心。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>电脑版是生产力与风险的共同体。官方入口、短会话、周清单，三件套用好，它就是工具而不是落地窗。</p>\n<h2>浏览器扩展与共享电脑</h2>\n      <p>在浏览器使用网页版时，恶意扩展可能读取页面 DOM。公用或公司电脑尽量使用无痕模式 + 官方站，结束后清除数据。不要在同一浏览器登录大量无关插件与 WhatsApp Web 混用。</p>\n      <p>若公司统一部署 Chromium，要求 IT 审核扩展列表，是个人防护的延伸。</p>\n<h2>多标签页与遗忘登出</h2>\n      <p>有的人在数个浏览器配置文件里各开一套 WhatsApp Web，时间久了忘记关。建议给每个配置文件命名，手机端清设备时对照命名删除。公司要求用单一浏览器配置的 IT 政策，反而能降低遗忘登出概率。</p>\n<h2>休眠与屏保</h2>\n      <p>办公室电脑屏保时间短、回座需密码，能避免同事路过看到客户聊天。结合 WhatsApp Web 在手机端及时登出，比单靠「同事都是好人」可靠。离开工位按 Win+L 或同等锁屏应是肌肉记忆。</p>\n<h2>小结复述</h2><p>离开办公桌前做两件事：锁电脑、清网页版。比下班焦虑「有没有关」更省心。</p>\n<p>公司会议投屏时先退出 WhatsApp 桌面通知，避免私人OTP 被会议室大屏直播。</p>\n<p>浏览器保存密码功能勿与WhatsApp Web 混用同一弱主密码。</p>\n<p>卸载浏览器插件后再登网页版，可减少被劫持风险。</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"应急处理","title":"封锁、举报与外部求助：切断骗子的继续作战能力","excerpt":"从 App 内封锁举报，到通知银行与警方，整理一套「不继续受害、也不让通讯录继续受害」的行动清单。","read":"13 分钟","body":"<p>遇见骚扰与诈骗会话，停在「骂两句」或「已读不回」，会让对方继续换号试探、把你的沉默当成可培养目标。更有效的是：立刻止聊、封锁、举报、保全证据，并在涉及资金时同步升级到银行与执法渠道。</p>\n\n      <h2>App 内标准操作</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>打开对话 → 联系人信息或群信息。</li>\n        <li>选择<strong>封锁</strong>，阻断后续消息与来电。</li>\n        <li>选择<strong>举报</strong>，按提示提交，可附带近期聊天供平台审核。</li>\n        <li>垃圾群：退出后举报，避免被反复拉回。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>举报是帮助平台聚类诈骗网络，虽不保证立即抓捕，但能降低他人曝光。</p>\n\n      <h2>账号被盗时的对外顺序</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>先夺回主会话与技术后门。</li>\n        <li>用短信/电话/邮件公告，不只在已被控的 WhatsApp 内喊冤。</li>\n        <li>有钱款损失：银行止付、保留证据，必要时通过当地警方渠道报案。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>证据包建议</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>聊天全文截图（含对方号码、时间）。</li>\n        <li>转账记录与收款方信息。</li>\n        <li>钓鱼 URL 纯文本复制（勿再点）。</li>\n        <li>关联设备异常截图、短信码时间。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>反诈咨询请走官方帮助中心或当地反诈宣传渠道，勿把远程控制权交给「热心网友」。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>何时扩散预警</h2>\n      <p>若骗局利用你的被盗号向通讯录扩散，夺回后应在多群简短澄清，一次即可，附可核实的非 WhatsApp 联系方式。</p>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>封锁举报是缩短攻击者射程；对外求助是缩小资金损失。两手都要有清单，而不是临场发挥。</p>\n<h2>报案材料的现实预期</h2>\n      <p>电子证据需保留原始载体，截图尽量包含对方账号、时间、完整上下文。是否能立案、如何跨境协作，由办案机关依法判断；本网站不能替代法律意见。你仍应报备，形成时间记录，并对银行争议流程有帮助。</p>\n<h2>跨国骗局的现实</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp 诈骗常跨国，追赃难度大，但报案与银行争议仍有意义。把所有材料一次性交给办案机关，比在各平台碎片化发帖有效。家人应控制预期，同时坚持止付与时间线记录。</p>\n<h2>平台之外的联动</h2>\n      <p>同一骗子可能在 Telegram、Line 复制剧本。在一家平台封锁后，留意其他 App 是否来串线。把诈骗号码与 ID 记入个人黑名单共享给家人，减少重复踩雷。</p>\n<h2>事后复盘会</h2>\n      <p>受骗或险些受骗后，家庭开 20 分钟复盘：哪一步本可拒绝？下周改进哪一条设置？不追责，只改流程。把复盘记录贴冰箱，下次同类话术触发记忆。</p>\n<p>向亲友简述骗局套路，一条朋友圈胜过百次独自后怕；注意隐去个人敏感账号信息。</p>\n<p>记录向平台举报的回执编号，便于后续跟进或补充材料。</p>\n<p>向 WhatsApp 举报后仍应本地备份证据，平台处理不等于案件办结。</p>\n<p>保存骗子曾在其他平台使用的昵称变体，便于交叉检索预警。</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"家庭防护","title":"家人与长辈防护课：把复杂安全变成能执行的家规","excerpt":"长文版家庭防盗方案：家规条文、角色扮演演练、远程协助边界，以及如何帮长辈完成关键设置。","read":"15 分钟","body":"<p>许多家庭故事的起点是长辈接到「帮我把码念一下」。他们不是没有善心，而是把礼貌放在警惕之前。骗子精准利用「不好意思拒绝」「怕耽误孩子」的心理。守护长辈，不是丢给他们一篇长文，而是把复杂安全翻译成<strong>能背、能演、能拒绝</strong>的家规。</p>\n\n      <h2>六条冰箱贴家规</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>验证码谁都不给，含子女、含「客服」。</li>\n        <li>借钱必须打老电话，不在 App 里转。</li>\n        <li>不点「解封 / 中奖 / 投票」链接。</li>\n        <li>视频不跟陌生人共享屏幕。</li>\n        <li>每周日花半分钟看「已关联的设备」。</li>\n        <li>全家开两步验证，PIN 自己记好或子女帮保管。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15 分钟家庭实操课</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>子女在场帮每位长辈开两步验证、登记邮箱。</li>\n        <li>一起清关联设备，关掉锁屏验证码预览。</li>\n        <li>演练骗子台词与三句拒绝话术，互换角色。</li>\n        <li>约定「拿不准先打数码联络人」，建立不问罪文化。</li>\n        <li>写下紧急电话列表（子女、社区、银行），贴冰箱旁。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>远程协助的界限</h2>\n      <p>可以视频指导长辈点设置，但不要让他们安装来路不明的远程软件。子女也绝不索要父母验证码「代操作」——示范正确行为比替他们点按钮更重要。</p>\n\n      <h2>上当之后如何沟通</h2>\n      <p>先解决问题再教育：协助夺回、银行止付、安慰情绪。责备只会让他们下次隐瞒，直到损失更大。</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>目标不是把长辈变成专家，而是让他们在关键时刻有「可以挂断」的勇气。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>家规 + 演练 + 关键设置，是长期投资。今天的一节课，可能挡掉明年整栋楼的骗码链。</p>\n<h2>社区与教堂互助群的风险</h2>\n      <p>长辈常在信仰、邻里群里极富信任感。可请群主置顶「助理财不发验证码」公告，并由子女偶尔空降做一次 10 分钟分享。用群体规范降低个体拒绝压力。</p>\n      <p>为视力不佳者准备大字号截图教程，路径比文字更友好。</p>\n<h2>与反诈宣传结合</h2>\n      <p>很多城市公安机关有反诈宣传册或短视频，可与长辈一起观看，讨论剧情中的要码场景。官方素材常比子女唠叨更有说服力。遇到真实被骗新闻，也要温和讨论「如果是我们该怎么拒绝」。</p>\n<h2>药品与保健品骗局交叉</h2>\n      <p>卖保健品的话术常与要码盗号并行：先拉信任，再要验证码「开通会员价」。长辈参加养生讲座后回家，子女可主动问「今天有没有登记手机收到码」。关心式提问比质问更有效。</p>\n<h2>与社区中心合作</h2>\n      <p>街道、社区如有智能手机培训班，可报名而非子女单打。群体学习降低孤独感，也减少「就我笨」的沮丧。争取让验证码纪律写进课堂讲义。</p>\n<p>鼓励长辈把可疑聊天转发给子女「帮我看一眼」，赋予他们求助的权利。</p>\n<p>节日聚会全家合照发朋友圈时，避免露银行卡、快递单在背景。</p>\n<p>每月第一个周日设为家庭安全日，十分钟巡检即可。</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"认知纠偏","title":"端到端加密不等于不会被盗：你必须知道的边界","excerpt":"加密很重要，但它不验证对面是谁，也不保护你已解锁的屏幕、云备份配置错误，或你主动交出的验证码。","read":"13 分钟","body":"<p>「WhatsApp 很安全」这句话，在媒体和课堂里被重复太多遍，以至于不少人误以为：<strong>开了加密就不会被骗、不会被盗、不会被泄密。</strong>事实上，端到端加密主要解决「中间链路窃听」问题，对你亲手递出的验证码、被关联的网页端、被木马阅读的屏幕、对接收方截图的扩散，都不自动免疫。</p>\n\n      <h2>加密确实守护什么</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>个人聊天与通话在传输路径上的机密性（在官方设计假设内）。</li>\n        <li>降低运营商、公共 Wi-Fi 窃听明文聊天内容的传统风险。</li>\n        <li>配合密钥管理，构成产品安全叙事的重要一环。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>加密不自动守护什么</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>你把注册码、PIN 告诉「客服」后，对方合法登录作恶。</li>\n        <li>攻击者通过关联设备同步阅读历史与实时消息。</li>\n        <li>已解锁手机上的肩窥、录屏、远程共享。</li>\n        <li>接收方转发、截图、备份至不安全云端。</li>\n        <li>云备份未加密或密钥保管不当造成的第二副本泄露。</li>\n        <li>对面身份是否是你以为的那个人——加密不验人。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>一句话：管道牢，不代表柜台是真，也不代表你还会把钥匙交给陌生人。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>如何建立「完整安全感」</h2>\n      <p>把加密当作第一层，叠加：验证码纪律、两步验证、关联设备巡检、官方客户端、备份密钥治理、转账双渠道核身。缺任何一块，社工都能绕路进来。</p>\n\n      <h2>向家人解释的话术</h2>\n      <p>「加密像密封车厢，但你要是主动把车门打开让人进来，车厢再密封也救不了。」用比喻降低抵触。</p>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>尊重加密，但不神话加密。HoldLine要补的，正是「端到端之外」那一大块现实风险地图。</p>\n<h2>新闻记者与维权人士的提醒</h2>\n      <p>消息来源保护依赖的不只是加密，还有设备物理安全、联系人身份核验、备份策略。若从事敏感工作，应叠加专用手机、分号运营，并定期做威胁模型复盘，而不是单点迷信某个开关。</p>\n<h2>群聊与社区</h2>\n      <p>社区母题、大型群聊有时不享受与个人聊天相同的端到端默认模型（视官方说明与群类型而异）。参加公开群时，假设内容可能被管理员或举报流程外的机制看见。不要把「在群里说了」等同于「绝对私密」。</p>\n<h2>转发链与谣言</h2>\n      <p>加密聊天里转发的伪官方公告，源头往往不在加密保护范围之外——问题是你相信了内容。培养「先看官方帮助中心再转」的习惯，比争论技术细节更能保护亲友。</p>\n<h2>小结复述</h2>\n      <p>信任加密，更信任自己的操作流程。下次有人伸手要码，想起本文：那才是真的在试图穿过管道。</p>\n<p>把本文转发给爱说「WhatsApp 绝对安全」的朋友，用实例纠偏偏差。</p>\n<p>企业培训加入本节Quiz，员工答错率高的题列入下期重点。</p>\n<p>读完本文后去检查一次关联设备，用行动记住边界。</p>\n<p>技术条款更新时重读官方安全页，别用三年前的印象当代事实。</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"防护设置","title":"聊天备份与端到端加密备份：保数据也要保钥匙","excerpt":"备份能救命，也能变成第二份聊天拷贝。如何开启加密备份、保管密码/密钥/通行密钥，以及换机前必须检查的事项。","read":"14 分钟","body":"<p>备份救过无数次换机与丢机，也可能是第二份完整聊天记录的副本。WhatsApp 支持将聊天备份到 iCloud / Google 云端，并可开启端到端加密备份，使云服务商在正确配置下难以直接阅读内容。但若你把密钥纸条丢了，「能备份」也会变成「永远找不回」。</p>\n\n      <h2>是否建议开启</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>有大量工作、家庭沟通需要留存：建议备份 + 开启端到端加密。</li>\n        <li>极高敏感对话：评估聊天锁、消失消息，并意识到接收方仍可截图。</li>\n        <li>公司合规要求保留记录：与法务确认备份存储位置与访问控制。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>开启路径（以常见版本为准）</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>设置 → 聊天 → 聊天备份。</li>\n        <li>进入端到端加密备份。</li>\n        <li>按提示创建密码、64 位密钥或通行密钥（视设备支持）。</li>\n        <li>把恢复方式写入密码管理器 + 离线保险位置（纸质信封、保险箱）。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>官方与安全机构均说明：加密备份的密码/密钥丢失后，通常无法由平台替你恢复。保管与备份同等重要。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>换机与丢机预案</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>换机前确认最近一次备份成功。</li>\n        <li>测试一次：你是否还能找到密钥。</li>\n        <li>恢复完成后立刻巡检两步验证与关联设备。</li>\n        <li>丢机时先夺账号主权，再处理云备份权限（邮箱、云账户）。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>常见误区</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>把密钥照片发微信「以防万一」——等于明文托管。</li>\n        <li>以为未加密备份「只有我自己能看」——云账户被盗即裸奔。</li>\n        <li>公司让员工私人号备份工作群，离职后数据治理失控。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>备份是双刃剑：加密 + 密钥治理，才能既防丢数据又防多一份泄露。</p>\n<h2>家庭共享云账户的坑</h2>\n      <p>夫妻共用 iCloud/Google 账号时，加密备份密钥若只存在一人脑中，丢机时可能双方都无法恢复。应使用共同保险箱记录密钥，或各自备份到受控企业密码库。离异或分户时记得轮换云登录与备份权限。</p>\n<h2>恢复演练年课</h2>\n      <p>每年一次在备用机上试恢复加密备份，确认密钥仍正确。演练可发现云账户欠费、密钥污损等隐藏问题。真丢机时的心态会从容许多。</p>\n<h2>执法调取边界</h2>\n      <p>不同司法辖区对云端调取规则不同。端到端加密备份提高调取难度，但不替你做法律判断。涉案时咨询执业律师；平时做好密钥自有与合法留存策略即可。</p>\n<h2>小结复述</h2>\n      <p>备份成功灯亮时，同步检查密钥是否仍在。两个绿灯才算完成。</p>\n<p>告诉家人密钥存放处（非密码本身），避免自己昏迷时无人能恢复重要对话。</p>\n<p>团队共享密钥使用双人控制，避免一人离职带走全部恢复权。</p>\n<p>云账户开启登录提醒，防备份通道被旁路攻破。</p>\n<p>笔记本记录最后成功备份日期，超三十天未备份则手机提醒。</p>\n<p>重大诉讼前按律师意见决定是否暂停自动备份，以免误删证据链。</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"防护设置","title":"应用锁与生物识别：给 WhatsApp 再加一把门锁","excerpt":"手机解锁后，应用锁能挡住顺手一点开的窥视。说明如何开启，以及它与系统锁屏、聊天锁定的分工。","read":"12 分钟","body":"<p>系统锁屏挡「陌生人拿起整机」；WhatsApp 应用锁挡「手机已解锁但人离开半分钟」的场景——家人顺手点开、同事借看照片、孩子乱滑屏幕。官方提供的指纹、面容或设备凭据二次验证（名称因版本而异），属于低摩擦强化。</p>\n\n      <h2>开启步骤</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp 设置 → 隐私（或安全）→ 应用锁 / 指纹锁。</li>\n        <li>按提示验证系统生物识别或 PIN。</li>\n        <li>确认自动锁定时间（立即或 1 分钟）符合习惯。</li>\n        <li>检查通知快速回复是否会绕过锁（按版本调整）。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>与其他防护分工</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>系统锁屏：</strong>第一道，必备。</li>\n        <li><strong>应用锁：</strong>第二道，防已解锁环境。</li>\n        <li><strong>聊天锁：</strong>针对单个会话的隐藏与二次打开。</li>\n        <li><strong>两步验证：</strong>远程盗号主力，与应用锁互补。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>局限与注意</h2>\n      <p>应用锁挡不住你把手机递给骗子扫关联码，也挡不住你已把验证码发出去。生物识别在部分司法场景可能被强制解锁，高敏人群可评估纯 PIN 策略。</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>开启应用锁的三分钟，常为「借手机一分钟」后悔买单。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>应用锁是贴身小锁，与系统锁屏组成卧室门 + 保险柜的组合，而不是替代品。</p>\n<h2>不同品牌手机的差异</h2>\n      <p>部分国产定制系统在杀后台后会要求重新解锁 WhatsApp；可在电池优化里给 WhatsApp 白名单，避免应用锁形同虚设又让人烦躁关掉功能。iPhone 用户还应注意通知预览在锁屏 Widget 中的暴露。</p>\n      <p>旅行时若临时关闭生物识别（受伤、手套），请确保系统 PIN 足够强，而不是完全停用应用锁。</p>\n<h2>紧急情况下的可及性</h2>\n      <p>若担心突发疾病时家人无法读取 WhatsApp 联系医生，可准备单独写在信封里的医疗联络卡，而不是长期关闭应用锁。安全与可及性可分层设计：日常上锁，紧急信息走纸质或电话快捷拨号。</p>\n<h2>老年指纹识别衰退</h2>\n      <p>指纹磨损时识别失败，老人可能愤怒关闭应用锁。可改用设备 PIN 作为应用锁后备，或多录几个手指。耐心调试一次，比长期裸奔好。</p>\n<h2>机型差异清单</h2>\n      <p>三星、小米、OPPO 菜单路径不同，购买新机迁移时重新开一遍应用锁。不要假设换机助手会继承安全设置。收好说明书电子版，换机周对照检查项打勾。</p>\n<p>向常借你手机拍照的人说明应用锁用途，减少误触删改。</p>\n<p>夜间充电放卧室时仍建议开启应用锁，以防伴侣误传孩子不当内容非本意。</p>\n<p>教学视频发给长辈：如何开关应用锁，减少误关。</p>\n<p>双手潮湿时指纹失败正常，备用 PIN 应好记但非六位同号。</p>\n<p>双手残疾用户可用系统无障碍替代方案，仍不必关闭整机锁屏。</p>\n<p>出差刘海贴膜更换后重新录入指纹，减少误锁。</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"防护设置","title":"聊天锁定、阅后即焚与消失消息：减少残留暴露","excerpt":"这些功能不能防账号被接管，但能降低手机被翻看、历史消息久留的风险。也要明白截图与转发仍然存在。","read":"13 分钟","body":"<p>即使账号未被盗，消息长期留存也会带来纠纷、分手后泄露、同事误解等风险。WhatsApp 提供聊天锁定、默认消失消息、一次性查看媒体等功能，用于减少「躺在手机里的旧话」。它们不能阻止账号被接管，但能降低物理翻看与旧记录被翻出的概率。</p>\n\n      <h2>聊天锁定</h2>\n      <p>可将指定聊天移入锁定文件夹，打开需再验证生物识别或 PIN，列表默认不展示内容预览。适合共用手机或不想会话被一眼看到的情境。注意：对方设备上仍可能留存记录；锁定不替代两步验证。</p>\n\n      <h2>消失消息</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>可对单聊开启 24 小时 / 7 天 / 90 天等周期（以版本为准）。</li>\n        <li>减少「三年前一句话被截图」的风险，但无法阻止即时截图与转发。</li>\n        <li>群聊开启前需与成员沟通，避免误会证据被删。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>一次性查看媒体</h2>\n      <p>适合发送敏感图片/视频。接收方只可打开一次，并有限制截图的机制（以官方能力为准）。仍要相信对方不会用第二台设备翻拍。</p>\n\n      <h2>使用边界</h2>\n      <p>这些功能不是防弹衣；账号主权仍靠验证码纪律与关联设备巡检。也不会替你向对方证明「此话从没存在过」——法律场景请咨询专业人士。</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>与家人约定：消失消息开启前要口头告知，避免「你怎么删了」争吵。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>把锁定与消失消息当卫生工具：少留垢，不等于不生垢。主权安全仍看门把与钥匙。</p>\n<h2>与客户沟通的平衡点</h2>\n      <p>商务聊天若开启消失消息，可能与客户期望的「可查询记录」冲突。可对内使用锁聊 + 消失，对外保留邮件或 CRM 副本；法律留存义务行业需咨询合规。技术和制度要一起设计。</p>\n<h2>纠纷取证困境</h2>\n      <p>消失消息可能让你在维权时缺少聊天记录。对可能需要证据的交易，使用邮件或带时间戳的合同系统留痕；消失消息适合纯情感或低争议场景。事前想清「这话要不要在一年后还在」，再选策略。</p>\n<h2>未成年人锁定与监护</h2>\n      <p>家长为孩子锁敏感聊天时，应同时保留危机对话渠道（如直接电话），避免孩子遇险却因锁死无法呼救。锁定是降暴露面，不是切断亲子沟通。</p>\n<h2>小结复述</h2>\n      <p>消失消息适合低风险闲聊；合同与金钱话题请走可审计渠道。功能选型匹配风险级别。</p>\n<p>定期检查锁定聊天列表，删去不再需要的项，降低忘记开启的风险。</p>\n<p>律师函或维权前请咨询是否需要保留原始聊天导出记录。</p>\n<p>更新系统后重开消失消息选项，防升级重置。</p>\n<p>工作机与个人号分离时，各自评估不同消失消息策略。</p>\n<p>备份恢复后检查聊天锁目录是否仍生效，必要时重新锁定。</p>\n<p>消失消息开启前告知对方期望，避免合作误会。</p>\n<p>系统大版本升级前一晚导出必要聊天 PDF 备查。</p>\n<p>今晚打开一个锁聊核对是否生效，十秒钟。</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"场景专题","title":"商务号与个人号：店铺运营中的防盗与防冒充","excerpt":"客户群、订单沟通、员工代管——商务场景有额外风险。如何隔离权限、防止客服号被接管后群发诈骗。","read":"14 分钟","body":"<p>个人号被盗伤亲友情；店铺、微商、客服号被盗会在几小时内向客户群发「临时换收款码」「补缴运费」，直接砸招牌与现金流。商务场景还叠加员工代管、多机登录、平板放柜台等放大因素。</p>\n\n      <h2>权限与架构建议</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>经营主号必开两步验证，PIN 放企业密码库，离职即轮转。</li>\n        <li>限制关联设备数量，员工离职当天手机端全踢。</li>\n        <li>公用平板不得 24 小时挂会话；设强锁屏与应用锁。</li>\n        <li>对外固定公示收款账户，教育客户：聊天里临时改码要先二次确认。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>客户澄清模板</h2>\n      <p>「本店收款账户以门店告示 / 官网为准。若 WhatsApp 私聊要求变更收款方式，请先致电门店电话核实。」越早发，越少跟风转账。</p>\n\n      <h2>员工培训三不准</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>不准把店铺号验证码发个人微信「帮登」。</li>\n        <li>不准扫陌生「商家认证」「流量扶持」码。</li>\n        <li>发现异常群发：先断会话、按夺回流程、全渠道澄清。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>与个人号隔离</h2>\n      <p>能用企业工具解决的，不要把全部客户压在私人号；即便仍在个人号经营，也要分设备、分 PIN、分备份策略。</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>把「客户资金安全」写进 KPI，与销售额并列考核意识。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>商务号防盗是经营连续性工程。权限最小化、设备清单化、收款核验规范化，比公关稿便宜。</p>\n<h2>多平台收款一致性</h2>\n      <p>除 WhatsApp，客户也可能在 Facebook、Instagram 咨询。所有渠道公示同一套收款账户，并定期截图存档。员工接手账号时签署保管协议，明确验证码不共享。</p>\n      <p>大型促销日前做「账号健康检查」，比促销后追款轻松。</p>\n<h2>保险与应急预案</h2>\n      <p>评估商业险是否覆盖网络诈骗导致的客户赔付。与律师准备简短对外声明模板与临时收款冻结流程。账号被盗也是业务连续性事件，宜写入公司应急手册演练一次。</p>\n<h2>外包客服账号</h2>\n      <p>外包团队共用客服号时，须名单化管理每日谁登录；班次结束统一踢关联设备。外包商合同加入泄露追责与验证码保密条款，培训记录留档。</p>\n<h2>小结复述</h2>\n      <p>客户信任你的品牌，不是某一条随机 WhatsApp。标准化核身，品牌才不易被一条假消息击穿。</p>\n<p>季末盘点：本季有无异常收款？客户投诉里筛账号冒用关键词。</p>\n<p>节假日值班表注明谁持有客服号，避免交接真空期无人盯异常。</p>\n<p>新客户合同写明官方联络方式，减少假号讨价还价空间。</p>\n<p>logo 水印加入官方海报图，客户易辨认真伪收款图文。</p>\n<p>年度审计抽样员工是否知晓店铺号验证码保密制度。</p>\n<p>午间交接班口头报告有无异常登录，再换班营业。</p>\n<p>闭店打烊第一台手机应是检查关联设备。</p>\n<p>打印收银台告示：只认柜台二维码收款。</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"场景专题","title":"换机迁移防盗清单：在新旧手机交替时守住账号","excerpt":"换机窗口最容易手忙脚乱：备份、验证、关联设备、两步验证可能被跳过。一份迁移日检查表帮你平滑且安全地切换。","read":"13 分钟","body":"<p>换机那天，心理带宽被「照片转过来没有」占满，安全设置最容易被跳过：忘记开两步验证、忘记踢旧网页版、在店里让别人代扫关联。攻击者爱这个时间窗——你更愿意把手机递出去省时间。</p>\n\n      <h2>换机前 24 小时</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>确认备份最新且加密密钥可取。</li>\n        <li>确认两步验证邮箱能登录。</li>\n        <li>列出要保留的电脑会话，计划迁移后重连。</li>\n        <li>写好紧急联系人电话（非 WhatsApp）。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>换机当天顺序</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>新机只装官方 WhatsApp；旧机仍在手时再开始验证。</li>\n        <li>注册码输入时遮挡肩窥；不把码给店员。</li>\n        <li>恢复备份后，立刻确认两步验证仍开启。</li>\n        <li>关联设备页：踢旧会话，再在新电脑按需扫码。</li>\n        <li>开启应用锁、检查隐私与群邀请限制。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>旧机处理</h2>\n      <p>确认新机正常后，旧机退出 WhatsApp 或恢复出厂；出售前重复确认 iCloud/Google 账户已退出。</p>\n\n      <h2>常见失误</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>双机同时在线太久，旧机丢失未踢。</li>\n        <li>备份密钥忘迁移，聊天全空。</li>\n        <li>店员代操作扫了不明二维码。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>把换机当天日历标红：上午迁移，下午安全巡检，不让两件事混成一团。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>迁移的目标：唯一可信主设备、干净关联列表、不断档两步验证、密钥不丢。</p>\n<h2>跨生态迁移（iOS ↔ Android）</h2>\n      <p>换操作系统时，WhatsApp 聊天记录迁移受官方工具限制，可能无法完整转移。提前导出必要合同、发票到 PDF；验证号码时更警惕肩窥，因为用户往往在实体店焦虑操作。迁移完成后务必重做关联设备与两步验证，不要假设「换机助手帮你做好安全」。</p>\n<h2>数据擦除认证</h2>\n      <p>旧机出售前使用官方恢复出厂，并在设置中确认账户已移除。部分安卓机型需额外格式化 SD 卡。买二手机者也应用新号登录、全新两步验证，不信任前任机主的任何残留会话。</p>\n<h2>手表与副卡</h2>\n      <p>eSIM 手表若可独立收短信，迁移时确认验证码发到哪块屏。新机激活后测试 WhatsApp 来电与短信路由，避免验证码落在旧手表上被他人看见。</p>\n<h2>小结复述</h2>\n      <p>换机窗口再忙，也留十分钟给安全五步。迁移快乐建立在号还在你自己手里。</p>\n<p>迁移当晚发给好友一条非 WhatsApp 公告：「换机成功，诈骗勿信。」</p>\n<p>旧手机卡托、包装盒上的 ICCID 信息撕毁后再丢弃。</p>\n<p>好友名片导出 CSV 备份，迁移后对照是否导入完整。</p>\n<p>新机贴膜后再录入指纹，避免后期换膜导致识别失败。</p>\n<p>孩子旧手表若绑定旧号，换机后记得解绑再绑新机。</p>\n<p>迁移当晚不开陌生网页「测速」，专注官方流程。</p>\n<p>旧机 SIM 针妥善存放，防随手丢弃被捡。</p>\n<p>新机首周每晚十分钟安全复查，仅一周。</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"骗局识破","title":"群组诈骗与管理员风险：从拉群到假通知的全链路","excerpt":"陌生群、假客服群、管理员被盗后的公告——群是高频诈骗场域。教你设置入群门槛与识别假通知。","read":"14 分钟","body":"<p>群组有「多数人在场」的心理安全感，却也是诈骗扩大的扩音器。陌生拉群、假客服群、管理员号被盗后发钓鱼公告——都利用了你相信「群里不会全都是托」的假设。</p>\n\n      <h2>危险群画像</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>陌生号拉你进群后马上逼加「专员」私聊。</li>\n        <li>公告里只有短链「实名登记」「补贴领取」。</li>\n        <li>限时恐吓：不操作就清退、罚款、报警。</li>\n        <li>禁止截图外传，要求「只听管理员」。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>个人防护</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>隐私限制谁能拉你进群。</li>\n        <li>陌生群先观察，不点链接；必要时退出举报。</li>\n        <li>家庭、公司核心群：管理员必须开两步验证。</li>\n        <li>涉及改收款、改报销的通知：第二渠道确认。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>管理员职责</h2>\n      <p>定期清理僵尸号；开启新成员审核；异常公告出现先撤回再多渠道澄清；关键群备援副管理员。</p>\n\n      <h2>管理员号被盗</h2>\n      <p>立即夺回 → 其他成员代发「忽略刚才链接」 → 撤销邀请链接 → 视情况重建群并核验成员身份。</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>群越大，越要把「要点链接先私聊管理员语音确认」写成群公告。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>群是信任放大器。入群门槛、管理员加固、关键动作双确认，能挡掉多数群内爆发式骗局。</p>\n<h2>「只读群」里的假文件</h2>\n      <p>管理员设群只读后，骗子可能改用「文件」标签发 APK 或「订单表格」。提醒成员：群文件与群链接同等可疑。可关闭普通成员发送文件权限，或在入群公告写清「本群不发安装包」。</p>\n      <p>投资、返利类群名常带 emoji 与紧迫词，可设置关键词家庭过滤器互相提醒。</p>\n<h2>链上热情与 FOMO</h2>\n      <p>群内机器人或托儿会伪造「已上车」「已翻倍」截图，制造错失恐惧。看到晒单先问：能否链上验证？能否语音确认？群主反差超大收益时，默认剧本可疑。退群不丢人，钱没了才难开口。</p>\n<h2>假官方助手号</h2>\n      <p>群内出现的「WhatsApp 助手」「安全 bot」多为骗子私号改名。正版功能通知不会私聊要码。见到 bot 自称，忽略并在真官方渠道核实。</p>\n<h2>小结复述</h2>\n      <p>退群免费，信假公告很贵。遇到不对劲，先退再核实，比面子重要。</p>\n<p>向群主提议开启新成员审批，一次设置惠及全群。</p>\n<p>养号群常有托晒收益，勿以其热度判断真实性。冷思考一分钟再行动，能避开多数 FOMO。</p>\n      <p>若已在群投入资金，立即停止加码，保留证据，通过银行与正规渠道求助。</p>\n<p>不必每个群都活跃，精简群列表等于减少攻击面。</p>\n<p>对不常发言却频繁改群名的异常号，群主应直接移除。</p>\n<p>邀请链接设置定期轮换，降低外泄后长期拉人的风险。</p>\n<p>进群一周不说话专发截图的号，标记观察。</p>\n<p>夜间免打扰仍要看安全群艾特全体公告。</p>\n<p>见到暴富截图先存疑，受益逻辑自洽再判断。</p>\n<p>退出异常群不算失礼，是止损。</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"骗局识破","title":"AI 语音与深度冒充：当「听起来像本人」不再足够","excerpt":"合成语音让「打电话核实」也出现新风险。建立更强核身问题与双渠道确认，避免只靠声纹感觉。","read":"13 分钟","body":"<p>「借钱一定要打电话」曾是金科玉律，如今要升级：AI 语音克隆可以在几秒钟内模仿你亲友的声调，再配合被盗 WhatsApp 里的语境施压。技术进步没有取消核实，只是提高了核实深度。</p>\n\n      <h2>新规则：强核身，而不只是听声音</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>问只有双方知道的随机细节，避免固定问题被提前扒窃。</li>\n        <li>要求即时视频露脸 + 举手比随机数字。</li>\n        <li>大额启用延时到账或双人复核。</li>\n        <li>家庭预设「借钱暗号」，口头说不出则不转。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>旧规则仍然有效</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>拨打通讯录老号码，不用聊天新号。</li>\n        <li>拒绝礼品卡、虚拟货币等不可逆支付。</li>\n        <li>越是催保密、催立刻，越要慢下来。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>如何降低被克隆素材</h2>\n      <p>公开社交媒体少发长段高清语音/视频；隐私设置限制陌生人下载。不能完全杜绝，但能抬高攻击成本。</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>技术在变，原则不变：重大资金动作必须超出当前聊天窗口验证身份。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>别因 AI 放弃电话核实，要让核实变难：知识问题 + 视频临场 + 延迟支付组合出拳。</p>\n<h2>企业财务岗位的升级流程</h2>\n      <p>公司应要求：任何变更供应商账户，必须回拨合同留档电话，且双人签字。即使语音听起来是老板，也要走流程——真实老板应当为流程感到安全，而非被冒犯。</p>\n      <p>可录制双方同意的固定问候暗号，不定期更换，减少 AI 模型过拟合历史音频的可能。</p>\n<h2>跨境时差与 sleepy 决策</h2>\n      <p>凌晨电话最易击穿防线。约定家庭规则：23 点至 7 点之间的借款请求，天亮后再处理。真有急症应走 120 / 官方急救渠道，而不是 WhatsApp 转账。AI 语音+时差是常见组合。</p>\n<h2>公开语音样本管理</h2>\n      <p>播客、直播连麦会留下长时音色样本。公众人物更应使用延迟支付与多签流程；普通人也可减少在公开群发送长语音圈，降低被克隆素材质量。</p>\n<h2>小结复述</h2>\n      <p>声音可以合成，细节难以伪造。多问一句只有真人才答得出的问题，AI 脚本常会露馅。</p>\n<p>财务岗位轮岗时重申核身流程，新人不知暗号不能单独打款。</p>\n<p>公司可规定超阈值打款需两人到会签，系统延时一小时到账，为 AI 语音核实赢时间。</p>\n      <p>亲属约定暗号每年春节更换一次，写在家务本而非聊天里。</p>\n<p>视频会议大额决策录像留底，事后可追溯语音是否AI。</p>\n<p>语音留言信箱短促即可，减少被批量爬取训练素材长度。</p>\n<p>公共演讲录音如需发布，可轻微变调或加 BGM 抬高克隆成本。</p>\n<p>董事会决议大额支出仍需纸质签字存档。</p>\n<p>午休后大额转账强制冷却三十分钟再确认。</p>\n<p>情侣大额互转也走语音加暗号，不因亲密跳步。</p>\n<p>绑架话术要钱时先打110再转钱。</p>\n<p>测一次家人暗号问答，练手。</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"家庭防护","title":"青少年使用 WhatsApp：防盗号、防诱导与监护边界","excerpt":"少年人社交活跃、防备不同，易成骗码与诱导目标。给家长的设置建议、对话脚本与求助机制。","read":"14 分钟","body":"<p>青少年比长辈更会用手机，却未必更懂风险：游戏交易、饭圈抽奖、校园谣言、恋爱勒索，都能把「给个验证码」「发个自拍」包装成理所当然。家长的目标不是监控一切，而是红线清晰、求助不被罚。</p>\n\n      <h2>与孩子约定的红线</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>验证码、证件照、银行卡不给网友。</li>\n        <li>皮肤代充走官方平台，不私下 WhatsApp 转账。</li>\n        <li>被威胁曝光隐私：先告诉家长，而不是先付钱。</li>\n        <li>不装同学传的「破解版」「双开」。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>家长协助设置</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>开启两步验证；恢复邮箱可用家庭协商方案。</li>\n        <li>限制群邀请；头像对陌生人收敛。</li>\n        <li>系统与应用锁开启；定期一起看关联设备。</li>\n        <li>教使用封锁举报，而非硬刚辱骂。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>沟通技巧</h2>\n      <p>用案例讨论，而非羞辱；保证「报告可疑事先免责」。隐瞒往往因为怕手机被没收。</p>\n\n      <h2>学校与同伴</h2>\n      <p>与班主任共享官方反诈资源；鼓励孩子向同伴传播「要码即诈」而非二次嘲笑受害者。</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>青少年需要的是教练型家长，不是狱卒型家长。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>教育 + 设置 + 友好求助通道，比偷看聊天更能长期降低风险。</p>\n<h2>校园二手与游戏陪玩</h2>\n      <p>「低价皮肤」「陪玩押金」常伴随要验证码盗号或裸聊勒索。与孩子约定：凡涉及账号登录帮助一律拒绝，并截图给家长。学校心理辅导渠道应纳入数字勒索处置。</p>\n      <p>鼓励少年做同伴教育者，比单纯父母说教更有接受度。</p>\n<h2>校规与数字纪律</h2>\n      <p>与学校合作，把「不给验证码」写入学生手册示例，与防 bullying 同批教育。班级内建立举报可疑账号的匿名渠道，保护报告者。少年互相提醒比家长单打更有效。</p>\n<h2>心理健康与敲诈</h2>\n      <p>裸聊敲诈后少年可能自杀意念上升，家长发现异常聊天锁死前应寻求专业心理援助。合同约定「任何事可先说、后处理」，比单纯没收手机更能保命。</p>\n<h2>小结复述</h2>\n      <p>孩子愿早点跟你说，比偷看聊天更能降低损失。把「报告免罚」写进家庭协议并真正执行。</p>\n<p>和学校心理老师共享反诈资源，让孩子知道被骂也可找老师。</p>\n<p>引导少年关注官方反诈账号而不是猎奇诈骗集锦，减少模仿犯罪手法风险。</p>\n      <p>家长自己先不开改装 App，以身作则比道理更有说服力。</p>\n<p>设立家庭「可疑事件奖金」：主动举报骗局手法奖奶茶券，正向激励报告。</p>\n<p>游戏语音频道同样禁发验证码，与 WhatsApp 规则一致执行。</p>\n<p>寝室公约第一条可写：谁要验证码谁请客——用幽默记规则。</p>\n<p>运动会集体机勿混用个人 WhatsApp 登入场馆屏。</p>\n<p>每学期家长群转发本专题一次即可。</p>\n<p>班主任知情即可，不必全班公开孩子受骗细节。</p>\n<p>操场换机号要跟父母当天报备。</p>\n<p>班会分享反诈比处分更有效。</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"场景专题","title":"出行与漫游：旅游、出差时的 WhatsApp 防盗要点","excerpt":"换卡、租机、酒店 Wi-Fi、公用电脑，旅行场景叠加风险。出发前、旅途中、回国后的三段清单。","read":"13 分钟","body":"<p>旅行叠加陌生网络、临时 SIM、eSIM、酒店电脑、时差疲惫——四个因素叠在一起，验证码与关联设备特别容易失守。出发前打好开关，比在异国凌晨夺回账号轻松一个数量级。</p>\n\n      <h2>出发前</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>确认两步验证与邮箱异地可登。</li>\n        <li>应用锁开启；必要时锁定高敏聊天。</li>\n        <li>完成加密备份并确认密钥随身 offline 可查。</li>\n        <li>记录运营商与国际漫游挂失方式，以官方渠道为准。</li>\n        <li>告诉家人：旅行期间借钱消息要严核。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>旅途中</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>避免酒店商务中心保持 WhatsApp 登录。</li>\n        <li>不扫「退税」「签证加急」「景点免票」来路不明二维码。</li>\n        <li>租用手机谨慎登主号；用完登出并查关联设备。</li>\n        <li>公共环境输入验证码遮挡；警惕肩窥与虚假 Wi-Fi 门户。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>回国后 48 小时</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>关联设备大清扫，踢旅行临时会话。</li>\n        <li>若用过当地 SIM，确认无异常呼叫转移。</li>\n        <li>视情况轮换两步验证 PIN。</li>\n        <li>回顾行程中是否点过可疑链，有则加固。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>把「回国第一天清网页版」写进行李unpack 清单，与洗衣并列。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>旅行防盗＝少登陌生设备 + 不留长会话 + 回国复盘。把安全当行程模块，而不是回来才补的作业。</p>\n<h2>eSIM 与双卡切换</h2>\n      <p>旅行 eSIM 启用后，确认哪张卡接收 WhatsApp 短信；避免旧卡仍插在手机里却被当地奸商复制。回国卸载旅行 eSIM 后，重查两步验证与关联设备，防止漫游期间扫过的二维码留下会话。</p>\n      <p>长途航班 Wi-Fi 若有门户登录页，注意是否仿造品牌诱导安装证书。</p>\n<h2>证件与手机分开放</h2>\n      <p>背包被盗时，手机和护照一起丢会让换卡与身份核验雪上加霜。分开放置，云存护照扫描件加密保存。旅行保险涵盖设备盗抢时，报案号对理赔很重要，以保单条款为准。</p>\n<h2>边境口岸 SIM 摊贩</h2>\n      <p>口岸廉价 SIM 可能预设恶意短信转发。尽量在机场官方柜台购卡，第一时间查运营商 App 业务开通。入境第一天就做关联设备检查，别等回国。</p>\n<h2>小结复述</h2>\n      <p>旅行相册可以晒，验证码和登录码不行。发朋友圈前检查屏幕截屏有没有入镜 OTP。</p>\n<p>返程机票出票后设日历提醒：落地当日安全复盘。</p>\n<p>给行李箱挂联系卡用邮箱前缀而非全号，降低号码被抄已用于社工的概率。</p>\n<p>酒店保险箱放护照与备用机，手机随身不离视线高度。</p>\n<p>租车 GPS 若需扫码绑定，用流量不连陌生车载 Wi-Fi 再扫。</p>\n<p>回程海关排队勿借陌生人手机打电话，防关联设备借机扫码。</p>\n<p>返程后第一周陌生电诈上升，保持高核身门槛。</p>\n<p>旅行箱外侧勿贴含手机号快递条。</p>\n<p>机场退税队旁勿给陌生人代登热点。</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"总览","title":"HoldLine防盗总手册：30 天加固计划与终身清单","excerpt":"把前 29 篇收成可执行计划：今天、本周、本月、每季要做什么，以及被盗当日的一页纸流程。","read":"16 分钟","body":"<p>三十篇读完却不动手，效果约等于零。这篇把所有方法收成时间表：今天要什么、本周要什么、每季要什么，以及被盗当天的「一页纸」。你可以打印贴墙，也可以在日历里设重复提醒。</p>\n\n      <h2>今天（60 分钟内必做）</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>开两步验证 + 恢复邮箱 + 安全保管 PIN。</li>\n        <li>检查并清理已关联设备。</li>\n        <li>向家人同步：验证码谁都不给。</li>\n        <li>系统强锁屏 + WhatsApp 应用锁。</li>\n        <li>确认客户端来自官方商店。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>本周内完成</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>隐私页逐项收紧：头像、群邀请、状态。</li>\n        <li>需要备份则开端到端加密备份并保管密钥。</li>\n        <li>家庭 15 分钟会：演练骗码与借钱核实。</li>\n        <li>清理陌生群与可疑联系人。</li>\n        <li>商务使用者盘点谁能碰主设备与电脑会话。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>本月延展</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>帮长辈/青少年完成同等基础加固。</li>\n        <li>咨询运营商可否增加换卡保护。</li>\n        <li>全家收藏夺回流程入口。</li>\n        <li>评估聊天锁与消失消息策略。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>每季 30 分钟复盘</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>再扫关联设备；可选更新 PIN。</li>\n        <li>确认邮箱、备份密钥仍可访问。</li>\n        <li>回顾季度内是否出现过要码、假投票、共享屏幕请求。</li>\n        <li>更新紧急联系人列表与家庭暗号。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>被盗当日一页纸</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>本机重新验证号码，注册码不外传。</li>\n        <li>清关联设备，重设两步验证。</li>\n        <li>短信/电话通知亲友止损。</li>\n        <li>有钱款损失联系银行，必要时走当地警方渠道。</li>\n        <li>复盘入口，补上缺口。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>终身四句：</strong>验证码是钥匙；两步验证是第二道门；关联设备是后门清单；借钱转账要双渠道核实。背下来，比收藏三十篇更管用。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>如何持续</h2>\n      <p>选一位家庭「数码联络人」，负责提醒季度复盘；公司可并入 IT 月报。习惯的价值在于重复，而不是一次性完美。</p>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>HoldLine 30 篇的终点不是成为研究员，而是把清醒变成日程。今天完成必做五项，你已经跑赢大多数用户——剩下的，交给时间表慢慢补齐。</p>\n<h2>把清单嵌进现有习惯</h2>\n      <p>将「关联设备检查」与刷牙绑定；将「季度 PIN 轮换」与交房租同一天；公司可把复盘写进 OKR 安全教育完成率。自动化提醒胜过硬记。</p>\n      <p>若 30 篇里你只记住一篇，请回到第一篇验证码纪律；若你愿意执行一篇，请执行本篇时间表。知识 + 日程，才等于真正的HoldLine。</p>\n<h2>评估自己的完成度</h2>\n      <p>每季度自评 0–3 分：验证码纪律、两步验证、关联设备、备份密钥、转账核身。低于 2 分的项下周优先补课。分数制比「好像都做了」更诚实。邀请家人互评，增加社会监督。</p>\n<h2>版本号与更新</h2>\n      <p>本清单与官方 App 功能以当前常见版本为准，大版本更新后重新扫一遍设置路径。把HoldLine首页加入浏览器收藏，季度回来时复习是否有新骗局专题值得全家共读。</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"新加坡","source":"新加坡警察部队（SPF）","title":"新加坡警方预警：WhatsApp 账号接管骗局再度抬头","excerpt":"骗子利用已被盗账号向通讯录要验证码，或以视频通话要求屏幕共享偷看六位码，再冒充事主借钱或骗取银行卡信息。","body":"<p>2024 年 6 月 7 日，新加坡警察部队发布防诈通告，提醒公众警惕 WhatsApp 账号接管骗局再度活跃。通告指出，骗子在控制受害人账号后，会向其通讯录发送消息，以「借钱」「代买礼品卡」等理由骗取转账；部分案件还会进一步索取银行卡照片与支付 OTP，造成未授权扣款。</p>\n      <h2>通报中的两类主要手法</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>熟人骗码：</strong>受害人收到「联系人」消息，声称验证码发错，请帮忙转发六位数。该联系人账号往往已被盗。</li>\n        <li><strong>屏幕共享窥码：</strong>骗子伪装成政府或银行人员发起视频通话，要求共享屏幕「核验资料」，再触发 WhatsApp 验证码显示在屏幕上被对方看见。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>官方建议</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>验证码、银行资料、OTP 绝不外传。</li>\n        <li>即使是通讯录好友的异常请求也要警惕。</li>\n        <li>对屏幕共享请求先用其他方式核实身份。</li>\n        <li>开启两步验证；设置手机锁屏并注意物理接触。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine解读：这则通告把「骗码」与「隔空看码」并列，说明防御不能只盯着「会不会把数字打出来发送」。</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"新加坡","source":"新加坡警察部队（SPF）","title":"新加坡再发通告：OTP 连环骗导致账号连续失守","excerpt":"骗子输入受害人号码触发 OTP，再由已被盗的「熟人」来要码；得手后复制同样话术攻击下一名联系人。","body":"<p>2026 年 3 月 12 日，SPF 再次就 WhatsApp 账号被入侵发布防诈提醒。流程被写得很清楚：骗子先用受害人手机号尝试登录 → 系统向受害人发送 OTP → 已失守的熟人账号发来消息，谎称「验证码本来是我的，发错到你那里了」→ 受害人交出 OTP 后被踢下线 → 骗子用新账号继续骗下一个人。</p>\n      <h2>受害人通常何时察觉</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>自己突然无法登录 WhatsApp；或</li>\n        <li>朋友告知「你正在向别人要验证码」。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>通告强调的加固动作</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>设置 → 已关联的设备：移除不明设备。</li>\n        <li>设置 → 账户 → 两步验证：开启。</li>\n        <li>配合 ScamShield 等官方防诈工具查证可疑请求。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>通告称当时未汇总到金钱损失，但账号失守本身足以启动下一轮诈骗，社会成本仍然很高。</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"新加坡","source":"新加坡警察部队（SPF）","title":"盗号后冒充借钱：新加坡两月至少 128 起、损失约 24.9 万新元","excerpt":"自 2026 年 9 月以来，骗子接管 WhatsApp 后假冒亲友、同事索取贷款或代付款，警方公布案件量与损失并重申 ScamShield。","body":"<p>2026 年 10 月 21 日通告显示：自同年 9 月起，至少 128 起案件涉及骗子控制 WhatsApp 后，在社交媒体/即时通讯中冒充事主向联系人借钱或要求「帮忙转账」，累计损失至少约 249,000 新元。</p>\n      <h2>常见借口</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>银行转账限额到了，请你代付。</li>\n        <li>自己转账不方便，请先借一笔。</li>\n        <li>冒充子女，要求支付「AI/交流项目」费用。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>警方 ADD-CHECK-TELL 框架</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD：</strong>安装 ScamShield，过滤可疑来电与短信。</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK：</strong>用官方渠道核实；对异常转账请求直接打电话问本人。</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL：</strong>告知亲友与当局；若已转账立刻联系银行并报警。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine提醒：盗号往往只是第一阶段，第二阶段才是向通讯录「变现」。防盗与防借钱核身要一起做。</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"瑞士","source":"瑞士国家网络安全中心（NCSC）","title":"瑞士 NCSC：十月假投票骗案激增，单月逾 50 宗相关报告","excerpt":"熟人账号发来「帮小朋友投票」链接，假站收短信码或诱导扫描关联设备二维码，造成接管或静默同步。","body":"<p>NCSC 在 2026 年第 43 周周报中称，WhatsApp 账号被盗/被入侵报告再度显著上升；仅 10 月就收到 50 多份此类骗局报告，且多起攻击成功。手法核心是「善意投票」：消息来自通讯录中已被控账号，以学校或舞蹈比赛拉票为名，催你快点点开。</p>\n      <h2>假站的两条杀招</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>让你输入手机号与「短信验证码」——实为 WhatsApp 注册码，填完等于帮骗子在新设备激活账号。</li>\n        <li>展示二维码，诱导用「关联设备」扫描——账号仍可用，但骗子网页端已挂上。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>机构建议</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>即便熟人拉票也先换渠道核实。</li>\n        <li>验证码不外传、不进网页。</li>\n        <li>只在自有设备上为官方网页版扫码。</li>\n        <li>开启两步验证 PIN。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC 提醒：域名会不断更换以绕过过滤，不能靠「记住某一个坏网址」一劳永逸。</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"瑞士","source":"瑞士 NCSC","title":"瑞士春夏已现「假竞赛投票」盗号潮，秋季再度回潮","excerpt":"NCSC 早在第 21 周就描述过「为孩子投票」话术；秋季周报显示同类攻击再次扩大，说明话术可长期复用。","body":"<p>瑞士 NCSC 在春季相关周报中已记录：受害者收到看似熟人的消息，请求为某孩子的比赛投票；点击后落入盗号陷阱并可能失去 WhatsApp 访问权。到了秋季（第 43 周），机构明确写到「这类攻击并不新鲜，但近期报告上升说明它又好用了」。</p>\n      <h2>为什么要单独记住这则</h2>\n      <p>同一国家、同一机构、前后两季重复预警，证明假投票不是一次性新闻，而是可季节性复燃的「成熟产品」。家庭群与学校群尤其要预设规则：拉票链接默认先核实。</p>\n      <h2>给中文用户的迁移意义</h2>\n      <p>话术可以本地化成「助力打卡」「抽奖投票」「公益联名」，结构不变：熟人转发 + 短时压力 + 要码或扫码。HoldLine第 04 篇长文即对应该结构。</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"爱尔兰","source":"爱尔兰国家网络安全中心（NCSC）","title":"爱尔兰 NCSC：验证码骗局升级，商务 WhatsApp 暴露面受关注","excerpt":"顾问文件详述骗子如何先控熟人号，再制造紧迫感索要验证码；并提醒商务场景下账号失守可能牵出更多数据风险。","body":"<p>爱尔兰 NCSC 于 2024 年 8 月发布 WhatsApp 验证码骗局建议书，描述攻击链：取得手机号 → 在登录页输入该号触发真实验证码 → 通过已失守联系人身份联系受害人 → 以紧急协助为由索码 → 得手后接管并继续扩散。</p>\n      <h2>特别提醒：商务用途</h2>\n      <p>文件强调企业员工用 WhatsApp 处理工作时，账号一旦失守，暴露的不只是私人聊天，还可能包括客户沟通与内部协调信息。这与HoldLine「商务号专题」相互印证：经营号需要更严的设备与 PIN 保管制度。</p>\n      <h2>核心句子</h2>\n      <p>验证码绝不该分享；WhatsApp 不会主动向你要码，其他任何人也不该要。</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"开曼群岛","source":"开曼群岛皇家警察（RCIPS）","title":"加勒比警方连续预警：伪冒本地来电 + AI 语音模仿助攻盗号","excerpt":"RCIPS 称接管骗局持续有人中招；出现伪造本地区号来电，并有报告称骗子使用 AI 模仿语音制造可信度。","body":"<p>开曼群岛皇家警察在 2024 年多次更新 WhatsApp 账号接管骗局警告。8 月通告回顾经典路径：短信/WhatsApp/电话制造恐慌 → 索取六位验证码 → 账户易主 → 冒充事主诈骗联系人。9 月更新则提到：近期受害者接到看起来像本地（345）号码的电话，实为境外伪造来电显示；有报告称口音特征明显，且存在 AI 深伪音频模仿。</p>\n      <h2>警方列明「不要做」的事</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>不要回复可疑消息</li>\n        <li>不要打开附件</li>\n        <li>不要点击消息中的链接</li>\n        <li>不要生成并把 WhatsApp 六位码发给任何人</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>国际趋势观察：电话显示号码可伪造，AI 声音可模仿——「听起来像本地/像本人」不再等于安全。</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"特立尼达和多巴哥","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT：品牌大使投票话术钓鱼，得手后改商务资料继续扩散","excerpt":"攻击者以「帮我投票当品牌大使」开场骗取验证码；控制账号后可能改为商务资料，再对通讯录二次钓鱼。","body":"<p>特立尼达和多巴哥网络安全事件响应团队（TTCSIRT）在 2024 年 11 月警告正在发生的 WhatsApp 钓鱼活动。话术示例包括请对方帮忙投票支持自己成为品牌大使，语气礼貌、像朋友拜托。</p>\n      <h2>攻击阶段</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>伪装成认识的人请求「帮忙」。</li>\n        <li>诱使受害人交出 MFA/验证码。</li>\n        <li>完成账号接管。</li>\n        <li>可能把资料改成商务形象，提高后续诈骗可信度。</li>\n        <li>对受害人通讯录发动下一轮钓鱼。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>该通报提醒我们：界面上的「商务账号」标记并不自动等于可信商家，尤其当对方突然改资料并开始群发奇怪请求时。</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"中国香港","source":"香港警方 / 媒体报道（SCMP 等）","title":"香港警示即时通讯骑劫回潮：首季损失超 2000 万港元，九成涉 WhatsApp","excerpt":"网络安全及科技罪案调查科公布首季 864 宗账户被骑劫案，损失约 2040 万港元；逾九成涉及 WhatsApp。","body":"<p>据公开报道，香港警方在 2024 年 4 月前后警示即时通讯账户骑劫骗局回潮：同年第一季度录得 864 宗相关案件，损失约 2040 万港元，其中超过 90% 涉及 WhatsApp。骗子通过钓鱼链接等方式取得关键信息接管账号，再读取通讯录实施诈骗。</p>\n      <h2>本地常见后续动作</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>冒充事主向亲友借钱或要求购买点数卡。</li>\n        <li>利用过往聊天语气降低怀疑。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>防御呼应</h2>\n      <p>警方一贯建议：开两步验证、检查关联设备、慎点链接、转账前电话核实。香港用户还可配合 Scameter 等官方查骗工具。</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"中国香港","source":"香港警方（公开社交媒体通报）","title":"2025 年 3 月单月 93 宗 WhatsApp 骑劫，损失逾千万港元","excerpt":"警方提醒尤其提防「海外亲友紧急要钱」；强调登出不明设备、开两步验证、不点可疑链接、不分享验证码。","body":"<p>2025 年 4 月相关报道援引警方信息：仅 3 月便录得 93 宗 WhatsApp 账户骑劫案，损失超过 1000 万港元。骗子接管后研究聊天记录，模仿语气，再编造医疗或旅行紧急状况逼你转账。</p>\n      <h2>警方四条自我保护提示（报道归纳）</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>登出无法识别的关联设备，开启两步验证。</li>\n        <li>不要点击 WhatsApp 里的可疑链接。</li>\n        <li>绝不分享密码或验证码。</li>\n        <li>联系人突然要钱，先打电话核实。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>数字说明：即便整体某些诈骗类别后来有所回落，单月千万级损失仍足以证明威胁未消失。</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"中国香港","source":"香港警方公开案情（媒体报道）","title":"香港巨额个案：骑劫 WhatsApp + 深伪声线，单案涉款约 1.45 亿港元","excerpt":"骗徒长期监察聊天，假冒公司人员并用深伪技术模仿声线，诱导受害人转移虚拟资产。","body":"<p>据 2025 年相关案情披露，香港当年最大型骗案之一涉及 WhatsApp 账户骑劫，涉款约 1.45 亿港元。骗子并非得手后立刻要钱，而是持续观察对话；作案时假冒公司职员，并使用深伪技术模仿其声线，通过语音讯息指示受害人把虚拟资产转入另一钱包。</p>\n      <h2>这则新闻为什么重要</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>说明「听起来像同事」可能是合成音频。</li>\n        <li>高价值转账需要超出聊天与语音的双人/双渠道确认。</li>\n        <li>账户被静默同步（例如关联设备）时，骗子能获得语气与业务上下文。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine对应阅读：AI 语音冒充专题 + 关联设备手册 + 商务号防护。</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"英国","source":"英国 Action Fraud","title":"英国 Action Fraud：伪装好友索要六位激活码，报告数攀升","excerpt":"骗子先控熟人号，再以「验证码发错」为由索码；官方提出 THINK. CALL. 与两步验证建议。","body":"<p>英国反诈机构 Action Fraud 曾警告：收到逾 60 份与 WhatsApp 账号接管相关的报告。骗子控制你通讯录中某人的账号后，先正常寒暄，同时用你的号码触发六位激活码短信，再谎称码发错请你转发。</p>\n      <h2>官方行动建议</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>在 App 内举报并封锁。</li>\n        <li>永远不要分享激活码。</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——异常请求先打电话确认。</li>\n        <li>设置 → 账户 → 两步验证 → 开启。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>尽管警示发布较早，其描述的攻击模板与 2024–2026 年新加坡、爱尔兰、瑞士通报高度同构，说明这是跨年、跨国的「标准作业程序」。</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"英国","source":"英国 Action Fraud","title":"社区与宗教群成目标：先骗注册码，再向群友紧急求援","excerpt":"Action Fraud 称自当年 1 月起收到 268 份相关报告；骗子渗透大型社区/宗教群，以视频通话注册为名索要一次性密码。","body":"<p>另一则 Action Fraud 警示聚焦大型社区、校友、工作与宗教 WhatsApp 群。骗子可能先用伪头像/昵称取得信任，再以「加入群视频通话需要一次性密码」为名索要注册码，从而把受害人 WhatsApp 迁到新设备；得手后开启两步验证锁死原机主，并向群友或亲友紧急借钱。</p>\n      <h2>为何群环境更危险</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>成员彼此有「互助」文化，紧急求助成功率更高。</li>\n        <li>群内昵称与头像容易被模仿。</li>\n        <li>一次接管可触达数十上百人。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>管理员应推动全员开两步验证，并规定：群内任何要码、要钱，必须场外核实。</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"马来西亚","source":"马来西亚通讯及多媒体委员会（MCMC）","title":"MCMC：关注 WhatsApp 被黑/被封投诉，警惕收费「代找回」","excerpt":"委员会区分「账号被接管」与「平台封禁」；提醒勿分享验证码与 PIN，勿相信收费恢复中介，应走官方渠道。","body":"<p>2026 年 9 月 25 日，MCMC 发表媒体声明，称注意到公众对 WhatsApp 账号被入侵、被封锁或无法使用的关切，并已将相关个案转介 Meta 厘清处理。声明特别澄清：账号被不法人士接管后，可能被滥发诈骗/垃圾信息并违反服务条款，进而遭到平台封禁——这是两类相关但不同的问题。</p>\n      <h2>给用户的关键提醒</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>通过 WhatsApp 官方渠道恢复或申诉。</li>\n        <li>不要分享验证码或安全 PIN。</li>\n        <li>开启两步验证，远离可疑链接与不明应用。</li>\n        <li>提防以收费、索要个人信息或验证码为条件的「代找回」服务。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine补充：任何让你「先把验证码给他才能找回」的人，都可能是二次行骗。</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"印度","source":"公开技术/警政访谈报道（如 Indian Express 等）","title":"印度观察：OTP 钓鱼、SIM 换卡、合并通话听码多线并行","excerpt":"报道梳理骗子如何结合社工与电信侧手段接管 WhatsApp，并引用 Meta 建议：永不分享六位码、开启两步验证。","body":"<p>印度主流科技报道汇总多种接管路径：OTP 钓鱼（假客服/假好友要码）、SIM swapping（号卡被过户后接收短信）、短暂物理接触后关联 WhatsApp Web，以及「合并通话」让受害人在不知情中接通验证语音、旁听 OTP。</p>\n      <h2>值得记的红旗</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>未请求却收到 WhatsApp 验证码。</li>\n        <li>好友突然要 OTP。</li>\n        <li>无故被登出。</li>\n        <li>提示号码在新设备注册。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>防护组合</h2>\n      <p>报道与 Meta 回应共同强调：不分享 PIN/验证码、开两步验证、慎合并陌生通话、定期检查关联设备、限制隐私可见范围。对中文读者，这些条目与HoldLine 01–08、17 篇一一对应。</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"国际（安全厂商观察）","source":"ESET 等安全研究文章","title":"安全厂商提醒：关联设备静默同步，手机「看起来正常」也已被看光","excerpt":"最快自检是打开「已关联的设备」；陌生浏览器会话应立即登出。并强调两步验证要绑定恢复邮箱。","body":"<p>ESET 等安全文章指出：许多用户以为「没被踢下线就没被盗」。实际上攻击者可能只挂上 WhatsApp Web/桌面会话，实时阅读聊天。30 秒自检方法：设置 → Linked Devices，不认识的一律 Log out。</p>\n      <h2>其他妥协信号</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>异常登出后又要登录。</li>\n        <li>出现你未发送消息的回复。</li>\n        <li>联系人反馈异常索要。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>恢复邮箱的重要性</h2>\n      <p>文章强调两步验证开启时务必添加恢复邮箱——若别人抢先设 PIN，有无邮箱可能决定你是几分钟找回还是等待多日。这与 WhatsApp 帮助中心的夺回说明一致。</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"新加坡","source":"ScamShield / Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield 图解：WhatsApp 接管四步，以及为何「谁要 OTP 都是诈骗」","excerpt":"官方问答把攻击拆成触发 OTP、熟人索码、踢下线、冒充扩散四步，并给出两步验证与关联设备清理指引。","body":"<p>新加坡 ScamShield 相关问答用极清楚的四步描述接管：1）骗子用你的号登录触发 OTP；2）已被盗的联系人来要你转发；3）你交出后被登出；4）骗子用你的身份重复骗下家。</p>\n      <h2>官方原级提醒</h2>\n      <p>任何分享 WhatsApp OTP 的请求，不论看起来来自谁，都应视为诈骗。OTP 只属于接收者本人；平台与「客服」都不会这样要码。</p>\n      <h2>保护清单</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>永不分享 OTP</li>\n        <li>开启两步验证</li>\n        <li>审查并移除陌生关联设备</li>\n        <li>若被盗，用非 WhatsApp 渠道通知联系人</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"全球（平台方）","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp 官方安全页：两步验证、只用正版、群组控制与举报工具","excerpt":"平台重申多层防护与用户可操作开关；明确假冒客户端会带来严重隐私与安全风险。","body":"<p>WhatsApp 官方安全中心概述其默认隐私、垃圾信息检测、异常接管提示等能力，并给用户可执行清单：开启两步验证、始终使用官方应用、管理群组添加权限、学习识别骗局、善用封锁与举报、必要时重新注册夺回账号。</p>\n      <h2>对「下软件防盗」读者的意义</h2>\n      <p>官方立场非常明确：先把正版客户端与内置开关用好，而不是寻找来路不明的第三方「防盗插件」。HoldLine工具页的排序也遵循这一原则。</p>\n      <h2>举报的价值</h2>\n      <p>举报不一定让你立刻拿回损失，但能帮助平台打击滥用账号，降低你的通讯录继续被精准打击的概率。</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"牙买加","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"牙买加 CIRT：钓鱼与劫持并存，强调登出关联设备并重开两步验证","excerpt":"警报描述恶意链接与账号劫持风险；若怀疑失守，应清理关联设备、重置两步验证，并向 spam@whatsapp.com 等渠道举报钓鱼（以官方当前指引为准）。","body":"<p>牙买加网络安全事件响应团队曾发布面向 WhatsApp 用户的威胁上升警报，指出诈骗信息与账号劫持并行。钓鱼可能伪装成正规机构，诱导点击假登录页或交出验证码；劫持成功后可冒充事主、读取联系人并扩散。</p>\n      <h2>失守后的技术动作（通报归纳）</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>设置 → Linked Devices：登出全部不明设备。</li>\n        <li>重开/重设两步验证。</li>\n        <li>向联系人预警。</li>\n        <li>按官方说明举报钓鱼样本。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>该警报与欧美、亚洲通稿再次对齐：清理关联设备 + 两步验证是国际通用急救包。</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"全球","source":"公开报道援引 Meta 数据","title":"平台治理侧写：Meta 披露打击诈骗广告与关联诈骗中心的 WhatsApp 账号","excerpt":"报道援引：Meta 在 2025 上半年撤销逾 680 万个与诈骗中心相关的 WhatsApp 账号；同时提醒用户端仍需自保。","body":"<p>在香港等地区就科技罪案召开的公开说明中，有报道援引 Meta 数据：2025 上半年撤销了超过 680 万个与诈骗中心相关的 WhatsApp 账号，并在其他平台清理大量诈骗广告。这说明平台侧在持续打击，但无法替代用户端的两步验证与验证码纪律。</p>\n      <h2>阅读这篇的正确姿势</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>不要因为「平台会封号」就放松个人设置。</li>\n        <li>被盗号用来群发的账号，可能很快被封，但你的亲友可能在被封前已经完成转账。</li>\n        <li>个人能做的最快动作，仍是：不给码、开两步、清设备、要钱先核实。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>国际新闻合集的结论：从加勒比到西欧、从东南亚到东亚，话术细节不同，结构高度相似。把HoldLine 30 篇长文当成「操作手册」，把本页当成「为什么现在就要做」的证据墙。</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"国际动态：WhatsApp 被盗与账号接管最新观察","lead":"以下 20 篇为HoldLine根据各国警方、网络安全机构与公开报道整理的专题稿，聚焦近年 WhatsApp 账号被接管、骗码、假投票与冒充借钱等态势。文中数据与日期来自公开通稿；界面与损失数字可能随后续统计调整，请以原文机构发布为准。"},"toolsIntro":{"title":"推荐下载：降低 WhatsApp 被盗风险的软件与工具","lead":"没有「一键防盗神器」。真正有效的是：官方 WhatsApp、系统安全能力、正规防诈 App，再加上两步验证与验证码纪律。本页只推荐可从官方渠道获取的工具，并写清它能做什么、不能做什么。"},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"必装","name":"WhatsApp 官方客户端","platforms":"iOS · Android","summary":"唯一应安装的即时通讯本体。改装版、增强版、网盘 APK 都是高风险。","why":["官方客户端才会持续获得安全更新、诈骗提示与账户恢复能力。","非官方包可能内置后门，端到端加密也救不了「端点已沦陷」。","警方与 Meta 反复提醒：只用官方应用商店中的 WhatsApp。"],"steps":["iPhone：仅从 App Store 搜索 WhatsApp Messenger 安装/更新。","Android：优先 Google Play；部分地区可用手机厂商官方应用商店中的正式版。","安装后立刻开启两步验证，并检查「已关联的设备」。"],"stepsCaption":"从官方商店安装，并立刻开启两步验证","caution":"不要从群文件、网盘、短链下载所谓 Plus / GB / 防撤回版。","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp 安全中心","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play · WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"推荐","name":"WhatsApp 桌面版 / 网页版（官方）","platforms":"Windows · macOS · 浏览器","summary":"方便电脑回复，但必须从官方入口关联；公用电脑用完立刻在手机端登出。","why":["官方桌面端比随便搜索到的「Web 登录页」安全得多。","关联设备是静默偷窥的高发入口，正规客户端便于你主动管理会话。"],"steps":["在手机 WhatsApp → 已关联的设备 → 关联新设备。","电脑打开官方桌面应用，或浏览器手动输入官方网页地址后扫码。","公用设备不要勾选保持登录；用完回手机踢掉会话。"],"stepsCaption":"手机关联设备 → 官方扫码 → 用完登出","caution":"绝不扫描聊天里或陌生网站出示的「登录/投票」二维码。","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp 桌面下载（官网）","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"必开","name":"WhatsApp 内置安全功能（免费）","platforms":"App 内设置","summary":"两步验证、诈骗提示（Scam Alert）、应用锁、隐私限制——不需要另装第三方「防盗插件」。","why":["两步验证直接提高接管成本，是各国警方通稿里的标准建议。","Scam Alert 可在设备侧提示可疑会话（以你版本是否提供为准）。","应用锁降低「手机已解锁被顺手打开」的风险。"],"steps":["设置 → 账户 → 两步验证 → 开启，并绑定可登录的邮箱。","设置中查找诈骗提示 / Scam Alert（若有）并打开。","开启应用锁（指纹/面容）；隐私里限制陌生人拉群。","每周检查一次「已关联的设备」。"],"stepsCaption":"设置账户两步验证，绑定恢复邮箱","caution":"内置功能不能阻止你主动把验证码发给骗子——纪律仍是第一防线。","links":[{"label":"如何防范可疑消息（帮助中心）","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"地区推荐","name":"ScamShield（新加坡防诈套件）","platforms":"iOS · Android","summary":"新加坡政府相关防诈工具：可协助拦截可疑来电/短信，并查验可疑信息。当地用户强烈建议安装。","why":["新加坡警方通报 WhatsApp 盗号与冒充借钱案时，常直接建议安装 ScamShield。","可过滤部分诈骗短信与来电，降低「骗码电话/钓鱼短信」到达率。","官网提供查证渠道与预警信息。"],"steps":["只从官方短链或 App Store / Google Play 安装。","按指引开启通话/短信过滤相关权限。","遇到可疑请求，可结合其查证功能与热线渠道核实。"],"stepsCaption":"官方商店下载并开启过滤权限","caution":"非新加坡号码/环境可能不适用；不要下载仿冒「ScamShield」山寨包。","links":[{"label":"ScamShield 官网说明","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS 下载（官方跳转）","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android 下载（官方跳转）","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"地区推荐","name":"Scameter / Scameter+（香港防骗视伏器）","platforms":"网页 · App（以警方公布为准）","summary":"香港警方推广的查骗工具，可用于查证可疑平台、账户等。香港用户建议配合使用。","why":["香港「网上账户骑劫」案件中 WhatsApp 占比极高，警方持续呼吁使用官方防骗工具。","转账或点击前先查证，能减少盗号后的二次金钱损失。"],"steps":["通过香港警方 CyberDefender / 官方公布渠道进入 Scameter。","对陌生收款账户、可疑网站先查再操作。","同时在 WhatsApp 开启两步验证并清查关联设备。"],"stepsCaption":"官方入口查证可疑链接或账户","caution":"查证工具不能替代「不给验证码」；盗号本身仍要靠账户设置与习惯。","links":[{"label":"香港政府相关防骗资讯入口","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"推荐","name":"Google Play 保护机制 / 系统安全更新","platforms":"Android · iOS","summary":"让系统与应用商店替你挡住大量恶意安装包，减少木马偷短信、偷屏幕的风险。","why":["部分盗号会配合恶意 App 读取通知或辅助操作。","及时系统补丁能缩小已知漏洞窗口。"],"steps":["Android：开启 Google Play 保护机制；只允许官方商店安装。","iOS/Android：打开自动更新，定期安装系统补丁。","拒绝「允许未知来源安装」的临时诱惑。"],"stepsCaption":"开启系统保护并保持更新","caution":"系统防护挡不住社工骗码；它是设备层，不是话术层。","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect 说明","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"推荐","name":"密码管理器（保管两步验证 PIN）","platforms":"跨平台","summary":"用来安全保存 WhatsApp 两步验证 PIN 与邮箱密码，避免设成弱口令或写在聊天里。","why":["许多人因「怕忘记」而设 123456，或把 PIN 发到自己另一个聊天备份——反而更危险。","正规密码管理器可生成并加密保存高强度口令。"],"steps":["从官方商店安装 Bitwarden、1Password、或系统自带钥匙串/密码等可信产品。","为 WhatsApp 两步验证 PIN 单独建一条记录。","密码管理器本身开启强主密码与生物识别。"],"stepsCaption":"用密码库安全保存 PIN","caution":"不要用未知「PIN 保管 App」；更不要把 PIN 存进未加密备忘录云同步到到处。","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden 官网","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"可选","name":"正规手机安全软件（防木马，不防骗术）","platforms":"Android 为主","summary":"若你经常侧载应用或设备环境复杂，可选知名厂商安全套件；它主要防恶意软件，不是防熟人要码。","why":["可辅助发现可疑应用、钓鱼站点（能力因产品而异）。","对「短信窃取木马」一类威胁有一定价值。"],"steps":["只从 Google Play / App Store 安装知名产品（如 Malwarebytes、Bitdefender 等，按你地区可用性选择）。","安装后做一次全盘扫描，复查短信/无障碍权限异常应用。","不要同时堆叠多个互相冲突的安全套件。"],"stepsCaption":"官方商店安装后全盘扫描","caution":"任何杀毒都无法阻止你主动交出验证码；也绝不下载「WhatsApp 专杀盗号」无名 APK。","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes 官网","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["名称含「WhatsApp 防盗 / 找回大师 / 破解监视」却要求关闭 Play 保护的安装包","要求你把验证码发给「客服机器人」才能开通防护的工具","收费「代找回账号」并索要远程控制或注册码的服务","来自网盘、Telegram 频道、短视频评论区的神秘 APK"]},"ms":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"peraturan teras","title":"Kod pengesahan tidak akan diteruskan: pintu masuk yang paling biasa dan membawa maut kepada penggodaman akaun","excerpt":"Kod pendaftaran enam digit adalah sama dengan kunci akaun. Meminta kod daripada kenalan, menggesa kod daripada perkhidmatan pelanggan palsu dan mengisi kod di tapak memancing—semuanya pada dasarnya adalah pisau yang sama. Artikel ini menerangkan seni pertuturan, prinsip dan penolakan pertuturan sekali gus.","read":"12 minit","body":"<p>Jika anda hanya boleh mengingati satu peraturan anti-kecurian WhatsApp, ingat yang ini:<strong>Jangan sekali-kali beritahu sesiapa kod pengesahan itu dan jangan masukkannya pada mana-mana halaman web yang \"nampak rasmi\".</strong>Apabila polis dan agensi keselamatan siber di banyak tempat di seluruh dunia melaporkan kes pengambilalihan akaun WhatsApp, mereka hampir selalu menyenaraikan \"menipu kod pengesahan enam digit\" di bahagian atas senarai. Alasannya mudah - WhatsApp menggunakan nombor telefon bimbit sebagai identiti akaun, dan kod pendaftaran dalam mesej teks atau mesej suara adalah kunci untuk \"membuktikan bahawa anda adalah pemilik\" pada peranti baharu itu.</p>\n      <p>Ramai orang tersilap percaya bahawa akaun penggodaman mesti memerlukan kemahiran penggodaman lanjutan. Pada hakikatnya, sebahagian besar kes yang berjaya bergantung kepada<strong>kejuruteraan sosial</strong>: Penipu menggunakan kepercayaan, kesopanan, rasa tergesa-gesa, dan mentaliti \"bantu saya\" untuk membuat anda menyerahkan kunci sendiri. Setelah kod pengesahan diperoleh, pihak lain boleh melengkapkan pendaftaran pada telefon mudah alih mereka, menendang anda ke luar talian, dan kemudian berpura-pura menjadi anda untuk menipu orang seterusnya.</p>\n\n      <h2>验证码到底是什么</h2>\n      <p>当你或任何人试图用某个手机号登录/注册 WhatsApp 时，系统会向该号码发送一串通常为六位数的注册码（也可能通过语音电话播报）。正确输入这串数字，就等于告诉 WhatsApp：「持有这张 SIM、能收到短信的人，就是账号主人。」</p>\n      <p>因此它和银行卡动态口令、邮箱二次验证码属于同一类敏感信息。区别在于：WhatsApp 的验证码一旦被滥用，损失往往不只是「一个 App」，而是你的整本通讯录、群聊信任关系，以及亲友可能被二次诈骗的金钱风险。</p>\n\n      <h2>骗子最常用的话术长什么样</h2>\n      <p>话术会变，结构很少变。下面这些句子，近年在多个国家的警情通报里反复出现：</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>「验证码发错到你手机了，帮我回一下那六个数字。」——常见于熟人账号已被盗后的连环骗。</li>\n        <li>「我是 WhatsApp / Meta / 客服，你的号异常，把验证码发给我才能解封。」——官方不会这样要码。</li>\n        <li>「银行风控 / 快递丢失 / 税费退款，需要你提供 WhatsApp 验证码配合核验。」——跨渠道冒充。</li>\n        <li>「投票、抽奖、问卷只要 10 秒」——假站先收手机号，再收你刚收到的短信码。</li>\n        <li>「我帮你朋友报名抽奖，他把验证码发你了，你转给我就行。」——把你卷进别人的骗局叙事。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>判断标准只有一个：</strong>只要对方目标是「拿到你手机上刚出现的 WhatsApp 注册码」，无论故事多么感人、多么紧急、对方头像多么眼熟，都按盗号处理。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>为什么「熟人要码」特别危险</h2>\n      <p>骗子很少一上来就用陌生号骗你。更高效的做法是：先拿下 A，再用 A 的聊天窗口找 B。B 看到熟悉的名字和头像，防备会立刻下降。B 交出验证码后，骗子得到 B，再去骗 C——形成<strong>通讯录传染病</strong>。</p>\n      <p>所以当你收到「好友」索要验证码时，正确反应不是「他平时挺好的，应该没事」，而是：<strong>他的号可能已经不在他手里了。</strong>请改用电话打到你通讯录里原来的号码，或当面确认。不要用对方临时发来的「新号码」回拨作为唯一核身手段。</p>\n\n      <h2>正确拒绝话术（可直接复制）</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>「验证码我不会发给任何人，包括亲戚。你如果真是本人，打电话给我。」</li>\n        <li>「WhatsApp 官方不会要验证码。这条我当诈骗处理了。」</li>\n        <li>「我这边不能协助转码。你去重新登录自己的号就行。」</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>说完即可拉黑或举报，不必继续解释。骗子靠的就是把你拖进长对话，让你不好意思拒绝。</p>\n\n      <h2>如果你已经把码发出去了</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>立刻在本机打开 WhatsApp，尝试用自己的号码重新验证登录，把对方踢下线。</li>\n        <li>登录成功后检查「已关联的设备」，登出全部不明会话。</li>\n        <li>重设两步验证 PIN，并确认恢复邮箱仍是你的。</li>\n        <li>马上通知亲友：忽略任何「你」发出的借钱、要码、投票链接。</li>\n        <li>若已发生转账损失，联系银行并考虑报警。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>验证码不是「随便一个数字」，而是账号主权。HoldLine把这条放在第一篇，是因为它挡住的攻击面最大、成本最低、也最容易教给家人。把「谁要码都不给」练成肌肉反应，后面所有进阶设置才会真正发挥作用。</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"防护设置","title":"两步验证完整指南：PIN、恢复邮箱与常见坑","excerpt":"开启两步验证后，光有短信码往往不够。本篇按步骤写清怎么开、PIN 怎么设、邮箱为什么不能省，以及被抢先设 PIN 时怎么办。","read":"14 分钟","body":"<p>两步验证是 WhatsApp 官方反复推荐的基础防护。它的核心思想很朴素：就算骗子暂时拿到了你的短信验证码，在新设备完成注册时，还需要你另外设定的 PIN（或后续升级形态的密码）。少了第二道门，接管成本会显著上升。</p>\n      <p>很多用户「听说要开」却一直拖着，或开了却跳过邮箱。结果真正出事时，不是防不住，就是找回过程被拖成几天。这篇把完整做法与坑点一次写清楚。</p>\n\n      <h2>它能防什么，不能防什么</h2>\n      <p><strong>能显著加强：</strong>对方试图用你的号码在另一台手机上重新注册；你短暂泄露了一次短信码后的补救窗口；降低「只靠骗码」成功率。</p>\n      <p><strong>不能单独解决：</strong>你已经把 PIN 也告诉了对方；手机被木马实时偷看屏幕；你主动扫描了对方的关联二维码（网页端挂马）；你把解锁手机借给别人随意操作。</p>\n      <p>所以两步验证是「必要非充分」条件：必须开，但还要配合验证码纪律与关联设备巡检。</p>\n\n      <h2>开启路径（以常见版本为准）</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>打开 WhatsApp，进入<strong>设置</strong>。</li>\n        <li>点<strong>账户</strong> → <strong>两步验证</strong>。</li>\n        <li>选择开启，按提示创建 6 位 PIN（部分地区/版本可能引导设置更强密码）。</li>\n        <li>再次确认 PIN。</li>\n        <li>添加<strong>你能长期登录的电子邮箱</strong>作为恢复方式，并完成邮箱验证（若有）。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>不同手机品牌的菜单文案可能略有出入（例如从头像进入设置），但「账户 → 两步验证」这一层逻辑通常稳定。若找不到，用应用内搜索「两步」关键词。</p>\n\n      <h2>PIN 应该怎么设</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>不要用生日、纪念日、车牌、手机尾号、身份证片段。</li>\n        <li>不要用 123456、000000、888888、112233 这类模式。</li>\n        <li>不要与微信/支付宝/银行卡/邮箱密码相同或简单变换。</li>\n        <li>建议写进密码管理器，或写在只有你能看到的离线位置；不要发到群里「备份」。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>PIN 的目标不是「好记到不用写」，而是「别人猜不到、也套不走」。记不住可以保管，但不要因此设成弱密码。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>为什么恢复邮箱几乎必填</h2>\n      <p>开启两步验证后，若你忘记 PIN，官方流程往往依赖你登记过的邮箱来协助重置；若没有可用邮箱，你可能需要等待一段时间（公开资料中常见描述为最长约七天）才能在无 PIN 情况下继续。更糟的情况是：盗号者抢先开启两步验证并绑定他们的邮箱，你夺回时会更被动。</p>\n      <p>因此邮箱要满足：</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>是你本人长期使用、能收信的。</li>\n        <li>该邮箱本身也有强密码与二次验证。</li>\n        <li>不要用已经废弃、公司即将离职清理、或与他人共用的邮箱。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>开启之后还要做什么</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>立刻检查「已关联的设备」，清掉不明会话。</li>\n        <li>告诉家人：两步验证的 PIN 同样不能告诉「客服」。</li>\n        <li>每隔几个月或换机后，确认两步验证仍处于开启状态。</li>\n        <li>若曾怀疑泄露，直接关掉再重新开启，换一套新 PIN。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>被要求输入「我没设过的 PIN」</h2>\n      <p>这通常意味着：有人曾控制你的号并启用了两步验证。请先完成短信注册码验证（这一步常能把对方主会话踢下线），再按界面提示处理 PIN。详细应急见本站「陌生 PIN」专题。核心原则是：<strong>不要因为卡在 PIN 就放弃重新验证；也不要把短信码发给自称能帮你「破解 PIN」的人。</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>两步验证花费不到两分钟，却能把「骗到一次短信码就能偷号」变成「还得再过你设的门」。配合恢复邮箱与验证码纪律，它是HoldLine建议的「今天必须完成」事项之一。</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"防护设置","title":"已关联设备完全手册：如何发现静默偷窥","excerpt":"手机还能正常聊天，不代表没有第二双眼睛。网页版/电脑版一旦被挂上，对方可实时同步。教你检查、踢人、以及怎样避免再次中招。","read":"13 分钟","body":"<p>有一种盗号并不戏剧：你没有突然被登出，聊天也还在，直到某天亲友说「你刚才问我要验证码」，或你在设置里看见一个从不认识的浏览器会话。这就是<strong>关联设备静默接入</strong>——攻击者不一定夺走手机主会话，而是让 WhatsApp Web / 桌面端挂在旁边同步消息。</p>\n      <p>对骗子来说，这种方式极其划算：你越晚发现，他们就能读越多群聊、搜集越多借钱话术素材，甚至直接以你的口吻在对话里操作。</p>\n\n      <h2>关联设备机制简述</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp 允许你在手机主客户端之外，把电脑、平板、浏览器等关联为配套设备。正规用法是：在手机上打开「已关联的设备」→「关联新设备」，再扫描官方网页或桌面应用显示的二维码。</p>\n      <p>风险点在于：这个「扫码授权」流程本身是合法功能。骗子要做的，只是把<strong>他们控制的二维码</strong>放到假网站、假投票页、或直接在你解锁的手机上操作完成扫码。</p>\n\n      <h2>如何检查（请现在就做一次）</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>打开 WhatsApp → <strong>设置</strong> → <strong>已关联的设备</strong>（Android 有时在右上角更多菜单）。</li>\n        <li>查看列表中每一个条目：设备类型、大致活跃时间、名称是否眼熟。</li>\n        <li>对任何「我不记得关联过」的项目：点进去 → <strong>退出 / 登出</strong>。</li>\n        <li>若列表很长且你曾公用电脑登录，建议不熟悉的全部退出，需要时再重新官方关联。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>养成习惯：每周固定一天（例如周日晚上）花 30 秒扫一眼列表。借出手机、点击可疑链接、用过酒店电脑之后，立刻加查一次。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>高危场景清单</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>扫描了聊天里发来的「验证 / 投票 / 领奖」二维码。</li>\n        <li>在非官方网站看到二维码，并按指示用 WhatsApp 去扫。</li>\n        <li>把已解锁手机交给别人「帮忙看个东西」「帮我连网页版」。</li>\n        <li>在网吧/展会公用电脑勾选了保持登录，事后忘记登出。</li>\n        <li>电脑中毒后，浏览器里的 WhatsApp Web 会话被共用。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>发现陌生设备后的处理顺序</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>立即登出该设备（以及所有不确定设备）。</li>\n        <li>检查是否仍能控制主手机会话；若曾被挤下线，按夺回流程重新验证号码。</li>\n        <li>重设两步验证。</li>\n        <li>回顾最近点过的链接、扫描过的码，避免重复中招。</li>\n        <li>通知近期聊过敏感话题的联系人提高警惕。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>安全使用电脑版的原则</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>只从官方渠道获取桌面应用，或自己在浏览器输入官方网页地址。</li>\n        <li>关联动作只从手机 App 内发起，不从陌生页面「倒逼」你去扫。</li>\n        <li>公用设备不要保持登录；用完回手机端踢掉。</li>\n        <li>长期不用的电脑会话，宁可多余登出。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>「已关联的设备」是你账号的后门清单。它不是高级功能页，而是安全巡检页。把清查做成习惯，很多「我也不知道怎么被看光」的案例，其实在第一周就能被截住。</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"骗局识破","title":"假投票、假抽奖、假助力：熟人转发也不能信","excerpt":"「帮我女儿投一票」常常来自已被盗的账号。假站要短信码或诱导扫关联码，是近年增长很快的接管手法。","read":"12 minit","body":"<p>善意是社交软件里最容易被武器化的品质。骗子很清楚：直接跟你说「把验证码给我」会触发警报；但换成「帮小朋友投票，只要十秒」，很多人会点进去。欧洲等地的网络安全机构都曾公开预警：盗号者控制熟人账号后，群发投票/竞赛助力请求，再把流量导向仿冒网站。</p>\n\n      <h2>一场典型攻击如何展开</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>骗子已控制你朋友的 WhatsApp。</li>\n        <li>你收到朋友私聊或群消息：「拜托投一下，今天截止！」并附链接。</li>\n        <li>网页做得像学校、舞蹈比赛、公益投票，要求输入手机号。</li>\n        <li>你的手机收到 WhatsApp/短信验证码——其实是注册码。</li>\n        <li>你把码填进网页，对方立刻在另一台设备完成接管；或</li>\n        <li>网页展示二维码，叫你「用 WhatsApp 扫码完成投票」，实则完成设备关联。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>第一种让你失去主账号；第二种可能让你在「手机仍可用」的情况下被同步偷窥。两者都危险。</p>\n\n      <h2>为什么预览和页面看起来很真</h2>\n      <p>现代钓鱼会准备像样的 logo、倒计时、候选人照片，甚至做多语言。链接预览卡片也能做得像正规站点。你不能靠「好不好看」判断真伪，要靠<strong>它要求你做什么</strong>：</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>要 WhatsApp/短信验证码？高危。</li>\n        <li>要你用「关联设备」扫码？高危。</li>\n        <li>要你下载未知 APK 才能投票？高危。</li>\n        <li>要银行卡拍照、OTP？已经从盗号升级到盗刷。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>收到投票请求时的标准动作</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>先假设对方账号可能已被盗。</li>\n        <li>用电话或当面问：「你是不是在拉票？有没有发链接？」</li>\n        <li>在确认前，不点链接、不填码、不扫码。</li>\n        <li>若对方否认，提醒他检查关联设备并重设两步验证。</li>\n        <li>把原消息举报，避免继续在群里传播。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>真正的公开投票极少需要你提供 WhatsApp 注册码。需要登录的平台，也应走该平台自己的账号体系，而不是「通用手机验证码万能盒」。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>若你已经点过或填过</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>填过短信码：立刻重新验证自己的 WhatsApp 号码夺回，再清设备、开两步验证。</li>\n        <li>扫过二维码：马上到「已关联的设备」踢人。</li>\n        <li>下过安装包：停用未知应用，考虑杀毒/重装评估，并检查短信与辅助功能权限。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>家庭与公司里怎么统一口径</h2>\n      <p>一个人警惕还不够。假投票链接最容易在家族群、同学群、公司群里二次转发，每一次转发都在给骗子「洗白」来源。建议在固定群置顶一句短规则：「任何要 WhatsApp 验证码或扫码投票的链接，先打电话核实；未核实前不要点。」规则越短，越容易被遵守。</p>\n      <p>若你是群管理员，发现此类消息应立刻删除并提醒成员检查关联设备。若发送者是熟人，优先用其他渠道提醒对方「你的号可能已被盗」，而不是只在原聊天里回「这是诈骗」——因为对面可能根本不是本人在看。</p>\n\n      <h2>和「真投票」如何区分</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>正规活动几乎不会要你提供 WhatsApp 注册码。</li>\n        <li>正规活动若需登录，通常走自己的账号体系，而不是通用「短信码万能验证」。</li>\n        <li>正规活动不会诱导你用「已关联的设备」去扫来路不明的二维码。</li>\n        <li>真正紧急的公益/校园投票，也经得起你花一分钟打电话问本人。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>把「区分真假」的负担，从「看页面漂不漂亮」转移到「它有没有越权索取 WhatsApp 钥匙」。这一步想清楚，绝大多数假投票站会原形毕露。</p>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>假投票骗局打的是「熟人 + 善意 + 短时操作」组合拳。把规则改成：<strong>所有要码或扫码的投票，先核实、默认不信</strong>，就能避开一大类当前非常活跃的盗号路径。善意值得保留，但钥匙不能随手交出。</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"骗局识破","title":"熟人连环骗码：通讯录如何变成传播链","excerpt":"一个号失守，往往带着整本通讯录一起失火。拆解连环接管的传播机制，以及如何在第二环就切断。","read":"14 分钟","body":"<p>单独看，「把验证码发给朋友」像一次疏忽；从攻击者视角，这是可复制的增长策略。新加坡、马来西亚等地警方通报过的模式高度相似：骗子先用钓鱼或骗码拿下第一个号 → 事主被挤下线 → 骗子用该身份向整本通讯录索要「误发的验证码」 → 下一名受害人中招 → 链条继续。整个过程往往在两小时内完成十几层扩散，因为每一层发送者都是你「认识并信任」的人。</p>\n      <p>连环骗码也被称为「接管链」或「通讯录传染病」。它不依赖一次群发广告，而是利用人际网络的信任边不断跳跃。对防御方而言，这意味着：<strong>你的安全不只取决于自己守不守纪律，还取决于你通讯录里是否有薄弱环节</strong>——但更重要的是，当你处于「被要码」这一环时，你有机会成为整条链的终点。</p>\n\n      <h2>链条上的每一个角色</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>0 号入口：</strong>最早被骗码、中钓鱼、扫了假投票码，或关联设备被静默挂上的人。他们未必立刻发现被盗。</li>\n        <li><strong>传播节点：</strong>每个新被接管的账号，都自动成为下一波「可信发送者」。头像、昵称、近期聊天语境都是真的——只是操作者换了人。</li>\n        <li><strong>筛选节点：</strong>骗子会优先私聊近期聊过天的联系人、家人、同事，成功率远高于陌生推销。</li>\n        <li><strong>变现节点：</strong>积累够多号后，开始借钱、要银行卡截图、要支付 OTP、拉假投资群，把「信任资产」换成现金。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>对你而言，最关键的觉悟是：当你成为「被要码的那个人」时，你处在切断链条的最佳位置。你一拒绝，这一支就断了；你一妥协，你的通讯录会变成别人的弹药库。</p>\n\n      <h2>典型话术为何总能骗到人</h2>\n      <p>「验证码发错了，帮我念一下」这句话之所以有效，是因为它同时满足了三个心理按钮：<strong>熟人可信</strong>（头像没变）、<strong>互助义务</strong>（帮个小忙）、<strong>操作极简</strong>（复制六个数字）。骗子还会追加「别告诉别人」「我在开会不方便打电话」，阻止你用最有效的核实手段——语音通话。</p>\n      <p>在职场环境里，变体包括「人事系统要验证码」「公司 VPN 登记」；在家庭环境里，变体包括「医院挂号」「学校报名」。故事换皮，核心不变：要你交出手机刚收到的 WhatsApp 注册码。</p>\n\n      <h2>反常信号（即使头像很熟）</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>突然讨论验证码、OTP、银行卡照片、身份证正反面。</li>\n        <li>语气过分急切，阻止你打电话或视频。</li>\n        <li>深夜/工作时间用很不像本人的措辞（过于正式或过于急促）。</li>\n        <li>刚要完码，又开始借钱、拉群、发投资链接。</li>\n        <li>同一时段多个好友同时跟你说「某某在要我的码」。</li>\n        <li>对方称「号换了」却不愿用旧号码接听你的回拨。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>家规级规则：</strong>任何人在 WhatsApp 里要验证码，默认视为「对方号可能被盗」，先电话核实，绝不先给码。这条规则对子女、对父母、对老板都一视同仁。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>切断链条的家庭与公司做法</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>建立共识：验证码请求 = 默认诈骗警报，不是「不好意思拒绝」的社交场合。</li>\n        <li>用短信、邮件或家庭群（非 WhatsApp 亦可）群发一次提醒：「任何人用 WhatsApp 要码都当作号被盗。」</li>\n        <li>公司 IT 或行政准备固定公告模板，发现异常联系人立刻置顶，并指定值班人接听核实电话。</li>\n        <li>中招者夺回后，必须在相关群里用<strong>其他已验证渠道</strong>二次声明「已夺回，此前消息作废」。</li>\n        <li>定期家庭/团队演练：互相扮演骗码者，练习三句拒绝话术。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>如果你是被盗本人：止损时间线</h2>\n      <p>除了按本站「夺回流程」重新验证号码，请把「通知联系人」放在与「清关联设备」同等优先级。建议顺序：</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>夺回主会话并开启或重设两步验证。</li>\n        <li>立即用短信群发核心联系人：「我的 WhatsApp 刚才被盗，凡是要码、借钱、投票链接均非本人。」</li>\n        <li>请一位可信亲友用其他渠道在家族群/同事群代为公告。</li>\n        <li>回顾被盗前 48 小时你发过什么，判断链条可能延伸到哪一层。</li>\n        <li>若已有亲友转账，协助其联系银行并保留证据，必要时通过当地警方渠道报案。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>常见错误是：夺回后以为「结束了」，却没有对外警告，导致骗子在你重新上线前仍用残留信任继续借钱。</p>\n\n      <h2>常见误区</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>误区：「对方是亲戚，给一下没关系。」——亲戚的号同样会被盗，且骗子专挑亲属链。</li>\n        <li>误区：「我只给码不给 PIN 就安全。」——注册码足以完成接管；PIN 是第二道门，不能先送钥匙。</li>\n        <li>误区：「我很快撤消息就行。」——对方自动化脚本可在你反应前完成注册。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>连环骗码不是「偶发人际乌龙」，而是结构化、可规模化的传播攻击。把通讯录里的每一个人都训练成「拒绝点」，比只加固自己的手机更重要——因为攻击总会从最松的那一环进来。记住：你拒绝一次要码，可能同时保护了你背后十几位联系人的号与钱。</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"骗局识破","title":"屏幕共享盗号：视频通话里的隔空取码","excerpt":"假冒官员或银行人员要求共享屏幕，再让验证码出现在你的画面上。你没有「发送」数字，码却已经被看走。","read":"13 分钟","body":"<p>传统骗码需要你把数字打出来发给对方；屏幕共享骗局把这一步省略了。骗子通过 WhatsApp 视频、Zoom、Teams 甚至系统自带的「屏幕共享」功能建立「官方感」，再以账户解冻、税务核查、反洗钱排查、快递理赔为由，要求你立刻共享整个手机屏幕。随后他们指挥你打开短信、打开 WhatsApp 重新登录、或点开银行 App——六位注册码、支付动态口令、甚至两步验证界面都会完整出现在对方画面里。</p>\n      <p>多国警方通报中出现过伪装成执法机关、税务局、央行客服、银行风控的版本。对不熟悉数字业务的用户，这套流程看起来「很专业」：有视频、有工牌截图、有术语、还有倒计时压迫。你可能全程觉得自己「没有发送任何验证码」，但对方已经用眼抄走了数字。</p>\n\n      <h2>为什么共享屏幕格外危险</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>锁屏通知横幅可能在共享中直接显示验证码，无需你点开短信。</li>\n        <li>对方可指导你进入「已关联的设备」扫码、关闭两步验证、修改邮箱。</li>\n        <li>若你同时共享了电脑桌面，浏览器里保存的密码、会话 Cookie 也可能暴露。</li>\n        <li>骗局尾声常要求安装「远程协助」App，实则为长期控制通道。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>视频里常见的压迫话术</h2>\n      <p>「不共享就冻结账户」「不配合就涉嫌违法」「IT 正在远程抢救你的号」——这些都是为了让你在 30 秒内做出决定。正规金融机构与政府部门通常不会在私人 WhatsApp 视频里完成核身；需要核实身份时，应通过你<strong>自行查找</strong>的官方 App、官网或网点渠道进行，具体以官方帮助中心说明与当地正规渠道为准。</p>\n\n      <h2>防御清单（按优先级）</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>任何自称官方、银行、平台客服的视频来电，若要求共享屏幕：默认挂断。</li>\n        <li>挂断后用你自己保存的官方电话或 App 内客服入口回拨，不用聊天里给的号码。</li>\n        <li>若因工作安排必须屏幕共享（如 IT 远程排障），先退出银行与即时通讯 App，关闭锁屏通知预览，屏幕共享时禁止点开任何验证码相关界面。</li>\n        <li>教长辈：微信/FaceTime 里的「共享屏幕」与电脑投屏，对验证码而言与「发码」同等危险。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>家规建议：</strong>把「视频通话 + 共享屏幕 + 要操作手机」写成红色组合。长辈只需记住：见到这个组合就挂断，再让子女陪同事务。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>若已经共享过并可能泄露</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>立即停止使用共享，挂断全部远程会话。</li>\n        <li>在本机重新验证 WhatsApp 号码，清查「已关联的设备」。</li>\n        <li>重设两步验证 PIN 与恢复邮箱；银行与支付账户改密。</li>\n        <li>卸载可疑远程控制类应用，必要时请专业人士做设备体检。</li>\n        <li>通知亲友警惕「你」近期发出的异常消息。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>常见误区</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>「我只共享了一半屏幕就没事」——通知栏验证码仍可见。</li>\n        <li>「对方穿着制服就可信」——视频里的形象均可伪造。</li>\n        <li>「我挂断就安全了」——若码已被抄走，须立刻夺回并重设门禁。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>隔空取码证明：安全不只取决于「你肯不肯把码打出来」，还取决于「谁正在看你的屏幕」。把屏幕共享当成高权限操作，而不是视频聊天的附属功能；对验证码而言，眼睛与手指一样是泄密通道。</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"骗局识破","title":"合并通话听验证码：别把陌生人并进你的电话","excerpt":"骗子诱使你合并三方通话，让线路接听 WhatsApp 语音验证码——你没转发数字，码却被听走。","read":"12 minit","body":"<p>电话合并（三方通话、会议呼叫）本是日常功能，却被改造成盗号工具。公开报道中的手法大致是：陌生或半陌生来电，自称快递、医保、学校教务处，称需要你和「负责人」三方确认；或假装你的亲友出了急事，请你把电话转接给「律师」「警官」。当你按指示拨打某个号码并点「合并通话」时，线路另一端往往是 WhatsApp 刚发起的<strong>语音验证呼叫</strong>，自动播报六位注册码，骗子全程在线静听。</p>\n      <p>这类骗局在老年用户中尤其常见，因为「帮他接通一下」听起来像普通的电话礼仪，且不涉及在聊天里打字发码，心理防线更低。</p>\n\n      <h2>它和骗短信码有何不同</h2>\n      <p>短信码骗局依赖你「看见并转发」；合并通话骗局依赖你「听见却不知情」。有时骗子还会让你同时收到短信码，再让你在合并线路上「念给客服听」，实质仍是把注册码交给第三方。你可能全程没有在 WhatsApp 里输入任何内容，甚至以为自己只是在做善意转接。</p>\n\n      <h2>诈骗剧本的两段式结构</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>铺垫：</strong>制造紧急情境（涉案、退款、包裹被扣），让你愿意配合操作电话。</li>\n        <li><strong>导火线：</strong>给你一个「官方热线」号码，让你先呼叫再合并；该号码触发 WhatsApp 语音验证或银行 OTP 语音播报。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>警惕任何要求你「先拨某个固定号码再合并」的电话，无论对方自称谁。</p>\n\n      <h2>防护规则</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>不与陌生来电做合并通话；不帮陌生人「转接当局」。</li>\n        <li>亲友急事：挂断可疑线，打通讯录里的老号码核实。</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp 语音验证码与短信码同等机密，不能在任何通话中口播给他人。</li>\n        <li>若对方说「别挂断，合并就行」，几乎都是高危信号。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>运营商客服、银行、WhatsApp 官方均不会通过电话合并让你「收听验证码完成验证」。遇到此类说法，以官方帮助中心说明为准，不要继续通话。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>若你曾经合并过可疑通话</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>立刻查看 WhatsApp 是否仍在线、是否收到「已在其他设备注册」提示。</li>\n        <li>若仍可登录：清关联设备，重设两步验证；若被踢下线：按夺回流程重新验证。</li>\n        <li>向运营商咨询呼叫转移、无条件转移是否被恶意开通。</li>\n        <li>通知近期联系人：若收到「你」异常消息请警惕。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>不要把通话合并当成没有安全含义的操作。对验证码场景而言，耳朵与眼睛一样可以泄密。拒绝陌生合并、拒绝在电话里念码，是成本几乎为零却常被忽视的防线。</p>\n<h2>与运营商呼叫转移的关系</h2>\n      <p>部分诈骗在合并通话之外，还会诱导你在手机上开通「呼叫转移」或「无应答转移」，把将来所有来电转给骗子接听。合并通话偷听的是当下这一通；呼叫转移影响的是未来的每一通。若你曾按陌生指示拨打过特殊代码（例如以 ** 或 ## 开头的功能码），请立刻致电运营商官方客服，查询并关闭全部转移，具体以运营商官方说明为准。</p>\n      <p>日常可养成习惯：每隔几个月向运营商确认业务开通状态；发现无法接到别人电话时，把「是否被转移」与「是否换卡」列入同一优先排查清单。</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"风险加深","title":"SIM 卡劫持与换卡攻击：当短信收件箱不再属于你","excerpt":"号码被转到新卡后，验证码会进攻击者手机。理解运营商侧风险，并学会用两步验证与 SIM PIN 降低伤害。","read":"14 分钟","body":"<p>SIM swapping（换卡/号卡劫持）属于更重的攻击：对方通过伪造身份、社工话术或 利用运营商流程漏洞，让电信公司把你的号码激活到他们控制的 SIM 上。完成后，你的手机可能突然显示「无服务」，而本该发到你这里的短信 OTP，却在骗子手机里响起。WhatsApp 以手机号为主键，因此这是直指账号主权的打击。</p>\n      <p>若未开启两步验证，对方拿到短信码即可重新注册；即便你开了两步验证，你也会立刻陷入「运营商夺回号码」与「WhatsApp 夺回会话」的双线竞速。高调公众人物长期是目标，但普通用户也可能因经营号、投资群、或单纯通讯录质量被选中。</p>\n\n      <h2>可能出现的征兆</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>手机无故长时间无服务，飞行模式开关无效，或提示「未插入 SIM」。</li>\n        <li>突然收不到任何短信，但银行提示异常登录。</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp 提示号码已在其他设备完成注册。</li>\n        <li>亲友收到「你」的借钱消息，而你的手机根本无法打开 WhatsApp。</li>\n        <li>运营商向你发来换卡成功通知，而你并未办理。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>第一时间应急（按顺序）</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>用另一部手机或固话，拨打运营商官方客服（以官方 App、官网或账单上的渠道为准）申报异常换卡，要求冻结过户与补办 SIM。</li>\n        <li>号码恢复后，立刻在本机重新验证 WhatsApp，并清关联设备。</li>\n        <li>通知银行与支付平台关注异常 OTP 与转账；重设关键账户密码。</li>\n        <li>用短信告知亲友：近期任何 WhatsApp 借钱请求勿信。</li>\n        <li>保留运营商工单、短信记录，必要时向当地警方渠道报案。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>日常降风险措施</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>向运营商咨询可否设置换卡/过户的额外口令或到厅办理限制。</li>\n        <li>为 SIM 启用 PIN 码，防止实体卡被他人直接插入使用。</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp 必须开启两步验证，并绑定本人邮箱。</li>\n        <li>重要金融账户优先使用 App 令牌、硬件密钥，而不是单靠短信。</li>\n        <li>社交平台上避免公开完整手机号、身份证信息与住址组合。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>换卡攻击针对的是「谁控制短信收件箱」。运营商侧加锁 + WhatsApp 两步验证 + 失联预案，三件套缺一不可。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>夺回后的复盘</h2>\n      <p>确认骗子是否曾在你的号码上完成 WhatsApp 注册、是否群发过诈骗消息。若有，需用其他渠道澄清，并协助已被骗的亲友止付。考虑是否将号码关联的高价值账户暂时加强监控。</p>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>SIM 劫持把手机号从「联系方式」升级为「安全根」。建立「突然无服务＝最高警报」的条件反射，比事后争夺号码更省时间。</p>\n<h2>与银行、邮箱的联动风险</h2>\n      <p>换卡成功后，攻击者不只盯 WhatsApp，也可能在同一窗口重置银行短信验证、邮箱找回密码。因此在申报运营商的同时，主动致电银行客服说明情况、冻结异常渠道，并检查邮箱异地登录记录。把「手机号失联」视为账户矩阵级事件，而不是单一 App 故障。</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"骗局识破","title":"仿冒官网与钓鱼链接：看起来像，也不要点","excerpt":"从域名、话术压迫感到「要你做什么」，系统教你拆穿假登录页。需要网页版时，只走你自己输入的官方地址。","read":"13 分钟","body":"<p>钓鱼攻击的本质，是伪造你熟悉的信任符号：Meta 绿、锁形图标、客服话术、倒计时、甚至和真站几乎一样的域名拼写。攻击者不需要破解 WhatsApp 的端到端加密，只要诱使你在假「登录柜台上」亲手输入手机号与六位注册码，或下载套壳应用，就能把账号钥匙交出去。</p>\n      <p>近年钓鱼页还演化出「渐进式」流程：先让你填手机号「预约解封」，几秒后真的收到官方短信码——你误以为流程正规，遂在假站填入刚收到的码。攻击成功点，就在于用户把「能收到真短信」误当成「网站是真的」。</p>\n\n      <h2>常见投放渠道</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>WhatsApp 私聊、群发里的短链与「账号将永久封禁」横幅。</li>\n        <li>短信/邮件冒充 WhatsApp Business 审核、退款、高清通话开通。</li>\n        <li>搜索引擎广告位上的「WhatsApp Web 登录」假冒站。</li>\n        <li>已被盗账号转发的「官方公告」与假更新包。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>点开前的四问自检</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>我是否本来就在办这件事？无预兆的「24 小时内不验证就封号」高度可疑。</li>\n        <li>浏览器地址栏是不是我亲手输入的域名？警惕 whatsapp-verify、wa-login、多连字符、奇怪后缀。</li>\n        <li>页面索要的，是不是注册码、银行 OTP、助记词、远程桌面？任意一项都应退出。</li>\n        <li>是否诱导下载 APK、描述文件、或开启无障碍/屏幕录制？常见于安卓侧木马链。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>安全打开网页版/电脑版的唯一姿势</h2>\n      <p>自己打开浏览器，手动输入官方域名或使用官方桌面客户端；在手机 WhatsApp 内发起「关联设备」再扫码。切勿从聊天中的蓝字链跳转登录。扫码页上的二维码只能来自<strong>你刚打开的官方页面</strong>，不能扫聊天里他人发来的图。</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>把「官方入口必须自己建造」写成肌肉记忆：别人递过来的链接，只能当参考，不能当入口。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>点错之后分级处理</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>仅浏览：</strong>关页，不二次点击；警惕后续来电跟进。</li>\n        <li><strong>已填注册码：</strong>立刻本机重验号码，清设备，重设两步验证。</li>\n        <li><strong>已交银行卡/邮箱密码：</strong>对应账户紧急改密，联系银行；邮箱检查异地登录规则。</li>\n        <li><strong>已装未知 APK：</strong>断网卸载，必要时恢复出厂并做好各平台重登加固。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>把钓鱼链接截图举报给 WhatsApp，并在群里简短预警，减少连锁伤亡。</p>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>加密保护传输，不保护你走进假柜台的手。域名自制、行为自检、索码即退——三招能把大多数钓鱼拦在门外。</p>\n<h2>识别「真预览、假网页」组合</h2>\n      <p>聊天里的链接预览可以显示正规域名缩略图，实际跳转却是另一地址。长按链接查看完整 URL，或复制到备忘录检查，再决定是否打开。iOS/Android 上都要警惕「第一次点 A 域名，第二次跟到 B 域名」的重定向链。</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"应急处理","title":"账号被盗夺回全流程：按官方逻辑一步步做","excerpt":"重新用自己的号码完成注册验证，是把他人踢走的关键。本文按顺序写出夺回、清设备、通知亲友与避免二次失守。","read":"16 分钟","body":"<p>发现 WhatsApp 被盗时，人的第一反应常是混乱：想举报、想找人代刷、又被亲友追问「刚才那条借钱是不是你」。请先稳住节奏——根据 WhatsApp 帮助中心的公开说明，当问题是「他人正在使用你的号码」时，<strong>在你自己安全的主手机上，用本号码重新完成注册验证</strong>，是移除非法会话的核心步骤。正确输入你本人收到的短信或语音注册码后，对方在主设备上的使用通常会被挤下线。</p>\n      <p>本流程不保证一分钟搞定：你可能还要处理陌生两步验证 PIN、清理关联设备、对外公告止损。按顺序做，比并行乱试更省时。</p>\n\n      <h2>夺回前 60 秒：四问</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>我还能打开 WhatsApp 吗？能打开就先清「已关联的设备」，别急着和骗子聊天。</li>\n        <li>我是否已被踢回注册页？是则直接进入重验流程。</li>\n        <li>手机是否可能中毒或被远程控制？若是，先断网杀毒，否则夺回也会再失。</li>\n        <li>是否在同时遭遇换卡无信号？先联系运营商，再谈 App 夺回。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>标准夺回步骤</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>在<strong>可信的本机</strong>重装或打开官方 WhatsApp（仅官方商店来源）。</li>\n        <li>输入你的完整国际格式手机号，索取注册码。</li>\n        <li>收码时遮挡屏幕，<strong>绝不</strong>发给任何人；输入本机。</li>\n        <li>若要求两步验证 PIN：输入你记得的；若是陌生 PIN，见本站专题，但仍应完成 SMS 验证以先踢人。</li>\n        <li>登录后 → 设置 → 已关联的设备 → 全部退出不明项。</li>\n        <li>关闭再开启两步验证，换新 PIN，确认恢复邮箱仍属于你。</li>\n        <li>检查隐私与安全相关设置是否被篡改；排除非官方客户端。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>反直觉但重要：仅改两步验证 PIN，不能单独把已登入的骗子请走；重新验证号码才是关键。也不要在夺回前盲发「举报自己账号」类操作，以免干扰恢复，具体以官方帮助中心指引为准。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>夺回中的对外沟通</h2>\n      <p>并行做两件事：技术上踢人，人际上止血。</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>用短信/电话通知家人：过去 24 小时「我」在 WhatsApp 要钱要码均假。</li>\n        <li>请可信同事在非 WhatsApp 渠道代发公司群声明。</li>\n        <li>若已经发生转账，指导受害方立即联系银行止付，并保留聊天证据，视情况通过当地警方渠道报案。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>夺回后 24–72 小时加固</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>再次复查关联设备，开启应用锁。</li>\n        <li>复盘失守点：钓鱼？熟人要码？假投票？共享屏幕？</li>\n        <li>给核心联系人同步你的「核身规则」（例如借钱必打电话）。</li>\n        <li>若经营号被盗，按商务专题发布客户通知。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>常见错误</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>把注册码发给「代找回」陌生人——二次被盗。</li>\n        <li>夺回成功却未公告，亲友继续上当。</li>\n        <li>只改密码不清关联设备，网页端仍被同步。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>夺回拼顺序：先号码主权，再清后门，再人际止损，最后复盘加固。把这篇当应急卡片收藏，比慌忙搜索更可靠。</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"应急处理","title":"遇到陌生两步验证 PIN：等待、踢人与重设策略","excerpt":"盗号者可能抢先设置 PIN。说明短信码验证为何仍关键，不知 PIN 时如何按官方流程处理，以及等待期要做什么。","read":"13 分钟","body":"<p>两步验证本应是盾，却可能被攻击者抢先装在你的账号上，变成挡住你的墙。不少受害人在重新注册时发现：系统要输入两步验证 PIN，而自己从未设置——这说明有人曾控制该号码并启用了两步验证，甚至可能绑定了攻击者的恢复邮箱。</p>\n      <p>此时切忌慌乱寻找「代刷 PIN」服务；那是二次诈骗。公开资料中的官方逻辑是：<strong>短信/电话注册码</strong>证明你持有手机号；<strong>PIN</strong>是额外口令。两套体系要解决不同问题。</p>\n\n      <h2>先分清两种「码」</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>注册码：</strong>发送到手机的六位短信或语音码。输入后，通常会把其他设备上的非法会话挤下线。</li>\n        <li><strong>两步验证 PIN：</strong>你或攻击者早先设定的 6 位口令。若不知道且无法通过邮箱恢复，系统可能要求等待一段时间（常见公开描述为最长约 7 天）后再继续，以具体 App 提示为准。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>关键结论：<strong>不要因为卡在 PIN 就放弃输入注册码</strong>。先完成号码验证，往往已经能解除对方的即时操作权。</p>\n\n      <h2>若记得自己的 PIN 与邮箱</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>正常输入 PIN；或通过邮箱走官方找回流程。</li>\n        <li>登录后立刻换新 PIN、换新恢复邮箱。</li>\n        <li>清关联设备，检查是否有异常群发残留。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>若 PIN 完全陌生</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>仍要完整走完注册码验证，把手机控制权抢回来。</li>\n        <li>按界面提示处理 PIN 等待或邮箱申诉；勿向陌生人透露注册码。</li>\n        <li>等待期间持续用短信/电话警告亲友：任何 WhatsApp 消息均不可信。</li>\n        <li>确认 SIM 未被再次换卡；邮箱主账户加固。</li>\n        <li>等待结束后第一时间登录，设置<strong>仅你知晓</strong>的新 PIN 与邮箱。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>等待七天很难熬，但这是官方风控的一部分。把这段时间用来止损通知与取证，比寻找「秒破 PIN」更靠谱。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>重获控制后的强制动作</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>新 PIN + 可登陆的恢复邮箱；旧 PIN 作废。</li>\n        <li>关联设备清零复查；应用锁开启。</li>\n        <li>复盘最初如何失守，避免第 8 天又被恶意设 PIN。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>陌生 PIN 令人窒息，但路径仍在：注册码踢人、官方规则处理 PIN、等待期对外止血、回来后重建门禁。耐心与顺序，比捷径更安全。</p>\n<h2>如何陪伴等待期的家人</h2>\n      <p>七天等待对长辈尤其煎熬，子女应每日两次简短同步进度，强调「不要找黄牛」。同时帮长辈把重要联系人暂时迁移到电话短信，避免骗子继续冒充。可以在纸上写下官方 App 内的提示文案，防止他们误信聊天里的假客服。</p>\n      <p>等待结束后第一时间完成的不是发状态，而是改 PIN、改邮箱、清设备——顺序不能乱。</p>\n<h2>写给子女的协助要点</h2>\n      <p>若父母卡在陌生 PIN，子女应远程陪跑官方流程，而不是尝试「第三方便捷解锁」。帮助父母用短信联系老同事，说明暂时无法用 WhatsApp，以免诈骗延续。保留运营商、邮箱服务商的工单号，等待期结束后一次性加固，比零散改密更不易遗漏。</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"排查信号","title":"账号异常信号大全：如何尽早发现被盗或被同步","excerpt":"从突然登出、陌生关联设备，到好友反馈你在要码——把预警信号分级，并给出对应动作。","read":"13 分钟","body":"<p>早一小时发现被盗，常常意味着少一轮亲友被骗、少一笔转账。许多案例并非「防不住」，而是信号出现时被视为「小 bug」或「朋友误会」。把下面分级清单当成巡检仪表，出现对应颜色就执行对应动作，而不是等「铁证如山」再补救。</p>\n\n      <h2>红色信号：立刻行动</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>无操作下被迫退出，或提示号码已在其他设备注册。</li>\n        <li>你没点登录，却连续收到多条注册验证码。</li>\n        <li>关联设备列表出现从未见过的浏览器、城市、设备名。</li>\n        <li>多位好友同时反馈：你在索要验证码或催转账。</li>\n        <li>你在线聊天，对方却说「你」在另一头回复不同内容。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>动作：</strong>立即重新验证号码 → 清所有关联 → 重设两步验证 → 短信通知亲友。</p>\n\n      <h2>橙色信号：当天必须处理</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>通知栏闪过不明聊天回复预览，但打开对话你没有发送。</li>\n        <li>群友私信问：刚才发的链接是不是你本人。</li>\n        <li>发现两步验证曾被关闭，或恢复邮箱被改（若可见）。</li>\n        <li>手机借出/点后出现异常耗电、陌生辅助功能开启。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>动作：</strong>关联设备全查；重设两步验证；检查近期点击记录与安装来源；告知密切联系人提高警惕。</p>\n\n      <h2>黄色信号：提高巡检频率</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>频繁收到「官方警告」外链却并非你主动操作。</li>\n        <li>半熟人反复试探能否借钱、能否帮忙扫码。</li>\n        <li>曾在酒店/网吧使用网页版，不确定是否已登出。</li>\n        <li>出国换卡后，短信有延迟，担心 OTP 被别人截获。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>动作：</strong>本周每日看关联设备；拒点陌生链；旅行专题里的回国后复盘。</p>\n\n      <h2>把「好友反馈」当最高优先级</h2>\n      <p>当朋友说「你在要我的码」时，先不要辩解「不可能」，而要先自查设备与会话。误判成本低，漏判成本高。若自查无果，也请朋友在群内简短预警，防止连环。</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>建立「红色电话」：与两位亲友约定，任何账号异常互拨语音确认，不用 WhatsApp 回问。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>记录与取证</h2>\n      <p>出现橙色以上信号，截图关联设备页、验证码短信时间、可疑聊天，便于日后银行或警方渠道举证。不要频繁点击钓鱼链「确认是不是假」。</p>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>信号分级，是为避免麻木与恐慌两个极端。把好友的一句质疑当火警，往往能在变现阶段前截停攻击。</p>\n<h2>建立个人「异常日志」</h2>\n      <p>用手机备忘或纸质本记录每一次异常验证码、陌生设备、好友质疑的日期与截图编号。日后若需要向银行或执法机关说明时间线，有序证据比零碎记忆更有力。日志本身不含敏感码，只记事件类型。</p>\n<h2>误报与漏报的平衡</h2>\n      <p>有时好友忘记自己刚刚发过奇怪链接，会误报你被黑。即便如此，轻量自查（关联设备、最近登录提示）仍是低成本动作。真漏报一次的损失远大于多查几次的三十秒。建议每季度与好友互相「抽检」：问一句「你最近有没有收到可疑消息冒充我？」</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"骗局识破","title":"好友借钱、换号、要保密：完整核实手册","excerpt":"盗号后的变现第一步常常是向通讯录借钱。教你一套不伤感情、又能挡下转账诈骗的核实流程。","read":"13 分钟","body":"<p>盗号后的「冷启动变现」，最常见的就是向通讯录借钱。骗子不是泛泛群发，而是读取历史聊天，模仿你的称呼、口癖、近期话题，写出一封「老同学救急」「工程款差一笔」的私信。你要防的，不是朋友会不会借钱，而是<strong>这条请求是否通过了不可伪造的核身</strong>。</p>\n\n      <h2>高危话术特征</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>强调马上、保密、别让家人知道，阻止第三方介入。</li>\n        <li>称手机坏了、号换了，要求加新号立刻转账。</li>\n        <li>拒绝或拖延视频露脸；语音也可能被 AI 模仿（见本站 AI 专题）。</li>\n        <li>收款方式突然变为礼品卡、USDT、陌生二维码、分多笔小额。</li>\n        <li>先小额「测试到账」，随即追加大额。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>推荐核实流程（固执一点没错）</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>在原聊天里暂停谈钱，说「我打电话确认下」——真朋友能理解。</li>\n        <li>拨打通讯录里<strong>存储已久的旧号码</strong>，不用对方刚发的新号。</li>\n        <li>问两个只有本人知道的非公开细节（上周见面地点、家庭暗号）。</li>\n        <li>仍不确定时，联系其配偶/同事交叉验证。</li>\n        <li>任何催促「来不及挂单/手术费」都不能替代核身；大额可约定延迟到账。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>真正的朋友在被质疑时很少羞辱你的谨慎。逼你「别问那么多、先转」的，更像诈骗脚本在抢窗口。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>已经转出怎么办</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>即刻联系银行/支付方尝试拦截或争议。</li>\n        <li>保存聊天、转账凭证、对方号码与时间线。</li>\n        <li>告知被骗朋友其号可能也被盗，并通过当地警方渠道报案，是否需要由办案机关认定。</li>\n        <li>在共同好友群简短预警，免二次伤害。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>家庭与公司公约模板</h2>\n      <p>「凡通过聊天 App 提出的紧急借款，必须语音或当面确认；无确认不转账。」把这句话写进员工手册与家庭微信群置顶。</p>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>核实流程是友谊的保险，不是不信任。被盗的那位朋友，同样需要你帮他挡住更多受害者。</p>\n<h2>群聊里的「批量借款」</h2>\n      <p>盗号者有时在多个群同时发借款模板，利用有人先转账带动从众心理。看到群里多人已转，并不代表本人已确认——要独立核身。管理员发现异常应暂时全员禁言，并用电话渠道核实。</p>\n      <p>公司财务规定：凡是变更收款账户，必须盖章 PDF 加电话回访，聊天截图不能单独作为依据。</p>\n<h2>小额测试转账的陷阱</h2>\n      <p>骗子先让受害方转几十元「确认友谊」，再追加大额。规则应是：无论金额大小，核身标准一致。不要为了「金额小无所谓」跳过电话确认。部分脚本专做累积小额，避开银行风控阈值。</p>\n<h2>情侣与合伙生意</h2>\n      <p>恋爱关系与合伙人间更容易因「不好意思核实」而转账。约定：涉及合伙款一律走公司账户与书面邮件，不通过个人 WhatsApp 决策。分手或纠纷期间应格外警惕冒充对方借钱。感情越浓，流程越要硬，这才是保护关系。</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"防护设置","title":"隐私设置完整加固：减少被社工利用的个人信息","excerpt":"头像、最后上线、群组邀请、状态可见范围……逐项说明建议值。隐私设置不能代替两步验证，但能减少骗子的素材。","read":"14 分钟","body":"<p>端到端加密守护的是消息在传输中的机密性，并不自动隐藏你的头像、签名、在线时间、群组曝光面。骗子会收集这些碎片，拼出更可信的剧本：知道你刚出国、知道你女儿参赛、知道你常用的称呼。收紧隐私，是缩小社工素材面积的低成本工作。</p>\n\n      <h2>建议逐项调整（设置 → 隐私）</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>头像：</strong>对非联系人收敛，减少陌生人克隆你的形象。</li>\n        <li><strong>最后上线与在线：</strong>按需限制，减轻被盯梢。</li>\n        <li><strong>关于：</strong>不写手机号、住址、排班、小孩学校全名。</li>\n        <li><strong>状态：</strong>控制受众；状态被转发仍会扩散，发前先评估。</li>\n        <li><strong>群组：</strong>「谁可以拉我进群」建议为「我的联系人」，防陌生拉群诈骗。</li>\n        <li><strong>通话：</strong>静音未知来电等选项（视版本），减骚扰。</li>\n        <li><strong>已读回执：</strong>体验功能；安全核心仍在验证码与设备。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>一步步操作习惯</h2>\n      <p>每改一项，用另一部小号或朋友的非联系账号侧测：陌生人能看到什么。每月换机或重大活动（婚礼、旅行）后可再扫一遍，因为 App 更新有时会恢复默认更开放选项。</p>\n\n      <h2>隐私与防盗的边界</h2>\n      <p>隐私收紧救不了「你主动发验证码」，但能让冷启动骗子少一半剧本素材。它与两步验证、关联设备巡检是不同层次，需要叠甲而非替代。</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>给长辈改隐私时，优先做「群邀请限制 + 头像对非联系人隐藏 + 关于栏去敏感信息」三项，性价比高。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>商务号的额外点</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>店铺头像可被仿冒，对外公告要用多渠道一致的品牌收款信息。</li>\n        <li>员工离职后，检查谁还能代表品牌号发消息。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>花十五分钟过一遍隐私页，是低成本的「减少被骗故事版本号」。配合验证码纪律，才能真正让骗子的话术变钝。</p>\n<h2>针对不同人群的推荐档</h2>\n      <p>长辈：群邀请限联系人、头像限联系人、关闭状态对公众展示。青少年：限制在线时间可见、减少陌生人发消息入口。商务号：头像与「关于」用于经营展示，但避免写个人住址；收款信息走固定模板消息而非随意口头更改。</p>\n      <p>每次 App 大版本更新后，隐私默认值可能被改动，换机或更新后应再巡检一轮。</p>\n<h2>截图与外界档案</h2>\n      <p>你的头像和签名可能早已被别人截图扩散。定期检查搜索引擎与人肉站点是否出现你的手机号+头像组合，若发现可向平台申诉删除。隐私设置是减少新增泄露，不是收回已流出素材；但仍值得做。</p>\n<h2>同步通讯录的副作用</h2>\n      <p>允许 WhatsApp 读取通讯录，会把亲友号码上传用于好友发现。若你设备遭窃，这份通讯录也成为攻击列表。可在隐私里关闭联系人上传（以版本选项为准），改用手动添加核心联系人。操作路径变化时查官方帮助中心说明。</p>\n<h2>小结复述</h2><p>隐私是减速带，不是护城河；它与验证码纪律、设备清单一起才构成完整防护。完成设置后记下日期，六个月后复查。</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"防护设置","title":"只用官方 WhatsApp：改装包与非商店安装的风险","excerpt":"所谓防撤回、多开、破解会员，可能是后门。讲清楚为何官方渠道更新如此重要，以及如何自查当前安装来源。","read":"13 分钟","body":"<p>WhatsApp 官方安全说明一再强调：非官方或改装客户端可能导致账号被盗、被封、或持续泄密。所谓「防撤回」「双开」「主题破解」「隐藏在线」的 APK，常在合法功能外壳下，夹带额外权限——读通知、无障碍点击、自动转发验证码——一旦端点沦陷，端到端加密也无法保护你主动显示在屏幕上的注册码。</p>\n\n      <h2>高危来源识别</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>群文件、网盘、论坛短链分发的 APK。</li>\n        <li>名称类似 WhatsApp Plus、GB、Delta 等 fork；图标略有差异。</li>\n        <li>安装向导要求关闭 Play 保护、打开未知来源。</li>\n        <li>要求登录 WhatsApp 同时索取短信全权管理。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>正确安装路径</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS 仅 App Store，开发者应为 WhatsApp LLC。</li>\n        <li>Android 优先 Google Play 或手机厂商官方商店正式版。</li>\n        <li>桌面端从 Meta 官方指引下载，不搜第三方「加速包」。</li>\n        <li>开启系统自动更新，修补已知漏洞。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>自查与迁移</h2>\n      <p>在商店页核对开发者、评价是否异常刷单、权限列表是否过度。若曾长期使用改装版：</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>备份必要聊天；卸载非官方客户端。</li>\n        <li>安装官方版，重新验证号码。</li>\n        <li>清关联设备，重设两步验证，改 PIN。</li>\n        <li>若怀疑木马：在设备安全前提下做杀毒或重置评估。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>「免费高级功能」常是交换条件：你省订阅费，骗子拿整座账号与通讯录。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>组织内推广话术</h2>\n      <p>在公司培训中把「改装 WhatsApp 等于自带后门」写成 IT 红线，与钓鱼识别一起考。</p>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>官方客户端是一切安全假设的地基。地基错了，上面的两步验证只是心理安慰。</p>\n<h2>企业 MDM 与双开器</h2>\n      <p>部分员工使用公司 MDM 或第三方「应用双开」在一份工作手机里登录两个 WhatsApp。若双开器来源不明，同样可能劫持通知栏验证码。企业 IT 应白名单仅允许官方包名，禁止侧载；员工个人机也应避免为图省事装 fork。</p>\n<h2>更新失败时的正确姿势</h2>\n      <p>若官方商店更新反复失败，不要转而下载论坛「完整包」。应清理商店缓存、检查系统时间、联系官方支持渠道。临时使用网页版回复，也比安装陌生 APK 更安全——网页版同样遵守关联设备规则。</p>\n<h2>二手手机预装陷阱</h2>\n      <p>购买二手安卓机时，检查是否预装非官方 WhatsApp 或带有「加速」「清理」类常驻应用。恢复出厂后仅从官方商店安装。若卖家声称「已帮你登好」，坚决重登并改 PIN。</p>\n<h2>小结复述</h2><p>每一次改装尝试都在赌你通讯录的价值高于那几个花哨功能。赌赢的永远是骗子。</p>\n<p>记住：正版免费，改装最贵。</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"日常习惯","title":"手机锁屏与物理安全：防身边人偷关联","excerpt":"很多网页版失守发生在「借看一分钟」之间。强锁屏、通知预览策略与借用后巡检，组成物理层防线。","read":"12 minit","body":"<p>数字防盗的讨论里，桌边那部已解锁的手机经常被低估。维修店代操作、同事玩笑式拿起、家人「帮我登一下网页版」、酒桌离席去洗手间——攻击者不需要远程技巧，只要在几十秒内完成一次扫码，关联设备会话就能长期同步你的消息。</p>\n\n      <h2>基础配置清单</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>系统锁屏：优先长密码或复杂 PIN，生物识别作便捷层，避免图案好猜。</li>\n        <li>自动锁屏时间设为最短可接受档（如 30 秒–1 分钟）。</li>\n        <li>锁屏通知隐藏验证码与聊天内容预览。</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp 内开启应用锁（指纹/面容），手机解锁后仍要再验一次。</li>\n        <li>敏感群聊可叠加聊天锁定（见相关专题）。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>借用与维修场景</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>不把已解锁手机交给他人独处，哪怕一分钟。</li>\n        <li>必须他人操作时你在场，禁止对方进入「关联设备」或系统 VPN/描述文件页。</li>\n        <li>拿回手机第一件事：打开关联设备列表，踢掉不认识条目。</li>\n        <li>送修前退出 WhatsApp 或确保强锁屏 + 关闭通知；要求店家书面不浏览个人隐私。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>公共环境肩窥</h2>\n      <p>地铁、机场输入注册码时请遮挡；关闭自动朗读通知。攻击者用肉眼肩窥 + 录像同样能拿到 OTP。</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>把手机借出前，可先手动锁屏。礼貌和五分钟解释，胜过事后夺回账号。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>失窃抢手机</h2>\n      <p>设备物理丢失后，除报警与运营商挂失外，尽快用新设备重验 WhatsApp（SIM 仍属你），清关联、改 PIN。若 SIM 也在机上，优先处理换卡。</p>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>物理安全是数字安全的地板。地板没了，站得再高也会摔。</p>\n<h2>儿童与保姆在家场景</h2>\n      <p>孩子会好奇滑动通知，家政人员可能代为接听电话。除应用锁外，可教育家人不要在未成年人面前念出验证码。对保姆借用手机拍照等合理需求，可改用相机模式而非直接给解锁桌面。</p>\n      <p>智能手表与平板若同号登录短信转发，也要纳入物理巡检范围。</p>\n<h2>可穿戴设备风险</h2>\n      <p>智能手表同步显示 WhatsApp 通知时，腕上同样可能暴露验证码。户外跑步、健身房场景应关闭手表上的敏感预览，或暂时静音 OTP 类短信到可穿戴层。借戴他人手表登录同一账号也会留下会话痕迹，应独立评估。</p>\n<h2>摄像头物理遮挡习惯</h2>\n      <p>视频通话诈骗有时引导你切前置摄像头展示环境。养成不用时关闭相机权限或物理滑盖的习惯，减少被诱导展示银行卡、身份证桌面的机会。孩子使用平板视频课时家长宜在场抽查。</p>\n<h2>小结复述</h2><p>把锁屏看成腰带，应用锁看成口袋扣；腰带没系，口袋扣也白搭。两者同时到位。</p>\n<p>睡前充电不把解锁手机放床头够不着的客人边；聚餐手机朝下扣放，减少验证码弹窗曝光。</p>\n<p>锁屏壁纸勿用含家庭住址照片的图像，避免遗失手机时一并泄露。</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"日常习惯","title":"WhatsApp 网页版与电脑版：安全使用长规范","excerpt":"从官方入口、保持登录选项、公用电脑流程到异常会话处理，给经常在电脑上回复消息的人一份完整规范。","read":"14 分钟","body":"<p>电脑端让我们更快回复客户与家人，却也把会话暴露在「更大屏幕、更长登录时间、更多共用环境」里。安全重点不是禁用电脑版，而是坚持：官方入口发起关联、清楚挂着哪些会话、在公共环境强制短生命周期。</p>\n\n      <h2>推荐安全工作流</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>手机打开官方 WhatsApp → 已关联的设备 → 关联新设备。</li>\n        <li>电脑仅打开官方桌面程序，或浏览器地址栏手写官方网址。</li>\n        <li>扫描<strong>电脑屏幕上刚从官方页弹出的码</strong>，不扫聊天图片。</li>\n        <li>私人电脑可勾选保持登录；公用电脑绝不勾选，结束立刻在手机端登出。</li>\n        <li>每周固定检查列表，踢掉不活跃但陌生条目。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>明确禁止</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>扫描聊天里「登录」「投票」「补助」二维码。</li>\n        <li>点击搜索引擎广告里的「WhatsApp Web」。</li>\n        <li>让别人用你的手机代扫「帮我挂一下」。</li>\n        <li>长期把公司前台电脑当服务器 24 小时挂着。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>多设备办公策略</h2>\n      <p>居家与工位各一台电脑时，命名会话有便于识别；离职或设备出售前，必须先在手机端移除关联，再重装系统。</p>\n\n      <h2>异常协同信号</h2>\n      <p>消息显示已读但你没打开；联系人称你发了奇怪附件——立刻查关联列表并重设两步验证。</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>把「周日晚上清网页版」与倒垃圾一样列入家务：30 秒换一周安心。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>电脑版是生产力与风险的共同体。官方入口、短会话、周清单，三件套用好，它就是工具而不是落地窗。</p>\n<h2>浏览器扩展与共享电脑</h2>\n      <p>在浏览器使用网页版时，恶意扩展可能读取页面 DOM。公用或公司电脑尽量使用无痕模式 + 官方站，结束后清除数据。不要在同一浏览器登录大量无关插件与 WhatsApp Web 混用。</p>\n      <p>若公司统一部署 Chromium，要求 IT 审核扩展列表，是个人防护的延伸。</p>\n<h2>多标签页与遗忘登出</h2>\n      <p>有的人在数个浏览器配置文件里各开一套 WhatsApp Web，时间久了忘记关。建议给每个配置文件命名，手机端清设备时对照命名删除。公司要求用单一浏览器配置的 IT 政策，反而能降低遗忘登出概率。</p>\n<h2>休眠与屏保</h2>\n      <p>办公室电脑屏保时间短、回座需密码，能避免同事路过看到客户聊天。结合 WhatsApp Web 在手机端及时登出，比单靠「同事都是好人」可靠。离开工位按 Win+L 或同等锁屏应是肌肉记忆。</p>\n<h2>小结复述</h2><p>离开办公桌前做两件事：锁电脑、清网页版。比下班焦虑「有没有关」更省心。</p>\n<p>公司会议投屏时先退出 WhatsApp 桌面通知，避免私人OTP 被会议室大屏直播。</p>\n<p>浏览器保存密码功能勿与WhatsApp Web 混用同一弱主密码。</p>\n<p>卸载浏览器插件后再登网页版，可减少被劫持风险。</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"应急处理","title":"封锁、举报与外部求助：切断骗子的继续作战能力","excerpt":"从 App 内封锁举报，到通知银行与警方，整理一套「不继续受害、也不让通讯录继续受害」的行动清单。","read":"13 分钟","body":"<p>遇见骚扰与诈骗会话，停在「骂两句」或「已读不回」，会让对方继续换号试探、把你的沉默当成可培养目标。更有效的是：立刻止聊、封锁、举报、保全证据，并在涉及资金时同步升级到银行与执法渠道。</p>\n\n      <h2>App 内标准操作</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>打开对话 → 联系人信息或群信息。</li>\n        <li>选择<strong>封锁</strong>，阻断后续消息与来电。</li>\n        <li>选择<strong>举报</strong>，按提示提交，可附带近期聊天供平台审核。</li>\n        <li>垃圾群：退出后举报，避免被反复拉回。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>举报是帮助平台聚类诈骗网络，虽不保证立即抓捕，但能降低他人曝光。</p>\n\n      <h2>账号被盗时的对外顺序</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>先夺回主会话与技术后门。</li>\n        <li>用短信/电话/邮件公告，不只在已被控的 WhatsApp 内喊冤。</li>\n        <li>有钱款损失：银行止付、保留证据，必要时通过当地警方渠道报案。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>证据包建议</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>聊天全文截图（含对方号码、时间）。</li>\n        <li>转账记录与收款方信息。</li>\n        <li>钓鱼 URL 纯文本复制（勿再点）。</li>\n        <li>关联设备异常截图、短信码时间。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>反诈咨询请走官方帮助中心或当地反诈宣传渠道，勿把远程控制权交给「热心网友」。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>何时扩散预警</h2>\n      <p>若骗局利用你的被盗号向通讯录扩散，夺回后应在多群简短澄清，一次即可，附可核实的非 WhatsApp 联系方式。</p>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>封锁举报是缩短攻击者射程；对外求助是缩小资金损失。两手都要有清单，而不是临场发挥。</p>\n<h2>报案材料的现实预期</h2>\n      <p>电子证据需保留原始载体，截图尽量包含对方账号、时间、完整上下文。是否能立案、如何跨境协作，由办案机关依法判断；本网站不能替代法律意见。你仍应报备，形成时间记录，并对银行争议流程有帮助。</p>\n<h2>跨国骗局的现实</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp 诈骗常跨国，追赃难度大，但报案与银行争议仍有意义。把所有材料一次性交给办案机关，比在各平台碎片化发帖有效。家人应控制预期，同时坚持止付与时间线记录。</p>\n<h2>平台之外的联动</h2>\n      <p>同一骗子可能在 Telegram、Line 复制剧本。在一家平台封锁后，留意其他 App 是否来串线。把诈骗号码与 ID 记入个人黑名单共享给家人，减少重复踩雷。</p>\n<h2>事后复盘会</h2>\n      <p>受骗或险些受骗后，家庭开 20 分钟复盘：哪一步本可拒绝？下周改进哪一条设置？不追责，只改流程。把复盘记录贴冰箱，下次同类话术触发记忆。</p>\n<p>向亲友简述骗局套路，一条朋友圈胜过百次独自后怕；注意隐去个人敏感账号信息。</p>\n<p>记录向平台举报的回执编号，便于后续跟进或补充材料。</p>\n<p>向 WhatsApp 举报后仍应本地备份证据，平台处理不等于案件办结。</p>\n<p>保存骗子曾在其他平台使用的昵称变体，便于交叉检索预警。</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"家庭防护","title":"家人与长辈防护课：把复杂安全变成能执行的家规","excerpt":"长文版家庭防盗方案：家规条文、角色扮演演练、远程协助边界，以及如何帮长辈完成关键设置。","read":"15 分钟","body":"<p>许多家庭故事的起点是长辈接到「帮我把码念一下」。他们不是没有善心，而是把礼貌放在警惕之前。骗子精准利用「不好意思拒绝」「怕耽误孩子」的心理。守护长辈，不是丢给他们一篇长文，而是把复杂安全翻译成<strong>能背、能演、能拒绝</strong>的家规。</p>\n\n      <h2>六条冰箱贴家规</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>验证码谁都不给，含子女、含「客服」。</li>\n        <li>借钱必须打老电话，不在 App 里转。</li>\n        <li>不点「解封 / 中奖 / 投票」链接。</li>\n        <li>视频不跟陌生人共享屏幕。</li>\n        <li>每周日花半分钟看「已关联的设备」。</li>\n        <li>全家开两步验证，PIN 自己记好或子女帮保管。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15 分钟家庭实操课</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>子女在场帮每位长辈开两步验证、登记邮箱。</li>\n        <li>一起清关联设备，关掉锁屏验证码预览。</li>\n        <li>演练骗子台词与三句拒绝话术，互换角色。</li>\n        <li>约定「拿不准先打数码联络人」，建立不问罪文化。</li>\n        <li>写下紧急电话列表（子女、社区、银行），贴冰箱旁。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>远程协助的界限</h2>\n      <p>可以视频指导长辈点设置，但不要让他们安装来路不明的远程软件。子女也绝不索要父母验证码「代操作」——示范正确行为比替他们点按钮更重要。</p>\n\n      <h2>上当之后如何沟通</h2>\n      <p>先解决问题再教育：协助夺回、银行止付、安慰情绪。责备只会让他们下次隐瞒，直到损失更大。</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>目标不是把长辈变成专家，而是让他们在关键时刻有「可以挂断」的勇气。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>家规 + 演练 + 关键设置，是长期投资。今天的一节课，可能挡掉明年整栋楼的骗码链。</p>\n<h2>社区与教堂互助群的风险</h2>\n      <p>长辈常在信仰、邻里群里极富信任感。可请群主置顶「助理财不发验证码」公告，并由子女偶尔空降做一次 10 分钟分享。用群体规范降低个体拒绝压力。</p>\n      <p>为视力不佳者准备大字号截图教程，路径比文字更友好。</p>\n<h2>与反诈宣传结合</h2>\n      <p>很多城市公安机关有反诈宣传册或短视频，可与长辈一起观看，讨论剧情中的要码场景。官方素材常比子女唠叨更有说服力。遇到真实被骗新闻，也要温和讨论「如果是我们该怎么拒绝」。</p>\n<h2>药品与保健品骗局交叉</h2>\n      <p>卖保健品的话术常与要码盗号并行：先拉信任，再要验证码「开通会员价」。长辈参加养生讲座后回家，子女可主动问「今天有没有登记手机收到码」。关心式提问比质问更有效。</p>\n<h2>与社区中心合作</h2>\n      <p>街道、社区如有智能手机培训班，可报名而非子女单打。群体学习降低孤独感，也减少「就我笨」的沮丧。争取让验证码纪律写进课堂讲义。</p>\n<p>鼓励长辈把可疑聊天转发给子女「帮我看一眼」，赋予他们求助的权利。</p>\n<p>节日聚会全家合照发朋友圈时，避免露银行卡、快递单在背景。</p>\n<p>每月第一个周日设为家庭安全日，十分钟巡检即可。</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"认知纠偏","title":"端到端加密不等于不会被盗：你必须知道的边界","excerpt":"加密很重要，但它不验证对面是谁，也不保护你已解锁的屏幕、云备份配置错误，或你主动交出的验证码。","read":"13 分钟","body":"<p>「WhatsApp 很安全」这句话，在媒体和课堂里被重复太多遍，以至于不少人误以为：<strong>开了加密就不会被骗、不会被盗、不会被泄密。</strong>事实上，端到端加密主要解决「中间链路窃听」问题，对你亲手递出的验证码、被关联的网页端、被木马阅读的屏幕、对接收方截图的扩散，都不自动免疫。</p>\n\n      <h2>加密确实守护什么</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>个人聊天与通话在传输路径上的机密性（在官方设计假设内）。</li>\n        <li>降低运营商、公共 Wi-Fi 窃听明文聊天内容的传统风险。</li>\n        <li>配合密钥管理，构成产品安全叙事的重要一环。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>加密不自动守护什么</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>你把注册码、PIN 告诉「客服」后，对方合法登录作恶。</li>\n        <li>攻击者通过关联设备同步阅读历史与实时消息。</li>\n        <li>已解锁手机上的肩窥、录屏、远程共享。</li>\n        <li>接收方转发、截图、备份至不安全云端。</li>\n        <li>云备份未加密或密钥保管不当造成的第二副本泄露。</li>\n        <li>对面身份是否是你以为的那个人——加密不验人。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>一句话：管道牢，不代表柜台是真，也不代表你还会把钥匙交给陌生人。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>如何建立「完整安全感」</h2>\n      <p>把加密当作第一层，叠加：验证码纪律、两步验证、关联设备巡检、官方客户端、备份密钥治理、转账双渠道核身。缺任何一块，社工都能绕路进来。</p>\n\n      <h2>向家人解释的话术</h2>\n      <p>「加密像密封车厢，但你要是主动把车门打开让人进来，车厢再密封也救不了。」用比喻降低抵触。</p>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>尊重加密，但不神话加密。HoldLine要补的，正是「端到端之外」那一大块现实风险地图。</p>\n<h2>新闻记者与维权人士的提醒</h2>\n      <p>消息来源保护依赖的不只是加密，还有设备物理安全、联系人身份核验、备份策略。若从事敏感工作，应叠加专用手机、分号运营，并定期做威胁模型复盘，而不是单点迷信某个开关。</p>\n<h2>群聊与社区</h2>\n      <p>社区母题、大型群聊有时不享受与个人聊天相同的端到端默认模型（视官方说明与群类型而异）。参加公开群时，假设内容可能被管理员或举报流程外的机制看见。不要把「在群里说了」等同于「绝对私密」。</p>\n<h2>转发链与谣言</h2>\n      <p>加密聊天里转发的伪官方公告，源头往往不在加密保护范围之外——问题是你相信了内容。培养「先看官方帮助中心再转」的习惯，比争论技术细节更能保护亲友。</p>\n<h2>小结复述</h2>\n      <p>信任加密，更信任自己的操作流程。下次有人伸手要码，想起本文：那才是真的在试图穿过管道。</p>\n<p>把本文转发给爱说「WhatsApp 绝对安全」的朋友，用实例纠偏偏差。</p>\n<p>企业培训加入本节Quiz，员工答错率高的题列入下期重点。</p>\n<p>读完本文后去检查一次关联设备，用行动记住边界。</p>\n<p>技术条款更新时重读官方安全页，别用三年前的印象当代事实。</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"防护设置","title":"聊天备份与端到端加密备份：保数据也要保钥匙","excerpt":"备份能救命，也能变成第二份聊天拷贝。如何开启加密备份、保管密码/密钥/通行密钥，以及换机前必须检查的事项。","read":"14 分钟","body":"<p>备份救过无数次换机与丢机，也可能是第二份完整聊天记录的副本。WhatsApp 支持将聊天备份到 iCloud / Google 云端，并可开启端到端加密备份，使云服务商在正确配置下难以直接阅读内容。但若你把密钥纸条丢了，「能备份」也会变成「永远找不回」。</p>\n\n      <h2>是否建议开启</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>有大量工作、家庭沟通需要留存：建议备份 + 开启端到端加密。</li>\n        <li>极高敏感对话：评估聊天锁、消失消息，并意识到接收方仍可截图。</li>\n        <li>公司合规要求保留记录：与法务确认备份存储位置与访问控制。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>开启路径（以常见版本为准）</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>设置 → 聊天 → 聊天备份。</li>\n        <li>进入端到端加密备份。</li>\n        <li>按提示创建密码、64 位密钥或通行密钥（视设备支持）。</li>\n        <li>把恢复方式写入密码管理器 + 离线保险位置（纸质信封、保险箱）。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>官方与安全机构均说明：加密备份的密码/密钥丢失后，通常无法由平台替你恢复。保管与备份同等重要。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>换机与丢机预案</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>换机前确认最近一次备份成功。</li>\n        <li>测试一次：你是否还能找到密钥。</li>\n        <li>恢复完成后立刻巡检两步验证与关联设备。</li>\n        <li>丢机时先夺账号主权，再处理云备份权限（邮箱、云账户）。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>常见误区</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>把密钥照片发微信「以防万一」——等于明文托管。</li>\n        <li>以为未加密备份「只有我自己能看」——云账户被盗即裸奔。</li>\n        <li>公司让员工私人号备份工作群，离职后数据治理失控。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>备份是双刃剑：加密 + 密钥治理，才能既防丢数据又防多一份泄露。</p>\n<h2>家庭共享云账户的坑</h2>\n      <p>夫妻共用 iCloud/Google 账号时，加密备份密钥若只存在一人脑中，丢机时可能双方都无法恢复。应使用共同保险箱记录密钥，或各自备份到受控企业密码库。离异或分户时记得轮换云登录与备份权限。</p>\n<h2>恢复演练年课</h2>\n      <p>每年一次在备用机上试恢复加密备份，确认密钥仍正确。演练可发现云账户欠费、密钥污损等隐藏问题。真丢机时的心态会从容许多。</p>\n<h2>执法调取边界</h2>\n      <p>不同司法辖区对云端调取规则不同。端到端加密备份提高调取难度，但不替你做法律判断。涉案时咨询执业律师；平时做好密钥自有与合法留存策略即可。</p>\n<h2>小结复述</h2>\n      <p>备份成功灯亮时，同步检查密钥是否仍在。两个绿灯才算完成。</p>\n<p>告诉家人密钥存放处（非密码本身），避免自己昏迷时无人能恢复重要对话。</p>\n<p>团队共享密钥使用双人控制，避免一人离职带走全部恢复权。</p>\n<p>云账户开启登录提醒，防备份通道被旁路攻破。</p>\n<p>笔记本记录最后成功备份日期，超三十天未备份则手机提醒。</p>\n<p>重大诉讼前按律师意见决定是否暂停自动备份，以免误删证据链。</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"防护设置","title":"应用锁与生物识别：给 WhatsApp 再加一把门锁","excerpt":"手机解锁后，应用锁能挡住顺手一点开的窥视。说明如何开启，以及它与系统锁屏、聊天锁定的分工。","read":"12 minit","body":"<p>系统锁屏挡「陌生人拿起整机」；WhatsApp 应用锁挡「手机已解锁但人离开半分钟」的场景——家人顺手点开、同事借看照片、孩子乱滑屏幕。官方提供的指纹、面容或设备凭据二次验证（名称因版本而异），属于低摩擦强化。</p>\n\n      <h2>开启步骤</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp 设置 → 隐私（或安全）→ 应用锁 / 指纹锁。</li>\n        <li>按提示验证系统生物识别或 PIN。</li>\n        <li>确认自动锁定时间（立即或 1 分钟）符合习惯。</li>\n        <li>检查通知快速回复是否会绕过锁（按版本调整）。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>与其他防护分工</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>系统锁屏：</strong>第一道，必备。</li>\n        <li><strong>应用锁：</strong>第二道，防已解锁环境。</li>\n        <li><strong>聊天锁：</strong>针对单个会话的隐藏与二次打开。</li>\n        <li><strong>两步验证：</strong>远程盗号主力，与应用锁互补。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>局限与注意</h2>\n      <p>应用锁挡不住你把手机递给骗子扫关联码，也挡不住你已把验证码发出去。生物识别在部分司法场景可能被强制解锁，高敏人群可评估纯 PIN 策略。</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>开启应用锁的三分钟，常为「借手机一分钟」后悔买单。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>应用锁是贴身小锁，与系统锁屏组成卧室门 + 保险柜的组合，而不是替代品。</p>\n<h2>不同品牌手机的差异</h2>\n      <p>部分国产定制系统在杀后台后会要求重新解锁 WhatsApp；可在电池优化里给 WhatsApp 白名单，避免应用锁形同虚设又让人烦躁关掉功能。iPhone 用户还应注意通知预览在锁屏 Widget 中的暴露。</p>\n      <p>旅行时若临时关闭生物识别（受伤、手套），请确保系统 PIN 足够强，而不是完全停用应用锁。</p>\n<h2>紧急情况下的可及性</h2>\n      <p>若担心突发疾病时家人无法读取 WhatsApp 联系医生，可准备单独写在信封里的医疗联络卡，而不是长期关闭应用锁。安全与可及性可分层设计：日常上锁，紧急信息走纸质或电话快捷拨号。</p>\n<h2>老年指纹识别衰退</h2>\n      <p>指纹磨损时识别失败，老人可能愤怒关闭应用锁。可改用设备 PIN 作为应用锁后备，或多录几个手指。耐心调试一次，比长期裸奔好。</p>\n<h2>机型差异清单</h2>\n      <p>三星、小米、OPPO 菜单路径不同，购买新机迁移时重新开一遍应用锁。不要假设换机助手会继承安全设置。收好说明书电子版，换机周对照检查项打勾。</p>\n<p>向常借你手机拍照的人说明应用锁用途，减少误触删改。</p>\n<p>夜间充电放卧室时仍建议开启应用锁，以防伴侣误传孩子不当内容非本意。</p>\n<p>教学视频发给长辈：如何开关应用锁，减少误关。</p>\n<p>双手潮湿时指纹失败正常，备用 PIN 应好记但非六位同号。</p>\n<p>双手残疾用户可用系统无障碍替代方案，仍不必关闭整机锁屏。</p>\n<p>出差刘海贴膜更换后重新录入指纹，减少误锁。</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"防护设置","title":"聊天锁定、阅后即焚与消失消息：减少残留暴露","excerpt":"这些功能不能防账号被接管，但能降低手机被翻看、历史消息久留的风险。也要明白截图与转发仍然存在。","read":"13 分钟","body":"<p>即使账号未被盗，消息长期留存也会带来纠纷、分手后泄露、同事误解等风险。WhatsApp 提供聊天锁定、默认消失消息、一次性查看媒体等功能，用于减少「躺在手机里的旧话」。它们不能阻止账号被接管，但能降低物理翻看与旧记录被翻出的概率。</p>\n\n      <h2>聊天锁定</h2>\n      <p>可将指定聊天移入锁定文件夹，打开需再验证生物识别或 PIN，列表默认不展示内容预览。适合共用手机或不想会话被一眼看到的情境。注意：对方设备上仍可能留存记录；锁定不替代两步验证。</p>\n\n      <h2>消失消息</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>可对单聊开启 24 小时 / 7 天 / 90 天等周期（以版本为准）。</li>\n        <li>减少「三年前一句话被截图」的风险，但无法阻止即时截图与转发。</li>\n        <li>群聊开启前需与成员沟通，避免误会证据被删。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>一次性查看媒体</h2>\n      <p>适合发送敏感图片/视频。接收方只可打开一次，并有限制截图的机制（以官方能力为准）。仍要相信对方不会用第二台设备翻拍。</p>\n\n      <h2>使用边界</h2>\n      <p>这些功能不是防弹衣；账号主权仍靠验证码纪律与关联设备巡检。也不会替你向对方证明「此话从没存在过」——法律场景请咨询专业人士。</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>与家人约定：消失消息开启前要口头告知，避免「你怎么删了」争吵。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>把锁定与消失消息当卫生工具：少留垢，不等于不生垢。主权安全仍看门把与钥匙。</p>\n<h2>与客户沟通的平衡点</h2>\n      <p>商务聊天若开启消失消息，可能与客户期望的「可查询记录」冲突。可对内使用锁聊 + 消失，对外保留邮件或 CRM 副本；法律留存义务行业需咨询合规。技术和制度要一起设计。</p>\n<h2>纠纷取证困境</h2>\n      <p>消失消息可能让你在维权时缺少聊天记录。对可能需要证据的交易，使用邮件或带时间戳的合同系统留痕；消失消息适合纯情感或低争议场景。事前想清「这话要不要在一年后还在」，再选策略。</p>\n<h2>未成年人锁定与监护</h2>\n      <p>家长为孩子锁敏感聊天时，应同时保留危机对话渠道（如直接电话），避免孩子遇险却因锁死无法呼救。锁定是降暴露面，不是切断亲子沟通。</p>\n<h2>小结复述</h2>\n      <p>消失消息适合低风险闲聊；合同与金钱话题请走可审计渠道。功能选型匹配风险级别。</p>\n<p>定期检查锁定聊天列表，删去不再需要的项，降低忘记开启的风险。</p>\n<p>律师函或维权前请咨询是否需要保留原始聊天导出记录。</p>\n<p>更新系统后重开消失消息选项，防升级重置。</p>\n<p>工作机与个人号分离时，各自评估不同消失消息策略。</p>\n<p>备份恢复后检查聊天锁目录是否仍生效，必要时重新锁定。</p>\n<p>消失消息开启前告知对方期望，避免合作误会。</p>\n<p>系统大版本升级前一晚导出必要聊天 PDF 备查。</p>\n<p>今晚打开一个锁聊核对是否生效，十秒钟。</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"场景专题","title":"商务号与个人号：店铺运营中的防盗与防冒充","excerpt":"客户群、订单沟通、员工代管——商务场景有额外风险。如何隔离权限、防止客服号被接管后群发诈骗。","read":"14 分钟","body":"<p>个人号被盗伤亲友情；店铺、微商、客服号被盗会在几小时内向客户群发「临时换收款码」「补缴运费」，直接砸招牌与现金流。商务场景还叠加员工代管、多机登录、平板放柜台等放大因素。</p>\n\n      <h2>权限与架构建议</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>经营主号必开两步验证，PIN 放企业密码库，离职即轮转。</li>\n        <li>限制关联设备数量，员工离职当天手机端全踢。</li>\n        <li>公用平板不得 24 小时挂会话；设强锁屏与应用锁。</li>\n        <li>对外固定公示收款账户，教育客户：聊天里临时改码要先二次确认。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>客户澄清模板</h2>\n      <p>「本店收款账户以门店告示 / 官网为准。若 WhatsApp 私聊要求变更收款方式，请先致电门店电话核实。」越早发，越少跟风转账。</p>\n\n      <h2>员工培训三不准</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>不准把店铺号验证码发个人微信「帮登」。</li>\n        <li>不准扫陌生「商家认证」「流量扶持」码。</li>\n        <li>发现异常群发：先断会话、按夺回流程、全渠道澄清。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>与个人号隔离</h2>\n      <p>能用企业工具解决的，不要把全部客户压在私人号；即便仍在个人号经营，也要分设备、分 PIN、分备份策略。</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>把「客户资金安全」写进 KPI，与销售额并列考核意识。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>商务号防盗是经营连续性工程。权限最小化、设备清单化、收款核验规范化，比公关稿便宜。</p>\n<h2>多平台收款一致性</h2>\n      <p>除 WhatsApp，客户也可能在 Facebook、Instagram 咨询。所有渠道公示同一套收款账户，并定期截图存档。员工接手账号时签署保管协议，明确验证码不共享。</p>\n      <p>大型促销日前做「账号健康检查」，比促销后追款轻松。</p>\n<h2>保险与应急预案</h2>\n      <p>评估商业险是否覆盖网络诈骗导致的客户赔付。与律师准备简短对外声明模板与临时收款冻结流程。账号被盗也是业务连续性事件，宜写入公司应急手册演练一次。</p>\n<h2>外包客服账号</h2>\n      <p>外包团队共用客服号时，须名单化管理每日谁登录；班次结束统一踢关联设备。外包商合同加入泄露追责与验证码保密条款，培训记录留档。</p>\n<h2>小结复述</h2>\n      <p>客户信任你的品牌，不是某一条随机 WhatsApp。标准化核身，品牌才不易被一条假消息击穿。</p>\n<p>季末盘点：本季有无异常收款？客户投诉里筛账号冒用关键词。</p>\n<p>节假日值班表注明谁持有客服号，避免交接真空期无人盯异常。</p>\n<p>新客户合同写明官方联络方式，减少假号讨价还价空间。</p>\n<p>logo 水印加入官方海报图，客户易辨认真伪收款图文。</p>\n<p>年度审计抽样员工是否知晓店铺号验证码保密制度。</p>\n<p>午间交接班口头报告有无异常登录，再换班营业。</p>\n<p>闭店打烊第一台手机应是检查关联设备。</p>\n<p>打印收银台告示：只认柜台二维码收款。</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"场景专题","title":"换机迁移防盗清单：在新旧手机交替时守住账号","excerpt":"换机窗口最容易手忙脚乱：备份、验证、关联设备、两步验证可能被跳过。一份迁移日检查表帮你平滑且安全地切换。","read":"13 分钟","body":"<p>换机那天，心理带宽被「照片转过来没有」占满，安全设置最容易被跳过：忘记开两步验证、忘记踢旧网页版、在店里让别人代扫关联。攻击者爱这个时间窗——你更愿意把手机递出去省时间。</p>\n\n      <h2>换机前 24 小时</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>确认备份最新且加密密钥可取。</li>\n        <li>确认两步验证邮箱能登录。</li>\n        <li>列出要保留的电脑会话，计划迁移后重连。</li>\n        <li>写好紧急联系人电话（非 WhatsApp）。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>换机当天顺序</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>新机只装官方 WhatsApp；旧机仍在手时再开始验证。</li>\n        <li>注册码输入时遮挡肩窥；不把码给店员。</li>\n        <li>恢复备份后，立刻确认两步验证仍开启。</li>\n        <li>关联设备页：踢旧会话，再在新电脑按需扫码。</li>\n        <li>开启应用锁、检查隐私与群邀请限制。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>旧机处理</h2>\n      <p>确认新机正常后，旧机退出 WhatsApp 或恢复出厂；出售前重复确认 iCloud/Google 账户已退出。</p>\n\n      <h2>常见失误</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>双机同时在线太久，旧机丢失未踢。</li>\n        <li>备份密钥忘迁移，聊天全空。</li>\n        <li>店员代操作扫了不明二维码。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>把换机当天日历标红：上午迁移，下午安全巡检，不让两件事混成一团。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>迁移的目标：唯一可信主设备、干净关联列表、不断档两步验证、密钥不丢。</p>\n<h2>跨生态迁移（iOS ↔ Android）</h2>\n      <p>换操作系统时，WhatsApp 聊天记录迁移受官方工具限制，可能无法完整转移。提前导出必要合同、发票到 PDF；验证号码时更警惕肩窥，因为用户往往在实体店焦虑操作。迁移完成后务必重做关联设备与两步验证，不要假设「换机助手帮你做好安全」。</p>\n<h2>数据擦除认证</h2>\n      <p>旧机出售前使用官方恢复出厂，并在设置中确认账户已移除。部分安卓机型需额外格式化 SD 卡。买二手机者也应用新号登录、全新两步验证，不信任前任机主的任何残留会话。</p>\n<h2>手表与副卡</h2>\n      <p>eSIM 手表若可独立收短信，迁移时确认验证码发到哪块屏。新机激活后测试 WhatsApp 来电与短信路由，避免验证码落在旧手表上被他人看见。</p>\n<h2>小结复述</h2>\n      <p>换机窗口再忙，也留十分钟给安全五步。迁移快乐建立在号还在你自己手里。</p>\n<p>迁移当晚发给好友一条非 WhatsApp 公告：「换机成功，诈骗勿信。」</p>\n<p>旧手机卡托、包装盒上的 ICCID 信息撕毁后再丢弃。</p>\n<p>好友名片导出 CSV 备份，迁移后对照是否导入完整。</p>\n<p>新机贴膜后再录入指纹，避免后期换膜导致识别失败。</p>\n<p>孩子旧手表若绑定旧号，换机后记得解绑再绑新机。</p>\n<p>迁移当晚不开陌生网页「测速」，专注官方流程。</p>\n<p>旧机 SIM 针妥善存放，防随手丢弃被捡。</p>\n<p>新机首周每晚十分钟安全复查，仅一周。</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"骗局识破","title":"群组诈骗与管理员风险：从拉群到假通知的全链路","excerpt":"陌生群、假客服群、管理员被盗后的公告——群是高频诈骗场域。教你设置入群门槛与识别假通知。","read":"14 分钟","body":"<p>群组有「多数人在场」的心理安全感，却也是诈骗扩大的扩音器。陌生拉群、假客服群、管理员号被盗后发钓鱼公告——都利用了你相信「群里不会全都是托」的假设。</p>\n\n      <h2>危险群画像</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>陌生号拉你进群后马上逼加「专员」私聊。</li>\n        <li>公告里只有短链「实名登记」「补贴领取」。</li>\n        <li>限时恐吓：不操作就清退、罚款、报警。</li>\n        <li>禁止截图外传，要求「只听管理员」。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>个人防护</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>隐私限制谁能拉你进群。</li>\n        <li>陌生群先观察，不点链接；必要时退出举报。</li>\n        <li>家庭、公司核心群：管理员必须开两步验证。</li>\n        <li>涉及改收款、改报销的通知：第二渠道确认。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>管理员职责</h2>\n      <p>定期清理僵尸号；开启新成员审核；异常公告出现先撤回再多渠道澄清；关键群备援副管理员。</p>\n\n      <h2>管理员号被盗</h2>\n      <p>立即夺回 → 其他成员代发「忽略刚才链接」 → 撤销邀请链接 → 视情况重建群并核验成员身份。</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>群越大，越要把「要点链接先私聊管理员语音确认」写成群公告。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>群是信任放大器。入群门槛、管理员加固、关键动作双确认，能挡掉多数群内爆发式骗局。</p>\n<h2>「只读群」里的假文件</h2>\n      <p>管理员设群只读后，骗子可能改用「文件」标签发 APK 或「订单表格」。提醒成员：群文件与群链接同等可疑。可关闭普通成员发送文件权限，或在入群公告写清「本群不发安装包」。</p>\n      <p>投资、返利类群名常带 emoji 与紧迫词，可设置关键词家庭过滤器互相提醒。</p>\n<h2>链上热情与 FOMO</h2>\n      <p>群内机器人或托儿会伪造「已上车」「已翻倍」截图，制造错失恐惧。看到晒单先问：能否链上验证？能否语音确认？群主反差超大收益时，默认剧本可疑。退群不丢人，钱没了才难开口。</p>\n<h2>假官方助手号</h2>\n      <p>群内出现的「WhatsApp 助手」「安全 bot」多为骗子私号改名。正版功能通知不会私聊要码。见到 bot 自称，忽略并在真官方渠道核实。</p>\n<h2>小结复述</h2>\n      <p>退群免费，信假公告很贵。遇到不对劲，先退再核实，比面子重要。</p>\n<p>向群主提议开启新成员审批，一次设置惠及全群。</p>\n<p>养号群常有托晒收益，勿以其热度判断真实性。冷思考一分钟再行动，能避开多数 FOMO。</p>\n      <p>若已在群投入资金，立即停止加码，保留证据，通过银行与正规渠道求助。</p>\n<p>不必每个群都活跃，精简群列表等于减少攻击面。</p>\n<p>对不常发言却频繁改群名的异常号，群主应直接移除。</p>\n<p>邀请链接设置定期轮换，降低外泄后长期拉人的风险。</p>\n<p>进群一周不说话专发截图的号，标记观察。</p>\n<p>夜间免打扰仍要看安全群艾特全体公告。</p>\n<p>见到暴富截图先存疑，受益逻辑自洽再判断。</p>\n<p>退出异常群不算失礼，是止损。</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"骗局识破","title":"AI 语音与深度冒充：当「听起来像本人」不再足够","excerpt":"合成语音让「打电话核实」也出现新风险。建立更强核身问题与双渠道确认，避免只靠声纹感觉。","read":"13 分钟","body":"<p>「借钱一定要打电话」曾是金科玉律，如今要升级：AI 语音克隆可以在几秒钟内模仿你亲友的声调，再配合被盗 WhatsApp 里的语境施压。技术进步没有取消核实，只是提高了核实深度。</p>\n\n      <h2>新规则：强核身，而不只是听声音</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>问只有双方知道的随机细节，避免固定问题被提前扒窃。</li>\n        <li>要求即时视频露脸 + 举手比随机数字。</li>\n        <li>大额启用延时到账或双人复核。</li>\n        <li>家庭预设「借钱暗号」，口头说不出则不转。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>旧规则仍然有效</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>拨打通讯录老号码，不用聊天新号。</li>\n        <li>拒绝礼品卡、虚拟货币等不可逆支付。</li>\n        <li>越是催保密、催立刻，越要慢下来。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>如何降低被克隆素材</h2>\n      <p>公开社交媒体少发长段高清语音/视频；隐私设置限制陌生人下载。不能完全杜绝，但能抬高攻击成本。</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>技术在变，原则不变：重大资金动作必须超出当前聊天窗口验证身份。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>别因 AI 放弃电话核实，要让核实变难：知识问题 + 视频临场 + 延迟支付组合出拳。</p>\n<h2>企业财务岗位的升级流程</h2>\n      <p>公司应要求：任何变更供应商账户，必须回拨合同留档电话，且双人签字。即使语音听起来是老板，也要走流程——真实老板应当为流程感到安全，而非被冒犯。</p>\n      <p>可录制双方同意的固定问候暗号，不定期更换，减少 AI 模型过拟合历史音频的可能。</p>\n<h2>跨境时差与 sleepy 决策</h2>\n      <p>凌晨电话最易击穿防线。约定家庭规则：23 点至 7 点之间的借款请求，天亮后再处理。真有急症应走 120 / 官方急救渠道，而不是 WhatsApp 转账。AI 语音+时差是常见组合。</p>\n<h2>公开语音样本管理</h2>\n      <p>播客、直播连麦会留下长时音色样本。公众人物更应使用延迟支付与多签流程；普通人也可减少在公开群发送长语音圈，降低被克隆素材质量。</p>\n<h2>小结复述</h2>\n      <p>声音可以合成，细节难以伪造。多问一句只有真人才答得出的问题，AI 脚本常会露馅。</p>\n<p>财务岗位轮岗时重申核身流程，新人不知暗号不能单独打款。</p>\n<p>公司可规定超阈值打款需两人到会签，系统延时一小时到账，为 AI 语音核实赢时间。</p>\n      <p>亲属约定暗号每年春节更换一次，写在家务本而非聊天里。</p>\n<p>视频会议大额决策录像留底，事后可追溯语音是否AI。</p>\n<p>语音留言信箱短促即可，减少被批量爬取训练素材长度。</p>\n<p>公共演讲录音如需发布，可轻微变调或加 BGM 抬高克隆成本。</p>\n<p>董事会决议大额支出仍需纸质签字存档。</p>\n<p>午休后大额转账强制冷却三十分钟再确认。</p>\n<p>情侣大额互转也走语音加暗号，不因亲密跳步。</p>\n<p>绑架话术要钱时先打110再转钱。</p>\n<p>测一次家人暗号问答，练手。</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"家庭防护","title":"青少年使用 WhatsApp：防盗号、防诱导与监护边界","excerpt":"少年人社交活跃、防备不同，易成骗码与诱导目标。给家长的设置建议、对话脚本与求助机制。","read":"14 分钟","body":"<p>青少年比长辈更会用手机，却未必更懂风险：游戏交易、饭圈抽奖、校园谣言、恋爱勒索，都能把「给个验证码」「发个自拍」包装成理所当然。家长的目标不是监控一切，而是红线清晰、求助不被罚。</p>\n\n      <h2>与孩子约定的红线</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>验证码、证件照、银行卡不给网友。</li>\n        <li>皮肤代充走官方平台，不私下 WhatsApp 转账。</li>\n        <li>被威胁曝光隐私：先告诉家长，而不是先付钱。</li>\n        <li>不装同学传的「破解版」「双开」。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>家长协助设置</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>开启两步验证；恢复邮箱可用家庭协商方案。</li>\n        <li>限制群邀请；头像对陌生人收敛。</li>\n        <li>系统与应用锁开启；定期一起看关联设备。</li>\n        <li>教使用封锁举报，而非硬刚辱骂。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>沟通技巧</h2>\n      <p>用案例讨论，而非羞辱；保证「报告可疑事先免责」。隐瞒往往因为怕手机被没收。</p>\n\n      <h2>学校与同伴</h2>\n      <p>与班主任共享官方反诈资源；鼓励孩子向同伴传播「要码即诈」而非二次嘲笑受害者。</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>青少年需要的是教练型家长，不是狱卒型家长。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>教育 + 设置 + 友好求助通道，比偷看聊天更能长期降低风险。</p>\n<h2>校园二手与游戏陪玩</h2>\n      <p>「低价皮肤」「陪玩押金」常伴随要验证码盗号或裸聊勒索。与孩子约定：凡涉及账号登录帮助一律拒绝，并截图给家长。学校心理辅导渠道应纳入数字勒索处置。</p>\n      <p>鼓励少年做同伴教育者，比单纯父母说教更有接受度。</p>\n<h2>校规与数字纪律</h2>\n      <p>与学校合作，把「不给验证码」写入学生手册示例，与防 bullying 同批教育。班级内建立举报可疑账号的匿名渠道，保护报告者。少年互相提醒比家长单打更有效。</p>\n<h2>心理健康与敲诈</h2>\n      <p>裸聊敲诈后少年可能自杀意念上升，家长发现异常聊天锁死前应寻求专业心理援助。合同约定「任何事可先说、后处理」，比单纯没收手机更能保命。</p>\n<h2>小结复述</h2>\n      <p>孩子愿早点跟你说，比偷看聊天更能降低损失。把「报告免罚」写进家庭协议并真正执行。</p>\n<p>和学校心理老师共享反诈资源，让孩子知道被骂也可找老师。</p>\n<p>引导少年关注官方反诈账号而不是猎奇诈骗集锦，减少模仿犯罪手法风险。</p>\n      <p>家长自己先不开改装 App，以身作则比道理更有说服力。</p>\n<p>设立家庭「可疑事件奖金」：主动举报骗局手法奖奶茶券，正向激励报告。</p>\n<p>游戏语音频道同样禁发验证码，与 WhatsApp 规则一致执行。</p>\n<p>寝室公约第一条可写：谁要验证码谁请客——用幽默记规则。</p>\n<p>运动会集体机勿混用个人 WhatsApp 登入场馆屏。</p>\n<p>每学期家长群转发本专题一次即可。</p>\n<p>班主任知情即可，不必全班公开孩子受骗细节。</p>\n<p>操场换机号要跟父母当天报备。</p>\n<p>班会分享反诈比处分更有效。</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"场景专题","title":"出行与漫游：旅游、出差时的 WhatsApp 防盗要点","excerpt":"换卡、租机、酒店 Wi-Fi、公用电脑，旅行场景叠加风险。出发前、旅途中、回国后的三段清单。","read":"13 分钟","body":"<p>旅行叠加陌生网络、临时 SIM、eSIM、酒店电脑、时差疲惫——四个因素叠在一起，验证码与关联设备特别容易失守。出发前打好开关，比在异国凌晨夺回账号轻松一个数量级。</p>\n\n      <h2>出发前</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>确认两步验证与邮箱异地可登。</li>\n        <li>应用锁开启；必要时锁定高敏聊天。</li>\n        <li>完成加密备份并确认密钥随身 offline 可查。</li>\n        <li>记录运营商与国际漫游挂失方式，以官方渠道为准。</li>\n        <li>告诉家人：旅行期间借钱消息要严核。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>旅途中</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>避免酒店商务中心保持 WhatsApp 登录。</li>\n        <li>不扫「退税」「签证加急」「景点免票」来路不明二维码。</li>\n        <li>租用手机谨慎登主号；用完登出并查关联设备。</li>\n        <li>公共环境输入验证码遮挡；警惕肩窥与虚假 Wi-Fi 门户。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>回国后 48 小时</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>关联设备大清扫，踢旅行临时会话。</li>\n        <li>若用过当地 SIM，确认无异常呼叫转移。</li>\n        <li>视情况轮换两步验证 PIN。</li>\n        <li>回顾行程中是否点过可疑链，有则加固。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>把「回国第一天清网页版」写进行李unpack 清单，与洗衣并列。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>旅行防盗＝少登陌生设备 + 不留长会话 + 回国复盘。把安全当行程模块，而不是回来才补的作业。</p>\n<h2>eSIM 与双卡切换</h2>\n      <p>旅行 eSIM 启用后，确认哪张卡接收 WhatsApp 短信；避免旧卡仍插在手机里却被当地奸商复制。回国卸载旅行 eSIM 后，重查两步验证与关联设备，防止漫游期间扫过的二维码留下会话。</p>\n      <p>长途航班 Wi-Fi 若有门户登录页，注意是否仿造品牌诱导安装证书。</p>\n<h2>证件与手机分开放</h2>\n      <p>背包被盗时，手机和护照一起丢会让换卡与身份核验雪上加霜。分开放置，云存护照扫描件加密保存。旅行保险涵盖设备盗抢时，报案号对理赔很重要，以保单条款为准。</p>\n<h2>边境口岸 SIM 摊贩</h2>\n      <p>口岸廉价 SIM 可能预设恶意短信转发。尽量在机场官方柜台购卡，第一时间查运营商 App 业务开通。入境第一天就做关联设备检查，别等回国。</p>\n<h2>小结复述</h2>\n      <p>旅行相册可以晒，验证码和登录码不行。发朋友圈前检查屏幕截屏有没有入镜 OTP。</p>\n<p>返程机票出票后设日历提醒：落地当日安全复盘。</p>\n<p>给行李箱挂联系卡用邮箱前缀而非全号，降低号码被抄已用于社工的概率。</p>\n<p>酒店保险箱放护照与备用机，手机随身不离视线高度。</p>\n<p>租车 GPS 若需扫码绑定，用流量不连陌生车载 Wi-Fi 再扫。</p>\n<p>回程海关排队勿借陌生人手机打电话，防关联设备借机扫码。</p>\n<p>返程后第一周陌生电诈上升，保持高核身门槛。</p>\n<p>旅行箱外侧勿贴含手机号快递条。</p>\n<p>机场退税队旁勿给陌生人代登热点。</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"总览","title":"HoldLine防盗总手册：30 天加固计划与终身清单","excerpt":"把前 29 篇收成可执行计划：今天、本周、本月、每季要做什么，以及被盗当日的一页纸流程。","read":"16 分钟","body":"<p>三十篇读完却不动手，效果约等于零。这篇把所有方法收成时间表：今天要什么、本周要什么、每季要什么，以及被盗当天的「一页纸」。你可以打印贴墙，也可以在日历里设重复提醒。</p>\n\n      <h2>今天（60 分钟内必做）</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>开两步验证 + 恢复邮箱 + 安全保管 PIN。</li>\n        <li>检查并清理已关联设备。</li>\n        <li>向家人同步：验证码谁都不给。</li>\n        <li>系统强锁屏 + WhatsApp 应用锁。</li>\n        <li>确认客户端来自官方商店。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>本周内完成</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>隐私页逐项收紧：头像、群邀请、状态。</li>\n        <li>需要备份则开端到端加密备份并保管密钥。</li>\n        <li>家庭 15 分钟会：演练骗码与借钱核实。</li>\n        <li>清理陌生群与可疑联系人。</li>\n        <li>商务使用者盘点谁能碰主设备与电脑会话。</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>本月延展</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>帮长辈/青少年完成同等基础加固。</li>\n        <li>咨询运营商可否增加换卡保护。</li>\n        <li>全家收藏夺回流程入口。</li>\n        <li>评估聊天锁与消失消息策略。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>每季 30 分钟复盘</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>再扫关联设备；可选更新 PIN。</li>\n        <li>确认邮箱、备份密钥仍可访问。</li>\n        <li>回顾季度内是否出现过要码、假投票、共享屏幕请求。</li>\n        <li>更新紧急联系人列表与家庭暗号。</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>被盗当日一页纸</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>本机重新验证号码，注册码不外传。</li>\n        <li>清关联设备，重设两步验证。</li>\n        <li>短信/电话通知亲友止损。</li>\n        <li>有钱款损失联系银行，必要时走当地警方渠道。</li>\n        <li>复盘入口，补上缺口。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>终身四句：</strong>验证码是钥匙；两步验证是第二道门；关联设备是后门清单；借钱转账要双渠道核实。背下来，比收藏三十篇更管用。</p></div>\n\n      <h2>如何持续</h2>\n      <p>选一位家庭「数码联络人」，负责提醒季度复盘；公司可并入 IT 月报。习惯的价值在于重复，而不是一次性完美。</p>\n\n      <h2>小结</h2>\n      <p>HoldLine 30 篇的终点不是成为研究员，而是把清醒变成日程。今天完成必做五项，你已经跑赢大多数用户——剩下的，交给时间表慢慢补齐。</p>\n<h2>把清单嵌进现有习惯</h2>\n      <p>将「关联设备检查」与刷牙绑定；将「季度 PIN 轮换」与交房租同一天；公司可把复盘写进 OKR 安全教育完成率。自动化提醒胜过硬记。</p>\n      <p>若 30 篇里你只记住一篇，请回到第一篇验证码纪律；若你愿意执行一篇，请执行本篇时间表。知识 + 日程，才等于真正的HoldLine。</p>\n<h2>评估自己的完成度</h2>\n      <p>每季度自评 0–3 分：验证码纪律、两步验证、关联设备、备份密钥、转账核身。低于 2 分的项下周优先补课。分数制比「好像都做了」更诚实。邀请家人互评，增加社会监督。</p>\n<h2>版本号与更新</h2>\n      <p>本清单与官方 App 功能以当前常见版本为准，大版本更新后重新扫一遍设置路径。把HoldLine首页加入浏览器收藏，季度回来时复习是否有新骗局专题值得全家共读。</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"新加坡","source":"新加坡警察部队（SPF）","title":"新加坡警方预警：WhatsApp 账号接管骗局再度抬头","excerpt":"骗子利用已被盗账号向通讯录要验证码，或以视频通话要求屏幕共享偷看六位码，再冒充事主借钱或骗取银行卡信息。","body":"<p>2024 年 6 月 7 日，新加坡警察部队发布防诈通告，提醒公众警惕 WhatsApp 账号接管骗局再度活跃。通告指出，骗子在控制受害人账号后，会向其通讯录发送消息，以「借钱」「代买礼品卡」等理由骗取转账；部分案件还会进一步索取银行卡照片与支付 OTP，造成未授权扣款。</p>\n      <h2>通报中的两类主要手法</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>熟人骗码：</strong>受害人收到「联系人」消息，声称验证码发错，请帮忙转发六位数。该联系人账号往往已被盗。</li>\n        <li><strong>屏幕共享窥码：</strong>骗子伪装成政府或银行人员发起视频通话，要求共享屏幕「核验资料」，再触发 WhatsApp 验证码显示在屏幕上被对方看见。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>官方建议</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>验证码、银行资料、OTP 绝不外传。</li>\n        <li>即使是通讯录好友的异常请求也要警惕。</li>\n        <li>对屏幕共享请求先用其他方式核实身份。</li>\n        <li>开启两步验证；设置手机锁屏并注意物理接触。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine解读：这则通告把「骗码」与「隔空看码」并列，说明防御不能只盯着「会不会把数字打出来发送」。</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"新加坡","source":"新加坡警察部队（SPF）","title":"新加坡再发通告：OTP 连环骗导致账号连续失守","excerpt":"骗子输入受害人号码触发 OTP，再由已被盗的「熟人」来要码；得手后复制同样话术攻击下一名联系人。","body":"<p>2026 年 3 月 12 日，SPF 再次就 WhatsApp 账号被入侵发布防诈提醒。流程被写得很清楚：骗子先用受害人手机号尝试登录 → 系统向受害人发送 OTP → 已失守的熟人账号发来消息，谎称「验证码本来是我的，发错到你那里了」→ 受害人交出 OTP 后被踢下线 → 骗子用新账号继续骗下一个人。</p>\n      <h2>受害人通常何时察觉</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>自己突然无法登录 WhatsApp；或</li>\n        <li>朋友告知「你正在向别人要验证码」。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>通告强调的加固动作</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>设置 → 已关联的设备：移除不明设备。</li>\n        <li>设置 → 账户 → 两步验证：开启。</li>\n        <li>配合 ScamShield 等官方防诈工具查证可疑请求。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>通告称当时未汇总到金钱损失，但账号失守本身足以启动下一轮诈骗，社会成本仍然很高。</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"新加坡","source":"新加坡警察部队（SPF）","title":"盗号后冒充借钱：新加坡两月至少 128 起、损失约 24.9 万新元","excerpt":"自 2026 年 9 月以来，骗子接管 WhatsApp 后假冒亲友、同事索取贷款或代付款，警方公布案件量与损失并重申 ScamShield。","body":"<p>2026 年 10 月 21 日通告显示：自同年 9 月起，至少 128 起案件涉及骗子控制 WhatsApp 后，在社交媒体/即时通讯中冒充事主向联系人借钱或要求「帮忙转账」，累计损失至少约 249,000 新元。</p>\n      <h2>常见借口</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>银行转账限额到了，请你代付。</li>\n        <li>自己转账不方便，请先借一笔。</li>\n        <li>冒充子女，要求支付「AI/交流项目」费用。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>警方 ADD-CHECK-TELL 框架</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD：</strong>安装 ScamShield，过滤可疑来电与短信。</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK：</strong>用官方渠道核实；对异常转账请求直接打电话问本人。</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL：</strong>告知亲友与当局；若已转账立刻联系银行并报警。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine提醒：盗号往往只是第一阶段，第二阶段才是向通讯录「变现」。防盗与防借钱核身要一起做。</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"瑞士","source":"瑞士国家网络安全中心（NCSC）","title":"瑞士 NCSC：十月假投票骗案激增，单月逾 50 宗相关报告","excerpt":"熟人账号发来「帮小朋友投票」链接，假站收短信码或诱导扫描关联设备二维码，造成接管或静默同步。","body":"<p>NCSC 在 2026 年第 43 周周报中称，WhatsApp 账号被盗/被入侵报告再度显著上升；仅 10 月就收到 50 多份此类骗局报告，且多起攻击成功。手法核心是「善意投票」：消息来自通讯录中已被控账号，以学校或舞蹈比赛拉票为名，催你快点点开。</p>\n      <h2>假站的两条杀招</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>让你输入手机号与「短信验证码」——实为 WhatsApp 注册码，填完等于帮骗子在新设备激活账号。</li>\n        <li>展示二维码，诱导用「关联设备」扫描——账号仍可用，但骗子网页端已挂上。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>机构建议</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>即便熟人拉票也先换渠道核实。</li>\n        <li>验证码不外传、不进网页。</li>\n        <li>只在自有设备上为官方网页版扫码。</li>\n        <li>开启两步验证 PIN。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC 提醒：域名会不断更换以绕过过滤，不能靠「记住某一个坏网址」一劳永逸。</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"瑞士","source":"瑞士 NCSC","title":"瑞士春夏已现「假竞赛投票」盗号潮，秋季再度回潮","excerpt":"NCSC 早在第 21 周就描述过「为孩子投票」话术；秋季周报显示同类攻击再次扩大，说明话术可长期复用。","body":"<p>瑞士 NCSC 在春季相关周报中已记录：受害者收到看似熟人的消息，请求为某孩子的比赛投票；点击后落入盗号陷阱并可能失去 WhatsApp 访问权。到了秋季（第 43 周），机构明确写到「这类攻击并不新鲜，但近期报告上升说明它又好用了」。</p>\n      <h2>为什么要单独记住这则</h2>\n      <p>同一国家、同一机构、前后两季重复预警，证明假投票不是一次性新闻，而是可季节性复燃的「成熟产品」。家庭群与学校群尤其要预设规则：拉票链接默认先核实。</p>\n      <h2>给中文用户的迁移意义</h2>\n      <p>话术可以本地化成「助力打卡」「抽奖投票」「公益联名」，结构不变：熟人转发 + 短时压力 + 要码或扫码。HoldLine第 04 篇长文即对应该结构。</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"爱尔兰","source":"爱尔兰国家网络安全中心（NCSC）","title":"爱尔兰 NCSC：验证码骗局升级，商务 WhatsApp 暴露面受关注","excerpt":"顾问文件详述骗子如何先控熟人号，再制造紧迫感索要验证码；并提醒商务场景下账号失守可能牵出更多数据风险。","body":"<p>爱尔兰 NCSC 于 2024 年 8 月发布 WhatsApp 验证码骗局建议书，描述攻击链：取得手机号 → 在登录页输入该号触发真实验证码 → 通过已失守联系人身份联系受害人 → 以紧急协助为由索码 → 得手后接管并继续扩散。</p>\n      <h2>特别提醒：商务用途</h2>\n      <p>文件强调企业员工用 WhatsApp 处理工作时，账号一旦失守，暴露的不只是私人聊天，还可能包括客户沟通与内部协调信息。这与HoldLine「商务号专题」相互印证：经营号需要更严的设备与 PIN 保管制度。</p>\n      <h2>核心句子</h2>\n      <p>验证码绝不该分享；WhatsApp 不会主动向你要码，其他任何人也不该要。</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"开曼群岛","source":"开曼群岛皇家警察（RCIPS）","title":"加勒比警方连续预警：伪冒本地来电 + AI 语音模仿助攻盗号","excerpt":"RCIPS 称接管骗局持续有人中招；出现伪造本地区号来电，并有报告称骗子使用 AI 模仿语音制造可信度。","body":"<p>开曼群岛皇家警察在 2024 年多次更新 WhatsApp 账号接管骗局警告。8 月通告回顾经典路径：短信/WhatsApp/电话制造恐慌 → 索取六位验证码 → 账户易主 → 冒充事主诈骗联系人。9 月更新则提到：近期受害者接到看起来像本地（345）号码的电话，实为境外伪造来电显示；有报告称口音特征明显，且存在 AI 深伪音频模仿。</p>\n      <h2>警方列明「不要做」的事</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>不要回复可疑消息</li>\n        <li>不要打开附件</li>\n        <li>不要点击消息中的链接</li>\n        <li>不要生成并把 WhatsApp 六位码发给任何人</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>国际趋势观察：电话显示号码可伪造，AI 声音可模仿——「听起来像本地/像本人」不再等于安全。</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"特立尼达和多巴哥","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT：品牌大使投票话术钓鱼，得手后改商务资料继续扩散","excerpt":"攻击者以「帮我投票当品牌大使」开场骗取验证码；控制账号后可能改为商务资料，再对通讯录二次钓鱼。","body":"<p>特立尼达和多巴哥网络安全事件响应团队（TTCSIRT）在 2024 年 11 月警告正在发生的 WhatsApp 钓鱼活动。话术示例包括请对方帮忙投票支持自己成为品牌大使，语气礼貌、像朋友拜托。</p>\n      <h2>攻击阶段</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>伪装成认识的人请求「帮忙」。</li>\n        <li>诱使受害人交出 MFA/验证码。</li>\n        <li>完成账号接管。</li>\n        <li>可能把资料改成商务形象，提高后续诈骗可信度。</li>\n        <li>对受害人通讯录发动下一轮钓鱼。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>该通报提醒我们：界面上的「商务账号」标记并不自动等于可信商家，尤其当对方突然改资料并开始群发奇怪请求时。</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"中国香港","source":"香港警方 / 媒体报道（SCMP 等）","title":"香港警示即时通讯骑劫回潮：首季损失超 2000 万港元，九成涉 WhatsApp","excerpt":"网络安全及科技罪案调查科公布首季 864 宗账户被骑劫案，损失约 2040 万港元；逾九成涉及 WhatsApp。","body":"<p>据公开报道，香港警方在 2024 年 4 月前后警示即时通讯账户骑劫骗局回潮：同年第一季度录得 864 宗相关案件，损失约 2040 万港元，其中超过 90% 涉及 WhatsApp。骗子通过钓鱼链接等方式取得关键信息接管账号，再读取通讯录实施诈骗。</p>\n      <h2>本地常见后续动作</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>冒充事主向亲友借钱或要求购买点数卡。</li>\n        <li>利用过往聊天语气降低怀疑。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>防御呼应</h2>\n      <p>警方一贯建议：开两步验证、检查关联设备、慎点链接、转账前电话核实。香港用户还可配合 Scameter 等官方查骗工具。</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"中国香港","source":"香港警方（公开社交媒体通报）","title":"2025 年 3 月单月 93 宗 WhatsApp 骑劫，损失逾千万港元","excerpt":"警方提醒尤其提防「海外亲友紧急要钱」；强调登出不明设备、开两步验证、不点可疑链接、不分享验证码。","body":"<p>2025 年 4 月相关报道援引警方信息：仅 3 月便录得 93 宗 WhatsApp 账户骑劫案，损失超过 1000 万港元。骗子接管后研究聊天记录，模仿语气，再编造医疗或旅行紧急状况逼你转账。</p>\n      <h2>警方四条自我保护提示（报道归纳）</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>登出无法识别的关联设备，开启两步验证。</li>\n        <li>不要点击 WhatsApp 里的可疑链接。</li>\n        <li>绝不分享密码或验证码。</li>\n        <li>联系人突然要钱，先打电话核实。</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>数字说明：即便整体某些诈骗类别后来有所回落，单月千万级损失仍足以证明威胁未消失。</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"中国香港","source":"香港警方公开案情（媒体报道）","title":"香港巨额个案：骑劫 WhatsApp + 深伪声线，单案涉款约 1.45 亿港元","excerpt":"骗徒长期监察聊天，假冒公司人员并用深伪技术模仿声线，诱导受害人转移虚拟资产。","body":"<p>据 2025 年相关案情披露，香港当年最大型骗案之一涉及 WhatsApp 账户骑劫，涉款约 1.45 亿港元。骗子并非得手后立刻要钱，而是持续观察对话；作案时假冒公司职员，并使用深伪技术模仿其声线，通过语音讯息指示受害人把虚拟资产转入另一钱包。</p>\n      <h2>这则新闻为什么重要</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>说明「听起来像同事」可能是合成音频。</li>\n        <li>高价值转账需要超出聊天与语音的双人/双渠道确认。</li>\n        <li>账户被静默同步（例如关联设备）时，骗子能获得语气与业务上下文。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine对应阅读：AI 语音冒充专题 + 关联设备手册 + 商务号防护。</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"英国","source":"英国 Action Fraud","title":"英国 Action Fraud：伪装好友索要六位激活码，报告数攀升","excerpt":"骗子先控熟人号，再以「验证码发错」为由索码；官方提出 THINK. CALL. 与两步验证建议。","body":"<p>英国反诈机构 Action Fraud 曾警告：收到逾 60 份与 WhatsApp 账号接管相关的报告。骗子控制你通讯录中某人的账号后，先正常寒暄，同时用你的号码触发六位激活码短信，再谎称码发错请你转发。</p>\n      <h2>官方行动建议</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>在 App 内举报并封锁。</li>\n        <li>永远不要分享激活码。</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——异常请求先打电话确认。</li>\n        <li>设置 → 账户 → 两步验证 → 开启。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>尽管警示发布较早，其描述的攻击模板与 2024–2026 年新加坡、爱尔兰、瑞士通报高度同构，说明这是跨年、跨国的「标准作业程序」。</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"英国","source":"英国 Action Fraud","title":"社区与宗教群成目标：先骗注册码，再向群友紧急求援","excerpt":"Action Fraud 称自当年 1 月起收到 268 份相关报告；骗子渗透大型社区/宗教群，以视频通话注册为名索要一次性密码。","body":"<p>另一则 Action Fraud 警示聚焦大型社区、校友、工作与宗教 WhatsApp 群。骗子可能先用伪头像/昵称取得信任，再以「加入群视频通话需要一次性密码」为名索要注册码，从而把受害人 WhatsApp 迁到新设备；得手后开启两步验证锁死原机主，并向群友或亲友紧急借钱。</p>\n      <h2>为何群环境更危险</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>成员彼此有「互助」文化，紧急求助成功率更高。</li>\n        <li>群内昵称与头像容易被模仿。</li>\n        <li>一次接管可触达数十上百人。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>管理员应推动全员开两步验证，并规定：群内任何要码、要钱，必须场外核实。</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"马来西亚","source":"马来西亚通讯及多媒体委员会（MCMC）","title":"MCMC：关注 WhatsApp 被黑/被封投诉，警惕收费「代找回」","excerpt":"委员会区分「账号被接管」与「平台封禁」；提醒勿分享验证码与 PIN，勿相信收费恢复中介，应走官方渠道。","body":"<p>2026 年 9 月 25 日，MCMC 发表媒体声明，称注意到公众对 WhatsApp 账号被入侵、被封锁或无法使用的关切，并已将相关个案转介 Meta 厘清处理。声明特别澄清：账号被不法人士接管后，可能被滥发诈骗/垃圾信息并违反服务条款，进而遭到平台封禁——这是两类相关但不同的问题。</p>\n      <h2>给用户的关键提醒</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>通过 WhatsApp 官方渠道恢复或申诉。</li>\n        <li>不要分享验证码或安全 PIN。</li>\n        <li>开启两步验证，远离可疑链接与不明应用。</li>\n        <li>提防以收费、索要个人信息或验证码为条件的「代找回」服务。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine补充：任何让你「先把验证码给他才能找回」的人，都可能是二次行骗。</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"印度","source":"公开技术/警政访谈报道（如 Indian Express 等）","title":"印度观察：OTP 钓鱼、SIM 换卡、合并通话听码多线并行","excerpt":"报道梳理骗子如何结合社工与电信侧手段接管 WhatsApp，并引用 Meta 建议：永不分享六位码、开启两步验证。","body":"<p>印度主流科技报道汇总多种接管路径：OTP 钓鱼（假客服/假好友要码）、SIM swapping（号卡被过户后接收短信）、短暂物理接触后关联 WhatsApp Web，以及「合并通话」让受害人在不知情中接通验证语音、旁听 OTP。</p>\n      <h2>值得记的红旗</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>未请求却收到 WhatsApp 验证码。</li>\n        <li>好友突然要 OTP。</li>\n        <li>无故被登出。</li>\n        <li>提示号码在新设备注册。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>防护组合</h2>\n      <p>报道与 Meta 回应共同强调：不分享 PIN/验证码、开两步验证、慎合并陌生通话、定期检查关联设备、限制隐私可见范围。对中文读者，这些条目与HoldLine 01–08、17 篇一一对应。</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"国际（安全厂商观察）","source":"ESET 等安全研究文章","title":"安全厂商提醒：关联设备静默同步，手机「看起来正常」也已被看光","excerpt":"最快自检是打开「已关联的设备」；陌生浏览器会话应立即登出。并强调两步验证要绑定恢复邮箱。","body":"<p>ESET 等安全文章指出：许多用户以为「没被踢下线就没被盗」。实际上攻击者可能只挂上 WhatsApp Web/桌面会话，实时阅读聊天。30 秒自检方法：设置 → Linked Devices，不认识的一律 Log out。</p>\n      <h2>其他妥协信号</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>异常登出后又要登录。</li>\n        <li>出现你未发送消息的回复。</li>\n        <li>联系人反馈异常索要。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>恢复邮箱的重要性</h2>\n      <p>文章强调两步验证开启时务必添加恢复邮箱——若别人抢先设 PIN，有无邮箱可能决定你是几分钟找回还是等待多日。这与 WhatsApp 帮助中心的夺回说明一致。</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"新加坡","source":"ScamShield / Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield 图解：WhatsApp 接管四步，以及为何「谁要 OTP 都是诈骗」","excerpt":"官方问答把攻击拆成触发 OTP、熟人索码、踢下线、冒充扩散四步，并给出两步验证与关联设备清理指引。","body":"<p>新加坡 ScamShield 相关问答用极清楚的四步描述接管：1）骗子用你的号登录触发 OTP；2）已被盗的联系人来要你转发；3）你交出后被登出；4）骗子用你的身份重复骗下家。</p>\n      <h2>官方原级提醒</h2>\n      <p>任何分享 WhatsApp OTP 的请求，不论看起来来自谁，都应视为诈骗。OTP 只属于接收者本人；平台与「客服」都不会这样要码。</p>\n      <h2>保护清单</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>永不分享 OTP</li>\n        <li>开启两步验证</li>\n        <li>审查并移除陌生关联设备</li>\n        <li>若被盗，用非 WhatsApp 渠道通知联系人</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"全球（平台方）","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp 官方安全页：两步验证、只用正版、群组控制与举报工具","excerpt":"平台重申多层防护与用户可操作开关；明确假冒客户端会带来严重隐私与安全风险。","body":"<p>WhatsApp 官方安全中心概述其默认隐私、垃圾信息检测、异常接管提示等能力，并给用户可执行清单：开启两步验证、始终使用官方应用、管理群组添加权限、学习识别骗局、善用封锁与举报、必要时重新注册夺回账号。</p>\n      <h2>对「下软件防盗」读者的意义</h2>\n      <p>官方立场非常明确：先把正版客户端与内置开关用好，而不是寻找来路不明的第三方「防盗插件」。HoldLine工具页的排序也遵循这一原则。</p>\n      <h2>举报的价值</h2>\n      <p>举报不一定让你立刻拿回损失，但能帮助平台打击滥用账号，降低你的通讯录继续被精准打击的概率。</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"牙买加","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"牙买加 CIRT：钓鱼与劫持并存，强调登出关联设备并重开两步验证","excerpt":"警报描述恶意链接与账号劫持风险；若怀疑失守，应清理关联设备、重置两步验证，并向 spam@whatsapp.com 等渠道举报钓鱼（以官方当前指引为准）。","body":"<p>牙买加网络安全事件响应团队曾发布面向 WhatsApp 用户的威胁上升警报，指出诈骗信息与账号劫持并行。钓鱼可能伪装成正规机构，诱导点击假登录页或交出验证码；劫持成功后可冒充事主、读取联系人并扩散。</p>\n      <h2>失守后的技术动作（通报归纳）</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>设置 → Linked Devices：登出全部不明设备。</li>\n        <li>重开/重设两步验证。</li>\n        <li>向联系人预警。</li>\n        <li>按官方说明举报钓鱼样本。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>该警报与欧美、亚洲通稿再次对齐：清理关联设备 + 两步验证是国际通用急救包。</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"全球","source":"公开报道援引 Meta 数据","title":"平台治理侧写：Meta 披露打击诈骗广告与关联诈骗中心的 WhatsApp 账号","excerpt":"报道援引：Meta 在 2025 上半年撤销逾 680 万个与诈骗中心相关的 WhatsApp 账号；同时提醒用户端仍需自保。","body":"<p>在香港等地区就科技罪案召开的公开说明中，有报道援引 Meta 数据：2025 上半年撤销了超过 680 万个与诈骗中心相关的 WhatsApp 账号，并在其他平台清理大量诈骗广告。这说明平台侧在持续打击，但无法替代用户端的两步验证与验证码纪律。</p>\n      <h2>阅读这篇的正确姿势</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>不要因为「平台会封号」就放松个人设置。</li>\n        <li>被盗号用来群发的账号，可能很快被封，但你的亲友可能在被封前已经完成转账。</li>\n        <li>个人能做的最快动作，仍是：不给码、开两步、清设备、要钱先核实。</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>国际新闻合集的结论：从加勒比到西欧、从东南亚到东亚，话术细节不同，结构高度相似。把HoldLine 30 篇长文当成「操作手册」，把本页当成「为什么现在就要做」的证据墙。</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"国际动态：WhatsApp 被盗与账号接管最新观察","lead":"以下 20 篇为HoldLine根据各国警方、网络安全机构与公开报道整理的专题稿，聚焦近年 WhatsApp 账号被接管、骗码、假投票与冒充借钱等态势。文中数据与日期来自公开通稿；界面与损失数字可能随后续统计调整，请以原文机构发布为准。"},"toolsIntro":{"title":"推荐下载：降低 WhatsApp 被盗风险的软件与工具","lead":"没有「一键防盗神器」。真正有效的是：官方 WhatsApp、系统安全能力、正规防诈 App，再加上两步验证与验证码纪律。本页只推荐可从官方渠道获取的工具，并写清它能做什么、不能做什么。"},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"必装","name":"WhatsApp 官方客户端","platforms":"iOS · Android","summary":"唯一应安装的即时通讯本体。改装版、增强版、网盘 APK 都是高风险。","why":["官方客户端才会持续获得安全更新、诈骗提示与账户恢复能力。","非官方包可能内置后门，端到端加密也救不了「端点已沦陷」。","警方与 Meta 反复提醒：只用官方应用商店中的 WhatsApp。"],"steps":["iPhone：仅从 App Store 搜索 WhatsApp Messenger 安装/更新。","Android：优先 Google Play；部分地区可用手机厂商官方应用商店中的正式版。","安装后立刻开启两步验证，并检查「已关联的设备」。"],"stepsCaption":"从官方商店安装，并立刻开启两步验证","caution":"不要从群文件、网盘、短链下载所谓 Plus / GB / 防撤回版。","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp 安全中心","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play · WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"推荐","name":"WhatsApp 桌面版 / 网页版（官方）","platforms":"Windows · macOS · 浏览器","summary":"方便电脑回复，但必须从官方入口关联；公用电脑用完立刻在手机端登出。","why":["官方桌面端比随便搜索到的「Web 登录页」安全得多。","关联设备是静默偷窥的高发入口，正规客户端便于你主动管理会话。"],"steps":["在手机 WhatsApp → 已关联的设备 → 关联新设备。","电脑打开官方桌面应用，或浏览器手动输入官方网页地址后扫码。","公用设备不要勾选保持登录；用完回手机踢掉会话。"],"stepsCaption":"手机关联设备 → 官方扫码 → 用完登出","caution":"绝不扫描聊天里或陌生网站出示的「登录/投票」二维码。","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp 桌面下载（官网）","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"必开","name":"WhatsApp 内置安全功能（免费）","platforms":"App 内设置","summary":"两步验证、诈骗提示（Scam Alert）、应用锁、隐私限制——不需要另装第三方「防盗插件」。","why":["两步验证直接提高接管成本，是各国警方通稿里的标准建议。","Scam Alert 可在设备侧提示可疑会话（以你版本是否提供为准）。","应用锁降低「手机已解锁被顺手打开」的风险。"],"steps":["设置 → 账户 → 两步验证 → 开启，并绑定可登录的邮箱。","设置中查找诈骗提示 / Scam Alert（若有）并打开。","开启应用锁（指纹/面容）；隐私里限制陌生人拉群。","每周检查一次「已关联的设备」。"],"stepsCaption":"设置账户两步验证，绑定恢复邮箱","caution":"内置功能不能阻止你主动把验证码发给骗子——纪律仍是第一防线。","links":[{"label":"如何防范可疑消息（帮助中心）","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"地区推荐","name":"ScamShield（新加坡防诈套件）","platforms":"iOS · Android","summary":"新加坡政府相关防诈工具：可协助拦截可疑来电/短信，并查验可疑信息。当地用户强烈建议安装。","why":["新加坡警方通报 WhatsApp 盗号与冒充借钱案时，常直接建议安装 ScamShield。","可过滤部分诈骗短信与来电，降低「骗码电话/钓鱼短信」到达率。","官网提供查证渠道与预警信息。"],"steps":["只从官方短链或 App Store / Google Play 安装。","按指引开启通话/短信过滤相关权限。","遇到可疑请求，可结合其查证功能与热线渠道核实。"],"stepsCaption":"官方商店下载并开启过滤权限","caution":"非新加坡号码/环境可能不适用；不要下载仿冒「ScamShield」山寨包。","links":[{"label":"ScamShield 官网说明","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS 下载（官方跳转）","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android 下载（官方跳转）","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"地区推荐","name":"Scameter / Scameter+（香港防骗视伏器）","platforms":"网页 · App（以警方公布为准）","summary":"香港警方推广的查骗工具，可用于查证可疑平台、账户等。香港用户建议配合使用。","why":["香港「网上账户骑劫」案件中 WhatsApp 占比极高，警方持续呼吁使用官方防骗工具。","转账或点击前先查证，能减少盗号后的二次金钱损失。"],"steps":["通过香港警方 CyberDefender / 官方公布渠道进入 Scameter。","对陌生收款账户、可疑网站先查再操作。","同时在 WhatsApp 开启两步验证并清查关联设备。"],"stepsCaption":"官方入口查证可疑链接或账户","caution":"查证工具不能替代「不给验证码」；盗号本身仍要靠账户设置与习惯。","links":[{"label":"香港政府相关防骗资讯入口","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"推荐","name":"Google Play 保护机制 / 系统安全更新","platforms":"Android · iOS","summary":"让系统与应用商店替你挡住大量恶意安装包，减少木马偷短信、偷屏幕的风险。","why":["部分盗号会配合恶意 App 读取通知或辅助操作。","及时系统补丁能缩小已知漏洞窗口。"],"steps":["Android：开启 Google Play 保护机制；只允许官方商店安装。","iOS/Android：打开自动更新，定期安装系统补丁。","拒绝「允许未知来源安装」的临时诱惑。"],"stepsCaption":"开启系统保护并保持更新","caution":"系统防护挡不住社工骗码；它是设备层，不是话术层。","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect 说明","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"推荐","name":"密码管理器（保管两步验证 PIN）","platforms":"跨平台","summary":"用来安全保存 WhatsApp 两步验证 PIN 与邮箱密码，避免设成弱口令或写在聊天里。","why":["许多人因「怕忘记」而设 123456，或把 PIN 发到自己另一个聊天备份——反而更危险。","正规密码管理器可生成并加密保存高强度口令。"],"steps":["从官方商店安装 Bitwarden、1Password、或系统自带钥匙串/密码等可信产品。","为 WhatsApp 两步验证 PIN 单独建一条记录。","密码管理器本身开启强主密码与生物识别。"],"stepsCaption":"用密码库安全保存 PIN","caution":"不要用未知「PIN 保管 App」；更不要把 PIN 存进未加密备忘录云同步到到处。","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden 官网","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"可选","name":"正规手机安全软件（防木马，不防骗术）","platforms":"Android 为主","summary":"若你经常侧载应用或设备环境复杂，可选知名厂商安全套件；它主要防恶意软件，不是防熟人要码。","why":["可辅助发现可疑应用、钓鱼站点（能力因产品而异）。","对「短信窃取木马」一类威胁有一定价值。"],"steps":["只从 Google Play / App Store 安装知名产品（如 Malwarebytes、Bitdefender 等，按你地区可用性选择）。","安装后做一次全盘扫描，复查短信/无障碍权限异常应用。","不要同时堆叠多个互相冲突的安全套件。"],"stepsCaption":"官方商店安装后全盘扫描","caution":"任何杀毒都无法阻止你主动交出验证码；也绝不下载「WhatsApp 专杀盗号」无名 APK。","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes 官网","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["名称含「WhatsApp 防盗 / 找回大师 / 破解监视」却要求关闭 Play 保护的安装包","要求你把验证码发给「客服机器人」才能开通防护的工具","收费「代找回账号」并索要远程控制或注册码的服务","来自网盘、Telegram 频道、短视频评论区的神秘 APK"]},"zh-TW":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"ja":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"ko":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"es":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"fr":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"de":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"pt-BR":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"pt-PT":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"it":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"ru":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"ar":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"hi":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"id":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"th":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"vi":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"tr":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"pl":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"nl":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"sv":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"da":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"fi":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"no":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"cs":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"el":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"he":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"uk":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"ro":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"hu":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"bn":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"tl":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"sw":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"fa":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]},"ur":{"articles":{"01-verification-code":{"category":"core rules","title":"The verification code will never be passed on: the most common and deadly entrance to account hacking","excerpt":"The six-digit registration code is equal to the account key. Requesting codes from acquaintances, prompting codes from fake customer service, and filling in codes at fishing sites—they are all essentially the same knife. This article explains the art of speech, principles and refusal of speech in one go.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>If you could only remember one WhatsApp anti-theft rule, remember this one:<strong>Never tell anyone the verification code, and don't enter it on any \"official-looking\" webpage.</strong>When police and cybersecurity agencies in many places around the world report WhatsApp account takeover cases, they almost always list \"scamming six-digit verification codes\" at the top of the list. The reason is simple - WhatsApp uses the mobile phone number as the account identity, and the registration code in the text message or voice message is the key to \"prove that you are the owner\" on the new device.</p>\n      <p>Many people mistakenly believe that hacking accounts must require advanced hacking skills. In reality, the vast majority of successful cases rely on<strong>social engineering</strong>: Scammers use trust, courtesy, a sense of urgency, and a \"do me a favor\" mentality to get you to hand over the keys yourself. Once the verification code is obtained, the other party can complete the registration on their mobile phone, kick you offline, and then pretend to be you to deceive the next person.</p>\n\n      <h2>What exactly is the verification code?</h2>\n      <p>When you or anyone tries to log in/register for WhatsApp using a mobile number, a registration code, usually six digits, is sent to the number (which may also be announced via a voice call). Entering this string of numbers correctly is equivalent to telling WhatsApp: \"The person who holds this SIM and can receive the text message is the account owner.\"</p>\n      <p>Therefore, it belongs to the same type of sensitive information as bank card dynamic passwords and email two-step verification codes. The difference is: once WhatsApp's verification code is abused, the loss is often not just \"an App\", but your entire address book, group chat trust relationships, and the financial risk of relatives and friends who may be defrauded again.</p>\n\n      <h2>What are the most common words used by scammers?</h2>\n      <p>The words will change, but the structure rarely changes. The following sentences have appeared repeatedly in police information bulletins in many countries in recent years:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"The verification code was sent to your mobile phone by mistake. Please help me get back those six numbers.\" - This is a serial scam that is common after an acquaintance's account has been stolen.</li>\n        <li>\"I am WhatsApp/Meta/Customer Service, your account is abnormal, please send me the verification code to unblock it.\" - Officials will not ask for codes like this.</li>\n        <li>\"Bank risk control/courier loss/tax refund requires you to provide WhatsApp verification code for verification.\" - Cross-channel impersonation.</li>\n        <li>\"Voting, lottery, and questionnaire only take 10 seconds\" - the fake site first collects your mobile phone number, and then the SMS code you just received.</li>\n        <li>\"I helped your friend sign up for the lottery, and he sent you the verification code. Just forward it to me.\" - Involving you in other people's scam narratives.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>There is only one judgment standard:</strong>As long as the other party's goal is to \"get the WhatsApp registration code that just appeared on your phone,\" no matter how touching or urgent the story is, or how familiar the other party's avatar is, it will be treated as a stolen account.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why “asking for code for acquaintances” is particularly dangerous</h2>\n      <p>Scammers rarely use unknown numbers to deceive you right away. A more efficient approach is to capture A first, and then use A's chat window to find B. When B sees a familiar name and avatar, his defenses will immediately drop. After B hands over the verification code, the scammer gets B and then deceives C - forming<strong>Address Book Infectious Diseases</strong>.</p>\n      <p>So when you receive a verification code from a \"friend\", the correct response is not \"He is usually fine, so he should be fine\", but:<strong>His number may no longer be in his possession.</strong>Please use the phone to call the original number in your address book instead, or confirm in person. Do not use the \"new number\" temporarily sent by the other party to call back as the only means of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>Correct rejection skills (can be copied directly)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I will not send the verification code to anyone, including relatives. If you are really who you are, call me.\"</li>\n        <li>\"WhatsApp officially doesn't ask for verification codes. I treat this as a scam.\"</li>\n        <li>\"I can't assist in transcoding. You can just log in to your account again.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>You can block or report them after you finish speaking, without further explanation. Scammers rely on dragging you into long conversations to make you feel embarrassed to say no.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you have already sent the code</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately open WhatsApp on this machine, try to re-verify the login with your own number, and kick the other party offline.</li>\n        <li>After successful login, check \"Associated Devices\" and log out of all unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Reset your 2-step verification PIN and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Immediately notify relatives and friends: Ignore any borrowing money, code request, or voting links sent by \"you\".</li>\n        <li>If a transfer loss has occurred, contact your bank and consider calling the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification code is not \"just any number\", but account sovereignty. HoldLine puts this in the first article because it blocks the largest attack surface, is the lowest cost, and is the easiest to teach to family members. Develop \"don't give it to anyone who wants it\" into a muscle reaction, and then all the advanced settings will really work.</p>"},"02-two-step-verification":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Two-Step Verification: PINs, Recovery Emails, and Common Pitfalls","excerpt":"After turning on two-step verification, the SMS code alone is often not enough. This article explains step-by-step how to open it, how to set a PIN, why you can’t save your email address, and what to do if you are preempted to set a PIN.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is the basic protection that WhatsApp officials repeatedly recommend. Its core idea is simple: even if the scammer temporarily gets your SMS verification code, you will still need an additional PIN (or password for subsequent upgrades) when the new device is registered. Without a second door, takeover costs will rise significantly.</p>\n      <p>Many users \"heard that they wanted to open it\" but kept putting it off, or opened it but skipped the mailbox. As a result, when something really happened, it was either impossible to prevent, or the recovery process was delayed for several days. This article writes down the complete approach and pitfalls at once.</p>\n\n      <h2>What can it protect against and what can’t it protect against?</h2>\n      <p><strong>Can significantly enhance:</strong>The other party tries to use your number to re-register on another mobile phone; the remediation window after you briefly leak the SMS code; and the success rate of \"only relying on deceptive codes\" is reduced.</p>\n      <p><strong>Cannot be solved alone:</strong>You have told the other party your PIN; a Trojan horse peeked at the screen of your phone in real time; you actively scanned the other party's associated QR code (the Trojan was hooked on the web page); you lent your unlocked phone to others to operate at will.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, two-step verification is a \"necessary but not sufficient\" condition: it must be turned on, but it must also cooperate with verification code discipline and related equipment inspections.</p>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp and enter<strong>set up</strong>.</li>\n        <li>point<strong>Account</strong>→<strong>two-step verification</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Select Enable and follow the prompts to create a 6-digit PIN (some regions/versions may prompt you to set a stronger password).</li>\n        <li>Confirm PIN again.</li>\n        <li>Add to<strong>An email address that you can log in to permanently</strong>as a recovery method and complete email verification (if applicable).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The menu copy of different mobile phone brands may be slightly different (for example, entering settings from the avatar), but the logic of \"Account → Two-step verification\" is usually stable. If you can't find it, use the in-app search for the \"two-step\" keyword.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to set PIN</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not use birthdays, anniversaries, license plates, mobile phone numbers, or ID fragments.</li>\n        <li>Do not use patterns such as 123456, 000000, 888888, and 112233.</li>\n        <li>Do not use the same WeChat/Alipay/bank card/email password or simply change it.</li>\n        <li>It is recommended to write it in a password manager, or write it in an offline location where only you can see it; do not send it to the group for \"backup\".</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal of a PIN is not to be \"so easy to remember that you don't have to write it down\", but rather \"that others can't guess it and can't cheat it.\" You can keep it if you can’t remember it, but don’t set a weak password because of this.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Why recovery email is almost required</h2>\n      <p>After turning on two-step verification, if you forget your PIN, the official process often relies on your registered email address to assist in resetting it; if there is no available email address, you may need to wait for a while (commonly described in public information as up to about seven days) before you can continue without a PIN. What's worse is that the hacker turns on the two-step verification first and binds their email, so you will be even more passive when you take it back.</p>\n      <p>Therefore, the email address must satisfy:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>It is one that you use for a long time and can receive mail.</li>\n        <li>The email itself also has a strong password and two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not use an email address that has been abandoned, is about to be cleared by the company, or is shared with others.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What should I do after opening it?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check \"Associated Devices\" immediately and clear unknown sessions.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: The PIN for two-step verification cannot be shared with “customer service” either.</li>\n        <li>Every few months or after switching devices, make sure two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>If you ever suspect a leak, just turn it off and on again to get a new PIN.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Asked for a \"PIN I didn't set up\"</h2>\n      <p>This usually means: Someone took control of your account and enabled two-step verification. Please complete the SMS registration code verification first (this step can often kick the other party's main session offline), and then follow the interface prompts to process the PIN. For detailed emergency information, please see the \"Unfamiliar PIN\" topic on this site. The core principles are:<strong>Don’t give up on re-verification just because your PIN is stuck; don’t send SMS codes to people claiming to help you “crack your PIN.”</strong></p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Two-step verification takes less than two minutes, but it can turn \"you can steal an account by cheating once with a text message code\" into \"you have to go through the door you set again.\" Together with recovery email and verification code discipline, it is one of the \"must be done today\" items recommended by HoldLine.</p>"},"03-linked-devices":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"The Complete Guide to Connected Devices: How to Spot Silent Peeping","excerpt":"Just because a mobile phone can still chat normally does not mean that there is no second pair of eyes. Once the web version/PC version is connected, the other party can synchronize in real time. Teach you how to check, kick, and avoid getting hit again.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>There is a kind of account hacking that is not dramatic: you are not suddenly logged out, and the chat is still there, until one day a relative or friend says, \"You just asked me for a verification code,\" or you see a browser session in the settings that you never recognize. this is<strong>Silent access of associated devices</strong>——The attacker does not necessarily take away the main session of the phone, but allows WhatsApp Web/Desktop to hang on the side to synchronize messages.</p>\n      <p>For scammers, this method is extremely cost-effective: the later you find out, the more group chats they can read, the more materials they can collect for money-loaning techniques, and even directly use your tone of voice to operate in the conversation.</p>\n\n      <h2>A brief description of the associated device mechanism</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp allows you to associate computers, tablets, browsers, etc. as companion devices in addition to the main client on your phone. The formal usage is: Open \"Associated Devices\" → \"Associate New Device\" on your mobile phone, and then scan the QR code displayed on the official website or desktop application.</p>\n      <p>The risk point is that this \"code scanning authorization\" process itself is a legal function. All the liar has to do is to<strong>The QR code they control</strong>Put it on a fake website, fake voting page, or scan the code directly on your unlocked phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to check (please do it now)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open WhatsApp →<strong>set up</strong>→<strong>Linked devices</strong>(Android sometimes has the More menu in the upper right corner).</li>\n        <li>Look at each entry in the list: device type, approximate active time, and whether the name looks familiar.</li>\n        <li>For any \"I don't remember linking\" items: Click through →<strong>Exit/Logout</strong>.</li>\n        <li>If the list is very long and you have logged in from a public computer, it is recommended that all unfamiliar users log out and reconnect with the official website if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make it a habit: Take 30 seconds to scan your list one day a week (like Sunday night). After lending your phone, clicking on a suspicious link, or using the hotel computer, check again immediately.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>List of high-risk scenarios</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scanned the \"Verify/Vote/Receive Prizes\" QR code sent in the chat.</li>\n        <li>I saw the QR code on an unofficial website and followed the instructions to scan it using WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Give your unlocked phone to someone else to \"help me look at something\" or \"help me connect to the web version\".</li>\n        <li>I checked \"Stay logged in\" on the public computer at the Internet cafe/exhibition, but forgot to log out afterwards.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp Web sessions in the browser were shared after the computer was infected.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Processing sequence after discovering a strange device</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of this device (and all in doubt) immediately.</li>\n        <li>Check whether you can still control the main mobile phone session; if you have been pushed offline, follow the retrieval process to re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Review recently clicked links and scanned codes to avoid repeated scams.</li>\n        <li>Notify contacts who have recently discussed sensitive topics to be alert.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Principles for safe use of PC version</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Only obtain desktop applications from official channels, or enter the official web address into your browser yourself.</li>\n        <li>The associated actions are only initiated from within the mobile app and will not \"force\" you to scan from unfamiliar pages.</li>\n        <li>Don’t stay logged in on public devices; when you’re done, go back to the mobile phone and kick it out.</li>\n        <li>It is better to log out of computer sessions that have not been used for a long time.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>\"Linked Devices\" is a list of backdoors to your account. It is not an advanced function page, but a security inspection page. By making checking into a habit, many \"I don't know how to get caught\" cases can actually be intercepted in the first week.</p>"},"04-fake-vote-lottery":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake voting, fake lottery, fake support: Don’t trust it even if it is forwarded by acquaintances","excerpt":"\"Vote for my daughter\" messages often come from compromised accounts. Fake websites asking for SMS codes or inducing scans of related codes are a rapidly growing acceptance method in recent years.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Kindness is the most easily weaponized quality in social media. Scammers are very clear: telling you directly \"Give me the verification code\" will trigger an alarm; but if you change it to \"Vote for the children, it only takes ten seconds\", many people will click in. Cyber ​​security agencies in Europe and other places have publicly warned: After hackers control the accounts of acquaintances, they send mass voting/competition support requests, and then direct the traffic to counterfeit websites.</p>\n\n      <h2>How a typical attack unfolds</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>A scammer has taken control of your friend’s WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>You receive a private message or group message from a friend: \"Please vote, the deadline is today!\" with a link.</li>\n        <li>The webpage is designed to look like a school, dance competition, or charity voting, requiring you to enter your mobile phone number.</li>\n        <li>You receive a WhatsApp/SMS verification code on your phone - actually a registration code.</li>\n        <li>You fill in the code into the web page, and the other party immediately completes the takeover on another device; or</li>\n        <li>The web page displays a QR code and tells you to \"scan the code with WhatsApp to complete your vote\", which actually completes the device association.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The first one will make you lose your main account; the second one may allow you to be peeped simultaneously while \"your phone is still available\". Both are dangerous.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why previews and pages look so real</h2>\n      <p>Modern fishing will prepare a decent logo, countdown timer, candidate photos, and even do multiple languages. Link preview cards can also be made to look like regular sites. You can’t judge authenticity by “good-looking or not”;<strong>what does it ask you to do</strong>:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Want WhatsApp/SMS verification code? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Do you want you to scan the QR code with a \"linked device\"? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Requiring you to download an unknown APK to vote? High risk.</li>\n        <li>Want bank card photos and OTP? It has been upgraded from account theft to fraud.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard actions when receiving a vote request</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Let’s first assume that the other party’s account may have been stolen.</li>\n        <li>Ask on the phone or in person: \"Are you canvassing for votes? Have you sent a link?\"</li>\n        <li>Before confirming, do not click on the link, do not fill in the code, and do not scan the code.</li>\n        <li>If the other party denies it, remind them to check the associated device and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Report the original message to avoid spreading it in the group.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Real public voting rarely requires you to provide your WhatsApp registration code. Platforms that require login should also use the platform's own account system instead of the \"universal mobile phone verification code universal box\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have already clicked or filled in</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After filling in the SMS code: Immediately re-verify your WhatsApp number and regain it, then clear the device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Scan the QR code: Immediately go to \"Associated Devices\" to kick someone.</li>\n        <li>Installation packages have been downloaded: Disable unknown apps, consider anti-virus/reinstall evaluation, and check SMS and accessibility permissions.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to have a unified voice at home and in the company</h2>\n      <p>It is not enough for one person to be vigilant. Fake voting links are most likely to be forwarded twice in family groups, classmate groups, and company groups. Each forwarding is \"whitewashing\" the source of the scammers. It is recommended to stick a short rule at the top of the fixed group: \"Any link that requires a WhatsApp verification code or scan the code to vote, call first to verify; do not click before verification.\" The shorter the rule, the easier it is to be followed.</p>\n      <p>If you are a group administrator, you should delete such messages immediately and remind members to check the associated devices. If the sender is an acquaintance, give priority to using other channels to remind the other party that \"your number may have been stolen\" instead of just replying \"this is a scam\" in the original chat - because the person on the other end may not be watching at all.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to distinguish it from \"real voting\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Legitimate events will almost never ask you for your WhatsApp registration code.</li>\n        <li>If you need to log in for formal activities, you usually use your own account system instead of the universal \"SMS code universal verification\".</li>\n        <li>Formal activities will not induce you to use \"associated devices\" to scan unknown QR codes.</li>\n        <li>Really urgent public welfare/campus voting can also withstand you taking a minute to call and ask yourself.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Shift the burden of \"distinguishing authenticity from fake\" from \"seeing whether the page is beautiful\" to \"whether it has exceeded its authority to ask for WhatsApp keys.\" If you think clearly about this step, the vast majority of fake polling stations will reveal their true colors.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The fake voting scam is a combination of \"acquaintance + goodwill + short-term operation\". Change the rule to:<strong>All votes that require QR codes or scan codes must be verified first and disbelieved by default.</strong>, you can avoid a large class of account hacking paths that are currently very active. Good intentions are worth keeping, but the keys should not be handed over.</p>"},"05-chain-takeover":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Serial deception codes of acquaintances: how the address book turns into a chain of transmission","excerpt":"If one account is lost, the entire address book is often destroyed along with it. Dismantle the transmission mechanism of serial takeovers and how to cut off the second link.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Viewed in isolation, \"sending the verification code to a friend\" looks like an oversight; from an attacker's perspective, it is a replicable growth strategy. The patterns reported by the police in Singapore, Malaysia and other places are very similar: the scammer first uses phishing or deceptive codes to get the first number → the victim is squeezed offline → the scammer uses this identity to ask for the \"incorrect verification code\" from the entire address book → the next victim is tricked → the chain continues. The entire process often completes more than a dozen layers of diffusion within two hours, because the sender at each layer is someone you \"know and trust.\"</p>\n      <p>Serial deceptive codes are also called \"takeover chains\" or \"address book epidemics.\" It does not rely on one-time mass advertising, but uses the trust edge of the human network to continuously jump. For the defender, this means:<strong>Your safety depends not only on your own discipline, but also on whether there are weak links in your address book</strong>——But more importantly, when you are in the \"being coded\" link, you have the opportunity to become the end of the entire chain.</p>\n\n      <h2>every role in the chain</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Entrance 0:</strong>The first people to be scammed, phished, or scanned fake voting codes, or whose associated devices were silently hung up. They may not immediately notice that it has been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Propagation node:</strong>Each newly taken over account automatically becomes the next wave of \"trusted senders\". The avatar, nickname, and recent chat context are all real—it’s just that the operator has changed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Filter nodes:</strong>Scammers will give priority to privately chatting with contacts, family members, and colleagues they have chatted with recently, and the success rate is much higher than that of cold sales.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monetization node:</strong>After accumulating enough accounts, you start to borrow money, ask for bank card screenshots, pay OTP, attract fake investment groups, and convert \"trust assets\" into cash.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The most critical realization for you is that when you become \"the one being coded,\" you are in the best position to cut the chain. As soon as you refuse, this branch will be broken; as soon as you compromise, your address book will become someone else's ammunition depot.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why do typical words always deceive people?</h2>\n      <p>The sentence \"The verification code was sent in error, read it for me\" is effective because it satisfies three psychological buttons at the same time:<strong>Acquaintances are trustworthy</strong>(The avatar has not changed),<strong>mutual aid obligations</strong>(Do me a small favor),<strong>Extremely simple operation</strong>(Copy six numbers). Scammers will also add \"Don't tell others\" and \"I'm in a meeting so it's not convenient for me to make a phone call\" to prevent you from using the most effective verification method - voice calls.</p>\n      <p>In a workplace environment, variations include \"Personnel system requires verification code\" and \"Company VPN registration\"; in a home environment, variations include \"Hospital registration\" and \"School registration\". The story has changed skin, but the core remains the same: you are asked to hand over the WhatsApp registration code you just received on your phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal signal (even if the avatar is familiar)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Suddenly discussing verification codes, OTP, bank card photos, and the front and back of ID cards.</li>\n        <li>The tone is overly eager and prevents you from making phone calls or video calls.</li>\n        <li>Use very uncharacteristic phrasing late at night/at work (too formal or too rushed).</li>\n        <li>Just when I was about to finish coding, I started borrowing money, recruiting groups, and sending investment links.</li>\n        <li>At the same time, multiple friends will tell you \"so-and-so is asking for my code\" at the same time.</li>\n        <li>The other party says \"the number has been changed\" but is unwilling to answer your call back using the old number.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House level rules:</strong>Anyone who asks for a verification code in WhatsApp will by default assume that \"the other party's number may have been stolen.\" They must call to verify first and never give the code first. This rule applies equally to children, parents, and bosses.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Household and company practices that cut the chain</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Build consensus: CAPTCHA request = default scam alert, not an “embarrassed to say no” social occasion.</li>\n        <li>Use SMS, email or family group (not WhatsApp) to send a group reminder: \"Anyone who uses WhatsApp to ask for a password will be treated as if their account has been stolen.\"</li>\n        <li>The company's IT or administration prepares a fixed announcement template, pins the abnormal contact person to the top immediately, and designates a person on duty to answer the verification call.</li>\n        <li>After the victim is recaptured, he must use it in the relevant group<strong>Other verified channels</strong>The second statement \"has been recaptured, and the previous news is invalid.\"</li>\n        <li>Regular family/team drills: pretend to be cheaters on each other and practice three rejection lines.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If You Were Stolen: Stop Loss Timeline</h2>\n      <p>In addition to re-verifying the number according to the \"Reclaim Process\" on this site, please put \"Notify Contacts\" at the same priority as \"Clear Associated Devices\". Suggested order:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Reclaim your main session and turn on or reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Immediately send a group text message to core contacts: \"My WhatsApp has just been stolen. Anyone who asks for codes, borrows money, or voting links does not belong to me.\"</li>\n        <li>Ask a trusted relative or friend to use other channels to make the announcement on your behalf within your family/colleague group.</li>\n        <li>Look back at what you posted in the 48 hours before the theft and determine where the chain might extend.</li>\n        <li>If a relative or friend has transferred money, help them contact the bank and keep the evidence. If necessary, report the case through local police channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>A common mistake is: thinking \"it's over\" after taking it back, but not warning the outside world, causing the scammer to continue to borrow money with residual trust before you go online again.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"The other party is a relative, so it doesn't matter if you give it to me.\" - Relatives' numbers can also be stolen, and scammers specialize in family links.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"It's safe if I only give you the code but not the PIN.\" - The registration code is enough to complete the takeover; the PIN is the second door, and the key cannot be given first.</li>\n        <li>Misunderstanding: \"I can just withdraw the message quickly.\" - The other party's automated script can complete the registration before you react.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Serial deception is not an \"accidental interpersonal error\", but a structured and scalable communication attack. Training everyone in your address book to be a \"denial point\" is more important than just strengthening your own phone - because attacks will always come in through the loosest link. Remember: if you refuse to ask for a code once, you may have protected the numbers and money of more than a dozen contacts behind you at the same time.</p>"},"06-screen-share-scam":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Screen sharing account theft: Air code capture during video calls","excerpt":"The fake official or banker asks to share your screen and then has a verification code appear on your screen. You didn't \"send\" the number, but the code has already been read.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traditional scam codes require you to type out the numbers and send them to the other party; screen sharing scams omit this step. Scammers use WhatsApp videos, Zoom, Teams and even the system's own \"screen sharing\" function to establish a \"sense of officialdom\", and then ask you to share your entire phone screen immediately on the grounds of account unfreezing, tax verification, anti-money laundering inspections, and express delivery claims. Then they instruct you to open a text message, open WhatsApp and log in again, or click on the bank app - the six-digit registration code, payment dynamic password, and even the two-step verification interface will all appear on the other party's screen.</p>\n      <p>Versions disguised as law enforcement agencies, tax bureaus, central bank customer service, and bank risk control have appeared in police reports from many countries. For users who are not familiar with digital business, this process looks \"very professional\": there are videos, screenshots of work badges, terminology, and countdown pressure. You may feel that you \"didn't send any verification code\" throughout the whole process, but the other party has already copied the numbers with their eyes.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why screen sharing is extra dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The lock screen notification banner may display the verification code directly in the share without you having to click on the text message.</li>\n        <li>The other party can guide you to enter \"Associated Devices\" to scan the QR code, turn off two-step verification, and modify your email address.</li>\n        <li>If you share your computer desktop at the same time, passwords and session cookies saved in the browser may also be exposed.</li>\n        <li>At the end of the scam, the scam often requires the installation of a \"remote assistance\" app, which is actually a long-term control channel.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Common oppression rhetoric in videos</h2>\n      <p>\"If you don't share, your account will be frozen.\" \"If you don't cooperate, you'll be suspected of breaking the law.\" \"IT is remotely rescuing your account.\" These are all designed to allow you to make a decision within 30 seconds. Formal financial institutions and government departments usually do not complete verification in private WhatsApp videos; when they need to verify identity, they should go through you<strong>Find it yourself</strong>The official App, official website or outlet channels, please refer to the official help center instructions and local formal channels for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>Defense List (by priority)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Any video call claiming to be official, bank, or platform customer service requires screen sharing: hang up by default.</li>\n        <li>After hanging up, use the official phone number you saved or the customer service portal in the app to call back instead of the number given in the chat.</li>\n        <li>If screen sharing is necessary due to work arrangements (such as remote IT troubleshooting), exit banking and instant messaging apps first, turn off lock screen notification previews, and do not click on any verification code-related interfaces during screen sharing.</li>\n        <li>Teach the elders: \"Share screen\" and computer screencasting in WeChat/FaceTime are as dangerous to verification codes as \"sending codes\".</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>House rules suggestions:</strong>Write \"video call + screen sharing + mobile phone operation\" in red. Elders only need to remember: hang up when they see this group, and let their children take care of the affairs.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If it has been shared and may be leaked</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately stop using the share and hang up all remote sessions.</li>\n        <li>Re-verify the WhatsApp number locally and check the \"associated devices\".</li>\n        <li>Reset two-step verification PIN and recovery email; change bank and payment account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Uninstall suspicious remote control applications and ask professionals to do a physical examination of the device if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends to be alert to unusual messages sent by \"you\" recently.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>\"I only shared half of the screen and it was fine\" - the notification bar verification code is still visible.</li>\n        <li>\"The other party is credible if he is wearing a uniform\" - the image in the video can be faked.</li>\n        <li>\"It's safe if I hang up\" - If the code has been taken away, you must take it back immediately and reset the access control.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Air-to-air code retrieval proves: Security not only depends on \"whether you are willing to type out the code\", but also depends on \"who is looking at your screen.\" Think of screen sharing as a high-privilege operation rather than an ancillary function of video chat; for verification codes, eyes are as much a leakage channel as fingers.</p>"},"07-call-merge-otp":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Merge calls and listen to verification codes: Don’t merge strangers into your calls","excerpt":"Scammers trick you into merging three-way calls and letting the line receive a WhatsApp voice verification code - you don't forward the digits, but the code is listened to.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>Phone merging (three-way calls, conference calls) is a daily function, but it has been transformed into a tool for stealing numbers. The techniques used in public reports are generally: Calls from strangers or semi-strangers claiming to be couriers, medical insurance, or school academic affairs offices, saying that they need confirmation from you and the \"person in charge\"; or pretending that your relatives or friends are in an emergency, asking you to transfer the call to a \"lawyer\" or \"police officer.\" When you follow the instructions to dial a number and click \"Merge Calls\", the other end of the line is often a WhatsApp call that has just been initiated.<strong>Voice verification call</strong>, automatically broadcast the six-digit registration code, and the scammer listens online throughout the process.</p>\n      <p>This type of scam is especially common among elderly users, because \"put him through\" sounds like ordinary phone etiquette, and does not involve typing and sending codes in the chat, so the psychological defense line is lower.</p>\n\n      <h2>How is it different from a scam SMS code?</h2>\n      <p>The SMS code scam relies on you \"seeing and forwarding\"; the merged call scam relies on you \"hearing but not knowing\". Sometimes scammers will also ask you to receive a text message code at the same time, and then ask you to \"read it to customer service\" on the combined line. In essence, you still hand over the registration code to a third party. You may not have entered anything into WhatsApp during the whole process, and you may even think that you are just making a friendly transfer.</p>\n\n      <h2>The two-part structure of a scam script</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>bedding:</strong>Create an emergency situation (involvement in a case, refund, package being detained) so that you are willing to cooperate with the phone.</li>\n        <li><strong>fuse:</strong>Give you an \"official hotline\" number to call first and then merge it; this number triggers WhatsApp voice verification or bank OTP voice broadcast.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Be wary of any call asking you to \"dial a fixed number first and then merge it together,\" no matter who the caller claims to be.</p>\n\n      <h2>Protection rules</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Do not merge calls with unknown calls; do not help strangers \"redirect to the authorities.\"</li>\n        <li>Urgent matters for relatives and friends: hang up the suspicious line and call the old number in your address book to verify.</li>\n        <li>The WhatsApp voice verification code is as confidential as the SMS code and cannot be spoken to others during any call.</li>\n        <li>If the other party says \"Don't hang up, just merge,\" it's almost always a high-risk sign.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Operator customer service, banks, and WhatsApp officials will not let you \"listen to the verification code to complete the verification\" through phone merging. If you encounter such statements, please refer to the official help center instructions and do not continue the call.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>If you have merged suspicious calls</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Immediately check whether WhatsApp is still online and whether you have received the \"Already registered on another device\" prompt.</li>\n        <li>If you can still log in: clear the associated device and reset the two-step verification; if you are kicked offline: follow the recapture process to re-verify.</li>\n        <li>Check with the operator whether call forwarding and unconditional forwarding have been enabled maliciously.</li>\n        <li>Notify recent contacts: Please be alert if you receive unusual messages from \"you\".</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Do not treat call merging as an operation without security implications. For CAPTCHA scenarios, ears can be just as leaky as eyes. Rejecting unfamiliar mergers and refusing to read codes on the phone are lines of defense that cost almost zero but are often ignored.</p>\n<h2>Relationship with carrier call forwarding</h2>\n      <p>In addition to merging calls, some scams will also induce you to activate \"call forwarding\" or \"no-answer forwarding\" on your mobile phone to forward all future calls to the scammer. The merged call eavesdropping affects the current call; the call transfer affects every future call. If you have ever dialed a special code (such as a function code starting with ** or ##) according to unfamiliar instructions, please call the operator's official customer service immediately to inquire and close all transfers. For details, please refer to the operator's official instructions.</p>\n      <p>Daily habits can be developed: confirm the service activation status with the operator every few months; when you find that you cannot receive calls from others, put \"whether to be transferred\" and \"whether to change the card\" in the same priority list.</p>"},"08-sim-swap":{"category":"Risks deepen","title":"SIM card hijacking and card swapping attacks: When your SMS inbox no longer belongs to you","excerpt":"After the number is transferred to the new card, the verification code will be entered into the attacker's phone. Understand carrier-side risks and learn to use two-step verification and SIM PIN to reduce harm.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>SIM swapping (card replacement/number card hijacking) is a more serious attack: the other party uses fake identities, social engineering skills, or exploits loopholes in the operator's process to let the telecommunications company activate your number to a SIM they control. After completion, your phone may suddenly display \"No Service\", and the SMS OTP that should have been sent to you instead rings on the scammer's phone. WhatsApp uses mobile phone numbers as the primary key, so this is a direct blow to account sovereignty.</p>\n      <p>If two-step verification is not turned on, the other party can re-register after receiving the SMS code; even if you turn on two-step verification, you will immediately fall into a two-line race between \"the operator takes back the number\" and \"WhatsApp takes back the session.\" High-profile public figures are long-term targets, but ordinary users may also be selected based on their business accounts, investment groups, or simply the quality of their address books.</p>\n\n      <h2>Possible signs</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The phone is out of service for a long time for no reason, the airplane mode switch is invalid, or it prompts \"SIM not inserted\".</li>\n        <li>Suddenly I can't receive any text messages, but the bank prompts me to log in abnormally.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp prompts that the number has been registered on other devices.</li>\n        <li>Relatives and friends receive money loan messages from \"you\", but your phone cannot open WhatsApp at all.</li>\n        <li>The operator sent you a notification that the card replacement was successful, but you did not handle it.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>First time emergency (in order)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Use another mobile phone or landline to call the operator's official customer service (according to the channel on the official app, official website or bill) to report an abnormal card change and request a freeze on the transfer and a replacement SIM.</li>\n        <li>After the number is restored, immediately re-verify WhatsApp on this machine and clear the associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Notify banks and payment platforms to pay attention to abnormal OTPs and transfers; reset key account passwords.</li>\n        <li>Text your friends and family not to trust any recent WhatsApp loan requests.</li>\n        <li>Keep operator work orders and text message records, and report the case to local police channels when necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Daily risk reduction measures</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask your operator whether you can set additional passwords for card replacement/transfer or restrictions on going to the office.</li>\n        <li>Enable a PIN for the SIM to prevent the physical card from being directly inserted and used by others.</li>\n        <li>WhatsApp must enable two-step verification and bind your email address.</li>\n        <li>For important financial accounts, App tokens and hardware keys are preferred instead of text messages alone.</li>\n        <li>Avoid disclosing complete mobile phone number, ID card information and address combination on social platforms.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The card swap attack targets \"who controls the SMS inbox\". Carrier side lock + WhatsApp two-step verification + loss of contact plan, a three-piece set is indispensable.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Review after recapture</h2>\n      <p>Check whether the scammer has completed WhatsApp registration on your number or sent a group of fraudulent messages. If so, you need to use other channels to clarify and help relatives and friends who have been defrauded to stop the payment. Consider whether to temporarily increase monitoring of high-value accounts associated with numbers.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>SIM hijacking upgrades the mobile phone number from \"contact information\" to \"security root\". Establishing the conditioned reflex of \"sudden no service = highest alarm\" saves time than fighting for the number afterwards.</p>\n<h2>Linkage risks with banks and email addresses</h2>\n      <p>After successfully changing the card, the attacker will not only target WhatsApp, but may also reset bank SMS verification and email password retrieval in the same window. Therefore, while declaring the operator, take the initiative to call the bank customer service to explain the situation, freeze abnormal channels, and check the mailbox remote login record. Treat \"mobile phone number loss\" as an account matrix-level event rather than a single App failure.</p>"},"09-phishing-sites":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Fake official websites and phishing links: don’t click if they look like it","excerpt":"From the pressure of domain names and words to \"what do you want to do\", the system teaches you how to expose fake login pages. When you need the web version, just use the official address you entered yourself.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The essence of phishing attacks is to forge trust symbols that you are familiar with: Meta green, lock icon, customer service language, countdown, and even domain name spelling that is almost the same as the real website. The attacker does not need to break WhatsApp's end-to-end encryption. He only needs to trick you into entering your mobile phone number and six-digit registration code at a fake \"login counter\" or downloading a shell app, and then hand over your account keys.</p>\n      <p>In recent years, phishing pages have also evolved a \"progressive\" process: they first ask you to fill in your mobile phone number to \"make an appointment to unblock\", and then you actually receive the official SMS code a few seconds later - you mistakenly think that the process is formal, so you fill in the code you just received on the fake site. The success of the attack lies in the fact that users mistook “receiving real text messages” for “the website is real.”</p>\n\n      <h2>Common delivery channels</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Short links and \"Account will be permanently banned\" banners in WhatsApp private chats and group messages.</li>\n        <li>SMS/email pretending to be WhatsApp Business review, refund, HD call activation.</li>\n        <li>Fake \"WhatsApp Web Login\" site on search engine advertising space.</li>\n        <li>The \"official announcement\" and fake update package forwarded by the stolen account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Four self-checks before clicking on the button</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Am I doing this in the first place? The unexpected \"Account will be blocked if not verified within 24 hours\" is highly suspicious.</li>\n        <li>Is the domain name in the browser address bar the domain name I typed in myself? Be wary of whatsapp-verify, wa-login, multiple hyphens, and strange suffixes.</li>\n        <li>Does the page ask for registration code, bank OTP, mnemonic phrase, and remote desktop? Either one should exit.</li>\n        <li>Do you want to induce APK downloads, description files, or enable accessibility/screen recording? Commonly found in Android side Trojan chains.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The only way to safely open the web version/PC version</h2>\n      <p>Open the browser yourself, enter the official domain name manually or use the official desktop client; initiate \"Associate Device\" in WhatsApp on your mobile phone and scan the QR code. Never log in from the blue link in the chat. The QR code on the scan page can only come from<strong>The official page you just opened</strong>, you cannot scan pictures sent by others in the chat.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"the official entrance must be built by yourself\" as muscle memory: the link handed over by others can only be used as a reference, not as an entrance.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Grading after wrong clicks</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Browse only:</strong>Close the page, do not click twice; be wary of follow-up calls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Registration code filled in:</strong>Immediately re-verify the number locally, clear the device, and reset the two-step verification.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bank card/email password handed in:</strong>If you need to change the password of the corresponding account urgently, contact the bank; check the remote login rules by email.</li>\n        <li><strong>Unknown APK installed:</strong>Disconnect the network and uninstall, restore to factory settings if necessary, and re-login and reinforce each platform.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Report the screenshot of the phishing link to WhatsApp and give a brief warning in the group to reduce chain casualties.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Encryption protects the transmission, not your hand walking into the fake counter. Self-made domain name, self-checking of behavior, and withdrawal upon requesting the code - these three tricks can stop most phishing at the door.</p>\n<h2>Identify the combination of \"real preview and fake web page\"</h2>\n      <p>The link preview in the chat can show the thumbnail of the regular domain name, but the actual jump is to another address. Long press the link to view the full URL, or copy it to Notes to check before deciding whether to open it. On iOS/Android, beware of the redirection chain of \"click domain name A for the first time, and then follow domain name B for the second time.\"</p>"},"10-recover-account":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"The whole process of recovering an account after being stolen: follow the official logic step by step","excerpt":"Re-using your own number to complete the registration verification is the key to kicking others away. This article is written in order to recapture, clear equipment, notify relatives and friends, and avoid a second loss.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>When people find out that WhatsApp has been stolen, their first reaction is often confusion: they want to report it, they want to find someone to do it for them, and they are asked by their relatives and friends, \"Is it you who borrowed money just now?\" Please slow down first - according to the public instructions in the WhatsApp Help Center, when the problem is \"Someone is using your number\",<strong>On your own secure primary phone, use this number to complete the registration verification again</strong>, is the core step to remove illegal sessions. After correctly entering the SMS or voice registration code you received, the other party's use on the main device will usually be pushed offline.</p>\n      <p>This process is not guaranteed to be completed in one minute: you may also have to deal with unfamiliar two-step verification PINs, clean up associated devices, and announce stop losses to the outside world. Doing them in sequence saves more time than trying them in parallel.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recapture the First 60 Seconds: Four Questions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Can I still open WhatsApp? If you can open it, clear the \"associated devices\" first, and don't rush to chat with scammers.</li>\n        <li>Have I been kicked back to the registration page? If so, you will directly enter the re-inspection process.</li>\n        <li>Is it possible that the phone is poisoned or remotely controlled? If so, disconnect the internet and kill the virus first, otherwise you will lose it again if you take it back.</li>\n        <li>Are you experiencing no signal when changing cards at the same time? Contact the operator first and then talk about app recapture.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Standard recapture steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>exist<strong>Trusted native</strong>Reinstall or open official WhatsApp (only official store sources).</li>\n        <li>Enter your complete mobile phone number in international format to request a registration code.</li>\n        <li>Cover the screen when receiving codes.<strong>no way</strong>Send to anyone; enter this.</li>\n        <li>If a two-step verification PIN is required: enter the one you remember; if it is an unfamiliar PIN, see the topic on this site, but you should still complete the SMS verification to kick the person first.</li>\n        <li>After logging in → Settings → Connected devices → Exit all unknown items.</li>\n        <li>Turn off and on two-step verification, change your PIN, and confirm that the recovery email is still yours.</li>\n        <li>Check whether privacy and security related settings have been tampered with; exclude unofficial clients.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Counter-intuitive but important: Just changing the 2-step verification PIN won’t single out the logged-in scammer; re-verifying the number is key. Also do not blindly perform operations such as \"report your account\" before recapturing it to avoid interfering with the recovery. Please refer to the official help center guidelines for details.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>External communication during the recapture</h2>\n      <p>Do two things in parallel: kick people technically and stop the bleeding interpersonally.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Notify family members via SMS/call: “I” asked for money and passwords on WhatsApp in the past 24 hours.</li>\n        <li>Ask trusted colleagues to send the company group statement on their behalf through non-WhatsApp channels.</li>\n        <li>If a transfer has already occurred, instruct the injured party to immediately contact the bank to stop the payment, retain evidence of the chat, and report the case through local police channels as appropriate.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Reinforcement 24–72 hours after recapture</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Check the associated devices again and enable App Lock.</li>\n        <li>Review of lost points: Fishing? An acquaintance wants to code? Fake vote? Share screen?</li>\n        <li>Synchronize your \"core rules\" with core contacts (for example, you must call when borrowing money).</li>\n        <li>If the business account is stolen, customer notifications will be issued according to business topics.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send the registration code to a \"retrieval agent\" stranger - stolen twice.</li>\n        <li>The recapture was successful but no announcement was made, and relatives and friends continued to be fooled.</li>\n        <li>Only changing the password does not clear the associated device, but the web page is still synchronized.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Regain the order of fighting: first number sovereignty, then clear the back door, then stop losses in interpersonal relationships, and finally review and strengthen. Collecting this article as an emergency card is more reliable than searching hastily.</p>"},"11-unknown-pin-7days":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Encountering an unfamiliar 2-step verification PIN: Wait, kick, and reset your strategy","excerpt":"A thief may be able to set a PIN first. Explain why SMS code verification is still critical, how to follow the official process when you don’t know your PIN, and what to do during the waiting period.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Two-step verification is supposed to be a shield, but it may be installed on your account by attackers and become a wall blocking you. When re-registering, many victims find that the system requires them to enter a two-step verification PIN, but they have never set it up - this means that someone has controlled the number and enabled two-step verification, or it may even have been bound to the attacker's recovery email.</p>\n      <p>At this time, be sure not to panic and look for a \"PIN brushing\" service; that is a secondary fraud. The official logic in public information is:<strong>SMS/phone registration code</strong>Prove you have a mobile phone number;<strong>PIN</strong>is an additional password. The two systems have to solve different problems.</p>\n\n      <h2>Let’s first distinguish between the two types of “codes”</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Registration code:</strong>A six-digit text message or voice code sent to a mobile phone. Once entered, illegal sessions on other devices are often pushed offline.</li>\n        <li><strong>2-Step Verification PIN:</strong>A 6-digit password set earlier by you or the attacker. If you do not know and cannot restore via email, the system may require you to wait for a period of time (a common public description is about 7 days at most) before continuing, subject to specific App prompts.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Key takeaways:<strong>Don’t give up on entering your registration code just because you’re stuck on your PIN</strong>. By completing the number verification first, you can often remove the other party's immediate operation rights.</p>\n\n      <h2>If you remember your PIN and email</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Enter the PIN normally; or follow the official retrieval process via email.</li>\n        <li>Immediately change your PIN and recovery email after logging in.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and check whether any abnormal group messages remain.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>If the PIN is completely unknown</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You still need to complete the registration code verification to regain control of your phone.</li>\n        <li>Follow the interface prompts to handle PIN waiting or email appeal; do not disclose the registration code to strangers.</li>\n        <li>While waiting, continue to send text messages/phone calls to warn family and friends: any WhatsApp messages cannot be trusted.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the SIM card has not been changed again; strengthen the main email account.</li>\n        <li>Log in as soon as possible after waiting and set up<strong>Only you know</strong>new PIN and email address.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>It’s hard to wait seven days, but it’s part of official risk control. It is more reliable to use this time to stop loss notifications and collect evidence than to find a \"second-breaking PIN\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Forced actions after regaining control</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>New PIN + loginable recovery email; old PIN is invalid.</li>\n        <li>Clear and review associated devices; application lock is enabled.</li>\n        <li>Review how the account was initially lost to avoid malicious PIN setting on the 8th day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Unfamiliar PINs are suffocating, but the path is still there: kicking people with registration codes, dealing with PINs according to official rules, stopping the bleeding externally during the waiting period, and rebuilding access control after returning. Patience and sequence are safer than shortcuts.</p>\n<h2>How to accompany family members during the waiting period</h2>\n      <p>The seven-day wait is especially torturous for the elders. Children should briefly synchronize the progress twice a day and emphasize \"don't look for scalpers.\" At the same time, we help elders temporarily migrate their important contacts to phone messages to prevent scammers from continuing to impersonate them. You can write the prompt copy in the official app on paper to prevent them from mistaking the fake customer service in the chat.</p>\n      <p>The first thing to complete after waiting is not to send the status, but to change the PIN, change the email, and clean the device - the order cannot be messed up.</p>\n<h2>Tips for Helping Your Children</h2>\n      <p>If parents are stuck with an unfamiliar PIN, their children should accompany them through the official process remotely instead of trying \"third-party convenient unlocking\". Help parents contact old colleagues through text messages and explain that WhatsApp is temporarily unavailable to prevent the scam from continuing. Keep the work order number of the operator and mailbox service provider, and reinforce it once after the waiting period is over, which is less likely to be missed than sporadic encryption changes.</p>"},"12-signs-of-compromise":{"category":"Troubleshooting signals","title":"A complete collection of abnormal account signals: How to detect theft or synchronization as early as possible","excerpt":"From sudden logouts, unfamiliar associated devices, to feedback from friends that you are asking for code - classify early warning signals and provide corresponding actions.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Discovering the theft an hour earlier often means one less round of relatives and friends being defrauded and one less transfer. Many cases are not \"unpreventable\", but are regarded as \"little bugs\" or \"misunderstandings by friends\" when the signals appear. Think of the following grading list as an inspection instrument. When the corresponding color appears, perform the corresponding action instead of waiting for \"ironclad evidence\" before remediating.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red signal: act now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Forced to exit without any operation, or prompted that the number has been registered on another device.</li>\n        <li>You didn't click to log in, but you received multiple registration verification codes in succession.</li>\n        <li>Browsers, cities, and device names that have never been seen before appear in the associated device list.</li>\n        <li>Multiple friends reported at the same time: You are asking for a verification code or urging a transfer.</li>\n        <li>You chat online, but the other party says \"you\" and replies with different content on the other end.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Re-verify your number immediately → Clear all associations → Reset two-step verification → Notify relatives and friends via text message.</p>\n\n      <h2>Orange signal: must be processed that day</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A preview of an unknown chat reply flashed in the notification bar, but you didn't send it when you opened the conversation.</li>\n        <li>A group friend sent a private message asking: Is the link just sent yours?</li>\n        <li>It is found that two-step verification has been turned off, or the recovery email address has been changed (if visible).</li>\n        <li>After borrowing/clicking on the phone, the phone experienced abnormal power consumption and unfamiliar auxiliary functions were turned on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check all associated devices; reset two-step verification; check recent click records and installation sources; inform close contacts to be vigilant.</p>\n\n      <h2>Yellow signal: Increase the frequency of inspections</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Frequently receiving \"official warnings\" from external links is not done by you actively.</li>\n        <li>Semi-acquaintances repeatedly tried to see if they could borrow money or help scan QR codes.</li>\n        <li>I used the web version in a hotel/internet cafe and am not sure if I have logged out.</li>\n        <li>After I changed my card abroad, my text messages were delayed, and I was worried that my OTP would be intercepted by others.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><strong>action:</strong>Check related equipment every day this week; refuse to click on unfamiliar links; review after returning to China in the travel topic.</p>\n\n      <h2>Treat \"friend feedback\" as the highest priority</h2>\n      <p>When a friend says, \"You are asking for my code,\" don't argue that it's \"impossible,\" but check your device and session first. The cost of misjudgment is low, but the cost of missed judgment is high. If the self-examination fails, ask your friends to give a brief warning in the group to prevent a chain reaction.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Set up a \"red phone\": Make an agreement with two relatives and friends to call each other to confirm if there is any abnormality in the account, without replying via WhatsApp.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Recording and Evidence Collection</h2>\n      <p>If an orange or above signal appears, screenshots can be associated with the device page, verification code text message time, and suspicious chats to facilitate future evidence through bank or police channels. Do not frequently click on phishing links to \"confirm whether they are fake\".</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Signal classification is to avoid the two extremes of numbness and panic. Treating a question from a friend as a fire alarm can often stop the attack before the realization stage.</p>\n<h2>Create a personal \"exception log\"</h2>\n      <p>Use a mobile phone memo or a paper notebook to record the date and screenshot number of every abnormal verification code, unfamiliar device, and friend’s question. If you need to explain the timeline to a bank or law enforcement agency in the future, organized evidence is more powerful than fragmented memories. The log itself does not contain sensitive codes, only event types are recorded.</p>\n<h2>The balance between false positives and false negatives</h2>\n      <p>Sometimes friends forget that they have just sent a strange link and will falsely report that you have been hacked. Even so, lightweight self-checking (associated devices, recent login prompts) is still a low-cost action. The loss of one missed report is far greater than the 30 seconds spent checking several times. It is recommended to \"spot check\" each other with your friends every quarter: ask \"Have you received any suspicious messages impersonating me recently?\"</p>"},"13-friend-asks-money":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Borrow money from friends, change numbers, keep it confidential: complete verification manual","excerpt":"The first step to monetize a stolen account is often to borrow money from the address book. Teach you a verification process that does not hurt feelings and can prevent transfer fraud.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The most common \"cold start monetization\" after account hacking is to borrow money from the address book. Scammers don't send messages in general. Instead, they read historical chats, imitate your name, mannerisms, and recent topics, and write a private message saying \"an old classmate is asking for help\" and \"the project is short of payment.\" What you have to guard against is not whether your friends will lend you money, but whether<strong>Whether this request passed the unforgeable core</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk speech characteristics</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Emphasize immediately, keep it confidential, don’t let family members know, and prevent third parties from getting involved.</li>\n        <li>He claimed that his phone was broken and his number had been changed, and he asked to add a new number and transfer money immediately.</li>\n        <li>Reject or delay video exposure; voice may also be imitated by AI (see the AI ​​topic on this site).</li>\n        <li>The payment methods suddenly changed to gift cards, USDT, unfamiliar QR codes, and multiple small payments.</li>\n        <li>First, a small amount is \"tested\" and then a large amount is added.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Recommended verification process (it’s okay to be stubborn)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Stop talking about money in the original chat and say \"I'll call to confirm\" - a true friend will understand.</li>\n        <li>Call the address book<strong>Old number that has been stored for a long time</strong>, without the new number just issued by the other party.</li>\n        <li>Ask for two non-public details that only you know (where you met last week, family code).</li>\n        <li>If still unsure, contact your spouse/colleague to cross-check.</li>\n        <li>Any reminder \"too late to place orders/surgery fees\" cannot replace the verification; for large amounts, delay in arrival can be agreed upon.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>A true friend rarely shames your discretion when questioned. Forcing you to \"stop asking so many questions and transfer first\" is more like a scam script trying to grab the window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What should I do if it has been transferred out?</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Contact the bank/payer immediately to attempt to block or dispute.</li>\n        <li>Save chats, transfer vouchers, counterparty numbers and timelines.</li>\n        <li>Inform the friend who was defrauded that his account may have been stolen, and report the case through local police channels. Whether it needs to be determined by the case handling agency.</li>\n        <li>Give a brief warning in the mutual friend group to avoid secondary harm.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Family and Company Covenant Template</h2>\n      <p>\"Any emergency loan requested through the chat app must be confirmed by voice or in person; no transfer will be made without confirmation.\" Write this sentence into the employee handbook and pin it to the top of the family WeChat group.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The verification process is the insurance of friendship, not distrust. The friend who was stolen also needs you to help him prevent more victims.</p>\n<h2>\"Batch borrowing\" in group chat</h2>\n      <p>Hackers sometimes post loan templates to multiple groups at the same time, using someone to transfer money first to create a herd mentality. Seeing that many people in the group have transferred does not mean that I have confirmed that I need to be independent. If the administrator discovers an abnormality, he or she should temporarily ban all members and verify via telephone channels.</p>\n      <p>Company financial regulations: Any change to the collection account must be accompanied by a stamped PDF and a phone call. Screenshots of the chat cannot be used as a basis alone.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of small-amount test transfers</h2>\n      <p>The scammer first asks the victim to transfer tens of dollars to \"confirm friendship\", and then adds a larger amount. The rule should be: regardless of the amount, the verification standards should be the same. Don’t skip phone confirmation just because “the amount is small”. Some scripts are designed to accumulate small amounts and avoid bank risk control thresholds.</p>\n<h2>Couple and partnership business</h2>\n      <p>Romantic relationships and partners are more likely to transfer money due to \"embarrassment to verify\". Agreement: All partnership funds will be made through company accounts and written emails, and decisions will not be made through personal WhatsApp. During a breakup or dispute, you should be especially wary of pretending to be the other person to borrow money. The stronger the relationship, the harder the process must be. This is a protective relationship.</p>"},"14-privacy-settings":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Complete reinforcement of privacy settings: Reduce personal information used by social workers","excerpt":"Avatar, last seen online, group invitation, status visible range... the recommended values ​​are explained one by one. Privacy settings don’t replace two-step verification, but they can reduce scammers’ fodder.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of messages during transmission and does not automatically hide your avatar, signature, online time, or group exposure. Scammers will collect these fragments and put together a more believable script: they know that you have just left the country, know that your daughter is participating in the competition, and know the names you commonly use. Tightening privacy is a low-cost task to reduce the area of ​​social work materials.</p>\n\n      <h2>It is recommended to adjust item by item (Settings → Privacy)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>avatar:</strong>Be restrained from non-contacts and reduce the number of strangers cloning your image.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last online and online:</strong>Restrict on demand to reduce being tracked.</li>\n        <li><strong>about:</strong>Do not write down your mobile phone number, address, class schedule, or the full name of your child’s school.</li>\n        <li><strong>state:</strong>Control the audience; status will still spread if forwarded, evaluate before sending.</li>\n        <li><strong>Group:</strong>\"Who can add me to the group\" is recommended as \"My Contacts\" to prevent strangers from joining the group.</li>\n        <li><strong>call:</strong>Options such as muting unknown calls (depending on version) to reduce harassment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Read receipt:</strong>Experience the feature; the security core is still in the verification code and device.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Step by step operating habits</h2>\n      <p>Every time you change an item, use another trumpet or a friend's non-contact account to test what strangers can see. You can scan again every month after changing your phone or after a major event (wedding, travel), as app updates sometimes restore the default, more open options.</p>\n\n      <h2>The boundary between privacy and theft prevention</h2>\n      <p>Tightening privacy won't save \"you take the initiative to send a verification code\", but it can save cold start scammers half the script material. It is at a different level from two-step verification and associated device inspection, and needs to be stacked rather than replaced.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>When changing the privacy of elders, give priority to the three items of \"group invitation restrictions + hiding avatars from non-contacts + removing sensitive information from the about column\", which is cost-effective.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Extra points for business number</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Store avatars can be counterfeited, so public announcements must use consistent brand payment information across multiple channels.</li>\n        <li>After an employee leaves, check who can still send messages on behalf of the brand account.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Spending fifteen minutes to go through the privacy page is a low-cost \"version of the story to reduce being deceived.\" Cooperating with verification code discipline can truly blunt the scammer's words.</p>\n<h2>Recommended files for different groups of people</h2>\n      <p>Elders: Group invitations are limited to contacts, avatars are limited to contacts, and closed status is displayed to the public. Teenagers: Limit visibility during online time and reduce entry points for strangers to send messages. Business ID: The avatar and \"About\" are used for business display, but avoid writing personal addresses; payment information uses fixed template messages instead of random verbal changes.</p>\n      <p>After each major version update of the App, the privacy default value may be changed, and another round of inspection should be carried out after changing devices or updating.</p>\n<h2>Screenshots and external files</h2>\n      <p>Your avatar and signature may have been screenshotted and spread by others. Regularly check whether your mobile phone number + avatar combination appears in search engines and human flesh sites. If found, you can appeal to the platform for deletion. Privacy settings are to reduce new leaks, not to recover leaked material; but it is still worth doing.</p>\n<h2>Side effects of syncing contacts</h2>\n      <p>Allowing WhatsApp to read the address book will upload the numbers of relatives and friends for friend discovery. If your device is stolen, this address book also becomes an attack list. You can turn off contact uploading in privacy (subject to version options) and add core contacts manually instead. When the operation path changes, check the official help center for instructions.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Privacy is a speed bump, not a moat; it, together with verification code discipline and equipment lists, constitutes complete protection. Note the date when you complete the setup and review it six months later.</p>"},"15-official-app-only":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Only use official WhatsApp: Risks of modding packages and non-store installation","excerpt":"The so-called prevention of withdrawal, multiple openings, and membership cracking may be backdoors. Explain why official channel updates are so important and how to self-check the current installation source.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>WhatsApp’s official security instructions have repeatedly emphasized that unofficial or modified clients may lead to account theft, blocking, or continued leaks. The so-called \"anti-withdrawal\", \"double-open\", \"theme cracking\" and \"hidden online\" APKs are often hidden under the shell of legitimate functions, with additional permissions - read notifications, barrier-free clicks, automatic forwarding of verification codes - once the endpoint is compromised, end-to-end encryption cannot protect the registration code you actively display on the screen.</p>\n\n      <h2>High-risk source identification</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>APK distributed through group files, network disks, and forum short links.</li>\n        <li>The names are similar to WhatsApp Plus, GB, Delta and other forks; the icons are slightly different.</li>\n        <li>The installation wizard asks to turn off Play Protect and open unknown sources.</li>\n        <li>Requires login to WhatsApp and full management of text messages.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Correct installation path</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>iOS App Store only, developer should be WhatsApp LLC.</li>\n        <li>Android gives priority to Google Play or the official version of the official store of the mobile phone manufacturer.</li>\n        <li>Download the desktop version from Meta's official guide and do not search for third-party \"acceleration packages\".</li>\n        <li>Enable automatic system updates to patch known vulnerabilities.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Self-examination and migration</h2>\n      <p>Check the developer and reviews on the store page to see if there are any unusual fraudulent orders, and whether the permission list is excessive. If you have used the modified version for a long time:</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Back up necessary chats; uninstall unofficial clients.</li>\n        <li>Install the official version and re-verify your number.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices, reset two-step verification, and change PIN.</li>\n        <li>If a Trojan is suspected: perform an anti-virus or reset assessment on the premise that the device is safe.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>\"Free premium features\" are often exchanged: you save the subscription fee, and the scammer gets your entire account and address book.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Promote words within the organization</h2>\n      <p>In the company training, \"Modifying WhatsApp means bringing in a backdoor\" is written as an IT red line and is tested together with phishing identification.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The official client is the foundation for all security assumptions. The foundation is wrong, the two-step verification above is just psychological comfort.</p>\n<h2>Enterprise MDM and dual-opener</h2>\n      <p>Some employees use company MDM or third-party \"dual application opening\" to log in to two WhatsApps on one work phone. If the source of the dual-open device is unknown, it is also possible to hijack the notification bar verification code. Enterprise IT should whitelist only official package names and prohibit sideloading; employees' personal computers should also avoid installing forks to save trouble.</p>\n<h2>Correct posture when update fails</h2>\n      <p>If the official store update fails repeatedly, do not download the forum \"full package\" instead. You should clear the store cache, check the system time, and contact official support channels. Temporarily using the web version to reply is also safer than installing an unfamiliar APK - the web version also abides by the associated device rules.</p>\n<h2>Second-hand mobile phone pre-installation trap</h2>\n      <p>When buying a second-hand Android phone, check whether it has unofficial WhatsApp pre-installed or comes with resident apps such as \"acceleration\" and \"cleanup\". Only install from the official store after factory reset. If the seller claims to have \"already logged in for you\", resolutely log in again and change your PIN.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Every modding attempt is a bet that your address book is worth more than a few bells and whistles. The one who wins the bet is always the liar.</p>\n<p>Remember: the original version is free, modification is the most expensive.</p>"},"16-phone-lock-physical":{"category":"daily habits","title":"Mobile phone lock screen and physical security: preventing people around you from stealing connections","excerpt":"Many web version failures occur during \"just a minute of viewing\". Strong screen lock, notification preview policy and post-borrowing inspection form the physical layer defense line.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The unlocked phone at the table is often underestimated in discussions of digital theft. Operate on behalf of a repair shop, pick it up jokingly with colleagues, \"help me log into the web version\" from family members, leave the dinner table to go to the bathroom - attackers do not need remote skills. As long as they complete a code scan within tens of seconds, the associated device session can synchronize your messages for a long time.</p>\n\n      <h2>Basic configuration list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>System lock screen: Prioritize long passwords or complex PINs, and use biometrics as a convenient layer to avoid easy-to-guess patterns.</li>\n        <li>Set the automatic screen lock time to the shortest acceptable interval (e.g. 30 seconds – 1 minute).</li>\n        <li>Lock screen notifications hide verification codes and chat content previews.</li>\n        <li>If you enable app lock (fingerprint/face) in WhatsApp, you still need to verify it again after unlocking the phone.</li>\n        <li>Sensitive group chats can be overlaid with chat locks (see related topics).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Borrowing and repair scenarios</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Don’t leave your unlocked phone with others alone, even for a minute.</li>\n        <li>If you are present when others are required to operate, prohibit them from entering the \"Associated Devices\" or system VPN/Profile page.</li>\n        <li>The first thing you do when you get your phone back is to open the list of associated devices and remove the unfamiliar entries.</li>\n        <li>Before sending for repair, exit WhatsApp or make sure to lock the screen + turn off notifications; ask the store in writing not to browse personal privacy.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Shoulder view of public environment</h2>\n      <p>Please block the registration code when entering it in the subway or airport; turn off automatic reading notifications. The attacker can also obtain OTP by using naked eye shoulder peek + video recording.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Before lending your phone, you can manually lock the screen. Politeness and a five-minute explanation are better than taking back your account afterwards.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Stolen cell phone</h2>\n      <p>After the device is physically lost, in addition to reporting the loss to the police and the operator, recheck WhatsApp with a new device as soon as possible (the SIM is still yours), clear the association, and change the PIN. If the SIM is also on board, priority will be given to replacing the card.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Physical security is the floor of digital security. If the floor is gone, no matter how high you stand, you will fall.</p>\n<h2>Children and nanny at home scene</h2>\n      <p>Children will swipe notifications out of curiosity, and housekeeping staff may answer calls on their behalf. In addition to app lock, family members can be educated not to read out verification codes in front of minors. For reasonable needs such as the nanny borrowing a mobile phone to take pictures, the camera mode can be used instead of unlocking the desktop directly.</p>\n      <p>If smart watches and tablets log in with the same number and forward text messages, they must also be included in the scope of physical inspection.</p>\n<h2>Wearable device risks</h2>\n      <p>When a smartwatch synchronizes to display WhatsApp notifications, the verification code may also be exposed on the wrist. In outdoor running and gym scenes, sensitive previews on the watch should be turned off, or OTP text messages should be temporarily muted to the wearable layer. Borrowing someone else's watch to log in to the same account will also leave traces of the session and should be evaluated independently.</p>\n<h2>Camera physical blocking habits</h2>\n      <p>Video call scams sometimes direct you to switch to a front-facing camera display. Develop the habit of turning off camera permissions or physical sliders when not in use to reduce the chance of being induced to show your bank card or ID card desktop. Parents should be present for spot checks when their children use tablet video classes.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Think of the lock screen as a belt and the app lock as a pocket buckle; if the belt is not fastened, the pocket buckle is useless. Both are in place at the same time.</p>\n<p>When charging before going to bed, do not put your unlocked phone next to guests who are out of reach on the bedside; when dining, place your phone face down to reduce the exposure of verification code pop-ups.</p>\n<p>Do not use an image containing a photo of your home address as the lock screen wallpaper to avoid leaking it if you lose your phone.</p>"},"17-whatsapp-web-safety":{"category":"daily habits","title":"WhatsApp Web and PC: long rules for safe use","excerpt":"From the official entrance, keep logged in option, public computer process to abnormal session handling, a complete specification is given to people who often reply to messages on the computer.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>The computer allows us to respond to customers and family members faster, but it also exposes conversations to \"larger screens, longer login times, and more shared environments.\" The focus of security is not to disable the computer version, but to insist on: initiating association with the official entrance, knowing which sessions are hanging, and enforcing a short life cycle in public environments.</p>\n\n      <h2>Recommended security workflow</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the official WhatsApp on your phone → Linked devices → Link new devices.</li>\n        <li>Only open the official desktop program on your computer, or write the official website address by hand in the browser address bar.</li>\n        <li>scanning<strong>The code that just popped up from the official page on the computer screen</strong>, do not scan chat pictures.</li>\n        <li>For private computers, you can check to stay logged in; for public computers, never check it, and log out immediately on the mobile phone.</li>\n        <li>Check the list every week and kick out inactive but unfamiliar items.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>expressly prohibited</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the \"Login\", \"Vote\" and \"Subsidy\" QR codes in the chat.</li>\n        <li>Click on \"WhatsApp Web\" in the search engine ad.</li>\n        <li>Let someone else use your mobile phone to scan on your behalf and \"Hang it up for me\".</li>\n        <li>For a long time, the company's front desk computer was used as a server 24 hours a day.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Multi-device office strategy</h2>\n      <p>When you have two computers at home and one at work, naming the session makes it easy to identify; before leaving your job or selling the device, you must first remove the association on the mobile phone and then reinstall the system.</p>\n\n      <h2>Abnormal coordination signal</h2>\n      <p>The message shows that it has been read but you did not open it; the contact says that you sent a strange attachment - immediately check the association list and reset the two-step verification.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make \"cleaning the website on Sunday night\" as much of a chore as taking out the trash: 30 seconds can change a week of peace of mind.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The computer version is a community of productivity and risk. Official entrance, short conversation, and weekly list, when used well, it is a tool rather than a floor-to-ceiling window.</p>\n<h2>Browser extensions and shared computers</h2>\n      <p>When using the web version of the browser, a malicious extension may read the page DOM. Try to use incognito mode + official website on public or company computers, and clear the data when finished. Don’t mix a large number of irrelevant add-ons with WhatsApp Web on the same browser login.</p>\n      <p>If a company deploys Chromium uniformly, IT is required to review the extension list, which is an extension of personal protection.</p>\n<h2>Multiple tabs and forgetting to log out</h2>\n      <p>Some people open WhatsApp Web in several browser profiles and forget to close it after a long time. It is recommended to name each configuration file and delete it according to the name when clearing the device on the mobile phone. The company's IT policy requiring a single browser configuration can actually reduce the probability of forgetting to log out.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and screensaver</h2>\n      <p>Office computer screensavers are short and passwords are required to return to your seat, which can prevent colleagues from seeing customers chatting when they pass by. Combined with WhatsApp Web to log out in time on the mobile phone, it is more reliable than relying solely on \"colleagues are good people\". Pressing Win+L or equivalent to lock the screen when leaving your desk should be muscle memory.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2><p>Do two things before leaving your desk: lock your computer and clear the web page. It's more worry-free than worrying about \"whether it's okay\" after get off work.</p>\n<p>When casting company meetings, exit WhatsApp desktop notifications first to avoid private OTP being broadcast live on the big screen in the conference room.</p>\n<p>Do not mix the same weak master password in the browser's password saving function with WhatsApp Web.</p>\n<p>Uninstalling the browser plug-in before logging into the web version can reduce the risk of being hijacked.</p>"},"18-block-report":{"category":"Emergency treatment","title":"Blocking, reporting and external help: cutting off scammers’ ability to continue fighting","excerpt":"From blocking the report in the app to notifying the bank and the police, we compiled a list of actions to \"not continue to be victimized, nor to let the address book continue to be victimized.\"","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>When encountering harassment or fraud, if the conversation stops at \"a few words\" or \"has been read without replying\", the other party will continue to change numbers to test and regard your silence as a target for training. What is more effective is: immediately stop chatting, block, report, preserve evidence, and escalate to banking and law enforcement channels when funds are involved.</p>\n\n      <h2>Standard operations within the app</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open a conversation → contact information or group information.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>blockade</strong>, blocking subsequent messages and calls.</li>\n        <li>choose<strong>report</strong>, submit according to the prompts, and recent chats can be attached for review by the platform.</li>\n        <li>Spam group: report it after exiting to avoid being pulled back repeatedly.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Reporting helps the platform cluster fraud networks. Although it does not guarantee immediate arrest, it can reduce the exposure of others.</p>\n\n      <h2>External procedures when accounts are stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>First recapture the main session and technical backdoor.</li>\n        <li>Use text messages/phone calls/email announcements, not just to express injustice on WhatsApp to those who have been accused.</li>\n        <li>If there is a loss of money: the bank stops payment, retains evidence, and reports the case through local police channels if necessary.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Evidence package recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Screenshot of the full text of the chat (including the other party’s number and time).</li>\n        <li>Transfer records and payee information.</li>\n        <li>Copy the phishing URL in plain text (do not click again).</li>\n        <li>Associated device exception screenshots and SMS code time.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>For anti-fraud inquiries, please go to the official help center or local anti-fraud publicity channels, and do not give remote control rights to \"enthusiastic netizens\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>When is the spread warning</h2>\n      <p>If the scam uses your stolen account to spread to your address book, you should briefly clarify it in multiple groups after recovering it, once only, and attach verifiable non-WhatsApp contact information.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Blocking reports shortens the attacker's range; asking for help reduces financial losses. Have lists in both hands rather than winging it.</p>\n<h2>Realistic expectations for reporting materials</h2>\n      <p>Electronic evidence must retain the original carrier, and screenshots should include the other party’s account number, time, and complete context as much as possible. Whether a case can be filed and how to collaborate cross-border shall be judged by the case-handling agency in accordance with the law; this website cannot replace legal advice. You should still report it to create a time record and help with the bank's dispute process.</p>\n<h2>The reality of transnational scams</h2>\n      <p>WhatsApp fraud is often transnational and it is difficult to recover the stolen goods, but it still makes sense to report the crime and dispute it with the bank. Handing all the materials to the case-handling agency at once is more effective than posting in fragments on various platforms. Families should manage expectations while adhering to payment stops and timelines.</p>\n<h2>Linkage outside the platform</h2>\n      <p>The same scammer may be copying the script on Telegram, Line. After a platform is blocked, pay attention to whether other apps come to connect. Record scam numbers and IDs in your personal blacklist and share them with your family to reduce repeated scams.</p>\n<h2>Post review meeting</h2>\n      <p>After being scammed or nearly scammed, families do a 20-minute review: What step could have been taken to say no? Which setting should be improved next week? No accountability, just change the process. Post the review record on the refrigerator so that similar words will trigger your memory next time.</p>\n<p>Briefly describe the scam routine to relatives and friends. One circle of friends is worth more than a hundred times of being alone; be careful to hide sensitive personal account information.</p>\n<p>Record the receipt number reported to the platform to facilitate follow-up or supplementary materials.</p>\n<p>After reporting to WhatsApp, you should still back up the evidence locally. Processing by the platform does not mean the case is settled.</p>\n<p>Save nickname variations that scammers have used on other platforms to facilitate cross-retrieval and warning.</p>"},"19-family-elders":{"category":"home protection","title":"Family and Elder Protection Class: Turn complex safety into enforceable family rules","excerpt":"A long version of the home anti-theft plan: family rules, role-playing drills, remote assistance boundaries, and how to help elders complete key settings.","read":"15 minutes","body":"<p>Many family stories begin when an elder receives the message \"Read the code for me.\" It’s not that they don’t have good intentions, but they put politeness before vigilance. Scammers take advantage of the psychology of \"embarrassment to refuse\" and \"fear of delaying the child\". To protect the elders, it is not to throw them a long article, but to translate complex safety into<strong>Able to memorize, act, and refuse</strong>house rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>Six house rules for refrigerator magnets</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>No one will give the verification code, including my children and \"customer service\".</li>\n        <li>To borrow money, you must call the old phone number and do not transfer it in the app.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on the \"Unblock/Winning/Vote\" link.</li>\n        <li>Videos do not share the screen with strangers.</li>\n        <li>Spend half a minute every Sunday looking at \"Connected Devices.\"</li>\n        <li>Use two-step verification for the whole family. Remember the PIN yourself or your children can keep it for you.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>15-minute hands-on family lesson</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The children were present to help each elder open two-step verification and register their email address.</li>\n        <li>Clear the associated devices together and turn off the lock screen verification code preview.</li>\n        <li>Practice the liar's lines and three rejection lines, swapping roles.</li>\n        <li>Make an agreement to \"call the digital contact first if you are not sure\" and establish a culture of no guilt.</li>\n        <li>Write down a list of emergency phone numbers (children, community, bank) and post it next to the refrigerator.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>The limits of remote assistance</h2>\n      <p>You can video-guide your elders on the settings, but don’t let them install remote software from unknown sources. Children will never ask for verification codes from their parents to \"operate on their behalf\" - it is more important to model correct behavior than to click buttons for them.</p>\n\n      <h2>How to communicate after being fooled</h2>\n      <p>Solve the problem first and then educate: assist in recapture, bank stop payment, and comfort. Blaming will only make them hide it next time, until the damage is greater.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The goal is not to turn elders into experts, but to give them the courage to \"hang up\" at critical moments.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>House rules + walkthroughs + key settings are a long-term investment. One lesson today may block the entire building's chain of scam codes next year.</p>\n<h2>Risks of Community and Church Support Groups</h2>\n      <p>Elders often have a strong sense of trust in their faith and neighborhood groups. You can ask the group owner to pin the \"Assistant Cai will not issue verification code\" announcement to the top of the page, and have your children occasionally drop in to share it for 10 minutes. Using group norms to reduce individual rejection pressure.</p>\n      <p>A tutorial on screenshots of large fonts is prepared for people with poor vision. Paths are more friendly than text.</p>\n<h2>Combined with anti-fraud propaganda</h2>\n      <p>Many city public security agencies have anti-fraud brochures or short videos, which you can watch with your elders to discuss important scenes in the plot. Official materials are often more convincing than children’s nagging. When encountering real news about being deceived, we should also have a gentle discussion about \"how should we refuse if it is true?\"</p>\n<h2>Drug and supplement scams intersect</h2>\n      <p>The rhetoric of selling health care products often goes hand in hand with asking for codes to steal accounts: first gain trust, and then ask for verification codes to \"activate membership prices.\" When the elders return home after attending a health lecture, their children can take the initiative to ask, \"Have you registered to receive a code on your mobile phone today?\" Concerned questions are more effective than questioning.</p>\n<h2>Work with community centers</h2>\n      <p>If there are smartphone training classes in streets or communities, you can sign up instead of singles for your children. Group learning reduces loneliness and the frustration of “I’m the stupid one.” Strive to have verification code discipline written into classroom handouts.</p>\n<p>Encourage elders to forward suspicious chats to their children to “have a look” and give them the right to ask for help.</p>\n<p>When taking family photos at holiday gatherings and sending them to WeChat Moments, avoid showing bank cards and express delivery orders in the background.</p>\n<p>The first Sunday of every month is set as Family Safety Day, and a ten-minute inspection is all it takes.</p>"},"20-encryption-myths":{"category":"cognitive correction","title":"End-to-end encryption doesn’t mean it won’t be stolen: The boundaries you need to know","excerpt":"Encryption is important, but it doesn’t verify who’s on the other side, and it doesn’t protect your unlocked screen, misconfigured cloud backups, or verification codes you actively hand over.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>The phrase \"WhatsApp is safe\" has been repeated so many times in the media and in classrooms that many people mistakenly believe that:<strong>With encryption turned on, you will not be cheated, stolen, or leaked.</strong>In fact, end-to-end encryption mainly solves the problem of \"intermediate link eavesdropping\". It is not automatically immune to the verification code you hand over, the associated web page, the screen read by Trojans, and the proliferation of screenshots of the recipient.</p>\n\n      <h2>What does encryption actually protect?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confidentiality of personal chats and calls along the transmission path (within official design assumptions).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the traditional risk of carriers and public Wi-Fi eavesdropping on clear text chat content.</li>\n        <li>Together with key management, it forms an important part of the product security narrative.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What encryption doesn’t automatically protect</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After you tell the \"customer service\" your registration code and PIN, the other party logs in legally to do evil.</li>\n        <li>An attacker synchronizes reading history and real-time messages through associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Unlocked shoulder viewing, screen recording, and remote sharing on mobile phones.</li>\n        <li>The recipient forwards, screenshots, and backs up to an unsecured cloud.</li>\n        <li>The second copy is leaked due to unencrypted cloud backup or improper key storage.</li>\n        <li>Whether the identity of the other person is who you think it is - encryption does not identify the person.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>In a word: Just because the pipe is tight doesn’t mean the counter is genuine, nor does it mean you will give the key to a stranger.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>How to create a \"complete sense of security\"</h2>\n      <p>Treat encryption as the first layer, superimposing: verification code discipline, two-step verification, related equipment inspection, official client, backup key management, and dual-channel transfer core. If any piece is missing, social workers can find a way around it.</p>\n\n      <h2>Words to explain to family members</h2>\n      <p>\"Encryption is like sealing a car, but if you take the initiative to open the door and let people in, the car cannot be saved no matter how sealed it is.\" Use metaphors to reduce resistance.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Respect cryptography, but don’t mythologize cryptography. What HoldLine wants to make up for is the large piece of real risk map \"beyond end-to-end\".</p>\n<h2>A reminder for journalists and activists</h2>\n      <p>Source protection relies not just on encryption, but also on device physical security, contact authentication, and backup policies. If you are engaged in sensitive work, you should use dedicated mobile phones and semicolon operations, and conduct regular threat model reviews instead of relying on a single switch.</p>\n<h2>Group chats and communities</h2>\n      <p>Community-themed, large-scale group chats sometimes do not enjoy the same end-to-end default model as individual chats (depending on official instructions and group type). When participating in a public group, assume that the content may be seen by administrators or mechanisms outside of the reporting process. Don't equate \"told it in the group\" with \"absolutely private\".</p>\n<h2>Forward chains and rumors</h2>\n      <p>The source of pseudo-official announcements forwarded in encrypted chats is often outside the scope of encryption protection - the problem is that you believe the content. Cultivating the habit of \"checking the official help center first before switching\" will protect your family and friends better than arguing about technical details.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Trust encryption and trust your own operation process. The next time someone reaches for a code, think of this article: That's really trying to get through a pipe.</p>\n<p>Forward this article to friends who like to say \"WhatsApp is absolutely safe\" and use examples to correct the deviation.</p>\n<p>Corporate training has been added to this Quiz section, and questions with a high error rate among employees will be highlighted in the next issue.</p>\n<p>After reading this article, go check the associated equipment and remember the boundaries through actions.</p>\n<p>Re-read the official safety page when the technical terms are updated, and don’t rely on impressions from three years ago and contemporary facts.</p>"},"21-encrypted-backup":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat backup and end-to-end encrypted backup: Protect your data as well as your keys","excerpt":"Backups are a life saver and can also turn into a second copy of chats. How to enable encrypted backup, keep passwords/keys/passkeys, and what must be checked before switching devices.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Backup has saved countless phone changes and lost phones, and may also be a second copy of complete chat history. WhatsApp supports backing up chats to iCloud/Google Cloud, and can turn on end-to-end encrypted backup, making it difficult for cloud service providers to read the content directly if configured correctly. But if you lose the key slip, \"can be backed up\" will also become \"can never be found\".</p>\n\n      <h2>Is it recommended to enable</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>There are a lot of work and family communications that need to be retained: it is recommended to back up + turn on end-to-end encryption.</li>\n        <li>Extremely sensitive conversations: Assess chat locks, disappearing messages, and realize the recipient can still take screenshots.</li>\n        <li>Company compliance requirements for record retention: Confirm backup storage location and access control with legal affairs.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Open path (subject to common versions)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Settings → Chat → Chat Backup.</li>\n        <li>Enter end-to-end encrypted backup.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to create a password, 64-bit key, or passkey (depending on device support).</li>\n        <li>Write recovery method into password manager + offline safe location (paper envelope, safe).</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Officials and security agencies have stated that after the password/key for encrypted backup is lost, the platform usually cannot recover it for you. Safekeeping and backup are equally important.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>Plans for machine replacement and machine loss</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Before switching machines, confirm that the latest backup was successful.</li>\n        <li>Test once: whether you can still find the key.</li>\n        <li>After the recovery is completed, immediately inspect the two-step verification and associated devices.</li>\n        <li>When the computer is lost, first seize account sovereignty, and then handle cloud backup permissions (email, cloud account).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Common misunderstandings</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Send a photo of the key to WeChat \"just in case\" - it's equivalent to clear text hosting.</li>\n        <li>Thinking that \"only I can see\" the unencrypted backup - the cloud account is stolen and I run away naked.</li>\n        <li>The company lets employees back up their work groups with their private accounts, and data management gets out of control after they leave.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Backup is a double-edged sword: encryption + key management can prevent data loss and prevent another copy from being leaked.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls of family shared cloud accounts</h2>\n      <p>When a couple shares an iCloud/Google account, if the encrypted backup key exists in only one person's mind, neither party may be able to recover it if the phone is lost. Keys should be recorded using a common safe or individually backed up to a controlled enterprise password vault. Remember to rotate cloud login and backup permissions when you get divorced or split up.</p>\n<h2>Restoration drill annual course</h2>\n      <p>Once a year, try restoring the encrypted backup on the backup machine to confirm that the key is still correct. The drill can reveal hidden problems such as cloud account arrears and key corruption. When you really lose your chance, your mentality will be much calmer.</p>\n<h2>law enforcement retrieval boundary</h2>\n      <p>Different jurisdictions have different rules for cloud retrieval. End-to-end encrypted backups make retrieval more difficult, but they do not make legal judgments for you. Consult a licensed attorney when involved in a case; just prepare a strategy for owning your own keys and legally retaining them.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>When the backup success light turns on, sync checks whether the key is still there. Two green lights are required to complete the process.</p>\n<p>Tell your family where the key is (not the password itself) so no one can recover important conversations if you pass out.</p>\n<p>The team's shared key is controlled by two people to prevent one person from leaving and taking away all recovery rights.</p>\n<p>Enable login reminders for cloud accounts to prevent backup channels from being breached by bypass.</p>\n<p>The notebook records the date of the last successful backup, and if it has not been backed up for more than 30 days, the mobile phone will remind you.</p>\n<p>Before major litigation, decide whether to suspend automatic backup according to the lawyer's opinion to avoid accidentally deleting the evidence chain.</p>"},"22-app-lock-biometrics":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"App Lock and Biometrics: Adding a Door Lock to WhatsApp","excerpt":"After the phone is unlocked, App Lock can block prying eyes when opening it easily. Explain how to enable it and its division of labor with system lock screen and chat lock.","read":"12 minutes","body":"<p>The system lock screen blocks \"a stranger picks up the phone\"; the WhatsApp application lock blocks \"the phone is unlocked but the person leaves for half a minute\" - family members click on it, colleagues borrow photos to look at, and children swipe the screen randomly. The officially provided second-step verification of fingerprint, face or device credentials (the name varies depending on the version) is a low-friction enhancement.</p>\n\n      <h2>Start steps</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>WhatsApp Settings → Privacy (or Security) → App Lock / Fingerprint Lock.</li>\n        <li>Follow the prompts to verify your system biometrics or PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the auto-lock time (immediate or 1 minute) is consistent with your custom.</li>\n        <li>Check if notification quick replies bypass locks (tweaked by version).</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Division of labor with other protection</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>System lock screen:</strong>The first one is a must.</li>\n        <li><strong>App lock:</strong>The second step is to prevent the environment from being unlocked.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chat lock:</strong>Hide and re-open a single session.</li>\n        <li><strong>Two-step verification:</strong>The main force for remote account hacking, complementary to application lock.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>limitations and cautions</h2>\n      <p>App lock cannot stop you from handing your phone to a scammer to scan the associated code, nor can it stop you from sending the verification code. Biometric identification may be forced to unlock in some judicial scenarios, and people with high sensitivity can evaluate the pure PIN strategy.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The three minutes it takes to turn on App Lock often end up regretting paying for \"borrowing the phone for a minute\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The application lock is a small personal lock that forms a bedroom door + safe combination with the system lock screen, rather than a replacement.</p>\n<h2>Differences between different brands of mobile phones</h2>\n      <p>Some domestic customized systems will require you to re-unlock WhatsApp after killing the background; you can whitelist WhatsApp in battery optimization to avoid the application lock being ineffective and annoying to turn off the function. iPhone users should also be aware of the exposure of notification previews in lock screen widgets.</p>\n      <p>If you temporarily turn off biometrics (injury, gloves) while traveling, make sure the system PIN is strong enough rather than disabling App Lock entirely.</p>\n<h2>Accessibility in case of emergency</h2>\n      <p>If you are worried that your family will not be able to read WhatsApp to contact the doctor in the event of a sudden illness, you can prepare a medical contact card written in a separate envelope instead of closing the app lock for a long time. Security and accessibility can be designed in layers: daily locks, emergency information on paper or phone quick dial.</p>\n<h2>Fingerprint recognition decline in old age</h2>\n      <p>If the recognition fails when the fingerprint is worn, the elderly may angrily close the app lock. Use your device PIN as a fallback for App Lock instead, or record a few extra fingers. It is better to be patient and debug once than to run naked for a long time.</p>\n<h2>List of model differences</h2>\n      <p>Samsung, Xiaomi, and OPPO have different menu paths. When you buy a new phone and migrate, you need to re-enable the app lock. Don't assume that Switch Assistant will inherit security settings. Keep the electronic version of the manual and check the check items during the replacement week.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of App Lock to people who often lend your phone to take pictures to reduce accidental deletions.</p>\n<p>It is still recommended to turn on the app lock when charging at night and putting it in the bedroom to prevent your partner from misinforming your child about inappropriate content.</p>\n<p>Instructional video sent to elders: How to open and close the app lock to reduce mis-opening.</p>\n<p>It is normal for fingerprints to fail when your hands are wet. The backup PIN should be easy to remember but not the same six-digit number.</p>\n<p>Users with disabled hands can use system accessibility alternatives without having to turn off the entire machine lock screen.</p>\n<p>After changing the bangs film on a business trip, re-enter the fingerprint to reduce accidental locking.</p>"},"23-chat-lock-disappearing":{"category":"Protection settings","title":"Chat locking, ephemeral and disappearing messages: reducing residual exposure","excerpt":"These functions cannot prevent the account from being taken over, but they can reduce the risk of the phone being browsed and historical messages retained for a long time. Also understand that screenshots and reposts still exist.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Even if the account has not been stolen, retaining the information for a long time will bring risks such as disputes, leakage after a breakup, and misunderstandings among colleagues. WhatsApp provides functions such as chat locking, disappearing messages by default, and one-time viewing of media to reduce \"old conversations lying on the phone.\" They cannot prevent account takeover, but they can reduce the probability of physical review and old records being dug out.</p>\n\n      <h2>chat lock</h2>\n      <p>The specified chat can be moved to a locked folder, and opening requires re-verification of biometrics or PIN. The list does not display content previews by default. Suitable for sharing mobile phones or situations where you don’t want the conversation to be seen at a glance. Note: A record may still be left on the other party's device; locking does not replace 2-step verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>disappear message</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cycles such as 24 hours/7 days/90 days can be enabled for individual chats (subject to the version).</li>\n        <li>Reduces the risk of \"a sentence from three years ago being screenshotted\", but cannot prevent instant screenshots and forwarding.</li>\n        <li>It is necessary to communicate with members before starting a group chat to avoid misunderstandings and evidence being deleted.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>View media all at once</h2>\n      <p>Suitable for sending sensitive pictures/videos. The recipient can only open it once, and there is a mechanism to limit screenshots (subject to official capabilities). You still have to trust that the other party will not use a second device to remake the video.</p>\n\n      <h2>Use boundaries</h2>\n      <p>These features are not bulletproof; account sovereignty still relies on verification code discipline and related equipment inspections. It will not prove to the other party for you that \"this statement has never existed\" - please consult a professional for legal scenarios.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Make an agreement with your family: You must verbally inform the disappearing message before opening it to avoid arguments over \"Why did you delete it?\"</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Use locked and disappearing messages as hygiene tools: less dirt does not mean no dirt. Sovereign security still depends on doorknobs and keys.</p>\n<h2>Balance point in communication with customers</h2>\n      <p>If disappearing messages are turned on in business chat, it may conflict with the \"queryable records\" expected by customers. You can use lock chat + disappear internally, and retain emails or CRM copies externally; industries with legal retention obligations need to consult compliance. Technology and systems must be designed together.</p>\n<h2>Difficulties in Obtaining Evidence from Disputes</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages may leave you without chat records when defending your rights. For transactions that may require evidence, use email or a time-stamped contract system to leave traces; disappearing messages are suitable for purely emotional or low-controversy scenarios. Think beforehand, \"Will these words still be there in a year's time?\" before choosing a strategy.</p>\n<h2>Locking and Guardianship of Minors</h2>\n      <p>When parents lock sensitive chats for their children, they should also maintain crisis dialogue channels (such as direct phone calls) to prevent their children from being in danger but unable to call for help due to the lock. Lockdown is about reducing exposure, not cutting off parent-child communication.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Disappearing messages are suitable for low-risk small talk; please use auditable channels for contracts and money topics. Feature selection matches risk level.</p>\n<p>Check the locked chat list regularly and delete items you no longer need to reduce the risk of forgetting to open it.</p>\n<p>Before writing a lawyer's letter or defending your rights, please inquire whether you need to retain the original chat export record.</p>\n<p>After updating the system, re-enable the disappearing message option to prevent upgrade reset.</p>\n<p>When the work machine and personal account are separated, they each evaluate different disappearing message strategies.</p>\n<p>After backup and restoration, check whether the chat lock directory is still in effect and re-lock it if necessary.</p>\n<p>Inform the other party of your expectations before opening the disappearing message to avoid misunderstandings about cooperation.</p>\n<p>Export necessary chat PDFs the night before a major system version upgrade for future reference.</p>\n<p>Tonight, open a lock chat to check whether it takes effect, ten seconds.</p>"},"24-business-vs-personal":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Business ID and Personal ID: Anti-theft and anti-counterfeiting in store operations","excerpt":"Customer base, order communication, employee management - business scenarios have additional risks. How to isolate permissions and prevent mass fraud after the customer service account is taken over.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Stolen personal accounts hurt family and friends; stolen store, micro-business, and customer service accounts will send \"temporary payment code replacement\" and \"additional freight payment\" to customers within a few hours, directly damaging the brand and cash flow. Business scenarios are also superimposed with amplification factors such as employee hosting, multi-machine login, and tablet placement at the counter.</p>\n\n      <h2>Permissions and architecture recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The main business account must be opened for two-step verification, and the PIN must be placed in the corporate password database, and it will be rotated upon resignation.</li>\n        <li>Limit the number of associated devices and remove all mobile devices on the day employees leave.</li>\n        <li>Public tablets are not allowed to hang up sessions 24 hours a day; set a strong screen lock and app lock.</li>\n        <li>The collection account is regularly disclosed to the public and customers are educated: temporary code changes in chat require a second confirmation.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Customer Clarification Template</h2>\n      <p>\"The payment account of our store is subject to the store notice/official website. If you ask to change the payment method through WhatsApp private message, please call the store first to verify.\" The earlier you send it, the less likely you are to follow the trend of transfers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Three mistakes in employee training</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>You are not allowed to send the store number verification code to your personal WeChat to help you log in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan unfamiliar \"merchant certification\" or \"traffic support\" codes.</li>\n        <li>If an abnormal mass message is found: disconnect the conversation first, follow the recapture process, and clarify through all channels.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Isolate from personal number</h2>\n      <p>If it can be solved with enterprise tools, don't put all customers on private accounts; even if you are still operating on personal accounts, you must separate devices, PINs, and backup strategies.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"customer fund security\" into the KPI and assess awareness alongside sales.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Business number theft prevention is a business continuity project. The permissions are minimized, the equipment is listed, and the payment verification is standardized, which is cheaper than a public relations draft.</p>\n<h2>Multi-platform payment consistency</h2>\n      <p>In addition to WhatsApp, customers may also inquire on Facebook and Instagram. All channels publicize the same set of collection accounts, and take regular screenshots and archives. When employees take over the account, they sign a custody agreement and make it clear that the verification code will not be shared.</p>\n      <p>Doing an \"account health check\" before a major promotion is easier than chasing money after the promotion.</p>\n<h2>Insurance and emergency plans</h2>\n      <p>Evaluate whether commercial insurance covers customer compensation due to online fraud. Prepare a brief external statement template and temporary collection freeze process with a lawyer. Account theft is also a business continuity event and should be written into the company's emergency manual and rehearsed.</p>\n<h2>Outsourced customer service account</h2>\n      <p>When outsourcing teams share customer service accounts, they must manage who logs in every day through a list; the associated equipment must be deleted at the end of the shift. The outsourcer contract includes leak liability and verification code confidentiality clauses, and training records are kept on file.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Customers trust your brand, not some random WhatsApp message. By standardizing the core, the brand will not be easily penetrated by a piece of false news.</p>\n<p>End-of-quarter inventory: Are there any unusual collections this quarter? In customer complaints, accounts are screened for fraudulent use of keywords.</p>\n<p>The holiday duty list indicates who holds the customer service number to avoid unattended exceptions during the handover vacuum period.</p>\n<p>New customer contracts specify official contact information to reduce bargaining space for fake accounts.</p>\n<p>The logo watermark is added to the official poster image, making it easy for customers to identify the authenticity of the payment collection image and text.</p>\n<p>Annual audit samples whether employees know the store number verification code confidentiality system.</p>\n<p>At noon, the shift handover will give a verbal report on whether there are any abnormal logins, and then the business will be resumed at the next shift.</p>\n<p>The first mobile phone you use when closing a store should be to check related equipment.</p>\n<p>Print cashier notice: Only recognize the QR code at the counter for payment.</p>"},"25-new-phone-migration":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Anti-theft checklist for mobile phone migration: Keep your account number when switching between old and new mobile phones","excerpt":"The switch window is the easiest to panic: backup, verification, linking devices, and two-step verification may be skipped. A migration day checklist to help you make the switch smoothly and safely.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>On the day of switching phones, the mental bandwidth is occupied by \"photos transferred or not\", and security settings are most easily skipped: forgetting to enable two-step verification, forgetting to kick out the old web version, or asking others to scan the link for you in the store. Attackers love this window of time - you'd rather hand over your phone to save time.</p>\n\n      <h2>24 hours before replacement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the backup is up to date and the encryption key is available.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification email can log in.</li>\n        <li>List computer sessions to keep and plan to reconnect after migration.</li>\n        <li>Write down an emergency contact number (not WhatsApp).</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Order on the day of replacement</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Only install official WhatsApp on the new phone; start the verification while the old phone is still in hand.</li>\n        <li>When entering the registration code, cover your shoulders; do not give the code to the clerk.</li>\n        <li>After restoring the backup, immediately confirm that two-step verification is still on.</li>\n        <li>Associated device page: Kick the old session, and then scan the QR code on the new computer as needed.</li>\n        <li>Turn on app lock, check privacy and group invitation restrictions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Old machine disposal</h2>\n      <p>After confirming that the new phone is normal, exit WhatsApp or restore the old phone to factory settings; confirm again that the iCloud/Google account has been logged out before selling.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The two machines were online at the same time for too long, and the old machine was lost and not activated.</li>\n        <li>I forgot to migrate the backup key, and all chats were empty.</li>\n        <li>The clerk scanned the unknown QR code on behalf of the operator.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Mark the calendar in red on the day of the replacement: migration in the morning and safety inspection in the afternoon, so as not to mix the two things together.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The goal of migration: the only trusted master device, a clean association list, continuous two-step verification, and no key loss.</p>\n<h2>Cross-ecosystem migration (iOS ↔ Android)</h2>\n      <p>When changing operating systems, WhatsApp chat history migration is limited by official tools and may not be completely transferred. Export necessary contracts and invoices to PDF in advance; be more wary of over-the-shoulder peeping when verifying numbers, because users are often anxious about operating in physical stores. After the migration is complete, be sure to redo the associated device and two-step verification. Don't assume that \"the device replacement assistant will help you with security.\"</p>\n<h2>Data erasure certification</h2>\n      <p>Before selling the old machine, use the official factory reset method and confirm that the account has been removed in the settings. Some Android models require additional SD card formatting. People who buy second-hand phones also use a new account to log in, a new two-step verification, and do not trust any remaining sessions of the previous owner.</p>\n<h2>Watches and secondary cards</h2>\n      <p>If the eSIM watch can receive text messages independently, confirm which screen the verification code is sent to when migrating. After activating the new phone, test the routing of WhatsApp calls and text messages to avoid the verification code being left on the old watch and being seen by others.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>No matter how busy the replacement window is, there are still ten minutes left for the Five Steps to Safety. The joy of migration is based on the fact that the account is still in your own hands.</p>\n<p>On the night of the migration, I sent a non-WhatsApp announcement to my friends: \"Successful phone change, don't believe in scams.\"</p>\n<p>Tear off the ICCID information on the old mobile phone card tray and packaging box before discarding.</p>\n<p>Export CSV backup of friends' business cards, and check whether the import is complete after migration.</p>\n<p>Enter fingerprints after the film is affixed to the new phone to avoid recognition failure caused by film replacement later.</p>\n<p>If your child's old watch is bound to an old number, remember to unbind it and then bind it to the new phone after changing the phone.</p>\n<p>On the night of migration, do not open unfamiliar web pages to \"test speed\" and focus on the official process.</p>\n<p>The SIM pin of old phones should be stored properly to prevent it from being thrown away and picked up.</p>\n<p>A safety review of the new machine will be conducted every night for ten minutes in the first week, which is only one week.</p>"},"26-group-scams":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"Group Fraud and Administrator Risks: The Full Link from Group Pulling to False Notifications","excerpt":"Strange groups, fake customer service groups, and announcements after administrators are stolen—groups are areas of high-frequency fraud. Teach you how to set the threshold for group entry and identify fake notifications.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Groups provide the psychological safety of \"most people are present\", but they are also a loudspeaker for the expansion of fraud. Groups created by strangers, fake customer service groups, and phishing announcements made after the administrator's account has been stolen—all take advantage of your assumption that \"not everyone in the group is a scammer.\"</p>\n\n      <h2>Danger group portrait</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>After a strange account adds you to the group, it immediately forces you to add a \"specialist\" for a private chat.</li>\n        <li>There are only short links in the announcement for \"real-name registration\" and \"subsidy receipt\".</li>\n        <li>Time-limited threats: refund, fine, or call the police if no action is taken.</li>\n        <li>Screenshots are not allowed to be uploaded to outsiders, and \"only listen to administrators\" is required.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>personal protection</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Privacy limits who can add you to the group.</li>\n        <li>For strangers, observe first and do not click on links; exit and report if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Family and company core groups: Administrators must enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notices involving changes in payment collection and reimbursement: confirmation through the second channel.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Administrator Responsibilities</h2>\n      <p>Regularly clean up zombie accounts; start new member review; withdraw abnormal announcements first and then clarify through multiple channels; backup deputy administrator for key groups.</p>\n\n      <h2>Administrator account stolen</h2>\n      <p>Take it back immediately → Send \"Ignore the link just now\" to other members → Revoke the invitation link → Rebuild the group as appropriate and verify the membership identity.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The bigger the group is, the more important it is to write a group announcement that says \"confirm key points with the administrator via private message first\".</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Groups are trust amplifiers. The group entry threshold, administrator reinforcement, and double confirmation of key actions can block most explosive scams in the group.</p>\n<h2>Fake documents in the \"read-only group\"</h2>\n      <p>After the administrator sets the group to be read-only, scammers may use the \"Documents\" tab to send APKs or \"Order Forms\" instead. Members are reminded: group files are equally suspicious as group links. You can turn off the permission for ordinary members to send files, or write clearly \"This group does not send installation packages\" in the group announcement.</p>\n      <p>Investment and rebate category names often include emojis and emergency words, and keyword family filters can be set to remind each other.</p>\n<h2>On-chain enthusiasm and FOMO</h2>\n      <p>Robots or shills in the group will fake screenshots of \"on board\" and \"doubled\" to create a fear of missing out. When I see the order posted, I first ask: Can it be verified on-chain? Can I confirm by voice? When the group leader makes huge profits, the default script is suspicious. There is no shame in quitting the group, but it will be difficult to speak out if the money is gone.</p>\n<h2>Fake official assistant number</h2>\n      <p>Most of the \"WhatsApp Assistant\" and \"Security Bot\" that appear in the group are private accounts renamed by scammers. Genuine function notifications will not require codes via private chat. If you see a bot claiming to be a bot, ignore it and check with real official channels.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>It's free to leave the group, but announcements of falsehoods are expensive. If something goes wrong, retreat first and then verify. This is more important than saving face.</p>\n<p>Propose to the group owner to enable new member approval, and one setting will benefit the entire group.</p>\n<p>There are often benefits from raising accounts, so don’t judge the authenticity by their popularity. Thinking for a minute before taking action can avoid most FOMO.</p>\n      <p>If you have invested money in the group, stop adding money immediately, keep the evidence, and seek help through banks and formal channels.</p>\n<p>Every group does not have to be active, and streamlining the group list equals a reduced attack surface.</p>\n<p>For abnormal accounts that rarely speak but frequently change group names, the group owner should directly remove them.</p>\n<p>Set invitation links to rotate regularly to reduce the risk of attracting people for a long time after leaks.</p>\n<p>If you join the group and don't talk for a week, just post screenshots, mark it for observation.</p>\n<p>Do not disturb at night still requires all announcements from the security group Aite.</p>\n<p>Be suspicious first when you see a screenshot of a sudden fortune, and then make a judgment based on the logic of the benefits.</p>\n<p>Exiting the abnormal group is not rude, it is a stop loss.</p>"},"27-ai-voice-impersonation":{"category":"Spot the scam","title":"AI Voice and Deep Impersonation: When “Sounding Like You” Is No Longer Enough","excerpt":"Synthetic voice also brings new risks to “call verification”. Establish stronger core questions and dual-channel confirmation to avoid relying solely on voiceprint perception.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>\"Always call when borrowing money\" was once a golden rule, but now it needs to be upgraded: AI voice cloning can imitate the tone of your relatives and friends in a few seconds, and then match the contextual pressure in the stolen WhatsApp. Technological progress has not eliminated verification, but only increased the depth of verification.</p>\n\n      <h2>New rule: focus on the body, not just the sounds</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Ask random details known only to both parties to avoid fixed questions being pickpocketed in advance.</li>\n        <li>Request instant video to show your face + raise your hand to match a random number.</li>\n        <li>Enable delayed arrival or double review for large amounts.</li>\n        <li>The family has a default \"borrowing code\", and if it cannot be spoken, it will not be changed.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Old rules still apply</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You can dial the old number in your address book without chatting with the new number.</li>\n        <li>Reject irreversible payments such as gift cards and virtual currency.</li>\n        <li>The more you press for confidentiality and immediateness, the more you have to slow down.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to reduce cloned material</h2>\n      <p>Do not post long high-definition voice/videos on public social media; privacy settings limit strangers from downloading. It cannot be completely eliminated, but it can increase the cost of attacks.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Technology is changing, but the principle remains the same: major financial actions require identity verification beyond the current chat window.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Don’t give up on phone verification because of AI, make it harder: knowledge questions + video presence + late payment combo.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade process for corporate finance positions</h2>\n      <p>The company should require that any change to the supplier's account must be made by calling back the phone number recorded in the contract and signed by both parties. Even if the voice sounds like the boss's, follow the process - a real boss should feel safe from the process, not offended.</p>\n      <p>Fixed greeting codes agreed by both parties can be recorded and changed from time to time to reduce the possibility of the AI ​​model overfitting historical audio.</p>\n<h2>Cross-border jet lag and sleepy decisions</h2>\n      <p>Early morning phone calls are the easiest to break through the defense line. Agreed family rules: Borrowing requests between 23:00 and 7:00 will be processed after dawn. If there is a real emergency, you should go to 120/official emergency channels instead of WhatsApp transfer. AI voice + time difference is a common combination.</p>\n<h2>Public voice sample management</h2>\n      <p>Podcasts and live broadcasts will leave long-term tone samples. Public figures should use delayed payment and multi-signature processes; ordinary people can also reduce sending long voice circles in public groups to reduce the quality of cloned materials.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Sound can be synthesized and details are difficult to fake. Ask more questions that only a real person can answer, and the AI ​​script will often reveal its secrets.</p>\n<p>The verification process is reiterated during the rotation of financial positions. Newcomers cannot make payments alone if they do not know the password.</p>\n<p>The company can stipulate that two people must be present to sign the payment beyond the threshold, and the system will delay the payment by one hour to gain time for AI voice verification.</p>\n      <p>Relatives agree that the secret code should be changed every Spring Festival and should be written in the housework book instead of chatting.</p>\n<p>Video conference recordings of large-scale decisions are kept, and whether the voice is AI can be traced afterwards.</p>\n<p>The voice message mailbox can be short to reduce the length of training materials that are crawled in batches.</p>\n<p>If public speaking recordings need to be released, the key may be slightly changed or BGM may be added to increase the cost of cloning.</p>\n<p>Large-scale expenditures resolved by the board of directors still need to be signed and archived in paper.</p>\n<p>After the lunch break, large transfers will be forced to cool down for thirty minutes before confirmation.</p>\n<p>Couples transfer large sums to each other by voice and code, and do not skip steps because of intimacy.</p>\n<p>When asking for money for kidnapping, first call 110 and then transfer the money.</p>\n<p>Test your family's secret code and practice your skills.</p>"},"28-teens-kids-safety":{"category":"home protection","title":"Teenagers using WhatsApp: Prevent account theft, inducement and guardianship boundaries","excerpt":"Young people are socially active and have different defenses, making them easy targets for deception and inducement. Setting suggestions, dialogue scripts and help-seeking mechanisms for parents.","read":"14 minutes","body":"<p>Teenagers are better at using mobile phones than their elders, but they may not be more aware of the risks: game transactions, fan circle draws, campus rumors, and love blackmail all make \"give a verification code\" and \"send a selfie\" a matter of course. The goal of parents is not to monitor everything, but to have clear red lines and not be punished for asking for help.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red lines agreed with children</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Verification codes, ID photos, and bank cards will not be given to netizens.</li>\n        <li>Skin recharge is done through the official platform and no private WhatsApp transfers are allowed.</li>\n        <li>Threatened to expose privacy: tell parents first, rather than paying first.</li>\n        <li>Don't pretend to be the \"cracked version\" or \"double version\" passed down by your classmates.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Parental Assistance Setup</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; use the family negotiation plan to recover your email.</li>\n        <li>Limit group invitations; avatars are restrained from strangers.</li>\n        <li>System and App Lock are on; check associated devices together regularly.</li>\n        <li>Teach people to block reports instead of being abusive.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>communication skills</h2>\n      <p>Use case discussions instead of shaming; ensure \"disclaimer of responsibility before reporting suspicious cases\". Concealment is often due to fear of having the phone confiscated.</p>\n\n      <h2>school and peers</h2>\n      <p>Share official anti-fraud resources with your class teacher; encourage children to spread the word “It’s a scam” to their peers instead of ridiculing the victim twice.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Teenagers need coaching parents, not jailer parents.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Education + settings + friendly help channels can reduce risks in the long term better than peeking in chats.</p>\n<h2>Campus second-hand and game play</h2>\n      <p>\"Low-priced skins\" and \"playing deposits\" are often accompanied by verification code hacking or nude chatting for blackmail. Make an agreement with your children: Any assistance involving account login will be rejected and a screenshot will be sent to the parents. School psychological counseling channels should be incorporated into digital blackmail disposal.</p>\n      <p>Encouraging teenagers to be peer educators is more acceptable than simply preaching from parents.</p>\n<h2>School Rules and Digital Discipline</h2>\n      <p>Cooperate with the school to write \"no verification code\" into the student handbook as an example, and educate it in the same batch as anti-bullying. An anonymous channel for reporting suspicious accounts will be established within the class to protect the reporter. Teenagers reminding each other is more effective than parents fighting alone.</p>\n<h2>Mental health and blackmail</h2>\n      <p>Teenagers may have increased suicidal thoughts after nude chat blackmail, and parents should seek professional psychological assistance before discovering abnormal chat locks. The contract stipulates that \"anything can be discussed first and dealt with later\", which is more life-saving than simply confiscating the mobile phone.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Children are willing to tell you early, which can reduce losses more than peeking and chatting. Write \"no penalty for reporting\" into the family agreement and implement it.</p>\n<p>Share anti-fraud resources with school psychologists to let children know that they can go to the teacher if they are scolded.</p>\n<p>Guide teenagers to pay attention to official anti-fraud accounts instead of looking for novel fraud collections to reduce the risk of imitating criminal methods.</p>\n      <p>Parents should not open modified apps themselves. Leading by example is more convincing than reasoning.</p>\n<p>Establish a family \"suspicious event bonus\": proactively report scam tactics and reward milk tea coupons to provide positive incentives for reporting.</p>\n<p>The game voice channel is also prohibited from sending verification codes, which is consistent with WhatsApp rules.</p>\n<p>The first rule of the dormitory agreement can be written: Whoever asks for the verification code gets a treat - use humor to remember the rules.</p>\n<p>Do not use personal WhatsApp to log in to the stadium screen together with the collective machine at the sports meeting.</p>\n<p>You only need to forward this topic once per semester to the parent group.</p>\n<p>The class teacher only needs to know about it, and there is no need to disclose the details of the child's deception to the whole class.</p>\n<p>The playground number change must be reported to parents on the same day.</p>\n<p>The class meeting shared that anti-fraud is more effective than punishment.</p>"},"29-travel-roaming":{"category":"Scenario topic","title":"Travel and roaming: WhatsApp anti-theft tips when traveling and on business trips","excerpt":"Changing cards, renting an airplane, hotel Wi-Fi, public computers, travel scenarios add risks. Three checklists before departure, during the trip, and after returning home.","read":"13 minutes","body":"<p>Traveling is compounded by unfamiliar networks, temporary SIMs, eSIMs, hotel computers, and jet lag fatigue—these are four factors that make it easy for verification codes and associated devices to be lost. Turning on the switch before departure is an order of magnitude easier than regaining your account in the early morning in a foreign country.</p>\n\n      <h2>Before departure</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Confirm that the two-step verification and email address can be logged in from different locations.</li>\n        <li>App lock is on; lock high-sensitivity chats if necessary.</li>\n        <li>Complete the encrypted backup and confirm that the key is available offline.</li>\n        <li>Record the operator and international roaming loss reporting method, please refer to the official channels.</li>\n        <li>Tell your family: Check carefully any news about borrowing money while traveling.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>on the road</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Avoid hotel business centers keeping WhatsApp logged in.</li>\n        <li>Do not scan the QR codes for \"Tax Refund\", \"Expedited Visa\", \"Free Tickets for Attractions\" and unknown origins.</li>\n        <li>Use a rented mobile phone to log in to your main account with caution; log out after use and check related devices.</li>\n        <li>Block verification codes when entering in public environments; be wary of shoulder peeping and fake Wi-Fi portals.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>48 hours after returning home</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Associated equipment cleaning, kicking off temporary travel sessions.</li>\n        <li>If you have used a local SIM, confirm that there is no abnormal call transfer.</li>\n        <li>Rotate 2-Step Verification PIN as appropriate.</li>\n        <li>Review whether any suspicious links have been clicked during the trip, and reinforce them if so.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>Write \"clean up on the first day back home\" in your unpacking list and put it alongside the laundry.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>Travel anti-theft = less frequent use of unfamiliar devices + no long sessions + review after returning home. Treat safety as a module of your journey, rather than something you have to make up for later.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and dual-SIM switching</h2>\n      <p>After the travel eSIM is activated, confirm which card receives WhatsApp text messages; avoid the old card being copied by local profiteers while it is still inserted in the phone. After uninstalling the travel eSIM when you return home, recheck the two-step verification and associated devices to prevent QR codes scanned during roaming from leaving behind sessions.</p>\n      <p>If there is a portal login page for Wi-Fi on long-distance flights, pay attention to whether the brand is fake and induced to install the certificate.</p>\n<h2>Keep your ID and mobile phone separate</h2>\n      <p>When your backpack is stolen, losing your mobile phone and passport together will make card replacement and identity verification even more difficult. Store them separately, and store the scanned passport in the cloud in an encrypted manner. When travel insurance covers equipment theft, the report number is important for claim settlement, and the terms of the policy shall prevail.</p>\n<h2>SIM vendor at border crossing</h2>\n      <p>Port cheap SIMs may be programmed with malicious SMS forwarding. Try to purchase the card at the official airport counter and check the operator's app for service activation as soon as possible. Check related equipment on the first day you enter the country, don’t wait until you return home.</p>\n<h2>Summary retelling</h2>\n      <p>Travel photo albums can be posted, but verification codes and login codes cannot. Before posting to Moments, check whether the screenshot is included in the OTP.</p>\n<p>A calendar reminder will be set after the return ticket is issued: safe review on the day of landing.</p>\n<p>Use the email prefix instead of the full number on the contact card hanging on your suitcase to reduce the chance that the number will be copied and used for social work.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and backup machine in the hotel safe, and keep your phone at eye level.</p>\n<p>If you need to scan the QR code to bind a car rental GPS, use an unfamiliar car Wi-Fi connection and scan again.</p>\n<p>When queuing up at customs on your return trip, don’t borrow a stranger’s mobile phone to make calls, and use anti-related equipment to scan the QR code.</p>\n<p>In the first week after returning, the number of phone calls from strangers increased, keeping the threshold high.</p>\n<p>Do not put a courier slip with your mobile phone number on the outside of the suitcase.</p>\n<p>Do not allow strangers to check in at the hotspot when standing next to the tax refund queue at the airport.</p>"},"30-master-checklist":{"category":"Overview","title":"HoldLine Anti-Theft Master Manual: 30-Day Hardening Plan and Lifetime Checklist","excerpt":"Turn the first 29 into actionable plans: what to do today, this week, this month, every quarter, and a one-page process for the day it was stolen.","read":"16 minutes","body":"<p>After reading thirty articles but not taking action, the effect is approximately zero. This article compiles all the methods into a schedule: what you want today, what you want this week, what you want every quarter, and the \"one page\" on the day it was stolen. You can print it and put it on your wall, or set a recurring reminder in your calendar.</p>\n\n      <h2>Today (must do within 60 minutes)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification + recovery email + keep PIN safe.</li>\n        <li>Check and clean associated devices.</li>\n        <li>Sync with family members: No one will give you the verification code.</li>\n        <li>System strong screen lock + WhatsApp app lock.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the client is from the official store.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Completed within this week</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The privacy page has been tightened one by one: avatar, group invitation, and status.</li>\n        <li>If backup is needed, start end-to-end encrypted backup and keep the key.</li>\n        <li>15-minute family meeting: practice cheat codes and loan verification.</li>\n        <li>Clean up unfamiliar groups and suspicious contacts.</li>\n        <li>Business users take stock of who has access to the host device and computer sessions.</li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h2>Extension this month</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Help elders/young people complete the same basic reinforcement.</li>\n        <li>Ask your operator if they can add card replacement protection.</li>\n        <li>The whole family collects and regains the entrance to the process.</li>\n        <li>Evaluate chat lock and disappearing message strategies.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>30-minute review every quarter</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Scan the associated device again; optionally update the PIN.</li>\n        <li>Confirm that the email and backup keys are still accessible.</li>\n        <li>Review whether there were any code requests, fake votes, or screen sharing requests during the quarter.</li>\n        <li>Update emergency contact lists and family passwords.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>One page on the day it was stolen</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>This machine re-verifies the number and the registration code will not be transmitted externally.</li>\n        <li>Clear associated devices and reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Notify relatives and friends via text messages/phone calls to stop losses.</li>\n        <li>If you have lost money, contact your bank and, if necessary, go to the local police.</li>\n        <li>Review the entrance and fill the gap.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Four sentences for life:</strong>The verification code is the key; the two-step verification is the second door; the associated equipment is the backdoor list; borrowing money and transferring money require double channel verification. Memorizing it is more effective than collecting thirty articles.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>how to last</h2>\n      <p>Select a family \"digital contact\" to be responsible for reminding quarterly reviews; the company can incorporate it into monthly IT reports. The value of a habit lies in repetition, not one-time perfection.</p>\n\n      <h2>summary</h2>\n      <p>The end point of the 30 HoldLine articles is not to become a researcher, but to turn sobriety into an agenda. If you complete the five must-do items today, you will have outperformed most users - leave the rest to your schedule to fill in slowly.</p>\n<h2>Embed lists into existing habits</h2>\n      <p>Bind \"Associated Device Check\" to brushing your teeth; put \"Quarterly PIN Rotation\" on the same day as rent payment; the company can write reviews into the OKR security education completion rate. Automated reminders beat hard memorization.</p>\n      <p>If you only remember one of the 30 articles, please go back to the first verification code discipline; if you are willing to implement one article, please implement this timetable. Knowledge + schedule equals the real HoldLine.</p>\n<h2>Assess your own completion</h2>\n      <p>Quarterly self-assessment: 0–3 points: verification code discipline, two-step verification, associated devices, backup keys, and transfer verification. Items with scores lower than 2 points will be given priority to make up classes next week. The points system is more honest than \"seems to have done it all.\" Invite family members to evaluate each other and increase social supervision.</p>\n<h2>Version numbers and updates</h2>\n      <p>This list and official App functions are based on the current common version. Scan the setting path again after major version updates. Add the HoldLine homepage to your browser favorites and review it every quarter to see if there are any new scam topics worth reading for the whole family.</p>"}},"news":{"01-singapore-spf-2024-06":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore police warns: WhatsApp account takeover scam is on the rise again","excerpt":"Scammers use stolen accounts to ask for verification codes from address books, or use video calls to request screen sharing to peek at the six-digit code, and then pretend to be the victim to borrow money or defraud bank card information.","body":"<p>On June 7, 2024, the Singapore Police Force issued an anti-fraud notice to remind the public to be alert to the reactivation of WhatsApp account takeover scams. The notice points out that after taking control of the victim's account, scammers will send messages to the victim's address book and use the excuses of \"borrowing money\" or \"buying gift cards on behalf of others\" to obtain transfers; in some cases, they will further ask for bank card photos and payment OTP, resulting in unauthorized deductions.</p>\n      <h2>Two main types of techniques in notifications</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Familiar cheat code:</strong>The victim received a message from the \"contact\" claiming that the verification code was sent incorrectly and please help forward the six-digit number. The contact account has often been stolen.</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen sharing code:</strong>The scammer pretends to be a government or bank employee and initiates a video call, asking to share the screen to \"verify information\", and then triggers the WhatsApp verification code to be displayed on the screen for the other party to see.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>official advice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Verification codes, bank information, and OTP will never be shared externally.</li>\n        <li>Be wary even of unusual requests from address book friends.</li>\n        <li>Use another way to verify your identity before making screen sharing requests.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification; set up a phone lock screen and be mindful of physical contact.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine Interpretation: This notice juxtaposes \"cheating codes\" and \"reading codes from a distance\", indicating that defense cannot just focus on \"whether the numbers can be typed out and sent.\"</p></div>"},"02-singapore-spf-2026-03":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Singapore issues another notice: OTP serial fraud leads to continuous loss of accounts","excerpt":"The scammer enters the victim's number to trigger the OTP, and then the \"acquaintance\" who has already stolen it asks for the code; after he succeeds, he copies the same words to attack the next contact.","body":"<p>On March 12, 2026, SPF once again issued an anti-fraud alert regarding the hacking of WhatsApp accounts. The process is clearly written: the scammer first uses the victim's mobile phone number to try to log in → the system sends an OTP to the victim → a message from an acquaintance whose account has been lost, falsely claiming that \"the verification code was originally mine, but it was sent to you by mistake\" → the victim hands over the OTP and is kicked offline → the scammer uses a new account to continue to deceive the next person.</p>\n      <h2>When do victims usually notice</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You suddenly cannot log into WhatsApp; or</li>\n        <li>The friend informed \"You are asking someone for a verification code.\"</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Reinforcement actions highlighted in the announcement</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked devices: Remove unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification: Enable.</li>\n        <li>Work with official anti-fraud tools such as ScamShield to verify suspicious requests.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The notice stated that no monetary losses were collected at that time, but the loss of the account itself was enough to start the next round of fraud, and the social cost was still high.</p>"},"03-singapore-spf-2026-10":{"region":"Singapore","source":"Singapore Police Force (SPF)","title":"Pretending to borrow money after account hacking: There were at least 128 cases in Singapore in two months, resulting in losses of approximately S$249,000.","excerpt":"Since September 2026, after scammers took over WhatsApp and pretended to be relatives, friends, and colleagues to claim loans or make payments on their behalf, the police announced the number of cases and losses and reiterated ScamShield.","body":"<p>The notice on October 21, 2026 shows: Since September of the same year, at least 128 cases have involved scammers taking control of WhatsApp and pretending to be the victims on social media/instant messaging to borrow money from contacts or ask for \"help to transfer money\", with cumulative losses of at least approximately S$249,000.</p>\n      <h2>common excuses</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The bank transfer limit has been reached, please pay on your behalf.</li>\n        <li>It is inconvenient to transfer the money yourself, so please borrow one first.</li>\n        <li>Pretend to be a child and demand payment for the \"AI/Exchange Project\" fee.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>POLICE ADD-CHECK-TELL FRAMEWORK</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>ADD:</strong>Install ScamShield to filter suspicious calls and text messages.</li>\n        <li><strong>CHECK:</strong>Use official channels to verify; call the person directly for abnormal transfer requests.</li>\n        <li><strong>TELL:</strong>Inform relatives, friends and authorities; if money has been transferred, contact your bank immediately and call the police.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine reminds: Account stealing is often only the first stage, and the second stage is to \"monetize\" the address book. Anti-theft and anti-loan measures should be done together.</p></div>"},"04-switzerland-ncsc-2026-10":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Swiss National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Switzerland NCSC: Voting fraud cases surged in October, with more than 50 related reports in a single month","excerpt":"An acquaintance's account sends a \"Vote for the Kids\" link, and the fake site receives a text message code or induces the user to scan the QR code of the associated device, resulting in takeover or silent synchronization.","body":"<p>NCSC stated in its 43rd weekly report in 2026 that reports of stolen/compromised WhatsApp accounts have increased significantly again; in October alone, more than 50 reports of such scams were received, and many attacks were successful. The core of the technique is \"goodwill voting\": the message comes from the accused account in the address book, urging you to open it in the name of canvassing votes for school or dance competition.</p>\n      <h2>Two killer moves for fake sites</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>It asks you to enter your mobile phone number and \"SMS verification code\" - which is actually a WhatsApp registration code. Filling it out will help the scammer activate your account on a new device.</li>\n        <li>Display the QR code and induce scanning with an \"associated device\" - the account is still available, but the scammer's web page has been hung up.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h2>Institutional recommendations</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Even if you are canvassing for votes with an acquaintance, you should check through another channel first.</li>\n        <li>The verification code will not be transmitted externally or entered into the web page.</li>\n        <li>Only scan the QR code for the official web version on your own device.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification PIN.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>NCSC reminds: Domain names will be constantly changed to bypass filtering, and you cannot rely on \"remembering a bad URL\" once and for all.</p>"},"05-switzerland-ncsc-week21":{"region":"Switzerland","source":"Switzerland NCSC","title":"There has been a wave of \"fake contest voting\" account thefts in Switzerland in the spring and summer, and it is back again in the fall.","excerpt":"The NCSC described the \"vote your children\" rhetoric as early as Week 21; the fall weekly report showed that similar attacks have expanded again, indicating that the rhetoric can be reused for a long time.","body":"<p>The Swiss NCSC has documented in a related weekly report in the spring: Victims receive messages from seemingly acquaintances asking to vote for a child's competition; after clicking, they fall into a scam and risk losing access to WhatsApp. By the fall (week 43), the agency clearly wrote that \"this type of attack is not new, but the recent rise in reports suggests that it is becoming more useful.\"</p>\n      <h2>Why should I remember this alone?</h2>\n      <p>The same country, the same agency, and repeated warnings in two seasons prove that fake voting is not a one-time news, but a \"mature product\" that can be revived seasonally. In particular, family groups and school groups need to preset rules: canvassing links will be verified first by default.</p>\n      <h2>Migration implications for Chinese users</h2>\n      <p>The words can be localized into \"helping to check in\", \"voting in lottery\" and \"charity co-branding\". The structure remains unchanged: forwarding by acquaintances + short-term pressure + asking for or scanning the QR code. The 04th long article of HoldLine corresponds to this structure.</p>"},"06-ireland-ncsc-2024-08":{"region":"Ireland","source":"Ireland’s National Cyber ​​Security Center (NCSC)","title":"Ireland NCSC: CAPTCHA scam escalates, business WhatsApp exposure comes under scrutiny","excerpt":"The advisory document details how scammers first take control of the accounts of acquaintances, and then create a sense of urgency to ask for verification codes; it also reminds that in business scenarios, the loss of accounts may lead to more data risks.","body":"<p>Ireland's NCSC issued a WhatsApp verification code scam advisory in August 2024, describing the attack chain: obtain a mobile phone number → enter the number on the login page to trigger a real verification code → contact the victim through a lost contact identity → ask for the code on the pretext of emergency assistance → take over after success and continue to spread.</p>\n      <h2>Special reminder: for business purposes</h2>\n      <p>The document emphasizes that when corporate employees use WhatsApp to handle work, once the account is lost, not only private chats will be exposed, but also customer communication and internal coordination information. This is mutually confirmed with HoldLine’s “Business Account Special Topic”: Business accounts require stricter equipment and PIN storage systems.</p>\n      <h2>core sentence</h2>\n      <p>Verification codes should never be shared; WhatsApp won’t ask you for them, and neither should anyone else.</p>"},"07-cayman-rcips-2024":{"region":"cayman islands","source":"Royal Cayman Islands Police (RCIPS)","title":"Caribbean police continue to warn: fake local calls + AI voice imitation assists account theft","excerpt":"RCIPS said takeover scams continued; calls were being made from forged local area codes and there were reports of scammers using AI to mimic voices to create credibility.","body":"<p>The Royal Cayman Islands Police has issued multiple updated WhatsApp account takeover scam warnings in 2024. The August Notice reviews the classic path: creating panic through text messages/WhatsApp/phone calls → requesting six-digit verification codes → changing accounts → pretending to be the owner and defrauding contacts. The September update mentioned: Recently, victims have received calls that look like local (345) numbers, which are actually fake caller IDs from abroad; there are reports that accent features are obvious and there is AI deepfake audio imitation.</p>\n      <h2>Police list \"don'ts\"</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don't reply to suspicious messages</li>\n        <li>Do not open attachments</li>\n        <li>Don't click on links in messages</li>\n        <li>Do not generate and send WhatsApp six-digit codes to anyone</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>International Trend Observation: Phone display numbers can be forged, and AI voices can be imitated—“sounding local/like me” no longer equals security.</p></div>"},"08-trinidad-ttcsirt-2024-11":{"region":"Trinidad and Tobago","source":"TTCSIRT","title":"TTCSIRT: Brand Ambassador Voting is a fishing trick, and after success, the business information is changed and continues to spread","excerpt":"The attacker started by saying \"Vote for me to be a brand ambassador\" to defraud the verification code; after taking control of the account, he may change it to business information, and then phish the address book again.","body":"<p>The Trinidad and Tobago Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team (TTCSIRT) warned in November 2024 of an ongoing WhatsApp phishing campaign. Examples of words include asking the other person to vote for you to become a brand ambassador, using a polite, friend-like tone.</p>\n      <h2>attack phase</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Pretend to be someone you know and ask for \"help.\"</li>\n        <li>Trick victims into handing over MFA/verification codes.</li>\n        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>\n        <li>The information may be changed to a business image to increase the credibility of subsequent fraud.</li>\n        <li>Launch the next round of phishing against the victim’s address book.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>The advisory reminds us that the \"Business Account\" mark on the interface does not automatically equal a trusted merchant, especially when the other party suddenly changes their information and starts sending strange requests in groups.</p>"},"09-hongkong-scmp-2024-04":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police/Media Reports (SCMP, etc.)","title":"Hong Kong warns of resurgence of instant messaging robberies: losses exceeded HK$20 million in the first quarter, 90% involved WhatsApp","excerpt":"The Cybersecurity and Technology Crime Bureau announced 864 account hijacking cases in the first quarter, with losses of approximately HK$20.4 million; more than 90% involved WhatsApp.","body":"<p>According to public reports, the Hong Kong police warned of the resurgence of instant messaging account hijacking scams around April 2024: 864 related cases were recorded in the first quarter of the same year, with a loss of approximately HK$20.4 million, of which more than 90% involved WhatsApp. Scammers obtain key information through phishing links and other methods to take over the account, and then read the address book to commit fraud.</p>\n      <h2>Common local follow-up actions</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Impersonate the victim to borrow money from relatives and friends or ask to buy point cards.</li>\n        <li>Use the tone of past chats to reduce suspicion.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>defensive response</h2>\n      <p>The police have always recommended: open two-step verification, check related equipment, click on links carefully, and call to verify before transferring money. Hong Kong users can also cooperate with official fraud detection tools such as Scameter.</p>"},"10-hongkong-2025-03":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong Police (public social media notification)","title":"In March 2025, there were 93 cases of WhatsApp hijacking, with losses exceeding 10 million Hong Kong dollars.","excerpt":"The police remind people to be especially wary of \"urgent requests for money from relatives and friends overseas\"; they emphasize logging out of unknown devices, turning on two-step verification, not clicking on suspicious links, and not sharing verification codes.","body":"<p>Relevant reports in April 2025 quoted police information: In March alone, 93 cases of WhatsApp account robberies were recorded, with losses exceeding HK$10 million. After taking over, the scammer studies the chat history, mimics the tone, and then fabricates a medical or travel emergency to force you to transfer money.</p>\n      <h2>Four self-protection tips for the police (report summary)</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Log out of the unrecognized associated device and enable two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Do not click on suspicious links in WhatsApp.</li>\n        <li>Never share passwords or verification codes.</li>\n        <li>If a contact suddenly asks for money, call first to verify.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Figure description: Even if the overall number of fraud categories has subsequently declined, losses in the tens of millions in a single month are still enough to prove that the threat has not disappeared.</p>"},"11-hongkong-2025-deepfake":{"region":"Hong Kong, China","source":"Hong Kong police disclose the case (media reports)","title":"Huge case in Hong Kong: Hijacking WhatsApp + deepfake voices, involving about HK$145 million in a single case","excerpt":"Scammers monitor chats for a long time, impersonate company personnel and use deepfake technology to imitate voices, inducing victims to transfer virtual assets.","body":"<p>According to relevant case disclosures in 2025, one of the largest fraud cases in Hong Kong that year involved WhatsApp account hijacking, involving approximately HK$145 million. The scammer did not ask for money immediately after he succeeded, but continued to observe the conversation. When committing the crime, he pretended to be a company employee, used deepfake technology to imitate his voice, and instructed the victim through voice messages to transfer the virtual assets to another wallet.</p>\n      <h2>Why is this news important?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Explain that \"sounds like a colleague\" may be synthesized audio.</li>\n        <li>High value transfers require dual person/dual channel confirmation beyond chat and voice.</li>\n        <li>When accounts are synced silently (e.g. by linking devices), scammers can gain tone and business context.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine corresponding reading: AI voice impersonation topic + related equipment manual + business number protection.</p></div>"},"12-uk-actionfraud-code":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"UK Action Fraud: Pretending to be a friend and asking for a six-digit activation code, the number of reports is rising","excerpt":"The scammer first takes over the account of an acquaintance, and then asks for the verification code on the grounds that \"the verification code was sent incorrectly\"; the official suggested THINK. CALL. and two-step verification.","body":"<p>UK anti-fraud agency Action Fraud has warned that it has received more than 60 reports related to WhatsApp account takeovers. After the scammer takes control of someone's account in your address book, they will first exchange normal greetings, and at the same time use your number to trigger a six-digit activation code text message, and then falsely claim that the code was sent incorrectly and ask you to forward it.</p>\n      <h2>Official recommendations for action</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report and block within the app.</li>\n        <li>Never share activation codes.</li>\n        <li><strong>THINK. CALL.</strong>——For abnormal requests, call first to confirm.</li>\n        <li>Settings → Account → Two-Step Verification → Turn on.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Although the warning was issued earlier, the attack template it describes is highly homogeneous with the 2024-2026 notifications from Singapore, Ireland, and Switzerland, indicating that this is a \"standard operating procedure\" that spans multiple years and across countries.</p>"},"13-uk-actionfraud-groups":{"region":"U.K.","source":"UK Action Fraud","title":"Community and religious groups become targets: first deceive the registration code, and then ask for emergency help from the group members","excerpt":"Action Fraud said it had received 268 reports since January of that year; scammers infiltrated large community/religious groups and asked for one-time passwords in the name of video call registration.","body":"<p>Another Action Fraud alert focused on large community, alumni, work and religious WhatsApp groups. The scammer may first use a fake avatar/nickname to gain trust, and then ask for a registration code in the name of \"joining a group video call requires a one-time password\" to move the victim's WhatsApp to a new device. After obtaining it, they can turn on two-step verification to lock the original owner, and ask for an emergency loan from group friends or relatives.</p>\n      <h2>Why group environments are more dangerous</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Members have a culture of \"mutual help\" among themselves, and the success rate of emergency help is higher.</li>\n        <li>Nicknames and avatars in the group are easy to imitate.</li>\n        <li>One takeover can reach dozens or hundreds of people.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Administrators should encourage all members to enable two-step verification and stipulate that any request for code or money in the group must be verified off-site.</p>"},"14-malaysia-mcmc-2026-09":{"region":"Malaysia","source":"Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC)","title":"MCMC: Pay attention to complaints about WhatsApp being hacked/blocked, and be wary of charging for \"recovery services\"","excerpt":"The committee distinguishes between \"account takeover\" and \"platform ban\"; it reminds not to share verification codes and PINs, not to trust paid recovery agencies, and to use official channels.","body":"<p>On September 25, 2026, MCMC issued a media statement stating that it had noticed public concerns about WhatsApp accounts being hacked, blocked or unusable, and had referred relevant cases to Meta for clarification and processing. The statement specifically clarified: After an account is taken over by an illegal person, it may be spammed with fraudulent/spam messages and violate the terms of service, and then be banned by the platform - these are two related but different problems.</p>\n      <h2>Key reminders for users</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Reinstate or appeal through WhatsApp official channels.</li>\n        <li>Don't share verification codes or security PINs.</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification and stay away from suspicious links and unknown apps.</li>\n        <li>Be wary of \"retrieval\" services that require fees, personal information, or verification codes.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>HoldLine added: Anyone who asks you to \"give the verification code to him before you can retrieve it\" may be committing a second fraud.</p></div>"},"15-india-call-merge":{"region":"India","source":"Publicly available technical/police interview reports (such as Indian Express, etc.)","title":"India Observation: OTP phishing, SIM card replacement, combined call code listening and multi-line parallelization","excerpt":"The report combed through how scammers combined social engineering and telecommunications methods to take over WhatsApp, and quoted Meta’s recommendations: never share six-digit codes and enable two-step verification.","body":"<p>Mainstream technology reports in India summarize various takeover paths: OTP phishing (fake customer service/fake friends ask for codes), SIM swapping (receiving text messages after the number card is transferred), short physical contact and then association with WhatsApp Web, and \"merged calls\" that allow the victim to connect to the verification voice and listen to the OTP without knowing it.</p>\n      <h2>Red flags worth remembering</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Received a WhatsApp verification code without requesting it.</li>\n        <li>My friend suddenly wants OTP.</li>\n        <li>Logged out for no reason.</li>\n        <li>Prompt number to register on new device.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Protection combination</h2>\n      <p>The report and Meta's response jointly emphasized: do not share PIN/verification code, enable two-step verification, carefully combine unknown calls, regularly check associated devices, and limit the scope of privacy visibility. For Chinese readers, these entries correspond one-to-one with HoldLine 01–08 and 17.</p>"},"16-eset-linked-devices":{"region":"International (Security Vendor Observation)","source":"ESET and other security research articles","title":"Security manufacturer reminds: Associated devices are synchronized silently, and the phone “looks normal” has also been exposed","excerpt":"The quickest self-check is to open \"Connected Devices\"; unfamiliar browser sessions should be logged out immediately. And emphasize that the two-step verification must be bound to the recovery email address.","body":"<p>Security articles such as ESET point out that many users think that \"if they are not kicked offline, they are not stolen.\" An attacker could actually just hang onto a WhatsApp web/desktop session and read the chat in real time. 30-second self-test method: Settings → Linked Devices, log out if you don’t recognize it.</p>\n      <h2>Other signs of compromise</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>You have to log in again after logging out abnormally.</li>\n        <li>A reply to your unsent message appears.</li>\n        <li>Contact feedback is abnormal.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h2>Importance of recovering your mailbox</h2>\n      <p>The article emphasizes that you must add a recovery email when two-step verification is turned on - if someone else sets a PIN first, the presence of an email may determine whether you can retrieve it in a few minutes or wait for many days. This is consistent with recapture instructions from the WhatsApp Help Center.</p>"},"17-scamshield-explain":{"region":"Singapore","source":"ScamShield/Ask.gov.sg","title":"ScamShield Illustration: Four Steps to WhatsApp Takeover, and Why “Anyone Asking for OTP is a Scam”","excerpt":"The official Q&A breaks down the attack into four steps: triggering OTP, asking for codes from acquaintances, kicking people offline, and spreading impersonation. It also provides two-step verification and related device cleaning guidelines.","body":"<p>The Q&A related to ScamShield in Singapore describes the takeover in a very clear four-step manner: 1) The scammer logs in with your account to trigger the OTP; 2) The stolen contacts come and ask you to forward them; 3) You are logged out after handing them over; 4) The scammer uses your identity to repeatedly deceive others.</p>\n      <h2>Official primary level reminder</h2>\n      <p>Any request to share a WhatsApp OTP, no matter who it appears to be from, should be considered a scam. OTP belongs only to the recipient; neither the platform nor the \"customer service\" will ask for codes like this.</p>\n      <h2>protection list</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Never share OTP</li>\n        <li>Turn on two-step verification</li>\n        <li>Review and remove unfamiliar associated devices</li>\n        <li>If stolen, notify contacts using non-WhatsApp channels</li>\n      </ul>"},"18-whatsapp-official-security":{"region":"Global (platform side)","source":"WhatsApp Security","title":"WhatsApp official safe page: 2-step verification, only authentic ones, group control and reporting tools","excerpt":"The platform reiterates its multi-layered protection and user-operable switches; it is clear that counterfeit clients will bring serious privacy and security risks.","body":"<p>WhatsApp's official security center outlines its default privacy, spam detection, abnormal takeover prompts and other capabilities, and gives users a list of things to do: turn on two-step verification, always use official apps, manage groups and add permissions, learn to identify scams, make good use of blocking and reporting, and re-register to regain the account when necessary.</p>\n      <h2>What it means to readers of \"Download Software Anti-Theft\"</h2>\n      <p>The official position is very clear: use the genuine client and built-in switch first, rather than looking for third-party \"anti-theft plug-ins\" of unknown origin. The sorting of the HoldLine tool page also follows this principle.</p>\n      <h2>The value of reporting</h2>\n      <p>Reporting does not necessarily allow you to immediately recover your losses, but it can help the platform crack down on abusive accounts and reduce the probability that your address book will continue to be targeted accurately.</p>"},"19-jamaica-cirt":{"region":"jamaica","source":"Jamaica CIRT","title":"Jamaica CIRT: Phishing and hijacking coexist, it is emphasized to log out of the associated device and re-enable two-step verification","excerpt":"The alert describes the risk of malicious links and account hijacking; if you suspect it has been compromised, you should clean the associated device, reset the two-step verification, and report phishing to spam@whatsapp.com and other channels (subject to the current official guidelines).","body":"<p>Jamaica's Cyber ​​Security Incident Response Team has issued a rising threat alert for WhatsApp users, noting that scam messages go hand in hand with account hijacking. Phishing may disguise itself as a formal organization and induce people to click on a fake login page or hand over a verification code; after a successful hijack, they can impersonate the victim, read contacts and spread.</p>\n      <h2>Technical actions after the fall (summary of reports)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Settings → Linked Devices: Log out all unknown devices.</li>\n        <li>Re-enable/reset two-step verification.</li>\n        <li>Alert contacts.</li>\n        <li>Report fishing samples according to official instructions.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>This alert is once again aligned with the European, American and Asian press releases: Cleaning associated devices + two-step verification is an international first aid kit.</p>"},"20-meta-scam-accounts-takedown":{"region":"worldwide","source":"Public reports cite Meta data","title":"Platform Governance Profile: Meta reveals WhatsApp account of the Center to Combat Fraudulent Advertising and Related Scams","excerpt":"The report quoted: Meta will cancel more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers in the first half of 2025; at the same time, users are reminded that they still need to protect themselves.","body":"<p>In public statements on technology crimes held in Hong Kong and other regions, reports cited Meta data: In the first half of 2025, more than 6.8 million WhatsApp accounts related to fraud centers were revoked, and a large number of fraudulent advertisements were cleared on other platforms. This shows that the platform side is continuing to crack down, but it cannot replace the two-step verification and verification code discipline on the user side.</p>\n      <h2>The correct way to read this article</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Don’t relax your personal settings just because “the platform will ban your account”.</li>\n        <li>Accounts that are stolen and used for mass messaging may be blocked quickly, but your relatives and friends may have completed the transfer before being blocked.</li>\n        <li>The fastest action I can take is still: don’t give me the code, open a two-step process, clear the equipment, and verify before asking for money.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <div class=\"callout\"><p>The conclusion of the international news collection: from the Caribbean to Western Europe, from Southeast Asia to East Asia, the details of the discourse are different, but the structure is highly similar. Think of HoldLine's 30 long articles as an \"operation manual\" and this page as a wall of evidence for \"why you need to do it now.\"</p></div>"}},"newsIntro":{"title":"International news: Latest observations on WhatsApp theft and account takeover","lead":"The following 20 articles are feature articles compiled by HoldLine based on police, network security agencies and public reports from various countries, focusing on recent trends such as WhatsApp account takeovers, code fraud, fake voting and fake borrowing. The data and dates in this article are from the public release; the interface and loss figures may be adjusted according to subsequent statistics, please refer to the original organization's release."},"toolsIntro":{"title":"Recommended downloads: Software and tools to reduce the risk of WhatsApp theft","lead":"There is no \"one-click anti-theft artifact\". What is really effective is: official WhatsApp, system security capabilities, formal anti-fraud apps, plus two-step verification and verification code discipline. This page only recommends tools that can be obtained from official channels, and clearly states what it can and cannot do."},"tools":{"whatsapp-official":{"tier":"Must install","name":"WhatsApp official client","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"The only instant messaging body that should be installed. Modified versions, enhanced versions, and network disk APKs are all high-risk.","why":["Only official clients will continue to receive security updates, fraud tips, and account recovery capabilities.","Unofficial packages may have built-in backdoors, and end-to-end encryption cannot save \"the endpoint has been compromised.\"","The police and Meta have repeatedly reminded: Only use WhatsApp from the official app store."],"steps":["iPhone: Search WhatsApp Messenger installation/update from App Store only.","Android: Priority is given to Google Play; in some regions, the official version in the official app store of the mobile phone manufacturer is available.","Immediately after installation, turn on two-step verification and check \"Linked Devices\"."],"stepsCaption":"Install from the official store and turn on two-step verification immediately","caution":"Do not download the so-called Plus/GB/Anti-withdrawal version from group files, network disks, or short links.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp Security Center","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/security"},{"label":"Google Play·WhatsApp","href":"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.whatsapp"}]},"whatsapp-desktop":{"tier":"recommend","name":"WhatsApp Desktop/Web (Official)","platforms":"Windows · macOS · Browser","summary":"It is convenient for computer to reply, but it must be connected from the official entrance; log out on the mobile phone immediately after using the public computer.","why":["The official desktop version is much safer than the casually searched \"Web login page\".","Associated devices are common entry points for silent peeping, and regular clients allow you to proactively manage sessions."],"steps":["On mobile WhatsApp → Linked devices → Link new device.","Open the official desktop application on your computer, or manually enter the official webpage address in your browser and scan the QR code.","Do not check Keep logged in when using public devices; return to your mobile phone to kick off the session after use."],"stepsCaption":"Device associated with mobile phone → official scan code → log out after use","caution":"Never scan the \"Login/Vote\" QR code displayed in chat or on an unfamiliar website.","links":[{"label":"WhatsApp desktop download (official website)","href":"https://www.whatsapp.com/download"}]},"builtin-security":{"tier":"Must open","name":"WhatsApp built-in security (free)","platforms":"In-app settings","summary":"Two-step verification, Scam Alert, app lock, privacy restrictions - no need to install a third-party \"anti-theft plug-in\".","why":["Two-step verification directly increases the cost of taking over, which is a standard recommendation in police drafts in various countries.","Scam Alert can prompt suspicious sessions on the device side (depending on whether your version provides it).","App Lock reduces the risk of \"the phone being unlocked and being opened easily\"."],"steps":["Settings → Account → Two-step verification → Enable and bind a login email address.","Find Scam Alert (if available) in settings and turn it on.","Turn on application lock (fingerprint/face); restrict strangers from joining groups in privacy.","Check \"Linked Devices\" once a week."],"stepsCaption":"Set up two-step verification for your account and bind your recovery email","caution":"Built-in functionality doesn’t stop you from proactively sending verification codes to scammers—discipline is still the first line of defense.","links":[{"label":"How to protect against suspicious messages (Help Center)","href":"https://faq.whatsapp.com/573786218075805"}]},"scamshield":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"ScamShield (Singapore Anti-Fraud Suite)","platforms":"iOS·Android","summary":"Singapore government-related anti-fraud tools: can help intercept suspicious calls/text messages and check suspicious messages. Local users strongly recommend installation.","why":["When the Singapore police report cases of WhatsApp account theft and fake money lending, they often directly recommend installing ScamShield.","It can filter some fraudulent text messages and calls and reduce the arrival rate of \"fraud phone calls/phishing text messages\".","The official website provides verification channels and early warning information."],"steps":["Only install from official short links or App Store / Google Play.","Follow the instructions to enable call/text message filtering related permissions.","If you encounter a suspicious request, you can verify it by combining its verification function and hotline channels."],"stepsCaption":"Download from the official store and enable filtering permissions","caution":"It may not be applicable to non-Singapore numbers/environments; do not download fake \"ScamShield\" copycat packages.","links":[{"label":"ScamShield official website description","href":"https://www.scamshield.gov.sg/about-scamshield/what-is-scamshield/"},{"label":"iOS download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-ios"},{"label":"Android download (official jump)","href":"https://go.gov.sg/ss-android"}]},"scameter":{"tier":"Regional recommendations","name":"Scameter / Scameter+ (Hong Kong anti-fraud video app)","platforms":"Website · App (subject to police announcement)","summary":"The fraud detection tool promoted by the Hong Kong police can be used to verify suspicious platforms, accounts, etc. Hong Kong users are recommended to use it together.","why":["WhatsApp accounts for a very high proportion of \"online account hijacking\" cases in Hong Kong, and the police continue to call for the use of official anti-fraud tools.","Verification before transferring money or clicking can reduce secondary financial losses after account theft."],"steps":["Enter Scameter through Hong Kong Police CyberDefender / official announcement channels.","Check unfamiliar payment accounts and suspicious websites before proceeding.","At the same time, enable two-step verification on WhatsApp and inventory associated devices."],"stepsCaption":"Official portal to verify suspicious links or accounts","caution":"Verification tools cannot replace \"not giving a verification code\"; account hacking itself still depends on account settings and habits.","links":[{"label":"Hong Kong government related anti-fraud information portal","href":"https://www.cyberdefender.hk/"}]},"play-protect":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Google Play Protection Mechanism/System Security Update","platforms":"Android·iOS","summary":"Let the system and app store block a large number of malicious installation packages for you, reducing the risk of Trojans stealing text messages and screens.","why":["Some stolen accounts will cooperate with malicious apps to read notifications or assist in operations.","Timely system patches can narrow the window of known vulnerabilities."],"steps":["Android: Turn on Google Play Protect; only allow installation from official stores.","iOS/Android: Turn on automatic updates and install system patches regularly.","Refuse the temporary temptation to allow installation from unknown sources."],"stepsCaption":"Turn on system protection and keep it updated","caution":"System protection cannot stop social engineering deception; it is the equipment layer, not the language layer.","links":[{"label":"Google Play Protect instructions","href":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2812854"}]},"password-manager":{"tier":"recommend","name":"Password manager (safeguards 2-step verification PINs)","platforms":"Cross-platform","summary":"Used to safely store WhatsApp two-step verification PIN and email password to avoid setting weak passwords or writing them in chats.","why":["Many people set up 123456 for fear of forgetting, or send their PIN to another chat backup - which is even more dangerous.","A legitimate password manager can generate and encrypt strong passwords."],"steps":["Install trusted products such as Bitwarden, 1Password, or the system's own keychain/password from the official store.","Create a separate record for WhatsApp 2-step verification PIN.","The password manager itself turns on strong master passwords and biometrics."],"stepsCaption":"Safely store PINs with a password vault","caution":"Do not use unknown \"PIN to save the App\"; do not save the PIN in the unencrypted memo cloud and synchronize it everywhere.","links":[{"label":"Bitwarden official website","href":"https://bitwarden.com/"}]},"mobile-antivirus":{"tier":"Optional","name":"Regular mobile phone security software (anti-Trojan, not anti-fraud)","platforms":"Android-based","summary":"If you often sideload applications or the device environment is complex, you can choose a security suite from a well-known manufacturer; it mainly protects against malware, not against acquaintances.","why":["Can assist in discovering suspicious applications and phishing sites (capabilities vary by product).","It has certain value against threats such as \"SMS Stealing Trojans\"."],"steps":["Only install well-known products from Google Play / App Store (like Malwarebytes, Bitdefender, etc., select as per availability in your region).","After installation, do a full scan and review applications with abnormal SMS/accessibility permissions.","Don't stack multiple conflicting security suites at the same time."],"stepsCaption":"Scan the entire disk after installation in the official store","caution":"No anti-virus can prevent you from handing over the verification code; nor can you download the nameless APK of \"WhatsApp Killer and Stealer\".","links":[{"label":"Malwarebytes official website","href":"https://www.malwarebytes.com/"}]}},"toolsAvoid":["The installation package whose name contains \"WhatsApp Anti-Theft/Retrieval Master/Crack Monitor\" requires turning off Play Protection","Requires you to send the verification code to the \"customer service robot\" to activate the protection tool","A service that charges a fee for \"retrieval of account number\" and requests for remote control or registration code","Mysterious APKs from network disks, Telegram channels, and short video comment areas"]}};
